window.AW_IDX=[{"p":"one","t":"What the Hectare One system does","h":"","u":"en/one/overview.html","x":"The seeding control system with the Hectare One panel in one page — what it watches, what it drives, and what it records."},{"p":"one","t":"What the Hectare One system does","h":"","u":"en/one/overview.html","x":"The seeding control system with the Hectare One panel is made by Agriwelt. It watches sowing and the application of dry and liquid fertilizer on mounted and trailed implements — seed drills, planters, cultivators — and it drives the dispensers and the pump so that the set rate is actually applied.\nThe Hectare One panel: the operator's single point of contact with the system.\nThe panel sits in the cab. Everything else is on the implement: one seed sensor per section, and whatever the machine needs besides — see What is supplied."},{"p":"one","t":"What the Hectare One system does","h":"Seed","u":"en/one/overview.html#seed","x":"Applies seed to the rate the operator sets, through the dispenser electric motors — on row-crop planters and on grain drills.\nWatches the actual sowing against that rate and reports a section that falls behind.\nReports a blocked or empty tube by its number.\nCounts seeds, doubles and skips — on row-crop planters.\nAccepts a section shut-off signal from a third-party navigation system.\nSeed sensor for round seed pipes, 25–35 mm."},{"p":"one","t":"What the Hectare One system does","h":"Dry and liquid fertilizer","u":"en/one/overview.html#dry-and-liquid-fertilizer","x":"Applies liquid fertilizer to the rate through the electric pump.\nWatches dry and liquid lines and reports a section that falls behind or blocks, by number."},{"p":"one","t":"What the Hectare One system does","h":"The machine around the product","u":"en/one/overview.html#the-machine-around-the-product","x":"Hopper level, dispenser shaft rotation, fan speed, air pressure or vacuum, travel speed — each against its own limits.\nThe sensors of an autonomous fan-drive engine: oil pressure, battery charge, ignition, fuel level, air filter.\nWorking or transport position of the implement."},{"p":"one","t":"What the Hectare One system does","h":"Tramline","u":"en/one/overview.html#tramline","x":"Creates the tramline with actuators or by stopping dispenser motors.\nAdvances the bout by itself from the marker sensors.\nKeeps watching the sections that are shut off, so that a section leaking into the tramline is still reported."},{"p":"one","t":"What the Hectare One system does","h":"Records","u":"en/one/overview.html#records","x":"Treated area, separated into what was sown and what was lost to skips.\nDetailed statistics of the run, and reports written to the memory card.\nFirmware of the devices in the system, updated from the same card.\nNOTE\nThe system applies one product in the “Precise” mode (seeds per metre), up to four products by mass (kilograms per hectare) and one liquid product (litres per hectare).\nSEE ALSO\nThe panel and its seven buttons · Quick start · Terms"},{"p":"one","t":"What is supplied","h":"","u":"en/one/whats-supplied.html","x":"The minimum system, the optional devices a machine can carry, and the cables that connect them."},{"p":"one","t":"What is supplied","h":"","u":"en/one/whats-supplied.html","x":"The minimum system is the Hectare One panel and the seed sensors — one sensor per section. Agriwelt seed sensors have their own controller and are connected to the panel directly.\nThe Hectare One panel.\nEach section of the implement gets its own seed sensor.\nSeed sensor for round seed pipes, 25–35 mm."},{"p":"one","t":"What is supplied","h":"Optional devices","u":"en/one/whats-supplied.html#optional-devices","x":"Everything else is optional. What a machine carries is written into the panel's profile.\nDevice\nHow many\nWhat it is for\nPressure sensor\nUp to three\nPositive pressure or vacuum.\nHUB8 input device\nAs needed\nSensors of the “open collector” kind — speed, hopper, fan, dispenser, work position, markers, Wilger flow meters.\nDispenser electric motors\nWith their controller\nApplying seed or dry fertilizer to the rate.\nTramline actuators\nUp to four\nShutting sections off for the tramline.\nElectric pump controller\nOne\nLiquid fertilizer — UAN, micro-fertilizers and the like.\nThe inductive sensor below serves two jobs: it measures the travel speed, and it watches that a dispenser shaft is turning.\nInductive sensor for speed and for dispenser shaft rotation.\nThe HUB8 takes sensors that have no controller of their own and reports each of them to the panel as if it were a device.\nHUB8, the eight-channel input device for contact sensors.\nOne controller design drives the dispenser motors and the liquid fertilizer pump. That is why a machine cannot have a pump without also having the controller a motor would use.\nThe electric motor controller; the same unit drives the liquid fertilizer pump.\nNOTE\nThe panel also works with seed sensors made by DICKEY-John, Kuhn, Gaspardo and others."},{"p":"one","t":"What is supplied","h":"Cables","u":"en/one/whats-supplied.html#cables","x":"The panel takes its power through its own supply cable.\nPower supply cable of the Hectare panels.\nA HUB8 is joined to the system by a 4-pin Amphenol network cable.\nHUB8 network connection cable, Amphenol, 4-pin.\nThe sensors plug into the HUB8 through a harness.\nHUB8 sensor connection harness.\nSEE ALSO\nWhat the system does · Terms"},{"p":"one","t":"Safety","h":"","u":"en/one/safety.html","x":"What the panel can set in motion, who may operate it and from where, and how to handle the panel itself. Read before the first switch-on."},{"p":"one","t":"Safety","h":"","u":"en/one/safety.html","x":"Knowing these rules is the precondition for working with the system safely and without interruption. This manual is part of the delivery: keep it on the machine or in the tractor cab."},{"p":"one","t":"Safety","h":"The panel drives machinery","u":"en/one/safety.html#the-panel-drives-machinery","x":"The panel is not only an indicator. The dispenser electric motors, the liquid fertilizer pump and the tramline actuators are started by commands from the panel, and several of those commands work while the machine is standing still.\nWARNING\nMachinery starts while the machine stands still. Three things turn the metering shafts at a standstill: “Fill the disks with seeds” in the main menu, and “Fill the rotor cells” and “Calibration” in the mass calibration of an electric motor. On a motor with a calibration button wired to its controller, that button starts the shaft from the machine itself, out of sight of the cab. Before you use any of them, make sure nobody is near the metering units, the dispenser shafts, the fan or the tramline actuators. Filling the disks turns every disk at once, for up to about twenty seconds per motor.\nSpeed imitation makes the machine meter product without moving. With imitation switched on, the panel calculates as if the machine were driving and drives the dispenser motors and the pump accordingly. Switch imitation off before you leave the cab.\nBefore you approach the implement, stop the machine, switch the tractor ignition off and take the key with you. Do not rely on what the panel shows: a device whose control has been switched off reports nothing about its state.\nIMPORTANT\nAbove the Operator access level the panel shows no alarm messages. A blocked tube, an empty hopper or a failed section is not reported. Return to Operator before working, or switch the panel off and on. See Access levels."},{"p":"one","t":"Safety","h":"The operator's workplace","u":"en/one/safety.html#the-operators-workplace","x":"The system is operated by one person, from the driver's seat of the tractor. Do not operate the panel from outside the cab while the tractor is able to move or while anyone is near the implement."},{"p":"one","t":"Safety","h":"Intended use","u":"en/one/safety.html#intended-use","x":"The system is intended solely as a monitoring and control device for sowing and for the application of dry and liquid fertilizer on agricultural machines — mounted and trailed seed drills, planters and cultivators. Following every instruction in this manual is part of intended use. Any other use is not intended use; the manufacturer is not liable for damage arising from it, and the risk is borne by the user alone.\nBesides this manual, observe the national safety and environmental rules where the machine is worked, and the safety instructions of the implement and of the tractor."},{"p":"one","t":"Safety","h":"Handling the panel","u":"en/one/safety.html#handling-the-panel","x":"Do not subject the panel to impact or heavy vibration, and do not drop it.\nDo not touch the screen with sharp objects; they damage the touch surface.\nDo not open the housing. It is sealed and carries a pressure equaliser; opening it destroys the seal. Repairs are made by Agriwelt service only.\nKeep the harness connector, the GPS antenna connector and the memory card slot closed and clean when nothing is plugged in.\nClean with a damp cloth — no solvents, no pressure washer.\nConnect the panel only to a supply that meets the parameters in the device passport. Reversed polarity or a voltage outside the permitted range damages the electronics.\nDisconnect the panel before electric welding on the tractor or the implement."},{"p":"one","t":"Safety","h":"Warranty and liability","u":"en/one/safety.html#warranty-and-liability","x":"The general terms of sale and delivery of Agriwelt GmbH apply. Claims for warranty and for damage to persons or property are not accepted where the cause is use other than as intended; improper installation, commissioning or operation; disregard of this manual on commissioning, operation and maintenance; or unauthorised modification of the equipment."},{"p":"one","t":"Safety","h":"Signal words in this manual","u":"en/one/safety.html#signal-words-in-this-manual","x":"Signal word\nWhat it means\nDANGER\nAn immediate threat with a high degree of risk. Ignoring it leads to death or the severest injuries.\nWARNING\nA possible threat with a medium degree of risk. Ignoring it can lead to death or severe injuries.\nCAUTION\nA threat with a low degree of risk. Ignoring it can lead to minor or moderate injuries.\nIMPORTANT\nNo threat to people. Ignoring it damages equipment or costs work that has to be done again.\nNOTE\nInformation that helps you get more out of the system.\nTIP\nA shortcut or a better way of doing something.\nSEE ALSO\nA pointer to the topic where the subject is described in full. Not a safety instruction."},{"p":"one","t":"The panel and its seven buttons","h":"","u":"en/one/front-panel.html","x":"What is on the front and on the back of the Hectare One, and what each of the seven buttons does."},{"p":"one","t":"The panel and its seven buttons","h":"","u":"en/one/front-panel.html","x":"The Hectare One is operated with seven backlit buttons under the screen and with touches on the screen itself. Everything can be done with the buttons alone — with gloves on, on a rough field.\nScreen. 800 × 480 colour screen with a touch surface. Shown here: the home screen.\n“HOME”. Moves between the working screens. Held down on the home screen, it locks and unlocks product application.\n“MINUS”. Decreases the selected value.\n“UP”. Moves the selection to the previous item of a menu.\n“MENU”. Opens the main menu from the home screen. In a menu it enters the selected item; in a dialogue it confirms.\n“DOWN”. Moves the selection to the next item of a menu.\n“PLUS”. Increases the selected value.\n“BACK”. Leaves a menu or a screen; in a dialogue it cancels. On the home screen it opens the tramline bout menu.\nThe front of the panel. The buttons are always in this order.\nTIP\nThe backlight tells you which buttons work. The panel lights only the buttons that do something on the screen you are looking at; a dark button does nothing there. On the number pad “MENU” lights green — accept — and “BACK” red — cancel.\nWhat each button does on each screen is listed in Buttons and gestures."},{"p":"one","t":"The panel and its seven buttons","h":"The back of the panel","u":"en/one/front-panel.html#the-back-of-the-panel","x":"The back: mounting plate with ball joint, harness connector, GPS antenna connector and microSD slot.\nThere are no controls on the back. One harness connector of up to fourteen contacts carries everything to the panel. The two raised round caps are the GPS antenna connector and the dust-protected slot of the microSD card; the small opening is the pressure equaliser of the sealed housing. The ball joint lets you turn and tilt the panel freely on a flat surface in the cab. For mounting on a tube, an additional bracket is available."},{"p":"one","t":"Switching on","h":"","u":"en/one/switching-on.html","x":"What the panel does after power-on, which access level it starts at, and what a long splash screen means."},{"p":"one","t":"Switching on","h":"","u":"en/one/switching-on.html","x":"After the power is switched on, a splash screen with the logo appears. It stays until the panel has found every device connected to it — a few seconds — and then the home screen opens.\nNOTE\nIf the splash screen stays much longer than usual, the panel is waiting for a device that does not answer. Nothing is broken yet — but look at the alarms as soon as the home screen appears. See How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Switching on","h":"What state the panel starts in","u":"en/one/switching-on.html#what-state-the-panel-starts-in","x":"Access level: Operator. The panel starts at the Operator level after every power-on, whatever level was set before. Switching off and on is therefore also the quickest way back to Operator — the only level at which alarm windows are shown. See Access levels.\nThe ordinary home screen. Even on a machine without seed sensors the panel starts on the ordinary home screen; the second home screen is opened by hand."},{"p":"one","t":"Switching on","h":"Switching off","u":"en/one/switching-on.html#switching-off","x":"The statistics are written every five minutes and when the machine stops. Switch the panel off only after you have stopped moving — otherwise the last stretch of work is lost from the statistics."},{"p":"one","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"","u":"en/one/interface-basics.html","x":"The five things everything on the panel is done through — working screens, the main menu, the context menu of a device, the number pad, and the windows that ask or tell."},{"p":"one","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"","u":"en/one/interface-basics.html","x":"Everything on the panel happens through five things: the working screens, the main menu, the context menu of a device, the number pad and the windows that ask or tell you something. The working screens have their own topic: Working screens."},{"p":"one","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"The main menu","u":"en/one/interface-basics.html#the-main-menu","x":"Press “MENU” on the home screen, or tap the screen with two fingers. The main menu opens over the home screen.\nSelected item. Black text on a light blue band. “UP” and “DOWN” move the band; “MENU” enters the item. Here “Fill the disks with seeds” — see Fill the disks.\n“Settings”. The settings of the system and of the machine. See The Settings menu.\nArrow head. The item opens another menu.\n“Service”. Commissioning and service items. See Service menu.\n“Information”. Opens the screen with the information about the system.\nThe main menu of a planter with dispenser motors in the “Precise” mode, standing still.\nIn any menu, an item is read by what stands on its right:\nWhat you see on the right\nWhat it means\nAn arrow head\nThe item opens another menu. Press “MENU”.\nA value between - and +\nChange it with “PLUS” and “MINUS”, right there in the list.\nA value between -[** and **]+\n“PLUS” or “MINUS” opens the number pad.\nNothing\nThe item performs an action. Press “MENU”.\nA menu page with values: the year is edited on the number pad, the other values in the list.\n“BACK” leaves a menu and returns one level up."},{"p":"one","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"The context menu of a device","u":"en/one/interface-basics.html#the-context-menu-of-a-device","x":"Every sensor, motor and indicator on the home screen has its own menu. Touch and hold the device — its icon or its column on the diagram — and its context menu opens. On the Motors screen and on the second home screen the same works for a motor tile.\nThe context menu of a seed sensor: information, control on or off, and the parameters of the sensor.\nThe first item, “Information”, opens the status screen of the device. The second switches its control: “Disable control” or “Enable control” — see Take a sensor into or out of control. Below them stand the parameters; which ones you see depends on the access level (Access levels)."},{"p":"one","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"Entering numbers","u":"en/one/interface-basics.html#entering-numbers","x":"When a value is written between square brackets, changing it opens the number pad.\nName of the parameter. Here “Width, cm”.\nValue. What you have typed so far.\nBackspace. Deletes the last digit. Touch and hold it to clear the whole value.\nDigits. 1 to 9 and 0.\nDecimal point and minus. A touch adds the point; touch and hold switches the minus sign, where a negative value is allowed.\nZero.\n“OK”. Accepts the value. “MENU” does the same.\nThe number pad. “BACK”, or a touch outside the pad, leaves without changing anything.\nA value outside the permitted range is not taken as typed: the panel sets the nearest limit instead."},{"p":"one","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"Windows that ask, and windows that tell","u":"en/one/interface-basics.html#windows-that-ask-and-windows-that-tell","x":"Every window has the same shape: one box in the middle of the screen, a coloured strip along its top with a word in it, the message under the strip, and the buttons — if any — along the bottom. Read the strip first:\nStrip\nWhat kind of window\nHow it closes\nYellow, “Confirmation”\nThe panel asks. Nothing happens until you answer.\nTouch “Yes” (green) or “No” (red). “PLUS” answers yes, “MINUS” no, “BACK” cancels.\nBlue, “Information”\nThe panel tells you what happened.\nSome close by themselves; others close with a touch, “PLUS” or “BACK”.\nRed, “FAILURE!”\nAn alarm.\nSee below.\nA confirmation window: the yellow strip, the question, Yes and No.\nThe information window below closes by itself.\nAn information window: the blue strip, the word Information, the message Done."},{"p":"one","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"Alarm windows","u":"en/one/interface-basics.html#alarm-windows","x":"An alarm window says what happened, on what kind of device, and which devices — their numbers are listed when several identical sensors report the same fault at once. While it is open, the panel beeps.\nWhat you do\nWhat happens\nTouch “Close” in the window, or press “HOME”\nThis alarm closes. The next one in the queue appears.\nHold “HOME”\nThe window closes and the panel returns to the home screen.\nThe other six buttons are dark and do nothing while an alarm window is open.\nIMPORTANT\nClosing the window stops the sound, not the fault. Alarms arrive one at a time and are closed one at a time. What each alarm means is in How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"","u":"en/one/quick-start.html","x":"The order of getting the machine ready, what to check before the field, what happens when you move off, and what to watch while working."},{"p":"one","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"","u":"en/one/quick-start.html","x":"Your machine may not have every step — skip what it does not have."},{"p":"one","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"Before the field","u":"en/one/quick-start.html#before-the-field","x":"Calibrate the speed sensor. Area, rates and deviations are all calculated from the speed; a wrong speed makes every other figure wrong. “Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters” → “Speed calibration”, at Agronomist level. See Calibrate the speed sensor.\nCalibrate the metering rollers — on grain drills, and on planters whose fertilizer dispenser is driven by an electric motor. From the context menu of each dispenser or motor, at Operator level, standing still. See Mass calibration of a motor.\nCheck the tramline rhythm, if the machine makes tramlines. See Set up the tramline rhythm.\nCheck the sensor parameters — the list is below.\nCheck each section: is it under control, does it apply product. See Four states of a section.\nChoose the field on the Statistics screen. For a new field, erase its statistics first. See Statistics screen.\nSet the rates — seeding, dry fertilizer, liquid fertilizer. See Change the target rate.\nCheck the access level. It must be Operator.\nSteps 1 and 2 are not at the same level. “Seeding machine parameters” and the speed calibration under it are Agronomist; the mass calibrations in the context menus are Operator. The usual order: raise the level to Agronomist (Enable the Agronomist level), do step 1, drop back to Operator, do the rest.\nIMPORTANT\nStep 8 is the one that ruins a day. From Agronomist level upwards the panel suppresses every alarm but one: a blocked tube is not reported. The one that still comes through is “User is Agronomist! Alarms will not be shown!” — raised once, when seeding starts at that level. Dropping back to Operator raises the suppressed alarms again. Check the level before every start, not once a season.\nSensor parameters to check\nSpeed sensor — “Min speed, km/h”, “Max speed, km/h”.\nDispenser sensor — “Period, s”, for each sensor.\nFan sensor — “Minimum, rpm”, “Maximum, rpm”, for each sensor.\nPressure sensor — “Minimum, Pa”, “Maximum, Pa”, for each sensor.\nLiquid fertilizer pump — “Pulses per liter”, “Max pressure, Bar”.\nSeed and dry fertilizer sensors — “Fraction”, “Correction”, “Recalculation,m” or “Recalculation,sec”; in the “Precise” or “Hybrid” mode also “Rate error,%”.\nLiquid fertilizer sensors — “Rate error,%” and, without a pump in the profile, “Pulses per liter”. Wilger flow meters: red 8900, green 15500, blue 3400 pulses per liter.\nElectric motors, “Precise” mode — “Holes in disk”, for any motor in the group.\nElectric motors, mass dosing — for each motor, “Weight, gr” divided by “Rotations” against the roller specification. The mass calibration fills both in.\nMost parameters are set per sensor. In the seed, dry fertilizer and liquid fertilizer sensor groups and in both motor groups, most parameters are common to the whole group — except sensor control and product application, which stay individual. Details: Sensor settings."},{"p":"one","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"Moving off","u":"en/one/quick-start.html#moving-off","x":"Put the implement into the working position.\nPlanters with dispenser motors: fill the disks — main menu, “Fill the disks with seeds”. Without it the first metres of every row are empty. See Fill the disks.\nGrain drills: nothing to start. The metering motors begin turning by themselves as soon as the product application control starts — the moment you move off. Move off briskly all the same: the rotor cells have to fill before the first metres are sown at the target rate.\nDrive.\nWARNING\nFilling the disks and starting the motors turn machinery while the machine stands still. Make sure nobody is near the metering units, the dispenser shafts or the fan.\nWhen the control starts. The manual lock must be off (Lock application by hand). If a work position sensor is fitted and switched on, the control begins once you move with the implement lowered; without such a sensor, as soon as you move. The alarms start a few seconds later — the delay is “Settings” → “Alarm delay”, 2 to 20 seconds.\nSound\nWhat it means\nOne short beep\nThe panel sees the machine seeding. From now on the sections are watched.\nThree short beeps\nThe seeding has stopped — at the headland, or the columns have sunk under the control line.\nOn grain drills in the “Flow” or “Hybrid” mode, let the panel find the alarm level itself: Set the alarm level automatically."},{"p":"one","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"While working","u":"en/one/quick-start.html#while-working","x":"Watch four things: the speed against the tractor, the fan and the pressure inside their range, the actual rate against the target, and that no sensor is alarming. The Table screen shows how each section is doing right now; the Statistics screen how the whole field has gone.\nIf the speed sensor fails, see Work without the speed sensor."},{"p":"one","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"","u":"en/one/check-after-installation.html","x":"A one-time procedure for the installer — prove step by step that every device answers, every seed sensor sits in its own row and the machine reads correctly, then record the result."},{"p":"one","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"","u":"en/one/check-after-installation.html","x":"Run this check once, when the panel and the devices are mounted and connected. Each step says what to do and what the screen must show. If a step does not give the result, the last column of the table at the end says where to go.\nYour machine may not have every device — skip the steps for what it does not have. Stay at the Operator level: alarm windows are shown only there (Access levels).\nWARNING\nKeep clear of the fan, the dispenser shafts and the metering units while the machine is being tested. Switch the drives off before you reach into a seed tube."},{"p":"one","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"The check","u":"en/one/check-after-installation.html#the-check","x":"Check the profile and the devices. Switch the panel on and wait for the home screen. There must be no “Connection lost” alarm, no white indicator with an exclamation mark and no red indicator with an X. Check the profile against the machine: the devices and the number of each (The profile). Then open the status screen of every sensor (Sensor settings) and step through them with “PLUS”. Every sensor shows “Link:” “Normal”; every seed sensor shows Status: Working. See The sensor status screen.\nA white indicator is correct only for a device that is not in your delivery (The profile).\nCheck that every seed sensor sits in its own row. Open the Table screen with “HOME” and open the table with the seed counters (The Table screen). It shows the number of seeds; read the count there, not on the view with seeds per metre, which shows values only while the machine moves. Zero the readings (Zero the readings). Drop a few seeds by hand through the seed tube of row 1; instead of seeds you may pass any small object, for example a long cable tie, through the seed tube several times — each pass is counted. Only the line of section 1 must react. Do the same with row 2, then with every other row in turn. A sensor that reacts in another row is connected in the wrong order — call your installation partner or the service support.\nThe seed counter table of an eight-row planter: seeds per section, deviation bars, total.\nCheck the counting. Only if your Table screen has the seed counter table. Zero the readings again. Drop a known number of seeds, for example 20, through one tube. Read the figure of that section on the seed counter table, which shows the number of seeds. On a precision planter it matches the number you dropped to within one seed, provided the “Fraction” of the seed sensors is set for the size of the seed. If the figure is clearly higher — up to twice the number — the fraction is set too small for this seed: a large seed, for example maize, is then counted as two; set the fraction by the table in Seed and fertilizer sensors. If the two differ in another way, write both numbers down and give them to the support (Before you contact support).\nCheck the work position sensor. Only if the machine has one. Raise the implement, then lower it. The status screen of the sensor must change between Position: Transporting and Position: Working. If it shows the opposite, the setting “Transporting position signal” of the sensor is the wrong way round (Hopper, implement position and engine sensors).\nCheck the speed. Drive. The speed field on the home screen must show a number in white. XX.X in red means the input device of the speed sensor does not answer. A yellow figure alternating with “IMITATION” means the speed is simulated, not measured. “SLOWER” and “FASTER” only say that you are outside the permitted range (Indicators). If the figure does not match the real speed, calibrate the sensor (Calibrate the speed sensor). If the sensor fails, see Work without the speed sensor.\nCheck the other sensors the machine has. One short check each:\nHopper sensor. Its status screen shows Pin: and the level, “0” or “1”. With the hopper filled the panel must not raise “Hopper empty” (Hopper empty).\nFan sensor. With the fan running, its status screen shows F= in Hz and no alarm “Low speed of rotation” or “High speed of rotation” (Fan sensor).\nPressure sensor. With the fan running, its status screen shows P= in kPa and no alarm “High pressure” or “Low pressure” (Pressure sensor).\nDispenser shaft sensor. Turn the shaft. Its status screen shows F= in Hz and no alarm “No rotation” (Dispenser sensor).\nMarker sensors. Each shows “Link:” “Normal” on its status screen (Marker sensors).\nTramline actuator. Its status screen shows Status: Pulled in, Pulled out or Working — not Unknown (Tramline actuator).\nRecord the result.\nThe firmware version. Press “MENU” on the home screen and choose “Information” in the main menu. The window shows the firmware version. Write it down.\nWrite down the date, the machine and the number of rows.\nTake a photo of the status screen of a sensor, or of the sensors one after another, showing “Link:” “Normal”.\nKeep the notes and the photos. You need them if you must contact the support (Before you contact support)."},{"p":"one","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"If a step fails","u":"en/one/check-after-installation.html#if-a-step-fails","x":"Step\nWhat you see\nWhere to go\n1\n“Connection lost” or “No connection”\nAny device: connection lost or wrong device type\n1\n“Wrong device type!”\nAny device: connection lost or wrong device type\n1\nWhite indicator for a device you have\nTake a sensor into or out of control\n1\n“Clogged”, “Light struck”, “Broken”\nSeed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken\n1\nDevice missing from the profile\nThe profile\n2, 3\nWrong row reacts, or the count differs\nBefore you contact support\n4\nPosition does not change, or is reversed\nHopper, implement position and engine sensors\n5\nNo speed, or a wrong speed\nSpeed sensor alarms, Calibrate the speed sensor, Work without the speed sensor\n6\nHopper, fan, pressure, dispenser\nHopper empty, Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms, Air pressure too high or too low"},{"p":"one","t":"About the pictures in this manual","h":"","u":"en/one/about-the-pictures.html","x":"The screens in this manual were shot on a test bench. What on them belongs to the bench and not to your machine."},{"p":"one","t":"About the pictures in this manual","h":"","u":"en/one/about-the-pictures.html","x":"The screens in this manual are real frames from a Hectare One panel, firmware 1.2.3, shot on a test bench instead of a machine in the field. A few things on them belong to the bench. Your panel shows the same screens without them.\nWhat you see in a picture\nWhy it is there\nWhat your panel shows instead\nA blue 0:00 in the top right corner, or the word TRIAL\nThe bench panel runs on a trial licence. While a trial is active, the clock slot cycles between the time left, the word TRIAL and blank.\nThe time of day. See Clock.\nIMITAT in yellow in the clock slot\nThe bench runs in an imitation mode.\nThe time of day.\nA yellow speed figure, or IMITATION in the speed field\nThe speed is simulated, not measured — there is no wheel turning on a bench.\nThe measured speed in white. See Speed.\nColumns that move although the machine stands still\nThe seed flow is imitated by the sensors on the bench.\nColumns that move only while seeding.\nRed hoppers, a red oil can or battery\nThe bench has no product and no engine.\nGreen icons while everything is in order.\nA red A box at the lower right of the home screen\nActuators named in the bench profile are not physically fitted, so the panel reports them as faulty.\nNothing — the box is not shown while every actuator is in order. See Actuator failure.\nA red × in place of an icon\nA device named in the bench profile is not connected.\nThe icon of the device.\nZeros on the Statistics screen\nThe bench statistics were empty when the frame was taken.\nYour figures.\nAnimated pictures are series of frames taken from the panel about seven seconds apart. They show how the screen changes while seeding, not how fast.\nNOTE\nSome window shapes changed in September 2026: question, information and alarm windows were brought to one shape with a coloured strip across the top. Pictures in this manual show the new shape."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen","h":"","u":"en/one/home-screen.html","x":"What every part of the home screen is — speed, indicators, area, rates, the section diagrams — on a drill standing still and on a planter at work."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen","h":"","u":"en/one/home-screen.html","x":"The home screen is the main working screen. It shows the diagrams of the section control sensors, the state indicators of the devices and the key figures of the work. What it shows follows the profile: every group of sensors and devices entered there gets its tiles, its columns, its rate field."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen","h":"A grain drill with seed and fertilizer","u":"en/one/home-screen.html#a-grain-drill-with-seed-and-fertilizer","x":"Travel speed. In km/h. Messages about the speed replace the figure. Speed\nHoppers. One icon per hopper sensor, numbered. Red — the level is below the sensor. Hopper\nMetering units. Rotation of the dispenser shafts; the icon turns while the shaft turns. Metering unit\nEngine sensors. Oil pressure, battery charge and ignition of the fan-drive engine. Engine sensors\nClock. The time of day; the slot also carries messages. Clock\nMemory card. Shown while a microSD card is in the slot. Memory card\nFan. The fan icon and its speed in rpm. Fan\nMarkers. Which marker arm is down. Markers\nTramline bout. The current bout over the number of bouts in the rhythm. Tramline bout\nArea. Hectares treated on the active field. Area\nProduct rate by weight. The rate of product 1, in kg/ha. Product rate by weight\nSeed diagram. One column per seed sensor; the white line is the control line. The section diagram\nSection numbers. The number of each section, under its column.\nRate field. Measured rate on top, target below, in seeds per second here. Change the target rate\nImplement position. Here a red × — the work position sensor does not answer. Implement position\nFertilizer diagram. Sections 25 to 36, with their own rate field in fertilizer units per second.\nActuator failure. At least one motor or tramline actuator is in an alarm state. Actuator failure\nA drill with 24 seed rows and 12 fertilizer rows, standing still on the bench: targets set, actual rates zero."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen","h":"A planter at work","u":"en/one/home-screen.html#a-planter-at-work","x":"Travel speed. Yellow here, because it is simulated on the bench. Speed\nHopper. Green — product above the sensor.\nPressure. Vacuum or positive pressure in kPa. Pressure\nArea. Hectares treated on the active field.\nPopulation. The actual seeding rate in thousands of seeds per hectare. Population\nMemory card.\nSection column. Green — the row sows within its tolerance. Column colours\nSkips bar. Yellow hatching on the left of a column: the share of skips in that row.\nDoubles bar. Blue hatching on the right of a column: the share of doubles.\nRate line. The target rate across all rows.\nTolerance ticks. Upper and lower limit of the permitted deviation, in every column.\nRate field. Measured seeds per metre on top, target below.\nImplement position. Green — lowered, in work.\nSection numbers.\nAn eight-row planter in the “Precise” mode, seeding.\nWhat each indicator means in all its states is in Indicators; the diagram itself — line, ticks, colours — in The section diagram."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen","h":"What you can do from the home screen","u":"en/one/home-screen.html#what-you-can-do-from-the-home-screen","x":"To do this\nDo this\nOpen the main menu\n“MENU”, or tap with two fingers\nChange the target rate\n“PLUS” / “MINUS” — Change the target rate\nLock or unlock product application\nHold “HOME” — Lock application by hand\nOpen the tramline bout menu\n“BACK” — Next tramline bout\nLet the panel set the alarm level\nDouble tap, while applying — Set the alarm level\nOpen the settings of a device\nTouch and hold its icon or column\nGo to the next working screen\n“HOME”, or swipe left — Working screens\nOpen the second home screen\nSwipe right — Second home screen\nAll buttons on all screens: Buttons and gestures."},{"p":"one","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"","u":"en/one/section-diagram.html","x":"How to read the columns of the home screen in the \"Precise\" and the \"Flow\" mode, and what the control line means."},{"p":"one","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"","u":"en/one/section-diagram.html","x":"Each column of the diagram is one section — one seed or fertilizer sensor. Its height is the flow through that section. How the rest is drawn depends on the seeding control mode: “Precise” (seeds per metre, with skips and doubles) or “Flow” (seeds per second against an alarm level)."},{"p":"one","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"“Precise” mode","u":"en/one/section-diagram.html#precise-mode","x":"Target rate. Grey, in seeds per metre (s/m). This is the figure “PLUS” and “MINUS” change.\nMeasured rate. White — the average of the rows now.\nTolerance. The permitted deviation from the target, in per cent.\nRate line. The target, drawn across all rows.\nUpper tick. The upper limit of the tolerance, in each column.\nLower tick. The lower limit of the tolerance.\nColumn. Green — the row sows within the tolerance.\nSkips bar. Yellow hatching on the left edge of a column. The taller it is, the more skips in that row.\nDoubles bar. Blue hatching on the right edge of a column. The taller it is, the more doubles.\nSection number.\nAn eight-row planter seeding at about 5 seeds per metre against a target of 4.3. Rows 3, 6 and 7 skip; rows 5 and 7 double."},{"p":"one","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"“Flow” mode","u":"en/one/section-diagram.html#flow-mode","x":"Alarm level. Grey, in seeds per second (s/s). The level under which a section counts as losing flow. “PLUS” and “MINUS” change it.\nMeasured rate. White — the average of the rows now.\nControl line. The alarm level, drawn across all rows.\nColumn above the line. Green — the row works.\nEmpty part of a column. Dark grey above the green: the higher the green reaches, the more flow.\nSection numbers. Fertilizer sections continue after the seed sections.\nFertilizer diagram. The same, for the fertilizer sensors.\nFertilizer rate field. Measured on top, alarm level below, in fertilizer per second (f/s).\nA drill with 24 seed and 12 fertilizer rows, seeding at 12 km/h. Every column stands above its line."},{"p":"one","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"The line: rows under it are not under control","u":"en/one/section-diagram.html#the-line-rows-under-it-are-not-under-control","x":"The line is the boundary of a partial blockage, not a decoration. A column above the line is a row that works; a column that sinks under it is a row that is losing flow — long before it blocks completely.\n“NOT UNDER CONTROL”. The rows of this diagram are not watched.\nColumns under the line. Every row delivers less than the level.\nControl line. Dragged above the real flow.\nAlarm level. 18 seeds per second — more than the machine sows.\nMeasured rate. 15 seeds per second.\nThe same drill with a level set too high. Both diagrams have dropped out of control.\nRows under the line are not under control on purpose: the panel refuses to guard a level that was set above what the machine really delivers. Lower the level until the columns stand above the line, or let the panel set it: Set the alarm level automatically.\nWhether the machine is seeding at all, the panel decides by the share of rows that are seeding — a sensor setting, 20 % by default. On the headland the rows drop out of control; on the next pass they come back, with or without a work position sensor. The panel says so with sound: three short beeps when the seeding stops, one short beep when it starts again.\nIMPORTANT\nRead the columns against the line, not against each other. Above the line the section works. Down at the line it does not — whatever the neighbours do.\nWhat every colour and symbol of a column means, and the four states of a section: Column colours and section states."},{"p":"one","t":"Column colours and the four states of a section","h":"","u":"en/one/section-states.html","x":"What each colour and symbol of a column on the section diagram means, and the four combinations of control and product application a section can be in."},{"p":"one","t":"Column colours and the four states of a section","h":"","u":"en/one/section-states.html","x":"How the diagram is built — rate line, ticks, control line — is in The section diagram. This page lists what a single column can look like."},{"p":"one","t":"Column colours and the four states of a section","h":"Column colours","u":"en/one/section-states.html#column-colours","x":"What you see\nWhat it means\nGreen column\nThe row sows normally.\nBlue column\nThe row sows more than the target allows (“Precise” and “Hybrid”).\nYellow column\nThe row sows less than the target allows (“Precise” and “Hybrid”).\nBackground blinking dark red, no column\nThe row sows almost nothing while the machine is seeding — a blocked or empty tube.\nWhite column with !\nThe control of this section is switched off. Nothing about it is watched.\nRed ×\nThe sensor does not answer.\nRed ?, column blinking red\nA device of the wrong kind answers in place of this sensor.\nYellow ?\nThe sensor has just received its settings, or it counts seeds but the panel has no speed to judge them by.\nGrey hatching over the whole column\nThe section is shut off for the tramline, or its product application is switched off.\nGreen hatching at the bottom of a grey column\nA small flow in a shut-off section, still within what is tolerated.\nRed hatching in a grey column\nThe shut-off section sows into the tramline."},{"p":"one","t":"Column colours and the four states of a section","h":"The four states of a section","u":"en/one/section-states.html#the-four-states-of-a-section","x":"Each section has two independent switches: whether the panel watches it (control), and whether the machine applies through it (product application).\nControl\nApplication\nWhat it means\nWhat the column shows\non\non\nNormal work.\nThe normal column.\non\noff\nTramline: the row deliberately does not sow, and the panel knows it.\nGrey hatching.\noff\non\nThe row sows, but nobody is checking it.\nWhite with !\noff\noff\nThe section is out of the work altogether.\nWhite with !\nControl is switched in the context menu of the sensor: Take a sensor into or out of control. “Product application” is an item of the same menu, at the Agronomist level; in tramline mode the actuators or motors switch the sections themselves and this item is ignored."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen on different machines","h":"","u":"en/one/home-screen-examples.html","x":"The same panel on a planter, a drill and machines of 44 and 96 rows — standing still and at work — so you can recognise your own screen."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen on different machines","h":"","u":"en/one/home-screen-examples.html","x":"What the home screen shows follows the profile. The pictures below were taken on one panel, firmware 1.2.3, set up as different machines. Bench artefacts — the blue clock, the yellow speed — are explained in About the pictures."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen on different machines","h":"An eight-row planter, standing still","u":"en/one/home-screen-examples.html#an-eight-row-planter-standing-still","x":"Travel speed. Yellow: the speed is simulated, not measured.\nHoppers. The first green, the second red — that hopper reports empty.\nMetering units. Two dispenser shaft sensors.\nFan. With its speed in rpm beside it.\nArea. Hectares treated on the active field.\nPopulation. The actual seeding rate in thousands per hectare.\nMemory card. A microSD card is in the slot.\nTolerance. The permitted deviation in force, written above the diagram.\nTolerance ticks. Upper and lower limit, in every column.\nRate line. The target rate.\nSection column. Empty: the machine stands still.\nRate field. Measured seeds per metre on top, target below.\nManual lock. The yellow hand: product application is locked by hand. Lock application by hand\nActuator failure. A fault of the dispenser electric motors. Motors screen\nSection numbers.\nEight seed sensors, two dispenser electric motors, two hoppers, two dispenser shaft sensors, one fan; “Precise” mode."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen on different machines","h":"Three machines, standing still","u":"en/one/home-screen-examples.html#three-machines-standing-still","x":"The target rates are set, the actual rates are zero. Forty-four seed sensors in two rows of twenty-two, two hoppers, two dosing shafts, a fan, one rate field in seeds per second; the planter icon at the right is green — the machine is in working position.\nThe home screen of a 44-row machine, standing still.\nTwenty-four seed sensors and twelve fertilizer sensors: two diagrams, two rate fields. Along the top two hoppers, two dosing shafts, oil pressure, battery, ignition and a fan.\nThe home screen of a drill with 24 seed and 12 fertilizer rows, standing still.\nEight seed sensors in the “Precise” mode with a pressure sensor: one row of columns with the tolerance band, the rate in seeds per metre and the count per hectare.\nThe home screen of an eight-row planter with a pressure sensor, standing still."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen on different machines","h":"At work","u":"en/one/home-screen-examples.html#at-work","x":"The eight-row planter seeding. Each column is one row; the tops move with every count, the hatching shows skips and doubles. Read it with The section diagram.\nThe home screen in the \"Precise\" mode while seeding.\nThe 24-row drill at 12 km/h. The series starts with the columns below the line and “NOT UNDER CONTROL” over both diagrams; then the target is lowered, the columns rise above the line and the rows are under control again.\nThe home screen of the 24-row drill: from not under control to under control.\nThe 44-row machine at 12 km/h: two diagrams of twenty-two columns, hoppers full, dosing shafts turning.\nThe home screen of the 44-row machine while seeding.\nThe largest machine: 48 seed and 48 fertilizer rows, three hoppers, three dosing shafts, two fans. It, too, starts under the line and comes back under control when the target comes down to the real rate.\nThe home screen of a 96-row machine while seeding."},{"p":"one","t":"The home screen on different machines","h":"A real machine at work","u":"en/one/home-screen-examples.html#a-real-machine-at-work","x":"Shot on a machine with 48 seed sensors in two rows, two hoppers, two dispenser shaft sensors and one fan, while sowing. The rate field is selected for editing: its background is lit, the target is white and the measured value grey. Unselected, it is the other way round.\nThe home screen of a 48-row machine at work, with the rate field selected."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"","u":"en/one/indicators.html","x":"Every indicator of the home screen with all its states — what you see, what it means, what to do."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"","u":"en/one/indicators.html","x":"Where each indicator sits is shown in The home screen. This page says what each one means, state by state."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"How to read any indicator","u":"en/one/indicators.html#how-to-read-any-indicator","x":"Almost every indicator follows the same rule. Learn it once and you can read an indicator you have never seen.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nGreen icon\nNormal. The device answers and its value is inside the limits.\nRed icon, blinking\nAlarm. The device answers, but the value has left the limits. An alarm window follows after the alarm delay.\nIcon alternating with a red ×\nNo answer from the device. The value is shown as XXXX.\nIcon alternating with a white [!]\nThe device is in the profile, but its control is switched off. The value is shown as !!!!.\nIcon alternating with a red ?\nA device of the wrong kind answers in its place. The value is shown as ????.\nYellow icon\nNot a fault. A state you have chosen or that needs your attention: implement raised, markers up, ignition off, application locked by hand.\nIMPORTANT\nA switched-off indicator is not a quiet one — it is a blind one. White with !!!! means the panel does not watch that device at all. Nothing will be reported about it, however wrong it goes."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Speed","u":"en/one/indicators.html#speed","x":"The speed field at the top left.\nThe travel speed in km/h. Messages about the speed replace the figure and blink about once a second, so that the figure stays readable.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nA number in white, e.g. 10.0\nNormal.\n“SLOWER” on red\nYou drive faster than the maximum set for the speed sensor. Slow down.\n“FASTER” on red\nYou drive slower than the minimum. Speed up.\nXX.X in red\nNo answer from the input device that carries the speed sensor.\n???? in red\nA device of the wrong kind answers in place of the speed sensor.\nA yellow number alternating with “IMITATION”\nThe speed is simulated from the setting of the speed sensor, not measured. Nothing that depends on the speed is real. See Work without the speed sensor.\nINCREASE IMITATION / DECREASE IMITATION\nThe simulated speed itself is outside the permitted range. Change the setting, not the travel speed.\nSensor1 / Sensor2\nOne of two speed sensors has failed.\n“Settings”\nThe minimum and the maximum speed contradict each other.\n“SLOWER” and “FASTER” are not alarm windows. They stay in the speed field for as long as the speed is wrong, because you have to see them while correcting it. See Speed sensor alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Clock","u":"en/one/indicators.html#clock","x":"The clock slot at the top right is shared by several messages.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nThe time in white\nNormal.\nBAT in red, for about a second in every four\nThe clock battery is low. The clock still runs; between the flashes the time is shown in red. Have the battery replaced.\nXX:XX in red, blinking\nThe clock chip does not answer. The panel has no date and time; everything it records carries the wrong one.\nIMITAT in yellow\nThe imitation mode of the service menu is on.\nBlue hours:minutes, then TRIAL, then blank\nA trial period is running; the blue figure is the time left. When the first row has no room for the clock, this loop appears only now and then."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Memory card","u":"en/one/indicators.html#memory-card","x":"Shows that a microSD card is in the slot. Two states only: the icon is there, or it is not."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Hopper","u":"en/one/indicators.html#hopper","x":"Two hopper icons; both report an empty hopper.\nA full green hopper is normal. An empty red one means the level has fallen below the sensor: refill. See Hopper alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Metering unit","u":"en/one/indicators.html#metering-unit","x":"Two metering-shaft icons.\nThe rotation of the dispenser shaft. While the shaft turns, the icon turns — the fastest way to see that the metering unit really runs. Red means no rotation, or not the speed required. See Rotation alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Fan","u":"en/one/indicators.html#fan","x":"The fan icon with its speed in rpm.\nThe fan speed in revolutions per minute. Green is normal; red — above the maximum or below the minimum. The × and the [!] have the usual meaning."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Pressure","u":"en/one/indicators.html#pressure","x":"The vacuum or the positive air pressure in the distribution system, in kPa. Green is normal; red — above or below the permitted range. See Pressure alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Engine sensors","u":"en/one/indicators.html#engine-sensors","x":"Oil pressure, battery charge and ignition on the home screen.\nThe sensors of the engine that drives the fan.\nIndicator\nGreen\nRed or yellow\nOil pressure\nNormal\nRed: the oil pressure has fallen below the permitted level.\nBattery charge\nNormal\nRed: the battery is not being charged.\nIgnition\nGreen key: ignition on, the engine sensors are watched\nYellow key: ignition off. Not a fault — but the engine is not watched either.\nFuel level\nNormal\nRed: little fuel left in the tank.\nAir filter\nNormal\nRed: the air filter is clogged; clean or replace it.\nIMPORTANT\nOil pressure and battery charge are watched only while the ignition is on. With the ignition off, their faults are not reported. See Tractor and engine alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Area","u":"en/one/indicators.html#area","x":"The area treated on the active field.\nThe area treated on the active field, in hectares."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Population","u":"en/one/indicators.html#population","x":"In the “Precise” mode: the actual seeding rate in thousands of seeds per hectare (ths/ha)."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Product rate by weight","u":"en/one/indicators.html#product-rate-by-weight","x":"The product rate in kg/ha.\nShown when the dispensers apply products by weight; one indicator per product, with its number. It shows the rate in kg per hectare.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nIcon green\nNormal. The machine applies what was asked of it.\nIcon red, blinking\nThe actual rate is outside the permitted deviation from the target.\nField lit, figure blinking\nThe target is being set. Touch and hold the field to type the target on the number pad, or use “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\nField lit, not blinking\nSetting is on, but another rate field is the one being changed. This one shows its target and waits."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Dosing by weight is not possible","u":"en/one/indicators.html#dosing-by-weight-is-not-possible","x":"A red blinking product icon at the right edge. The panel has worked out that the target cannot be held at any travel speed: the lowest speed at which the metering rollers can deliver the rate is already at or above the highest.\nWhat to do. Check the target rates first — one product asking too much closes the window for all of them. Then check the metering rollers: a roller of another size may be needed. The speed limits the panel calculated are on the “Information” screen of the speed sensor. See Dosing by weight alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Markers","u":"en/one/indicators.html#markers","x":"What you see\nWhat it means\nGreen, both arms down\nBoth markers are lowered.\nGreen, one arm down\nOnly that marker is lowered — left or right, as drawn.\nYellow, both arms up\nBoth markers are raised. Not a fault; on a machine that changes the tramline bout by the markers, the bout changes now."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Implement position","u":"en/one/indicators.html#implement-position","x":"Green — lowered, in work. Yellow — raised, in transport, not applying."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Tramline bout","u":"en/one/indicators.html#tramline-bout","x":"Shown only if the tramline is switched on. The large number is the current bout, the small one under it the number of bouts in the rhythm.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nBout figures, e.g. 1 over 3\nHow far you are from the next tramline.\nTramline icon grey\nThe bout changes by itself.\nTramline icon yellow\nThe bout does not change by itself: automatic change is switched off, or the sensor it depends on is missing, silent or not under control. Change it by hand.\nSee Move to the next tramline bout."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Manual lock","u":"en/one/indicators.html#manual-lock","x":"A yellow hand, blinking, at the right edge: product application is locked by hand. The machine applies nothing, however it moves. Not shown at all while application is allowed. It appears on machines with electric dispenser motors or a liquid fertilizer pump. See Lock application by hand."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Actuator failure","u":"en/one/indicators.html#actuator-failure","x":"The actuator failure indicator at the lower right.\nOne icon for all electric motors and tramline actuators together. Not shown at all while everything is in order.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nRed, blinking\nAt least one motor or actuator is in an alarm state.\nAlternating with white [!]\nThe control of at least one actuator is switched off. No fault is known — but that actuator is not watched.\nWhich one and why is on the Motors screen."},{"p":"one","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Liquid fertilizer pump","u":"en/one/indicators.html#liquid-fertilizer-pump","x":"Depending on the equipment, the pump has two or three indicators.\nIndicator\nWhat it shows\nRate, l/min\nThe main indicator. Its context menu holds the pump parameters.\nLoad, %\nHow hard the pump works. It disappears first when the screen has no room for it.\nPressure, bar\nOnly if the pressure sensor is fitted and switched on in the pump controller settings.\nRate. Besides green, red, × and [!]: a yellow pump with STOP — application is locked by hand or the implement is raised, and the pump will not start when the machine moves; a lit target figure — there are no section flow sensors in the profile, so the figure is the target, not a measurement.\nLoad. At 99 % and above it turns yellow: the pump is at its limit and cannot follow more speed. Drive slower, or the rate falls below the target. ---- blinking red: the controller reports no load figure.\nPressure. Red means the liquid pressure is too high — reduce the speed, or raise “Max pressure, Bar” in the pump controller settings if the line can really take it. ---- blinking red: the pressure sensor is broken or gives no reading. See Pump controller alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"The second home screen","h":"","u":"en/one/second-home-screen.html","x":"The home screen for a machine without seed sensors — what is on it, how to open and close it, and what its chevrons and tiles do."},{"p":"one","t":"The second home screen","h":"","u":"en/one/second-home-screen.html","x":"This screen was made for a system without seed sensors: a machine where the seeding rate is held by the dispenser electric motors and the panel monitors the main working units. On such a machine the ordinary home screen would be almost empty.\nOpen it from the home screen with a swipe to the right. Close it with “HOME” or a swipe to the left. It is not part of the circle of working screens, and the panel does not switch to it by itself — even on a machine without seed sensors it starts on the ordinary home screen.\nFan. The fan and its speed in rpm.\nSeed spacing. In seeds per metre.\nTravel speed. In km/h; yellow while it is simulated. Speed\nArea. Hectares treated on the active field.\nSettings symbol. A touch opens the main menu, the same as “MENU”.\nRed chevrons. A touch takes the outermost motor on that side out of control.\nGreen chevrons. A touch takes the next motor towards that side back into control.\nTarget rate. In thousands of seeds per hectare. Touch and hold it, then swipe up or down to change it.\nMotor 1. Its number and value; the icon alternates with the state sign — here STOP, the motor is not running.\nMotor 2. A red × and XXX — no connection; the motor is not driven.\nManual lock. The yellow hand: application is locked by hand. Touch and hold it, or hold “HOME”, to lock or unlock.\nThe second home screen of a machine with two dispenser motors and one fan, standing still.\nEach motor icon alternates, about once a second, between the picture of the motor and the sign of its state. In the other blink phase the same screen looks like this — the motors are drawn as motor symbols, and the word “IMITATION” has replaced the speed figure because the speed is simulated:\nThe same screen in the other blink phase."},{"p":"one","t":"The second home screen","h":"What you can do here","u":"en/one/second-home-screen.html#what-you-can-do-here","x":"To do this\nDo this\nOpen the main menu\n“MENU”, or touch the settings symbol\nChange the seeding rate\nTouch and hold the rate, then swipe up or down. It stays within the permitted limits.\nTake motors out of or into control\nTouch the red or green chevrons at either end of the rate strip. Confirm with “Yes” if the panel asks.\nOpen the settings of a motor\nTouch and hold the motor\nLock or unlock product application\nHold “HOME”, or touch and hold the hand. A short beep confirms.\nReturn to the home screen\n“HOME”, or swipe left\nWhat the motor states mean: Motors screen."},{"p":"one","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"","u":"en/one/working-screens.html","x":"The four working screens in their circle, the second home screen beside it, and every way — button or swipe — to get from one to another."},{"p":"one","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"","u":"en/one/working-screens.html","x":"The panel has four working screens in a circle, and a fifth that stands apart.\nScreen\nWhat it is for\nHome screen\nThe diagrams of the sections, the indicators of the devices, the speed, the area and the rates. The panel starts here.\nTable\nThe readings of every section in figures, as a list. Several tables, depending on the control mode.\nStatistics\nThe results of the work by field: area, path, quantities, quality.\nMotors\nThe dispenser electric motors, and the tramline actuators if the machine has them. Present only if the profile holds at least one motor or tramline actuator.\nSecond home screen\nA home screen for machines without seed sensors. Outside the circle.\nThe Motors screen was called the “Actuators” screen in earlier documentation. On most machines it shows the dispenser electric motors, so this manual calls it Motors."},{"p":"one","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"The circle","u":"en/one/working-screens.html#the-circle","x":"“HOME” moves to the next screen, always in the same direction, and returns to the home screen from the last one:\nHome screen → Table → Statistics → Motors → Home screen.\nWithout motors and tramline actuators in the profile, there is no Motors screen and Statistics returns straight to the home screen."},{"p":"one","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"Every way from one screen to another","u":"en/one/working-screens.html#every-way-from-one-screen-to-another","x":"A swipe has to be a long one, with one finger, across the screen — a short movement is not taken as a transition.\nFrom\nAction\nTo\nHome screen\n“HOME”, or swipe left\nTable\nHome screen\nSwipe right\nSecond home screen\nTable\n“HOME”, or swipe left\nStatistics\nTable\n“BACK”, or swipe right\nHome screen\nStatistics\n“HOME”, or swipe left\nMotors (home screen, if the machine has none)\nStatistics\nSwipe right\nTable\nStatistics\n“BACK”\nHome screen\nMotors\n“HOME”, “BACK”, or swipe left\nHome screen\nMotors\nSwipe right\nStatistics\nSecond home screen\n“HOME”, or swipe left\nHome screen\nNOTE\nA swipe to the right does not always mean the same. On the home screen it opens the second home screen. On every other working screen it is the way back."},{"p":"one","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"Buttons or gestures","u":"en/one/working-screens.html#buttons-or-gestures","x":"Almost everything on the working screens can be done with a button or with a gesture.\nScreen\nWhat it does\nButton\nGesture\nHome screen\nOpens the main menu\n“MENU”\nTap with two fingers\nHome screen\nSets the alarm level from the real rate\n—\nDouble tap with one finger, while applying\nHome screen\nOpens the second home screen\n—\nSwipe right\nTable\nScrolls the rows\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nSwipe up or down\nTable\nPrevious or next table\n“MINUS” / “PLUS”\nLong swipe left or right with two fingers\nTable\nAsks whether to zero the readings\n“MENU”\nTap with two fingers\nStatistics\nScrolls the report\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nSwipe up or down\nStatistics\nShows another field\n“MINUS” / “PLUS”\nSwipe left or right with two fingers\nStatistics\nOpens the menu of the screen\n“MENU”\nTap with two fingers\nTwo actions on the working screens have no button: the swipe right that opens the second home screen, and the double tap that sets the alarm level. Everything else can be reached from the buttons. The full list per screen: Buttons and gestures."},{"p":"one","t":"The Table screen","h":"","u":"en/one/table-screen.html","x":"The readings of every section in figures — which tables your machine has, what each column means, and how to read the colours."},{"p":"one","t":"The Table screen","h":"","u":"en/one/table-screen.html","x":"The Table screen shows the readings of every section as figures, one line per section. It answers the question the diagram cannot: which section exactly, and by how much. Open it from the home screen with “HOME”; leave it with “BACK”."},{"p":"one","t":"The Table screen","h":"A planter in the “Precise” mode","u":"en/one/table-screen.html#a-planter-in-the-precise-mode","x":"#. The number of the section.\nsds/m. Seeds per metre, measured now.\nQ.%. Quality — the share of seeds placed singly, as intended.\nDb%. Doubles — two seeds where one was intended.\nSk%. Skips — a gap where a seed was intended.\nks/Ha. The applied rate in thousands of seeds per hectare (in the second table: seeds per metre).\n%. The deviation of this section from the rate that was set.\nTable number. This table and how many there are, between (−) and (+) — “MINUS” and “PLUS” switch tables.\nSection number on yellow. Something in this line has drifted towards its limit.\nValue on yellow. The figure that drifted — here the skips of section 7.\nTotals. The same columns for the whole machine.\nThe first of three tables of an eight-row planter, while seeding.\nA value on yellow is drifting towards its limit; on red it is beyond it. A whole line in grey with ! is a section taken out of control; a line in red with × is a sensor that does not answer, with ? a device of the wrong kind.\nNOTE\nRead the deviation column first. A section 20 % below the others has a problem the average will not show — a partly blocked outlet, a worn disk, a sensor that needs cleaning."},{"p":"one","t":"The Table screen","h":"A drill in the “Flow” mode","u":"en/one/table-screen.html#a-drill-in-the-flow-mode","x":"#. The number of the section.\nsds/s. Seeds per second. Dashes while nothing is counted.\nDeviation scale. From −50 % through 0 to +50 %.\nDeviation bar. How far this section stands from the rate that was set.\n%. The same deviation as a figure.\nTable number. Table 1 of 2 — the second holds the fertilizer sections.\nLowest deviation. The section furthest below the rate.\nHighest deviation. The section furthest above the rate.\nRecalculation interval. The stretch over which the figures are gathered — “Recalculation,m” of the sensors.\nThe seed table of a drill with 24 seed and 12 fertilizer rows, standing still."},{"p":"one","t":"The Table screen","h":"Which tables your machine has","u":"en/one/table-screen.html#which-tables-your-machine-has","x":"Table\nShown when\nSeed counters — seeds per second or per metre, with the deviation bars\nAlways.\nRate per area (thousands per hectare) and rate per distance (seeds per metre)\nOnly in the “Precise” seeding control mode.\nDry fertilizer\nThe machine has fertilizer sensors and the fertilizer control mode is not “Precise”.\nLiquid fertilizer\nThe machine has liquid fertilizer sensors.\nIn the “Precise” mode the planter above has three tables: rate per area (1/3), rate per distance (2/3), and the seed counters (3/3) — the count of seeds each section has sown, beside the deviation bars, with the total for the machine in thousands at the bottom.\nThe seed counter table of the eight-row planter: seeds per section, deviation bars, total."},{"p":"one","t":"The Table screen","h":"Working with the screen","u":"en/one/table-screen.html#working-with-the-screen","x":"What you want\nButton\nOr gesture\nNext or previous table\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nLong swipe left or right with two fingers\nScroll the lines\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nSwipe up or down\nZero the readings\n“MENU”\nTap with two fingers\nGo to Statistics\n“HOME”\nSwipe left\nBack to the home screen\n“BACK”\nSwipe right\nZeroing: Zero the readings."},{"p":"one","t":"The Table screen","h":"The table while seeding","u":"en/one/table-screen.html#the-table-while-seeding","x":"The lines change with every count; a line that drifts turns yellow.\nThe table of the 24-row drill while seeding, seeds and fertilizer.\nOn large machines the rows do not fit on one page: “UP” and “DOWN” scroll them.\nThe table of the 96-row machine while seeding."},{"p":"one","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"","u":"en/one/statistics-screen.html","x":"The report of the work on each field, block by block and line by line — and the menu that chooses the field, saves the report and erases it."},{"p":"one","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"","u":"en/one/statistics-screen.html","x":"The Statistics screen is the report of the work: how far you drove, how much you covered, how much you sowed, and how evenly. It is kept separately for each field and survives switching off. Open it with “HOME” from the Table screen (twice from the home screen); leave it with “BACK”.\nThe report is one long list. Scroll it with “UP” and “DOWN”, or swipe up and down."},{"p":"one","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"The head and the work on the field","u":"en/one/statistics-screen.html#the-head-and-the-work-on-the-field","x":"Field number. The field whose report you see. “MINUS” and “PLUS” — or a two-finger swipe — show another field.\n(A). This is the active field — the one the panel writes to now.\nDate and time. Today's date and the time of day.\nPath. Distance driven, in km.\nArea. Area covered, in ha.\nOperating time. Hours, minutes and seconds of work.\nMovement speed. The average travel speed.\nStarted / Ended. When the work on this field began and ended.\nSeeding. The next block — see below.\nThe head of the report of an eight-row planter in the “Precise” mode, empty."},{"p":"one","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"The seeding block","u":"en/one/statistics-screen.html#the-seeding-block","x":"Seeds sown (x1000). The total, in thousands of seeds.\nQuality. The share of seeds placed singly, as intended. Judge the work by this number.\nDoubles. Two seeds where one was intended.\nSkips. A gap where a seed was intended.\nNot sown. The share of the run when nothing came out at all.\nActual / Target. What was applied against what was set.\nPopulation, ths/ha. In thousands per hectare; the deviation in brackets.\nRate, s/m. In seeds per metre; the target on the right.\nSown / Not sown / Unknown. The field split three ways, in hectares.\nBar. The same split as a coloured bar: green sown, red not sown, grey unknown.\nSeed sensors. The next block — section by section.\nUnknown is the area driven while the panel could not judge — not a fault, but not proof of work either.\nNOTE\nA large deviation is not always a fault of the machine. Before changing anything on the drill, check that the speed the panel sees matches the real one — a wrong speed moves every rate figure with it."},{"p":"one","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"Section by section","u":"en/one/statistics-screen.html#section-by-section","x":"Seed sensors. One block per section follows.\nSeed sensor 1. The section number, and the seeds it counted.\nq d s n. Quality, doubles, skips and not sown for this section.\nSown / not sown / unknown. The share of each for this section (s, n, ?) and the bar.\nPopulation. Thousands per hectare for this section, with its deviation.\nSpacing. Seeds per metre for this section, with its deviation.\nThis is where an uneven drill shows itself. A section with a lower quality than the rest, or a longer red part of its bar, points at that row: a blocked outlet, a worn disk, a sensor that needs cleaning."},{"p":"one","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"Blocks that appear on other machines","u":"en/one/statistics-screen.html#blocks-that-appear-on-other-machines","x":"Block\nWhat it holds\nWork without alarms control\nThe part of the work the panel was not watching — driven with the control switched off or locked.\nSpeed control\nHow far and how long you drove outside the permitted speed range.\nFertilizing, Liquid fertilizer\nFertilized / not fertilized / unknown, if the machine applies them.\nProduct\nFor a product applied by weight: the quantity applied, and the actual against the target rate in kg/ha.\nThe report of a drill with a product applied by weight."},{"p":"one","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"The menu of the screen","u":"en/one/statistics-screen.html#the-menu-of-the-screen","x":"Press “MENU”, or tap with two fingers.\nThe menu of the Statistics screen.\nItem\nWhat it does\n“Select field”\nMakes the field shown the active one — the panel writes to it from now on. Shown only while you look at a field that is not active. A field whose statistics are not empty cannot be selected until its data is erased; the panel says so and asks.\n“Statistics”\nSwitches the report between short and full. The short report keeps the totals and drops the per-section detail.\n“Mileage”\nPath in km and area in ha over the whole life of the system. These totals cannot be reset. Without a speed sensor, the accumulated running time instead.\n“Save to SD-card”\nWrites one field, or all of them, to the memory card. Shown while a card is in the slot.\n“Zero statistics”\nErases the statistics.\nIMPORTANT\nThe statistics are also erased by something else. Changing the seeding control mode between “Precise” and the others asks for confirmation and, if you confirm, erases the statistics of every field. Save what you need to the memory card first."},{"p":"one","t":"The Motors screen","h":"","u":"en/one/motors-screen.html","x":"The dispenser electric motors and the tramline actuators of the machine, one tile each — how to read a tile and how to reach a motor's settings."},{"p":"one","t":"The Motors screen","h":"","u":"en/one/motors-screen.html","x":"The Motors screen shows the dispenser electric motors of the machine, one tile each, and the tramline actuators if the machine has any. Look here when a row stops sowing and you want to know whether the motor is the reason.\nIt exists on any machine whose profile holds at least one electric motor or one tramline actuator. Reach it with “HOME” from the Statistics screen; leave it with “HOME” or “BACK”.\nMotor 1, STOP. Yellow — the motor is serviceable, but product application is not running.\nValue of motor 1. The driven shaft speed in rpm, or seeds per metre in the “Precise” mode.\nMotor 2, red ×. No connection to this motor.\nXXX. No value, because the motor does not answer.\nThe Motors screen of a machine with two dispenser motors and no tramline actuators."},{"p":"one","t":"The Motors screen","h":"Reading a tile","u":"en/one/motors-screen.html#reading-a-tile","x":"The colour of the symbol is the state. The value beside it is the driven shaft speed in rpm — or, in the “Precise” control mode, the seeds per metre measured by the seed sensor of that row.\nSymbol\nValue\nState\nMotor, green\nthe figure\nNormal. The motor is under control and driven.\nSTOP, yellow\nthe figure\nThe motor is serviceable, but product application is switched off. Nothing is wrong with it — the system is simply not applying.\nMotor, red\nthe figure\nThe motor is in an alarm state. Read the alarm for the reason: Motor controller alarms.\n×, red\nXXX\nNo connection. Check the cable and the connector first.\n?, red\n???\nThe panel cannot tell what this device is: another kind of device answers in its place, or the controller has stayed in its firmware update mode.\n!, outlined\n!!!\nThe motor is taken out of control by the operator. It is neither driven nor watched.\nNOTE\nYellow is not a fault. A screen full of yellow STOP signs means application is off — at the headland, or locked by hand from the home screen. Red is a fault; yellow is a state you chose."},{"p":"one","t":"The Motors screen","h":"Tramline actuators","u":"en/one/motors-screen.html#tramline-actuators","x":"Actuator symbol\nState\nPulled in or pulled out, green\nThe actuator has reached its position.\nPulled in or pulled out, yellow\nThe actuator is moving to that position.\nAlternating between in and out, yellow\nThe panel cannot tell the actuator's position.\nBlinking red\nThe actuator is in an alarm state: Tramline alarms.\nBlinking red × or ?\nNo connection, or a device of the wrong kind.\nBlinking white\nThe actuator is taken out of control."},{"p":"one","t":"The Motors screen","h":"Working with the screen","u":"en/one/motors-screen.html#working-with-the-screen","x":"What you want\nButton\nOr gesture\nThe settings of one motor\n—\nTouch and hold its tile\nBack to the Statistics screen\n—\nSwipe right\nBack to the home screen\n“HOME” or “BACK”\nSwipe left\nThe long press opens the context menu of the motor you pressed. The same menu is reached from the home screen, but here you see every motor of the machine at once and can choose the one that misbehaves. See Dispenser motor settings."},{"p":"one","t":"Change the target rate","h":"","u":"en/one/set-the-target-rate.html","x":"Raise or lower the rate the panel holds and guards, from the home screen, while seeding — with the buttons or by touch."},{"p":"one","t":"Change the target rate","h":"","u":"en/one/set-the-target-rate.html","x":"The rate field at the right of each diagram shows two numbers: the measured rate on top, the target below it. In the “Flow” mode the target is the alarm level of the diagram — see The section diagram."},{"p":"one","t":"Change the target rate","h":"With the buttons","u":"en/one/set-the-target-rate.html#with-the-buttons","x":"On the home screen, press “PLUS” or “MINUS” once. A rate field is selected and starts to blink; nothing has changed yet.\nThe rate field at the right selected for editing: lit background, target in white.\nPress “PLUS” to raise the target or “MINUS” to lower it, one step per press.\nIf the machine has more than one rate field — seed and fertilizer, or products by weight — press “DOWN” or “UP” to move to the next or the previous one.\nPress “BACK” to finish. The field stops blinking and the new target is in force."},{"p":"one","t":"Change the target rate","h":"By touch","u":"en/one/set-the-target-rate.html#by-touch","x":"Touch and hold the rate field of a diagram. It lights up.\nSwipe up over the screen to raise the target, down to lower it — one step per swipe.\nTouch the screen outside the diagrams to finish.\nFor a product applied by weight, touching and holding its kg/ha indicator opens the number pad instead: type the target and touch “OK” (Entering numbers). On the second home screen, touch and hold the rate, then swipe.\nIMPORTANT\nColumns under the line are not guarded. If the target is set far above what the machine really delivers, the columns sink under the control line and the panel shows “NOT UNDER CONTROL”. On a grain drill, let the panel find the level: Set the alarm level automatically."},{"p":"one","t":"Lock product application by hand","h":"","u":"en/one/lock-application-by-hand.html","x":"Stop all product application with one long press, and start it again — what the lock does and how to see that it is on."},{"p":"one","t":"Lock product application by hand","h":"","u":"en/one/lock-application-by-hand.html","x":"The manual lock stops all product application: the dispenser motors and the pump do not run, however the machine moves. Use it to cross a field already sown, to drive on the road with the implement lowered, or when you work without the speed sensor.\nOn the home screen, hold “HOME” until the panel beeps once.\nCheck the right edge of the screen. A yellow hand blinks there: application is locked.\nTo unlock, hold “HOME” again. One short beep; the hand disappears.\nOn the second home screen the same works with “HOME”, or by touching and holding the hand.\nIMPORTANT\nThe lock is announced by the screen only. Look for the yellow hand before you start a pass. A locked machine drives a whole field and sows nothing."},{"p":"one","t":"Lock product application by hand","h":"What the lock changes elsewhere","u":"en/one/lock-application-by-hand.html#what-the-lock-changes-elsewhere","x":"The motor tiles on the Motors screen show a yellow STOP — serviceable, not applying.\nThe pump rate indicator shows a yellow pump with STOP (Liquid fertilizer pump).\nThe product application control does not start when you move off — the lock must be off for the control to start (Quick start).\nThe work done while locked is counted in the statistics as Work without alarms control (Statistics).\nThe yellow hand is shown on machines with electric dispenser motors or a liquid fertilizer pump."},{"p":"one","t":"Set the alarm level automatically","h":"","u":"en/one/set-the-alarm-level.html","x":"Let the panel set the alarm level of the diagrams from the rate the machine really sows, instead of guessing it — grain drills in the \"Flow\" or \"Hybrid\" mode."},{"p":"one","t":"Set the alarm level automatically","h":"","u":"en/one/set-the-alarm-level.html","x":"On a grain drill the seeds arrive too close together to be counted one by one, so the panel guards each row against an alarm level: the line on the diagram. Set it too high and every row sinks under the line — “NOT UNDER CONTROL”. Set it too low and a half-blocked row goes unnoticed. The panel can set it for you from what the machine really sows.\nThis works in the “Flow” and “Hybrid” modes, for the seed and the dry fertilizer diagrams, and for liquid fertilizer in the “Flow” mode.\nStart seeding at your normal speed, with product flowing through every row.\nDouble tap the home screen with one finger.\nThe panel beeps once and lights the rate field: the new alarm level is set from the rate being sown now.\nThe diagrams with the columns above the line.\nCheck that every column stands above its line.\nThe double tap works only while product is being applied. Standing still, nothing happens.\nTIP\nRepeat it after a change of seed lot or speed. The level follows the rate at the moment of the tap. You can also correct it by hand with “PLUS” and “MINUS” — see Change the target rate.\nWhat the line means and why rows under it are not watched: The section diagram."},{"p":"one","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"","u":"en/one/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html","x":"Stop the panel from watching one sensor — a section that is shut off, a broken sensor — and take it back later."},{"p":"one","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"","u":"en/one/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html","x":"A sensor out of control is not watched: the panel raises no alarm about it, whatever it reports. Take a sensor out of control when its section is out of work on purpose or when the sensor is broken and you must finish the field. Take it back as soon as you can."},{"p":"one","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"Take a sensor out of control","u":"en/one/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html#take-a-sensor-out-of-control","x":"On the home screen, touch and hold the sensor — its column on the diagram, or its icon. Its context menu opens.\nThe context menu of a seed sensor.\nSelect “Disable control” with “UP” / “DOWN” and press “MENU”, or touch the item.\nThe panel asks “Are you sure that you want disable control?”. Touch “Yes”, or press “PLUS”.\nThe confirmation before control is switched off.\nThe column turns white with ! (an icon alternates with a white [!]). The sensor is no longer watched."},{"p":"one","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"Take it back into control","u":"en/one/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html#take-it-back-into-control","x":"Do the same and choose “Enable control”. The panel asks “Are you sure that you want enable control?”; confirm with “Yes”. A seed sensor taken back into control shows a yellow ? for a few seconds while it receives its settings.\nIMPORTANT\nA switched-off sensor is blind, not quiet. Nothing about it is reported, however wrong it goes."},{"p":"one","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"When the statistics must be erased","u":"en/one/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html#when-the-statistics-must-be-erased","x":"For some sensors the panel first asks to erase the statistics of the active field: it says “Data for the selected field must be erased!” and asks again. “Yes” erases the statistics of the active field — at the Operator level only after the Agronomist PIN — and changes the control; “No” leaves both as they are.\nThe speed sensor cannot be taken out of control. Motors are taken out of control the same way, from their tile on the Motors screen or with the chevrons on the second home screen. What the four combinations of control and product application mean: The four states of a section."},{"p":"one","t":"Zero the readings of the Table screen","h":"","u":"en/one/zero-the-readings.html","x":"Set the seed and fertilizer counters of the Table screen to zero — for a new field or a new seed lot — without touching the field statistics."},{"p":"one","t":"Zero the readings of the Table screen","h":"","u":"en/one/zero-the-readings.html","x":"The Table screen keeps counting from the moment its readings were last zeroed. Zero them when you start a new field or a new seed lot, so that the counters and deviations describe only the new work.\nOpen the Table screen: “HOME” on the home screen.\nPress “MENU”, or tap the screen with two fingers. The panel asks “Zero readings?”.\nThe question before zeroing: Yes in green, No in red.\nTouch “Yes”, or press “PLUS”. The counters of every seed, dry fertilizer and liquid fertilizer sensor are set to zero.\n“No”, “MINUS” or “BACK” leaves everything as it was.\nOnly the counters of the Table screen are zeroed. The field statistics are not touched; they are erased with “Zero statistics” in the menu of the Statistics screen.\nIMPORTANT\nZeroing cannot be undone. Do it when you start something new, not to tidy the screen."},{"p":"one","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"","u":"en/one/fill-the-disks.html","x":"Turn the seed disks of a planter with dispenser motors until every disk holds seed, so that sowing starts from the first metre — how to run it and read the result."},{"p":"one","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"","u":"en/one/fill-the-disks.html","x":"This applies only to planters with seed dispenser electric motors in the “Precise” control mode. Without motors in the profile, or in another mode, the menu item is not shown.\nWhen the disks are driven by electric motors, they stand empty until the motors run. Drive off with empty disks and the first metres of every row get nothing: each disk has to pick up seeds and carry them round to the drop point first. Filling turns the disks while the machine stands still, so that sowing starts from the first metre.\nWARNING\nThe disks turn while the machine stands still. Before you start, make sure nobody is near the metering units, the dispenser shafts or the drive. Every disk starts at once and keeps turning for up to about twenty seconds. From the cab the metering units cannot be seen.\nStop the machine. The item is shown only while the machine stands still.\nOn the home screen, press “MENU”. Select “Fill the disks with seeds” and press “MENU”.\nThe main menu with Fill the disks with seeds selected.\nThe panel asks “Are you sure?”. Touch “Yes”, or press “PLUS”. “No”, “MINUS” or “BACK” cancels.\nThe confirmation before the disks turn.\nWatch the tiles. Each motor turns its disk until the seed sensor of its row registers the first seed. At the end the panel beeps three times and shows “Operation complete.”\nTouch “Close”, or press “BACK”, to return to the home screen.\nTo stop before the end, touch “Cancel” or press “BACK”: the motors stop at once and their control goes back to the seeding logic."},{"p":"one","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"Reading the screen","u":"en/one/fill-the-disks.html#reading-the-screen","x":"Green tile. Done: the seed sensor registered a seed, the disk is filled.\nDark red tile. Failed: the disk could not be filled. The motor number is bright red.\nReason letters. Why the tile failed — see the table below. Here m g.\n“Operation complete.” Every motor has finished.\n“Close”. Returns to the home screen. While the operation runs, this button reads “Cancel”.\nThe end of the operation on an eight-motor planter: motor 1 filled, motors 2 to 8 failed.\nTile\nState\nDark grey\nWaiting its turn. Up to eight motors run at the same time; the rest wait.\nYellow\nRunning: the disk is turning.\nGreen\nDone.\nDark red\nFailed.\nEach motor turns its disk at 4 rpm of the driven shaft. If no seed is registered within 1.25 revolutions — about 19 seconds — that motor is stopped and marked as failed. A motor already in an alarm state is marked as failed at once."},{"p":"one","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"Letters on a failed tile","u":"en/one/fill-the-disks.html#letters-on-a-failed-tile","x":"The colour of the letter matters as much as the letter: red means a fault, white means taken out of control.\nA failed tile close up.\nLetter\nMeaning\nm red\nA fault of the dispenser electric motor during the operation.\nm white\nThe motor is taken out of control and cannot run.\ng red\nThe seed sensor registered no seed while the disk was turning.\ns red\nA fault of the seed sensor during the operation.\ns white\nThe seed sensor is taken out of control.\nIf the seed sensor is out of control, the panel cannot check the disk. A serviceable motor still turns it for about one and a quarter turns, so in practice the disk does get filled — but the panel reports that motor as failed, because it never saw a seed."},{"p":"one","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"","u":"en/one/tramline-rhythm.html","x":"What a tramline rhythm is, worked out for your drill and sprayer, and how to set it up on the panel step by step."},{"p":"one","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"","u":"en/one/tramline-rhythm.html","x":"A tramline is the lane the sprayer will drive in later. You make it now, while sowing, by shutting off one or two sections on certain bouts so that nothing is sown where the sprayer wheels will go. Which bouts those are depends on the width of the drill against the width of the sprayer. That pattern, repeating across the field, is the rhythm."},{"p":"one","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"How a rhythm works — an example","u":"en/one/tramline-rhythm.html#how-a-rhythm-works-an-example","x":"A 6-metre drill and an 18-metre sprayer: every third bout must carry the lane. The rhythm is three bouts long, and actuator 1 shuts its sections off on one of them.\nBout\nActuator 1\nWhat is sown\n1\nshuts its sections off\nAll rows except the tramline rows — the lane is made.\n2\nopen\nAll rows.\n3\nopen\nAll rows.\n1 again\nshuts off\nThe next lane, 18 m further.\nThe panel counts the bouts and shuts the sections off on the right one. After the last bout it starts again at bout 1.\nIMPORTANT\nGet the rhythm wrong and the field looks perfect until the sprayer arrives and has nowhere to drive. Check the bout count against the sprayer, not against the drill."},{"p":"one","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"What the panel allows","u":"en/one/tramline-rhythm.html#what-the-panel-allows","x":"Rhythms stored\nThree. One is active at a time. Keep the patterns of the machines you work with and switch between them.\nActuators in one rhythm\nFour.\nCut-off bouts per actuator\nFour different bouts of the same rhythm.\nBouts in a rhythm\nAt least one."},{"p":"one","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"Setting it up","u":"en/one/tramline-rhythm.html#setting-it-up","x":"The tramline items sit under “Seeding machine parameters”, which needs the Agronomist level: Enable the Agronomist level.\nOn the home screen press “MENU”, select “Settings” and press “MENU”.\nThe main menu.\nSelect “Seeding machine parameters” and press “MENU”.\nThe Settings menu; Seeding machine parameters at the bottom.\nSelect “Tramline” and press “MENU”.\nThe Seeding machine parameters menu with the Tramline item.\nSet “Tramline” to “Yes” with “PLUS”. Then select “Tramline rhythm settings” and press “MENU”.\nThe Tramline menu.\nOn “Rhythm”, choose the rhythm number with “PLUS” / “MINUS”, press “MENU” and choose “Select”. The word “Active” appears beside the number — without it the rhythm is stored but not used.\nThe rhythm menu: Select, Reset rhythm settings.\nMove down to “Bouts” and set the number of bouts in the rhythm.\nMove to the framed cell under the activation heading and press “MENU”. Choose the number of the actuator, then the bout on which it must shut its sections off.\nIf the pattern needs it, add more bouts to the same actuator — up to four — with the framed cell at the end of its row, or another actuator with the cell on the empty row below.\nPress “BACK” until the home screen returns. The rhythm is saved; the tramline indicator shows the current bout above and the number of bouts below.\nThe home screen with the tramline indicator, bout 1 of 1.\nReturn to the Operator level before you work — above it, no alarms are shown."},{"p":"one","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"The rhythm form","u":"en/one/tramline-rhythm.html#the-rhythm-form","x":"Rhythm. The number of the rhythm; “PLUS” and “MINUS” step through the three.\n“Active”. This rhythm is the one in use.\n“Bouts”. The number of bouts in the rhythm.\nActivation heading. “Tramline actuators activation” on a machine that makes tramlines with actuators; “Tramline motors activation” on one that stops dispenser motors.\nFramed cell. Adds an actuator (or motor) and its cut-off bout.\nA new rhythm: one bout, nothing shut off yet.\nIn the form, “UP” and “DOWN” move between lines, “MINUS” and “PLUS” along a line, “MENU” opens the selected cell. Moving to the next bout while working: Move to the next tramline bout."},{"p":"one","t":"Move to the next tramline bout","h":"","u":"en/one/next-tramline-bout.html","x":"How the bout changes by itself at the headland, what it needs to do so, and how to change or correct it by hand."},{"p":"one","t":"Move to the next tramline bout","h":"","u":"en/one/next-tramline-bout.html","x":"The tramline indicator on the home screen shows the current bout above and the number of bouts in the rhythm below. The bout can change by itself at every turn, or you change it by hand — both from the bout menu, opened with a short press of “BACK” on the home screen.\nThe bout menu when the bout can change by itself: Auto-switching and Active bout."},{"p":"one","t":"Move to the next tramline bout","h":"Automatic change","u":"en/one/next-tramline-bout.html#automatic-change","x":"The panel can change the bout by itself only if something tells it that a bout has ended:\nOn the machine\nWhat ends a bout\nMarker sensors in the profile\nAt least one marker sensor under control and answering. The change of the marker position ends the bout.\nNo markers\nThe work position sensor, under control and answering. Raising the implement out of work ends the bout.\nNeither\nAutomatic change is impossible. Change the bout by hand.\nOn top of that, “Auto-switching” must be “Yes”, and the machine must actually have been applying and then stopped. All of it together — otherwise the bout stays where it is. When the bout cannot change by itself, the tramline icon on the home screen is yellow (Tramline bout).\nOn the home screen, press “BACK”. The bout menu opens.\nOn “Auto-switching”, choose “Yes” with “PLUS”.\nPress “BACK” to close the menu.\nNOTE\nIf the bout does not advance at the headland, check in this order: is “Auto-switching” on; is the marker or position sensor under control; does it answer. The first is a setting, the other two are the machine."},{"p":"one","t":"Move to the next tramline bout","h":"By hand","u":"en/one/next-tramline-bout.html#by-hand","x":"What the bout menu offers depends on the machine:\nWithout a sensor that can end a bout, it offers “Next bout”. Select it and press “MENU”: the bout moves on by one and the menu closes. Do it at every turn.\nWith such a sensor, it offers “Auto-switching” instead. To correct the count — after a missed turn, say — set “Active bout” with “PLUS” / “MINUS”; the new bout takes effect when you close the menu.\n“Active bout” is in the menu on every machine. Check the indicator on the home screen before you start a pass.\nThe rhythm itself is set up in Set up the tramline rhythm."},{"p":"one","t":"Set the screen brightness","h":"","u":"en/one/screen-brightness.html","x":"Make the screen brighter, dimmer or automatic — one item in the Settings menu."},{"p":"one","t":"Set the screen brightness","h":"","u":"en/one/screen-brightness.html","x":"On the home screen press “MENU”, select “Settings” and press “MENU”.\n“Brightness,%” is the first item. Change it with “PLUS” and “MINUS”, from 0 to 100 in steps of 10.\nThe Settings menu with Brightness,% selected and set to AUTO.\nPress “BACK” to leave the menu. The new brightness stays in force.\n0 means automatic — the menu shows “AUTO”. The panel measures the light in the cab and sets the screen itself: bright in the sun, dim at night. Any other value fixes the screen at that percentage.\nThe button backlight always follows the screen: when the screen dims, the buttons dim with it.\nEverything else in the Settings menu: The Settings menu."},{"p":"one","t":"Work without the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/work-without-speed-sensor.html","x":"When the speed sensor fails in the field — finish the work on a simulated speed and control the application by hand."},{"p":"one","t":"Work without the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/work-without-speed-sensor.html","x":"Symptom. The speed field shows XX.X in red, or the speed stays at zero although the machine moves. Without a speed, the panel cannot calculate area, rates or deviations, and the dispenser motors do not know how fast to turn.\nCause. The speed sensor or its input device does not answer, or the wheel no longer gives pulses. See Speed sensor alarms.\nWhat to do. Finish the field on a simulated speed and switch the application on and off by hand.\nWARNING\nWith speed imitation on, the panel drives the dispenser motors and the pump as if the machine were moving — also while it stands. Switch imitation off before you leave the cab, and lock application whenever you stop.\nOn the home screen, touch and hold the speed field. Its context menu opens.\nSet “Imitation, km/h” to the speed you will drive. This item needs the Agronomist level: Enable the Agronomist level.\nReturn to the Operator level, so that alarms are shown again.\nThe speed field now shows a yellow figure alternating with “IMITATION” (Speed).\nControl the application with the manual lock: hold “HOME” to stop applying at the headland, hold it again to start on the next pass. While the lock is on, the yellow hand shows at the right edge and the speed reads zero. See Lock application by hand.\nDrive at the speed you set. The rates are right only as long as the real speed matches it.\nWhen the sensor is repaired, set “Imitation, km/h” back to 0.\nDetails of the speed sensor settings: Speed sensor."},{"p":"one","t":"Access levels","h":"","u":"en/one/access-levels.html","x":"The three access levels of the Hectare One, what each one allows, and how to switch between them."},{"p":"one","t":"Access levels","h":"","u":"en/one/access-levels.html","x":"Not every setting is open to everyone. The panel has three working access levels. After every power-on it starts at the lowest one, “Operator”.\nLevel\nWhat it allows\n“Operator”\nEveryday work. Some parameters cannot be changed, and the statistics cannot be reset.\n“Agronomist”\nResetting the statistics, the seeding machine parameters, the product rate error and the other commissioning settings. Needs a PIN.\n“Service”\nThe profile and the reset-to-defaults items. For the service department. Needs a PIN.\nA fourth level, “Debug”, exists above “Service”. It is used at manufacture only; see The Service menu.\nEvery settings table in this manual names the level an item needs. An item above your level is not shown in the menu at all."},{"p":"one","t":"Access levels","h":"Switch the level","u":"en/one/access-levels.html#switch-the-level","x":"The level is changed in the “Settings” menu, item “User”, and nowhere else — there is no button combination for it. Going up a level asks for the PIN; going down does not. Step by step, with the screens: Enable the Agronomist level.\nThe PIN is different for every panel. Take it from the device passport or ask the service support.\nIMPORTANT\nAt “Agronomist” and “Service” no alarm windows are shown. The panel warns about it in a red window — see User is Agronomist. Return to “Operator” before you work — or switch the panel off and on."},{"p":"one","t":"Access levels","h":"What happens to the alarms while the level is high","u":"en/one/access-levels.html#what-happens-to-the-alarms-while-the-level-is-high","x":"The alarms are not lost while they are hidden — they wait. When you lower the level, the panel drops the ones that have gone away in the meantime and shows the ones that are still true, one window after another. After an hour of commissioning at “Agronomist”, expect a short run of alarm windows on the way back down. It is the backlog, not a new fault. How the windows work: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Enable the Agronomist level","h":"","u":"en/one/enable-agronomist-level.html","x":"The steps, with the screen of each, to raise the access level to \"Agronomist\" or \"Service\" before changing settings, and to return to \"Operator\" afterwards."},{"p":"one","t":"Enable the Agronomist level","h":"","u":"en/one/enable-agronomist-level.html","x":"Most set-up work — sensor parameters, calibrations, device configuration — needs the “Agronomist” level. The “Service” level is raised the same way. What each level allows: Access levels.\nYou need the PIN of this panel. It is different for every panel; take it from the device passport or ask the service support.\nOn the home screen press “MENU”. The main menu opens.\nThe main menu.\nSelect “Settings” with “UP” / “DOWN” and press “MENU”. The “Settings” menu opens.\nThe Settings menu at the Operator level.\nPress “DOWN” until “User” is selected — it is near the end of the menu. Press “PLUS” until it reads “Agronomist” (or “Service”).\n“User” set to Agronomist.\nPress “MENU”. The PIN pad opens. Its heading names the level you chose — “Agronomist” — followed by the code of this panel; have that code at hand when you ask the service support for the PIN. Type the PIN and touch “OK”.\nThe PIN pad. The code beside the prompt is covered in this manual.\nThe “Settings” menu returns, now with the items of the new level — for example “Seeding machine parameters”. Press “BACK” to leave.\nIMPORTANT\nAt “Agronomist” and “Service” no alarm windows are shown. When you have finished, return to “Operator”: repeat steps 1–3, choose “Operator” with “MINUS” and press “MENU” — no PIN is asked going down. Switching the panel off and on does the same.\nWhen you come back down, the alarms that are still true are shown one after another. See Access levels."},{"p":"one","t":"The profile","h":"","u":"en/one/profile.html","x":"What the profile tells the panel, how to check it, what each profile item allows, and when the panel refuses a profile."},{"p":"one","t":"The profile","h":"","u":"en/one/profile.html","x":"The profile tells the panel which devices, and how many of each, are on the machine. A panel usually arrives with the profile already set. Check it before you set anything else: a device missing from the profile is never watched, and a device in the profile that is not fitted raises “Connection lost”."},{"p":"one","t":"The profile","h":"Check the profile on the home screen","u":"en/one/profile.html#check-the-profile-on-the-home-screen","x":"Switch the panel on and wait a few seconds. Then look at the home screen:\nNo “Connection lost” alarm — the panel has found every device the profile declares.\nNo white indicators with an exclamation mark — no device is left out of control.\nNo red indicators with an “X” — every device answers.\nWhat the indicators mean: Indicators. How to take a device into control or out of it: Take a sensor in and out of control.\nNOTE\nA profile is sometimes supplied with devices that are not in the delivery, so that the system can be extended later. Those indicators stay out of control on purpose. Do not switch their control on."},{"p":"one","t":"The profile","h":"Open the profile","u":"en/one/profile.html#open-the-profile","x":"The profile is at the “Service” level: raise the level (Enable the Agronomist level — choose “Service”), then “MENU” → “Service” → “Profile”.\n“Seed sensors” — the number of seed sensors. The selected item shows its value between – and +: change it with “MINUS” / “PLUS”.\n“Fertilizer sensors” — dry fertilizer sensors.\n“Liquid fertilizer flow sensors” — liquid fertilizer section sensors.\n“Hoppers” — hopper level sensors.\n“Dispensers” — dispenser shaft sensors.\n“Speed” — where the travel speed comes from.\n“Fans” — fan speed sensors.\n“Pressure sensors” — air pressure sensors.\nScroll bar. More items follow below — see the table.\nThe top of the “Profile” menu of a drill with eight seed rows, two hoppers, two dispensers and one fan."},{"p":"one","t":"The profile","h":"The profile items","u":"en/one/profile.html#the-profile-items","x":"Item\nRange\n“Seed sensors”\n2 … 120. The menu lets you enter 1, but such a profile is refused when it is saved.\n“Fertilizer sensors”\n0 … 60; seed and fertilizer sensors together not more than 120.\n“Liquid fertilizer flow sensors”\n0 … 60\n“Hoppers”\n0 … 9\n“Dispensers”\n0 … 9\n“Speed”\n“No” / “Control panel” (sensor wired to the panel) / “Input device” (one wheel sensor on the input device) / “Input device (2)” (two wheel sensors on the input device).\n“Fans”\n0 … 2\n“Pressure sensors”\n0 … 3\n“Work position sensor”\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Markers”\n“No” / “One” / “Both”\n“No battery charge”, “Emergency oil pressure”, “Ignition”, “Low fuel”, “Air filter”\nThe engine sensors — “No” / “Yes” each.\n“Seed dispenser motors”\n0 … 60\n“Fertilizer dispenser motors”\n0 … 30; both motor groups together not more than 60.\n“Tramline actuators”\n0 … 4\n“Liquid fertilizer pump controller”\n0 … 1\nOn a panel built for a system without electric motors, the two motor items are not in the menu."},{"p":"one","t":"The profile","h":"When the panel refuses a profile","u":"en/one/profile.html#when-the-panel-refuses-a-profile","x":"The panel checks the whole profile, not only the item you changed. It refuses a profile when:\nthe hopper, dispenser and engine sensors together come to more than 12;\nthey come to more than 10 while the pressure and fan sensors together come to more than four;\nthe pressure and fan sensors together come to more than five;\nthere are fertilizer motors but no seed motors, or fertilizer motors but no fertilizer sensors;\nthere are electric motors or a liquid fertilizer pump but no speed sensor;\nliquid fertilizer valves and a pump controller are declared at the same time."},{"p":"one","t":"The profile","h":"Save the profile","u":"en/one/profile.html#save-the-profile","x":"After changing the profile, select “Save profile” at the end of the “Profile” menu and press “MENU”. The panel asks “Are you sure?”.\nThe confirmation window.\nTouch “Yes”. The panel shows “Wait...”, then “Saved” and “Restart system”, and restarts by itself.\nIf nothing was changed, the panel only shows “No changes”. If the profile breaks one of the rules above, it shows “Invalid profile!” in red and saves nothing.\nSaving all the settings of a profile, and moving them on a memory card: Save and load settings."},{"p":"one","t":"Save and load settings","h":"","u":"en/one/save-and-load-settings.html","x":"Keep a copy of all the settings of the current profile in the panel or on a memory card, and bring it back — for example to move one panel between implements."},{"p":"one","t":"Save and load settings","h":"","u":"en/one/save-and-load-settings.html","x":"For the current profile the panel can save all the settings and restore them later — useful before you try something out. With a memory card in the slot, a profile can also be saved to the card together with its settings, so one panel can serve several implements.\nItem in the “Profile” menu\nWhat it does\n“Save settings” / “Load settings”\nKeep a copy of the settings inside the panel, and bring it back.\n“Save to SD-card” / “Load from SD-card”\nThe same with the memory card; the profile travels with its settings. Without a card, saving ends with “Insert SD-card”.\nAt the “Service” level the menu also holds “Save factory defaults”: after “Are you sure?” it saves the settings as “Save settings” does and also keeps them, with the profile, as the panel's factory copy, which no menu item of this firmware loads back.\nRaise the level (Enable the Agronomist level — choose “Service”) and open “MENU” → “Service” → “Profile” (The profile).\nScroll down to the item you need and press “MENU”. “Save settings” and “Load settings” ask “Are you sure?”.\nThe confirmation window.\nTouch “Yes”. The panel answers “Saved” or “Loaded”.\nThe panel confirms with “Saved”."},{"p":"one","t":"Save and load settings","h":"Profile files on the memory card","u":"en/one/save-and-load-settings.html#profile-files-on-the-memory-card","x":"Files are named automatically P0001.pfl and stored in the PROFILES folder of the card. You may rename a file — keep the name to eight characters, Latin letters and digits.\n“Load from SD-card” opens a list of the profiles on the card. A profile file written by one firmware version is very likely not readable by another: an incompatible profile is shown in red in that list."},{"p":"one","t":"The Settings menu","h":"","u":"en/one/settings-menu.html","x":"Every item of the panel's own \"Settings\" menu — brightness, volume, language, alarm delay, diagram settings, date and time — with its range and access level."},{"p":"one","t":"The Settings menu","h":"","u":"en/one/settings-menu.html","x":"The “Settings” menu holds the settings of the panel itself. Open it from the home screen: “MENU” → “Settings”. The settings of the individual sensors are elsewhere — see Sensor settings.\n“Brightness,%” — the backlight. “AUTO” lets the panel set it from its light sensor.\n“Volume” — the loudness of the panel's sounds.\n“Language” — the language of the screens.\n“Buttons sound” — a sound on every button press.\n“Seeding diagram settings” — how the seed diagram is drawn. Below.\n“Change Date&Time” — the clock. Below.\n“Alarm delay” — how long a fault must last before the alarm window opens.\n“Seeding machine parameters” — width, modes, tramline, calibrations. Seeding machine parameters.\nScroll bar. More items follow below.\nThe “Settings” menu of a drill with a speed sensor, at the “Agronomist” level.\nIMPORTANT\nThe menu does not always have the same items. The access level, the machine in the profile and the display mode of the diagrams change it. If an item named here is missing, check those three before looking for a fault."},{"p":"one","t":"The Settings menu","h":"The items","u":"en/one/settings-menu.html#the-items","x":"Item\nRange\nLevel\n“Brightness,%”\n0 … 100, in steps of 10. At 0 the item reads “AUTO” and the panel sets the backlight from its light sensor — brightening quickly, dimming slowly. Any other value fixes the backlight.\nOperator\n“Volume”\n0 … 100, in steps of 10\nOperator\n“Language”\nthe installed languages\nOperator\n“Buttons sound”\n“No” / “Yes”\nOperator\n“Seeding diagram settings”, “Fertilizer diagram settings”, “Liquid fertilizer diagram settings”\nSubmenus. Each is shown only when the machine has a speed sensor, its own sensors are in the profile and its diagram is not in the “Flow” mode.\nOperator\n“Change Date&Time”\nsubmenu\nOperator\n“Alarm delay”\n2 … 20 s, default 3 s. See How alarms work.\nOperator\n“Product rate error,%”\n3 … 20 %. Shown only when a product is dosed by weight or a liquid fertilizer pump controller is in the profile.\nAgronomist\n“Seeding machine parameters”\nSubmenu. Hidden when the machine has neither a speed sensor nor a tramline actuator.\nAgronomist\n“User”\nThe access level — see Access levels.\nOperator\n“Reset settings”\nReturns this menu to the factory values.\nService\nOn a machine with two groups of seed sensors, the two diagram items read “Seeding gr.A diagram” and “Seeding gr.B diagram”.\nThe settings are saved by themselves a few seconds after the last press. Wait a moment before you switch the panel off."},{"p":"one","t":"The Settings menu","h":"Diagram settings","u":"en/one/settings-menu.html#diagram-settings","x":"The diagram settings of the seed sensors.\nThere are three of these menus — seed, dry fertilizer, liquid fertilizer — with the same items:\nItem\nWhat it does\n“Skips & Doubles”\nShows the skip and double bars over the last stretch of the path. “Precise” mode only.\n“Grid”\n“No” — only the rate line; “Partial” — only the tolerance limits; “Full” — both.\n“Scale S&D,%”\nThe height of the skip and double bars, 5 … 50 % in steps of 5. “Precise” mode only.\n“Reset to defaults”\nReturns this menu to the factory values. Service level only."},{"p":"one","t":"The Settings menu","h":"Change Date and Time","u":"en/one/settings-menu.html#change-date-and-time","x":"Select “Change Date&Time” and press “MENU”. The date and time fields open.\nThe date and time fields.\nSelect each field with “UP” / “DOWN” and set it with “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\nSelect “Set” and press “MENU”. The clock takes the new values. “BACK” instead leaves the clock unchanged."},{"p":"one","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"","u":"en/one/seeding-machine-parameters.html","x":"The working width, the modes of seeding and fertilizing, the tramline, the mapping of sections to actuators, and the two calibrations that need the machine to move."},{"p":"one","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"","u":"en/one/seeding-machine-parameters.html","x":"“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters”. The item needs the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level) and is hidden on a machine with neither a speed sensor nor a tramline actuator. Which items it holds depends on the profile.\n“Width, cm” — the working width. The selected value stands between –[** and **]+.\n“Mode of seeding” — “Flow”, “Hybrid” or “Precise”.\n“Tramline” — the tramline submenu.\n“Speed calibration” — starts the speed calibration.\n“Reset to defaults” — factory values for this menu. Service level only.\nThe menu of a precision planter with a speed sensor and no fertilizer, dispensers or liquid fertilizer."},{"p":"one","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Width, cm","u":"en/one/seeding-machine-parameters.html#width-cm","x":"The working width of the implement. The worked area is calculated from it, so it must be entered. Press “MENU” on the item: the number pad opens. Type the digits — the key at the top right deletes the last one — and touch “OK” to accept, or press “BACK” to keep the old value.\nThe number pad for “Width, cm”."},{"p":"one","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Mode of seeding, Mode of fertilizers","u":"en/one/seeding-machine-parameters.html#mode-of-seeding-mode-of-fertilizers","x":"Three modes each:\nMode\nUnit\nFor\n“Flow”\nseeds per second\ngrain drills\n“Hybrid”\nseeds per metre\ngrain drills — recommended\n“Precise”\nseeds per metre, with skips and doubles counted\nprecision planters\nIMPORTANT\nSwitching between “Precise” and the other two modes demands a reset of the statistics if any field has work recorded in it."},{"p":"one","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Mode of liquid fertilizer","u":"en/one/seeding-machine-parameters.html#mode-of-liquid-fertilizer","x":"“Flow” (litres per minute) or “By area” (litres per hectare). Shown only when there is no liquid fertilizer pump controller in the profile — with a controller the panel chooses the mode itself."},{"p":"one","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Actuators to sensors mapping","u":"en/one/seeding-machine-parameters.html#actuators-to-sensors-mapping","x":"Which section sensors belong to which tramline actuator or electric motor. When that actuator shuts its sections off, their sensors switch to tramline monitoring.\nThe mapping screen: one line for the actuators, one for the motors.\nA mapping is a range: “Range start” and “Range end” — equal numbers mean a single section. Up to four ranges can be attached to one actuator. On a machine with metering motors the screen also has “Motors to sensors mapping”."},{"p":"one","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Tramline","u":"en/one/seeding-machine-parameters.html#tramline","x":"The rhythms of the tramline, in a submenu of its own with two items: “Tramline” (“No” / “Yes”) and “Tramline rhythm settings”.\nThe tramline submenu.\nSetting up a rhythm: Tramline rhythm."},{"p":"one","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Speed calibration and Dispenser calibration","u":"en/one/seeding-machine-parameters.html#speed-calibration-and-dispenser-calibration","x":"The two calibrations that need the machine to move. “Dispenser calibration” carries a number beside it: the dispenser to calibrate, or 0 for all of them at once.\nCalibrate the speed sensor\nCalibrate the dispenser rotation"},{"p":"one","t":"Sensor settings","h":"","u":"en/one/sensor-settings.html","x":"How to reach the settings of one sensor, what its context menu holds, and which calibrations exist."},{"p":"one","t":"Sensor settings","h":"","u":"en/one/sensor-settings.html","x":"Every sensor, actuator and motor on the machine has its own settings and its own status screen. Both are reached from the home screen. The parameters of each kind of sensor are in the topics that follow this one."},{"p":"one","t":"Sensor settings","h":"Reach a sensor","u":"en/one/sensor-settings.html#reach-a-sensor","x":"On the home screen hold “MENU” for a few seconds. The panel enters the sensor setting mode; the selected sensor is drawn inside a white rectangle.\nMove from sensor to sensor with “UP” / “DOWN”. “PLUS” takes the selected sensor into control, “MINUS” takes it out — see Take a sensor in and out of control.\nPress “MENU”. The context menu of the selected sensor opens.\nThe context menu of the speed sensor.\nPress “BACK” to leave the mode.\nHolding a sensor tile on the screen opens the same context menu directly."},{"p":"one","t":"Sensor settings","h":"The context menu","u":"en/one/sensor-settings.html#the-context-menu","x":"“Information” — opens the status screen of this sensor. The sensor status screen.\n“Disable control” / “Enable control” — takes the sensor out of monitoring, or back in. The first time in a session the panel asks for confirmation.\nThe parameters of this sensor — here the three of a fan sensor. Which of them you see depends on the access level.\nThe current value. Select the line; the value stands between – and + and changes with “MINUS” / “PLUS”.\n“Reset to defaults” — returns this sensor's parameters to the factory values. Service level only.\nThe context menu of a fan sensor at the “Agronomist” level.\nThe confirmation asked before control is switched off."},{"p":"one","t":"Sensor settings","h":"The status screen","u":"en/one/sensor-settings.html#the-status-screen","x":"“Information” opens the status screen of the sensor: its identity, its link, its live figures and its active alarms. How to read it: The sensor status screen."},{"p":"one","t":"Sensor settings","h":"Calibrations","u":"en/one/sensor-settings.html#calibrations","x":"Four calibrations exist. They are not variants of one procedure: each writes different parameters and is reached a different way.\nWhat it puts right\nWhere\nLevel\nMachine\nTravel speed — the pulses of the speed sensor over a known distance. Calibrate the speed sensor\n“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters” → “Speed calibration”\nAgronomist\ndrives 100 m\nDispenser rotation — “Wheel, pulses” against “Dispenser pulses”. Calibrate the dispenser rotation\n“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters” → “Dispenser calibration”\nAgronomist\nwheel and dispenser shaft turned together\nDispenser mass — “Weight, gr” and “Weight, pulses”. Calibrate the dispenser by weight\ncontext menu of the dispenser sensor\nOperator\nstanding\nMetering roller mass — “Weight, gr” and “Rotations” of one motor. Calibrate a metering motor by weight\ncontext menu of the dispenser electric motor\nOperator\nstanding\nThe two menu calibrations need the “Agronomist” level, like the menu that leads to them. The two mass calibrations are at the “Operator” level, because they have to be repeated whenever the product changes."},{"p":"one","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"","u":"en/one/sensor-status-screen.html","x":"How to read the status screen of a sensor — channel, address, type, link state, live figures and active alarms — and what each kind of sensor shows on it."},{"p":"one","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"","u":"en/one/sensor-status-screen.html","x":"Reached through “Information” in the context menu of a sensor (Sensor settings). It shows what the panel knows about one sensor."},{"p":"one","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"The screen","u":"en/one/sensor-status-screen.html#the-screen","x":"“Channel #” — which sensor of the whole list this is.\nPrevious sensor — or press “MINUS”. The screen stays open.\nNext sensor — or press “PLUS”.\n“Address:” — the address the sensor answers on. Not shown in this manual.\n“Type:” — the kind of sensor and its number, here hopper sensor 1. Some sensors show a line “Unit:” instead of the address — where they are connected, for example “Control panel”.\n“Link:” — the state of the communication. Table below.\nThe figures of this kind of sensor — here the level on the input of a hopper sensor. Table below.\n“Alarm 1 from 1” — the active alarms, one at a time. “UP” / “DOWN” step through them.\nThe name of the alarm shown.\nThe status screen of a hopper sensor whose hopper is empty.\nButton\nAction\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nNext / previous sensor — the screen stays open.\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nStep through the active alarms of this sensor.\n“MENU”\nThe context menu of this sensor.\n“BACK”\nBack to the sensor selection."},{"p":"one","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"Link states","u":"en/one/sensor-status-screen.html#link-states","x":"State\nMeaning\n“Normal”\nThe sensor answers.\n“Not controlled”\nThe sensor is out of control: neither the communication nor anything else is watched.\n“No connection”\nThe sensor does not answer. Red.\n“Wrong device type!”\nSomething answers, but not the kind of device the profile expects. Red.\n“IMITATION”\nSpeed sensor only: the speed is simulated, not measured. Yellow.\nThe figures are drawn only while the sensor is under control, answers, and is of the expected kind. A sensor out of control shows its identity and nothing else."},{"p":"one","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"What each kind of sensor adds","u":"en/one/sensor-status-screen.html#what-each-kind-of-sensor-adds","x":"The status screen of a speed sensor wired to the panel.\nSensor\nWhat the screen shows\nSpeed sensor\nThe pulse frequency F= in Hz; with two speed sensors F1= and F2=. Under it the two limits “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h”. On a machine that doses a product by weight with electric motors, the limits are those the motors can hold, narrowed by the two settings; a limit the settings cannot meet is written in red as an inequality. Without such motors a limit set to zero is shown as ----, switched off.\nHopper sensor\nPin: and the level on the input, \"0\" or \"1\".\nDispenser shaft sensor\nF= in Hz, to three decimals.\nFan sensor\nF= in Hz.\nPressure sensor\nP= in kPa.\nSeed sensor, dry fertilizer sensor\nStatus: and one of eight states — below.\nLiquid fertilizer flow sensor\nStatus: Working, or Working:Tramline in the tramline mode; under it Flow= in L/min.\nWork position sensor\nPosition: Working or Position: Transporting, and Pin: with the level on the input.\nIgnition, oil pressure, battery charge, fuel level, air filter\nPin: and the level on the input, \"0\" or \"1\".\nMarker sensor\nOnly the common lines.\nTramline actuator\nStatus: Pulled in, Pulled out, Working while the shutter moves, or Unknown.\nDispenser electric motor\nStatus: Working, or Faulty while any alarm of the motor is active; then Last fault: with the controller's code, if there has been one (codes). A universal controller adds IN1[ ] IN2[ ] IN3[ ] with the levels on its three inputs.\nLiquid fertilizer pump controller\nStatus: Working or Faulty. With a fault, Last fault: and the code. Without one: Flow= in L/min, Load= in per cent, and Pressure sensor: No if there is none, otherwise Pressure sensor= in Bar with the raw signal in mA in brackets. Dashes stand for a value the controller cannot give."},{"p":"one","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"The eight states of a seed or fertilizer sensor","u":"en/one/sensor-status-screen.html#the-eight-states-of-a-seed-or-fertilizer-sensor","x":"Status\nMeaning\nWorking\nNormal.\nWorking:Tramline\nNormal; the sensor is in the tramline control mode for the current bout.\nConfiguring\nThe sensor has received new settings and is applying them.\nCalibration in progress\nThe sensor runs its initial calibration. It takes a few seconds.\nClogged\nThe infrared emitter of the optical channel is blocked — most likely a blocked seed tube. Red.\nLight struck\nToo much sunlight falls into the optical channel; the sensor may miscount. Red.\nFaulty\nThe sensor has failed and must be replaced. Red.\nUnknown\nAnother fault that cannot be identified. Red."},{"p":"one","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/speed-sensor.html","x":"The parameters of the travel speed sensor — pulses, wheel diameter, speed limits, imitation — and what setting the imitation does by itself."},{"p":"one","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/speed-sensor.html","x":"Everything the panel counts — area, rates, deviations — is worked out from the distance travelled. A speed sensor that is out by a tenth makes every other figure on the panel wrong by a tenth. Set its parameters first, then calibrate it (Calibrate the speed sensor).\nOpen the context menu of the speed sensor as described in Sensor settings. All its parameters need the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level).\nThe context menu of the speed sensor. A limit that is switched off reads “No”.\nThe context menu also offers “Speed calibration” directly, under “Information”.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange\n“Pulses”\nThe pulse figure of the sensor. Set it before the calibration, which corrects it.\n1 … 1000\n“Count period”\nHow many pulses of the sensor the panel averages the travel speed over; a higher value gives a steadier figure. Acts only on a speed sensor wired to the panel itself.\n1 … 1000\n“Diameter, cm”\nThe wheel diameter. Set it before the calibration.\n10 … 200\n“Factor, %”\nThe drive ratio to the wheel; 100 is a direct 1 : 1 drive.\n1 … 10000\n“Seeding criteria”\n“Yes”: the alarms of the seed, dry fertilizer and liquid fertilizer sections are raised only while the machine moves. “No”: also while it stands. Hidden, and always “Yes”, in the “Precise” mode and on machines with metering motors or a liquid fertilizer pump controller.\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Min speed, km/h”\nBelow this speed the home screen shows “FASTER”. 0 switches the check off.\n0 … 99\n“Max speed, km/h”\nAbove this speed the home screen shows “SLOWER”. 0 switches the check off.\n0 … 99\n“Imitation, km/h”\nA simulated speed. 0 = the real sensor is used.\n0 … 20\n“Width, cm”\nThe track between the two wheels of a two-sensor installation.\n0 … 10000\n“GPS”, “GPS internal”\nTake the speed from a receiver instead of the wheel.\n“No” / “Yes”\nThe minimum must be below the maximum, otherwise the panel raises “Error in settings of Min/Max” (Speed sensor alarms).\nIMPORTANT\nSetting “Imitation, km/h” to anything but zero changes two other things by itself. The panel sets “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” to zero — they mean nothing against a simulated speed — and it locks the product application, so that nothing is dosed while the machine stands. The lock is the hand icon on the home screen; hold “HOME” to release it (Lock the application by hand).\nThe imitated speed is also held inside the range the dosing allows: a value outside it is pulled back to the nearest limit as you enter it. While the speed is imitated, the status screen of the sensor shows “IMITATION” in yellow. Working without a speed sensor: Work without a speed sensor."},{"p":"one","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"The status screen","u":"en/one/speed-sensor.html#the-status-screen","x":"The status screen shows the pulse frequency F= in Hz (with two sensors F1= and F2=) and the two limits in force. See The sensor status screen.\nThe status screen of a speed sensor wired to the panel."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/calibrate-speed.html","x":"Drive a marked 100 m so that the panel corrects the pulse figure of the travel speed sensor."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/calibrate-speed.html","x":"The calibration corrects the pulse figure of the speed sensor against a known distance. Before you start, set “Pulses” and “Diameter, cm” of the sensor (Travel speed sensor) — the calibration corrects the pulse figure; it cannot invent it. You need the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level).\nMark out 100 m on level ground and put the machine at the start.\nOpen “Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters”, select “Speed calibration” and press “MENU”. The panel shows “Pass 100m and press STOP” with a single “STOP” button.\nThe speed calibration window.\nDrive the marked distance and stop at the end.\nTouch “STOP”. The panel shows “Done” and closes the window by itself.\nThe panel confirms with “Done”.\nThe distance is fixed at 100 m; the panel does not ask for it. It waits half a second after “STOP” before reading the counter and checks the connection once more in that moment — a sensor that drops out on the last metre is reported as a lost connection, not as a bad result."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"When it does not work","u":"en/one/calibrate-speed.html#when-it-does-not-work","x":"The panel shows\nMeaning\n“No speed sensor”\nThe profile has no speed sensor. The calibration does not start.\n“Connection lost with the sensor! Calibration canceled!”\nThe sensor does not answer.\n“Sensor not controlled! Calibration canceled!”\nThe sensor is out of control. Take it into control (Take a sensor in and out of control).\n“FAILURE! The tractor did not move!”\nThe counter did not move: the sensor gave no pulses at all — not an inaccurate reading, none. Check the sensor and its cable.\n“Calibration canceled!”\nThe window was closed some other way than with “STOP”. Nothing has changed."},{"p":"one","t":"Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors","h":"","u":"en/one/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html","x":"The parameters shared by a group of optical seed or dry fertilizer sensors — target rates, fraction, sensitivity, rate error, skips and doubles, tramline tolerance."},{"p":"one","t":"Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors","h":"","u":"en/one/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html","x":"Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors are the same kind of device and share one parameter set. Almost everything here is common to the whole group: change it on one sensor and it changes for all sensors of the group. Only two things are set per sensor — whether it is under control (“Disable control” / “Enable control”) and “Product application”. Open the context menu of any sensor of the group as in Sensor settings.\nThe context menu of a seed sensor, top part.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange\nLevel\n“Flow rate”\nThe rate for the “Flow” mode, in seeds per second. Below it the section raises the low-rate alarm.\n1 … 1500\nOperator\n“Hybrid rate”\nThe target in the “Hybrid” mode.\n1 … 200\nOperator\n“Precise rate,x10”\nThe target in the “Precise” mode, in tenths.\n5 … 500\nOperator\n“Sensitivity”\nThe sensitivity of sensors of an older generation; the panel sends it to them. Shown only when such a sensor is connected.\n1 … 120\nAgronomist\n“Fraction”\nThe count filter of the sensors, set for the size of the seed or granule; the panel sends it to every sensor of the group. The rate, skip and double alarms tell you which way to change it.\n1 … 100\nOperator\n“Correction”\nA fine adjustment of seed detection for the sensor generation that uses it; the panel sends it to those sensors only, and the item appears only when one is connected. Default 300.\n100 … 3300, steps of 10\nOperator\n“Rate error,%”\nHow far the actual rate may stand from the target before an alarm.\n5 … 95, steps of 5\nAgronomist\n“Doubles error,%”\nThe share of doubles that raises an alarm. “Precise” mode only.\n0 … 95\nAgronomist\n“Skips error,%”\nThe share of skips that raises an alarm. “Precise” mode only.\n0 … 95\nAgronomist\n“Recalculation,m” / “Recalculation,sec”\nThe stretch the skips and doubles are counted over — in metres or in seconds, depending on the mode.\n10 … 50\nOperator\n“Percent of sowing,%”\nThe share of the seed rows under control that must sow before the panel takes the drill as seeding; until then the seed and dry fertilizer sensors raise no rate, skip, double or tramline alarm. Seed sensors only; hidden on machines with metering motors or a liquid fertilizer pump controller.\n5 … 80, steps of 5\nAgronomist\n“Tolerable sowing to tramline,%”\nHow much a section that should be shut off for the tramline may still deliver.\n0 … 50\nAgronomist\n“Group start address”\nSet at commissioning — see Device configuration.\nService\nTIP\n“Fraction” is the first setting to look at when the rate alarms do not match reality. Most rate, skip and double alarms name it, with the direction to change it: Rate alarms, Doubles and skips, Tramline alarms.\nThe target rates themselves are changed while you work, on the home screen — see Change the target rate. The status screen of these sensors shows one of eight states — see The sensor status screen. If a sensor reports “Clogged”, “Light struck” or “Broken”: Sensor clogged or broken."},{"p":"one","t":"Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors","h":"Fraction for the crop","u":"en/one/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html#fraction-for-the-crop","x":"Go by the size of the seed:\nCrop\n“Fraction”\nRapeseed\n1 – 5\nWheat\n5 – 10\nMaize\n10 – 30\nSunflower\n10 – 30\nOn pneumatic drills set 1 whatever the crop. Too high a fraction makes small seeds disappear from the count — the panel then reports too low a rate.\nIMPORTANT\nChange “Correction” only after talking to the service. It is needed in rare cases only; a wrong value makes the sensors miss seed or count dust."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer sensors and pump controller","h":"","u":"en/one/liquid-fertilizer.html","x":"The parameters of the liquid fertilizer section sensors and of the liquid fertilizer pump controller."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer sensors and pump controller","h":"","u":"en/one/liquid-fertilizer.html","x":"Liquid fertilizer is watched by flow sensors in the sections; on some machines a pump controller also drives the pump to the rate. Open the context menu of either as in Sensor settings."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer sensors and pump controller","h":"Liquid fertilizer section sensors","u":"en/one/liquid-fertilizer.html#liquid-fertilizer-section-sensors","x":"Parameter\nWhat it does\nRange\nLevel\n“Pulses per liter”\nThe pulses the flow sensor gives per litre; the panel turns them into litres per minute, litres per hectare and the volume applied. Hidden when a pump controller is in the profile — the controller's figures are used then.\n1 … 60000\nAgronomist\n“Rate error,%”\nHow far the actual rate may stand from the target before an alarm.\n5 … 95, steps of 5\nAgronomist\n“Tolerable leak to tramline,%”\nHow much a section shut off for the tramline may still deliver.\n0 … 50\nAgronomist\n“Group start address”\nSee Device configuration.\nService\nThe two target rates of this sensor — one for the “Flow” mode, one for the “By area” mode — have no menu item. They are set on the home screen with “PLUS” and “MINUS”, like every other target rate (Change the target rate). The mode itself: Seeding machine parameters."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer sensors and pump controller","h":"Liquid fertilizer pump controller","u":"en/one/liquid-fertilizer.html#liquid-fertilizer-pump-controller","x":"Parameter\nWhat it does\nRange\nLevel\n“Pulses per liter”\nHow many pulses of the pump drive make one litre; the panel works out the volume applied from it. The figure is kept in the pump controller: the panel reads it at start-up and writes a change back to it.\n100 … 10000, steps of 10\nAgronomist\n“Pressure sensor”\nWhich pressure sensor watches the supply line.\n0 … 2\nAgronomist\n“Pressure boundary, Bar”\nThe pressure the pressure sensor reads at the top of its range, from the sensor's specification; the panel scales the line pressure with it. Default 16.0 bar.\n0.0 … 50.0\nAgronomist\n“Max pressure, Bar”\nAbove it the controller raises “High pressure”.\n0.0 … 50.0\nOperator\n“Section control D.I.”\nHas no effect in this firmware: the panel does not read this input for the pump.\n“No” / “Shut off(0)” / “Shut off(1)”\nAgronomist\n“Start delay,mS”\nThe shortest time between two starts of the pump: a start that would come sooner waits until this time has passed. Default 30 ms.\n0 … 500, steps of 5\nService\n“Group start address”\nSee Device configuration.\nAgronomist\nThe status screen of the pump controller shows the flow, the pump load and the line pressure — see The sensor status screen. Alarms: Liquid fertilizer pump, Rate alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/dispenser-sensor.html","x":"The parameters of the dispenser shaft sensor — rotation control against the wheel, or weight control by the mass applied."},{"p":"one","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/dispenser-sensor.html","x":"The dispenser shaft sensor counts the turns of a dispenser driven from the ground wheel. The panel can watch it two ways: by its rotation against the speed sensor, or by the mass of product it applies. Open its context menu as in Sensor settings.\nThe context menu of a dispenser sensor with both controls switched off.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange\nLevel\n“Period, s”\nThe longest time between two shaft pulses. If the shaft gives no pulse for longer while the machine sows or moves, the panel raises “No rotation”. Default 10 s.\n1 … 60\nOperator\n“Rotation speed control”\nWatch the shaft rotation against the travel speed.\n“No” / “Yes”\nAgronomist\n“Wheel, pulses”\nSet by the rotation calibration.\n1 … 65535\nAgronomist\n“Dispenser pulses”\nSet by the rotation calibration.\n1 … 65535\nAgronomist\n“Maximum error, %”\nHow far the shaft speed may stand from the speed the travel speed calls for; beyond it, while the machine moves, the panel raises “Low speed of rotation” or “High speed of rotation”. Shown only when “Rotation speed control” is “Yes”. Default 10 %.\n1 … 50\nAgronomist\n“Weight control”\nWatch the dispenser by the mass applied instead of by its rotation.\n“No” / “Yes”\nAgronomist\n“Type of product”\nWhich product this dispenser meters.\nAgronomist\n“Weight, gr” / “Weight, pulses”\nBoth set by the calibration by weight.\n1 … 65535 each\nAgronomist\nWith “Weight control” switched on, the context menu also holds “Dispenser calibration(weight)”.\nTIP\nPrefer weight control to rotation control. Rotation control notices that the shaft has stopped, not that the product has. “Weight control” watches the kilograms per hectare applied and catches an empty or bridged hopper whose dispenser still turns."},{"p":"one","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"Calibrations","u":"en/one/dispenser-sensor.html#calibrations","x":"Rotation — for “Rotation speed control”: Calibrate the dispenser rotation.\nMass — for “Weight control”; repeat it whenever the product changes: Calibrate the dispenser by weight.\nThe status screen shows the shaft pulse frequency F= in Hz, to three decimals. Its alarms: Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms, and with weight control Dosing by weight."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate the dispenser rotation","h":"","u":"en/one/calibrate-dispenser-rotation.html","x":"Turn the drive wheel and the dispenser shaft together so that the panel learns how many shaft pulses belong to one wheel pulse."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate the dispenser rotation","h":"","u":"en/one/calibrate-dispenser-rotation.html","x":"This calibration teaches the panel how many dispenser shaft pulses belong to one wheel pulse, so that it can tell a stopped shaft from a moving machine. It is the basis of “Rotation speed control” of the dispenser sensor (Dispenser sensor). It writes “Wheel, pulses” and “Dispenser pulses”.\nBefore you start: the speed sensor and every dispenser taking part must be under control and answering. You need the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level).\nOpen “Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters” → “Dispenser calibration”. Set the number of the dispenser with “PLUS” / “MINUS” — or 0 for all of them at once — and press “MENU”. The panel shows “Calibration” and a progress bar.\nThe progress bar of the dispenser calibration.\nTurn the drive wheel and the dispenser shaft together — by hand, or by moving the machine. The bar fills as the counters rise.\nKeep turning until the bar reaches 100 %. That happens when the speed sensor and every dispenser taking part have each given at least 100 pulses. The bar follows whichever is furthest behind — a dispenser that does not turn holds it at zero.\nAt 100 % the panel beeps once and shows “Stop rotation!”. Stop everything.\nThe panel waits until no counter has changed for two seconds, then writes the result and shows “Done”.\n“To abort operation press BACK.” stands at the bottom of the screen; “BACK” stops the calibration at any moment.\nIf any sensor taking part is out of control or does not answer, the calibration ends with “Sensor not controlled! Calibration canceled!” or “Connection lost with the sensor! Calibration canceled!”.\nNOTE\nThe stored numbers are not the ones you watched. If the counts do not fit the parameter range, the panel halves both numbers together, again and again, until they fit. The ratio is kept; only the two stored figures come out smaller than what was counted."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate the dispenser by weight","h":"","u":"en/one/calibrate-dispenser-weight.html","x":"Turn a ground-driven dispenser into a container and weigh the product, so that the panel can watch the rate in kilograms per hectare."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate the dispenser by weight","h":"","u":"en/one/calibrate-dispenser-weight.html","x":"This calibration stores how many grams one dispenser shaft pulse delivers — “Weight, gr” and “Weight, pulses” of the dispenser sensor. Repeat it whenever the product changes. It is done standing, at the “Operator” level; the panel raises itself to “Agronomist” for the length of the calibration and puts the level back on exit.\nYou need: a container that holds 2 to 60 kg of the product, and scales.\nThe item is only there when “Weight control” is switched on for this dispenser (Dispenser shaft sensor).\nHold the tile of the dispenser sensor and choose “Dispenser calibration(weight)”. The first screen lists the target rates of the products in use, in kg/ha; the product of this dispenser is marked with > and <.\nTouch “Calibrate dispenser N” (or press “PLUS”). If the dispenser cannot be calibrated, a red line stands there instead with the reason: “Not controlled”, “No connection” or “FAILURE”. “Exit” (or “BACK”) leaves.\nThe second screen gives the preparation. Weigh the empty container and write the weight down.\nFill the hopper with the product and place the empty container under the dispenser.\nTurn the rotor until its cells are filled. This matters more than it looks: a rotor with empty cells gives short measure for the first turns, and that error lands in the stored figure.\nTouch “Go to calibration” (or press “UP”). “Cancel” (or “BACK”) steps back. The third screen counts “Impulses” and turns a rotor icon as they arrive.\nEmpty the container and place it under the dispenser again.\nTurn the dispenser rotor and fill the container as far as possible — not less than 2 kg and not more than 60 kg.\nTouch “Complete” (or press “MINUS”). The button appears only after more than ten pulses. “Cancel” asks “Are you sure?” before throwing the measurement away.\nWeigh the filled container. The number pad asks to “Enter the difference of the filled and empty container in grams”. Type the weight of the product alone — 2000 to 60000 g; the pad offers 10000 — and touch “OK”. The panel stores “Weight, gr” and “Weight, pulses”.\nNOTE\nA dispenser alarm during the calibration does not stop it — only a lost connection does. The measurement is mechanical, and the panel keeps out of the way until the container is weighed."},{"p":"one","t":"Dispenser electric motor","h":"","u":"en/one/dispenser-motor.html","x":"The parameters of an electric metering motor — gear ratio, direction, speed window, product, calibration figures and the calibration button."},{"p":"one","t":"Dispenser electric motor","h":"","u":"en/one/dispenser-motor.html","x":"An electric motor drives a dispenser at the speed the panel works out from the target rate and the travel speed. Open its context menu as in Sensor settings.\nThe context menu of a metering motor, top part.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange\nLevel\n“Holes in disk”\n“Precise” mode only.\n1 … 200\nOperator\n“Numerator” / “Denominator”\nTogether: the gear ratio between the motor and the dispenser shaft.\n1 … 500 each\nAgronomist\n“Rotation direction”\nThe direction the motor turns. Set it so that the dispenser turns the right way for how the motor is fitted; default “Forward”.\n“Forward” / “Backward”\nAgronomist\n“Rollback when jammed”\nBack the rotor off and try again instead of stalling.\n“No” / “Yes”\nAgronomist\n“Section control D.I.”\nLets a switch on the section-control input of the motor controller stop this motor: “Shut off(0)” stops it while the input reads 0, “Shut off(1)” while it reads 1; “No” ignores the input.\n“No” / “Shut off(0)” / “Shut off(1)”\nService\n“Start delay,mS”\nThe shortest time between the start of one metering motor and the next: a motor that would start sooner waits, so the motors start one after another. Default 30 ms.\n0 … 500, steps of 5\nService\n“Group start address”\nSet at commissioning — see Device configuration.\nService\n“Type of product”\nWhich of the four products this motor meters.\nAgronomist\n“Weight, gr” / “Rotations”\nBoth set by the calibration by weight.\n1 … 65535 each\nAgronomist\n“Minimum, rpm” / “Maximum, rpm”\nThe shaft speed window the panel may use.\n0.1 … 500.0 each\nAgronomist\n“Button input”\nDigital input of the motor controller used as a calibration button; “No” = none.\nNo, 1 … 3\nAgronomist\n“Activation signal”\nWhich state of that input counts as pressed.\n0 / 1\nAgronomist\nThe lower part of the same menu, with the calibration item.\n“Minimum, rpm” and “Maximum, rpm” are not a safety limit but the window the rate control works in. The dosing speed window on the calibration screen is worked out from them together with the roller figures. “Button input” and “Activation signal” let one person start the motor from the dispenser during the calibration — see Calibrate a metering motor by weight.\nThe status screen shows “Working” or “Faulty” and the last fault code (Read the fault code of an electric motor). Alarms: Metering motor alarms, Dosing by weight. The controller itself is set up in Configure the motor controllers. “Electric motors” in the context menu (“Service” level) opens the Motors screen on this motor — the same screen as “Service” → “Electric motors” (Motor controller diagnostics)."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate a metering motor by weight","h":"","u":"en/one/calibrate-motor-weight.html","x":"Let the electric motor turn the metering roller into a container and weigh the product, so that the panel knows how much one turn delivers."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate a metering motor by weight","h":"","u":"en/one/calibrate-motor-weight.html","x":"This calibration stores how much product one turn of the metering roller delivers — “Weight, gr” and “Rotations” of one motor. Repeat it whenever the product or the roller changes. It is done at the “Operator” level.\nYou need: the machine standing still, a container that holds 1 to 60 kg of the product, and scales.\nIMPORTANT\nThe item appears only while the machine is standing still. In motion the panel removes it from the context menu without a message. And while the calibration is open, every electric motor on the machine — seed and fertilizer — is in manual mode at zero speed: nothing meters. Leaving the calibration returns them all to automatic rate control.\nHold the tile of the motor and choose “Dispenser calibration(weight)”.\nThe motor's context menu with the calibration item at the top.\nThe first screen shows the target rates and, under “Product dosage speed limitation:”, the “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” the current settings allow. If it reads “Can not provide dosage!” in red, the roller fitted cannot deliver the rate at any speed — calibration will not repair that; fit another roller. Otherwise touch “Calibrate electric motor N” (or press “PLUS”).\nThe second screen prepares the rotor. Weigh the empty container and write the weight down. Fill the hopper and place the empty container under the dispenser.\nTouch “Fill the rotor cells” (or press “MENU”). The motor turns the driven shaft exactly two turns and stops by itself. “Stop motor” (“BACK”) interrupts it. Repeat until the cells are full.\nTouch “Go to calibration” (or press “UP”). The third screen shows “Rotations” to one decimal.\nEmpty the container and place it under the dispenser again.\nTouch “Calibration” (or press “MENU”): the motor runs. Touch “Stop motor” (or press “BACK”) to stop it. Run and stop as often as needed — the counter keeps a running total. Fill the container as far as possible, not less than 1 kg and not more than 60 kg.\nTouch “Complete” (or press “MINUS”). It appears once more than one full turn has been made.\nWeigh the filled container. The number pad asks for the difference of the filled and empty container in grams — 1000 to 60000 g. Type it and touch “OK”. The panel stores “Weight, gr” and “Rotations” for this motor.\nDuring the rotor filling the motor runs at the middle of its “Minimum, rpm” … “Maximum, rpm” window, but not faster than 60 rpm."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate a metering motor by weight","h":"Run the motor from a button at the dispenser","u":"en/one/calibrate-motor-weight.html#run-the-motor-from-a-button-at-the-dispenser","x":"If “Button input” of the motor is set to an input (1 … 3) and “Activation signal” is set to match that button (Dispenser electric motor), a button on that input starts and stops the motor exactly as “Calibration” and “Stop motor” do: the motor runs while the button is held and stops when it is released. One person can stand at the dispenser with the container instead of walking to the cab between runs. The screen shows the same buttons whether or not such a button is set up — the panel gives no hint of it."},{"p":"one","t":"Calibrate a metering motor by weight","h":"When the calibration stops by itself","u":"en/one/calibrate-motor-weight.html#when-the-calibration-stops-by-itself","x":"The calibration ends with a failure if the motor stops answering, if the motor raises an alarm, or if ten seconds pass without pulses from the rotation sensor — then the panel shows “No data from rotation sensor”. A jammed or unplugged rotation sensor ends the run rather than quietly storing zero."},{"p":"one","t":"Fan sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/fan-sensor.html","x":"The parameters of the fan speed sensor — the allowed speed band and the pulses per turn."},{"p":"one","t":"Fan sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/fan-sensor.html","x":"The fan sensor watches the speed of the fan of a pneumatic drill or planter. Open its context menu as in Sensor settings — the picture there shows exactly this menu.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange\nLevel\n“Minimum, rpm”\nBelow it the panel raises “Low speed of rotation”.\n500 … 9000, steps of 500\nOperator\n“Maximum, rpm”\nAbove it the panel raises “High speed of rotation”.\n500 … 9000, steps of 500\nOperator\n“Pulses”\nHow many pulses one turn of the fan gives.\n1 … 60\nAgronomist\nThe status screen shows the pulse frequency F= in Hz. Alarms: Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"Pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/pressure-sensor.html","x":"The parameters of the air pressure sensor — the smoothing filter and the allowed pressure band."},{"p":"one","t":"Pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/pressure-sensor.html","x":"The pressure sensor measures the positive pressure or the vacuum of the air system. Open its context menu as in Sensor settings.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange\nLevel\n“Filter”\nHow strongly the reading is smoothed.\n1 … 16\nAgronomist\n“Minimum, Pa”\nThe lower limit of the allowed band.\n0 … 10000, steps of 100\nOperator\n“Maximum, Pa”\nThe upper limit of the allowed band.\n0 … 10000, steps of 100\nOperator\nThe status screen shows the pressure P= in kPa. Alarms: Air pressure. Giving a new pressure sensor its address: Configure a pressure sensor."},{"p":"one","t":"Marker sensors","h":"","u":"en/one/marker-sensors.html","x":"The parameters of the marker sensors — which marker a sensor watches and which input state means \"lowered\"."},{"p":"one","t":"Marker sensors","h":"","u":"en/one/marker-sensors.html","x":"The marker sensors tell the panel which marker is down. The profile declares “No”, “One” or “Both” (The profile). Open the context menu of a marker sensor as in Sensor settings.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nLevel\n“Connected marker”\n“Left” or “Right”.\nAgronomist\n“Signal \"down\"”\nWhich state of the input means the marker is lowered.\nAgronomist\nThe status screen of a marker sensor shows only the common lines.\nNOTE\nThe marker sensors are wired to the tramline actuator controller. One such controller carries the actuator and both marker sensors — see Configure a tramline actuator controller."},{"p":"one","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"","u":"en/one/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html","x":"The settings of the two-state sensors — hopper level, work position of the implement, and the five tractor engine sensors."},{"p":"one","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"","u":"en/one/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html","x":"These sensors report one of two states and nothing more. Open the context menu of any of them as in Sensor settings. Their status screen shows Pin: and the level on the input, \"0\" or \"1\"."},{"p":"one","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"Hopper sensor","u":"en/one/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html#hopper-sensor","x":"The hopper sensor has no parameters of its own — its context menu holds only the three common items.\nThe context menu of a hopper sensor.\nAlarm: Hopper empty."},{"p":"one","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"Work position sensor","u":"en/one/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html#work-position-sensor","x":"The work position sensor tells the panel whether the implement is lowered for work or raised for transport. The status screen shows Position: Working or Position: Transporting.\nThe context menu of the work position sensor.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange\nLevel\n“Transporting position signal”\nWhich state of the input means the transport position.\n0 / 1\nAgronomist\nThe menu can also hold “Alarms criteria” (“Yes” / “No”, “Agronomist” level): with “Yes”, the default, the seed, fertilizer and dispenser shaft alarms are not raised while the implement is in the transport position; the item is hidden, and always “Yes”, on machines with metering motors or a liquid fertilizer pump controller."},{"p":"one","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"Engine sensors","u":"en/one/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html#engine-sensors","x":"“No battery charge”, “Emergency oil pressure”, “Ignition”, “Low fuel” and “Air filter” are five separate inputs, each switched on in the profile on its own (The profile).\nThe context menu of the oil pressure sensor.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nLevel\n“Activation signal”\nWhich state of the input is the one worth reporting.\nAgronomist\n“Signal delay, sec”\nHow long that state must hold before it counts. Defaults: 15 s for the fuel level, 5 s for the air filter.\nAgronomist\nAlarms: Tractor engine alarms."},{"p":"one","t":"Tramline actuator","h":"","u":"en/one/tramline-actuator.html","x":"The two parameters of the tramline actuator, where the rest of its settings live, and what its status screen shows."},{"p":"one","t":"Tramline actuator","h":"","u":"en/one/tramline-actuator.html","x":"The tramline actuator shuts the sections of the tramline bout. It has two parameters of its own, in its context menu (Sensor settings):\nParameter\nValues\nLevel\n“Cutoff position”\n“Pulled out” / “Pulled in” — the position of the actuator in which its sections are shut off. Default “Pulled out”.\nAgronomist\n“Switch”\n“when seeding” / “always” — when the actuator is switched. Default “when seeding”.\nAgronomist\nThe rest of its settings live elsewhere:\nWhat\nWhere\nThe address and the marker channels of its controller\nConfigure a tramline actuator controller\nThe rhythm — which bout shuts which actuator\nTramline rhythm\nWhich sections belong to the actuator\n“Actuators to sensors mapping” — Seeding machine parameters\nThe status screen of the actuator shows Status: Pulled in, Pulled out, Working while the shutter is moving, or Unknown. Alarms: Tramline alarms.\nNOTE\nOne actuator controller carries three things: the actuator itself and both marker sensors of the bout. That is why a machine with markers and a tramline needs only one such controller."},{"p":"one","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"","u":"en/one/device-configuration.html","x":"What device configuration is, the preparation it needs, and the three-item pattern every configuration menu follows."},{"p":"one","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"","u":"en/one/device-configuration.html","x":"When a system is commissioned, or a sensor is replaced, each new device must be given its address — the number by which the panel finds it on the sensor network. That is done in “Service” → “Sensors Configuration”, one device at a time.\nWARNING\nFor authorised service engineers. A device given the wrong address disappears from the panel or takes the place of another one. Follow these topics or the instructions of an Agriwelt GmbH engineer, and do not try menu items at random."},{"p":"one","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"Prepare","u":"en/one/device-configuration.html#prepare","x":"Disconnect every sensor from the network connector of the panel.\nConnect only the one device that is to be given a new address.\nSwitch the panel on. Alarms about lost communication appear — close them all (How alarms work).\nRaise the access level to “Agronomist” — see Enable the Agronomist level.\nPress “BACK”, then choose “Service” → “Sensors Configuration”. The menu offers four kinds of device.\nThe “Sensors Configuration” menu.\nIMPORTANT\nOne device on the cable at a time. The panel addresses whatever answers. With two devices of the same kind connected, which of them takes the new address is not defined."},{"p":"one","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"The pattern","u":"en/one/device-configuration.html#the-pattern","x":"All four menus work the same way:\nItem\nWhat it is\n“Sensor address” / “Device address”\nWhich device the menu talks to. Leave it at “AUTO” and the panel talks to the single device that answers. Pressing “MENU” on this item starts a search; each further press steps to the next device found.\n“New address”\nThe address the device is to take. Set it with “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\n“Set”\nWrites it. “Done” means the device has accepted it.\nCAUTION\n“MENU” on the address item is not a harmless look. It starts a search on the network and can change what the connected devices answer to. Use it only with a single device on the cable, never on an assembled machine.\nAfterwards, every time: disconnect the device and connect it again, return to the home screen and check that it appears where it should. Then set the level back to “Operator” — or switch the panel off and on, which does the same."},{"p":"one","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"The devices","u":"en/one/device-configuration.html#the-devices","x":"Device\nTopic\nSeed sensor\nConfigure a seed sensor\nPressure sensor\nConfigure a pressure sensor\nHUB8 input device\nConfigure a HUB8\nTramline actuator controller\nConfigure a tramline actuator controller\nElectric motor controllers — their own way in\nConfigure the motor controllers"},{"p":"one","t":"Configure a seed sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-seed-sensor.html","x":"Give a new or replaced seed sensor the number of its row."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure a seed sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-seed-sensor.html","x":"For a seed sensor the new address is simply the number of the row — the same number the operator sees under the column on the home screen. Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this sensor on the cable, “Agronomist” level.\nThe address of a seed sensor can only be changed while the sensor is working normally. A tube sensor is ready as soon as it is connected. An open sensor must sit on its bracket, or be held so that no daylight falls into the optical channel — the gap between the two glasses. In direct light the sensor reports a permanent seed flow and does not accept the new address.\nChoose “Service” → “Sensors Configuration” → “Seed sensor” and press “MENU”. The menu has three items.\nThe “Seed sensor” configuration menu.\nLeave “Sensor address” at “AUTO”.\nSelect “New address” and set the row number with “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\nSelect “Set” and press “MENU”. The panel answers “Done”.\nThe panel confirms with “Done”.\nDisconnect the sensor and connect it again. On the home screen, check that it appears under its row.\nWhen all sensors are done, set the level back to “Operator”, or switch the panel off and on."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure a pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-pressure-sensor.html","x":"Give a new or replaced air pressure sensor its address."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure a pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-pressure-sensor.html","x":"Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this sensor on the cable, “Agronomist” level. The menu has the same three items as every configuration menu. The address values are not printed in this manual: take them from the data sheet of the system.\nChoose “Service” → “Sensors Configuration” → “Pressure sensor” and press “MENU”. The menu shows “Sensor address”, “New address” and “Set”.\nLeave “Sensor address” at “AUTO”.\nSelect “New address”. The panel proposes the correct value for the first pressure sensor by itself; each further sensor takes the next one. Check the value against the data sheet and correct it with “PLUS” / “MINUS” if needed.\nSelect “Set” and press “MENU”. The panel answers “Done”.\nDisconnect the sensor and connect it again. On the home screen, check that its indicator appears.\nWhen all sensors are done, set the level back to “Operator”, or switch the panel off and on. Its parameters: Pressure sensor."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure a HUB8","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-hub8.html","x":"Tell a HUB8 input device which sensor is wired to each of its eight channels."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure a HUB8","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-hub8.html","x":"The HUB8 carries several sensors at once. Its menu is longer than the others: after the device address come its eight channels, each with a type and a number. Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this HUB8 on the cable, “Agronomist” level.\n“Device address” — which HUB8 the menu talks to. Leave it at “AUTO”.\n“Channel 1 type” — what kind of sensor is wired to channel 1.\n“Channel 1 address” — which sensor of that kind it is: hopper 1, dispenser 2, and so on.\nThe value of the selected item, changed with “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\nScroll bar. Channels 2 to 8 follow in the same pairs, then “Set”.\nThe HUB8 menu with its first channels.\nThe type and the number together make the sensor appear on the home screen under the right icon.\nChoose “Service” → “Sensors Configuration” → “HUB8” and press “MENU”.\nLeave “Device address” at “AUTO”.\nFor each channel that carries a sensor, set “Channel N type” and “Channel N address” with “PLUS” / “MINUS”. Leave a channel that carries nothing as it is.\nSelect “Set” at the end of the menu and press “MENU”. The panel answers “Done”.\nDisconnect the HUB8 and connect it again. On the home screen, check that each sensor appears under its icon.\nWhen all devices are done, set the level back to “Operator”, or switch the panel off and on."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure a tramline actuator controller","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-tramline-actuator.html","x":"Tell an actuator controller which tramline actuator it drives and which marker sensors are wired to it."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure a tramline actuator controller","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-tramline-actuator.html","x":"The actuator controller carries three things at once — the tramline actuator and the two marker sensors of the bout — and each has its own line. That is why one controller serves an actuator and both markers. Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this controller on the cable, “Agronomist” level.\n“Device address” — which controller the menu talks to. Leave it at “AUTO”.\n“Actuator” — the number of the tramline actuator this controller drives, or “No” if it drives none.\n“Input device, channel 1” — the first marker sensor, or “No”.\n“Input device, channel 2” — the second marker sensor, or “No”.\n“Set” — writes the three lines to the controller.\nThe configuration menu of a tramline actuator controller, all lines still at “No”.\nChoose “Service” → “Sensors Configuration” → “Tramline actuator” and press “MENU”.\nLeave “Device address” at “AUTO”.\nSet “Actuator”, “Input device, channel 1” and “Input device, channel 2” with “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\nSelect “Set” and press “MENU”. The panel answers “Done”.\nDisconnect the controller and connect it again. On the home screen, check that the actuator and the markers appear.\nWhen all devices are done, set the level back to “Operator”, or switch the panel off and on. The actuator's own settings: Tramline actuator; the markers: Marker sensors."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-motor-controllers.html","x":"Give a metering motor controller its address, choose the kind of controller, and set its parameters."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"","u":"en/one/configure-motor-controllers.html","x":"The controllers that drive the metering motors are not addressed from “Sensors Configuration”. They have their own way in, their own choice of controller kind and their own parameters.\nWARNING\nFor authorised service engineers. As with every device on the cable: one controller connected at a time. The panel writes to whichever controller answers; with two of them connected, which one takes the new address is not defined."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"Open the motor controller menu","u":"en/one/configure-motor-controllers.html#open-the-motor-controller-menu","x":"Raise the level to “Service” (Enable the Agronomist level — choose “Service”).\nPress “MENU” → “Service” → “Electric motors”. The Motors screen opens.\nThe Motors screen of motor 1, page “Registers”.\nPress “MENU” on the Motors screen. Two items appear, both at the “Agronomist” level: “Change address” and “Configuration”.\nThe menu of the Motors screen.\nOn a panel built for a system without electric motors, “Electric motors” is not in the “Service” menu at all."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"Change the address","u":"en/one/configure-motor-controllers.html#change-the-address","x":"Select “Change address” and press “MENU”. Three items open.\n“Motor address” — the address the controller answers on now. Leave it as found.\n“New address” — the address it is to take. Take it from the data sheet of the system; the values are not printed in this manual.\nSelect “Set” and press “MENU” to write it."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"Choose the controller kind","u":"en/one/configure-motor-controllers.html#choose-the-controller-kind","x":"“Configuration” opens the configuration of the controller. Four kinds are supported, offered by number:\nKind\nWhat it is\n“1)universal”\nThe universal controller: two DC channels or one stepper channel on the same board.\n“2)DC motor”\nA DC motor controller.\n“3)STEPPER fw0.006+”\nA stepper controller, firmware 0.006 and later.\n“4)BGxxCI”\nA BG45CI motor with the controller built in, on the older board.\nThe panel reads the kind back from the controller and opens on the one that answers. Choosing another kind by hand is how a replacement board is set up — not a way to change what a motor is."},{"p":"one","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"Set the parameters","u":"en/one/configure-motor-controllers.html#set-the-parameters","x":"Every configuration menu ends with “Set”, and “Set” carries a value of its own: the address group, 0 to 3. Nothing reaches the controller until “Set” is entered — the items above it change the numbers the panel holds, not those the controller runs on.\nKind\nWhat it is given\nSTEPPER\n“Pulses” 1 … 8192 — the pulses of one turn of the motor shaft; “Motor type” from a list; “Set”.\nBGxxCI\n“Pulses” 1 … 8192; “Set”. Nothing else — the controller carries the rest.\nDC motor\n“Pulses”, “Motor type”, the regulator terms “PID kP”, “PID kI”, “PID kD” (0 … 65535, read as hundredths), “TURBINA” 0/1, and two speed–time pairs “TIME1” / “SPEED1”, “TIME2” / “SPEED2”; “Set”.\nuniversal\nThe largest set, in three menus: “Work mode”, “Inputs setup” and “Parameters”.\nThe “Parameters” menu of the universal controller:\nParameter\nRange\n“Motor type”\n0 … 255, from the controller's own table\n“Pulses”\n1 … 8192 per shaft turn\n“Acceleration,Ms” / “Deceleration,Ms”\n50 … 65535 — the ramp up and down\n“Minimum, rpm” / “Maximum, rpm”\n0 … 65535 — the shaft speed window\n“PID kP”, “PID kI”, “PID kD”\n0 … 65535, hundredths\n“!! Temp,*C”\n0 … 200 — the temperature at which the controller alarms\n“I_peak,A” / “I_peak,mS”\nthe peak current and how long it may be drawn\n“I_push,A” / “I_push,Sec”\nthe breakaway current and its time\n“I_hilo,A” / “I_hilo,Sec”\nthe sustained-current threshold and its time\n“Set”\nwrites all of it, at the chosen address group\nIMPORTANT\nThe current and temperature limits protect the motor. They are the controller's own protection thresholds: set them from the data sheet of the motor, not by trying values until the alarms stop.\nThe items “PID kP”, “PID kI”, “PID kD”, “TURBINA”, “TIME1”, “SPEED1”, “TIME2”, “SPEED2”, “!! Temp,*C”, “I_peak,A”, “I_push,A”, “I_hilo,A” and their time partners read the same in every language of the panel.\nReading what the controller reports: Motor controller diagnostics."},{"p":"one","t":"Motor controller diagnostics","h":"","u":"en/one/motor-controller-diagnostics.html","x":"How to read what a metering motor controller reports about itself on the Motors screen — speed, error code, voltages, temperature, current, status bits and fault counters."},{"p":"one","t":"Motor controller diagnostics","h":"","u":"en/one/motor-controller-diagnostics.html","x":"The Motors screen is also the diagnostic screen of a motor controller. It shows, live, what the controller reports about itself. Open it with “MENU” → “Service” → “Electric motors” (“Service” level). The working view of the same screen is described in Motors screen.\n“Motor #1” — which motor is shown. (−) / (+) step to the previous / next motor.\n“Registers” — the name of the page.\nThe status word — sixteen bits of the controller; a lit (yellow) cell is a bit that is set.\nThe legend of the bits — the number and the name of each bit. Table below.\n“Speed” / “Target speed” — what the shaft is doing against what the panel asked for. A gap that does not close is the first sign of a load the motor cannot carry.\n“Error code” / “Act.posit.” — the controller's own error number (codes), and the position it counts itself at.\n“Volt.mot.,mV” / “Volt.elec.,mV” — the voltage at the motor and at the electronics, in millivolts. A motor voltage far below the electronics voltage means the supply sags under load.\n“Temperat.,C” / “Current,mA” — the controller's temperature and the current it draws. Compare the current with “I_hilo,A” set for this motor (Configure the motor controllers).\nThe “Registers” page of motor 1, standing."},{"p":"one","t":"Motor controller diagnostics","h":"The status bits","u":"en/one/motor-controller-diagnostics.html#the-status-bits","x":"Bit\nName\nBit\nName\n0\nEnable\n8\nToggle\n1\nError\n9\nCmdTgl\n2\nWarn.\n10\nCmdErr\n3\nMoving\n11\nStpHalt\n4\nReach\n12\nLimCur\n5\nLimit\n13\nLimVel\n6\nFolErr\n14\nLimPos\n7\nHomDone\n15\nLimSVel\nThe legend is drawn in English on every panel, whatever its language: it is a bit map of the controller, not a message to the operator."},{"p":"one","t":"Motor controller diagnostics","h":"The fault counters","u":"en/one/motor-controller-diagnostics.html#the-fault-counters","x":"The statistics page of motor 1 on field 1.\nThe faults this motor has collected on the chosen field — they are part of the field statistics, not of the motor:\nCounter\nWhat it counts\n“Jammed”\nThe rotor stalled and the controller had to give up or back off.\n“Disconnects”\nThe controller stopped answering.\n“Other”\nEverything else the controller reported, overcurrent and overtemperature included — they have no counter of their own.\n“In failure”\nThe distance in metres this motor spent in a fault state.\nEach counter is white at zero and red once it is not. A red figure does not mean the motor is faulty now — it means it was, somewhere on this field, and the seed placed there is worth a look."},{"p":"one","t":"The Service menu","h":"","u":"en/one/service-menu.html","x":"Every item of the \"Service\" menu, its access level, and which ones an engineer should leave alone."},{"p":"one","t":"The Service menu","h":"","u":"en/one/service-menu.html","x":"“MENU” → “Service”. Which items you see depends on the access level (Access levels). The panel has four levels — “Operator”, “Agronomist”, “Service” and “Debug”; “Debug” sits above “Service” and is used at manufacture only.\nThe “Service” menu, top part.\nItem\nLevel\nWhat it is\n“Profile”\nService\nWhich devices the machine has, and how many. The profile\n“Sensors Configuration”\nAgronomist\nGiving devices their address. Device configuration\n“Registers”\nDebug\nA raw register view. Not part of any procedure.\n“Electric motors”\nService\nThe metering motor controllers. Configure the motor controllers\n“Serial number”\nDebug\nThe serial number of the panel, entered once at manufacture.\n“Count seeds always”\nAgronomist\n“No” / “Yes” — keep the seed counter running even when the panel would otherwise stop it.\n“Enter key”\nAgronomist\nEnters the licence key that takes the panel out of the demonstration state.\n“Set trial”\nService\nStarts the demonstration period.\n“Trial, hours”\nService\n1 … 999 — how long that period lasts.\n“Imitation mode”\nService\n“No” / “Yes” — the panel works from simulated data. See the warning below.\n“Display type”\nDebug\nWhich display the firmware drives. Set at manufacture.\n“Launch updater”\nService\nRestarts the panel into the updater to take firmware from the memory card.\nThree further items — the tracker protocol and two switches for logging to the memory card — exist in the firmware but are set never to be shown. They cannot be reached at any level.\nWARNING\n“Imitation mode” is not a test aid to be left on. With it on, the panel works from figures it makes up: every number on every screen and every entry in the statistics is invented. Switch it off before the machine goes to work — and check it if a panel reports a plausible field it never sowed.\n“Imitation mode”, “Display type”, “Launch updater” and the two logging switches are shown in English whatever language the panel is set to."},{"p":"one","t":"How alarms work","h":"","u":"en/one/how-alarms-work.html","x":"When the Hectare One raises an alarm, what the alarm window shows, how to close it, and how to use the alarm topics of this manual."},{"p":"one","t":"How alarms work","h":"","u":"en/one/how-alarms-work.html","x":"An alarm is a window that opens over the working screen, with a warning tone. It tells you that one device — a sensor, an actuator, a motor controller — or the system as a whole has found a fault. The alarm topics in this section are grouped by the device that raises the alarm. Look for the words you see on the screen, not for a description of them."},{"p":"one","t":"How alarms work","h":"When the panel raises an alarm","u":"en/one/how-alarms-work.html#when-the-panel-raises-an-alarm","x":"A fault is not reported the moment it appears. The condition must hold without a break for the time set in “Alarm delay” before the alarm window opens.\nSetting\nValue\nWhere it is set\n“Settings” menu, item “Alarm delay”. Available at the “Operator” access level. See The Settings menu.\nRange\n2 to 20 seconds, in steps of 1 second.\nDefault\n3 seconds. A value outside the range is reset to the default by itself.\nA short delay makes the panel react quickly, but also to brief disturbances — a single bump, a momentary gap in the seed flow. A long delay keeps the screen quiet, but a real fault can run for up to twenty seconds before you are told.\nTIP\nIf the system alarms too often on a rough field, raise “Alarm delay” first. Change the sensor settings only when a longer delay does not help.\nTwo cases do not follow the delay:\nLost communication is counted in polls, not in seconds. “Connection lost” appears after three polls in a row without an answer from the device.\nRight after a sensor has been set up, its alarms are not processed for a short blocking period. This prevents a burst of alarms while a device is being configured."},{"p":"one","t":"How alarms work","h":"The alarm window","u":"en/one/how-alarms-work.html#the-alarm-window","x":"“FAILURE!” — the title of every alarm window, whatever the fault. It is not the fault itself.\nThe alarm text — the fault. Look it up under this text in the alarm topics.\nThe kind of device that raises the alarm — here a hopper sensor. The same text can come from different devices; this line tells you which.\nThe number of the device — here hopper 2. When several devices of the same kind raise the same alarm, they share one window and are listed together, for example #3-5,8.\n“Close” — closes this alarm. Below.\nThe alarm window of an empty hopper.\nThe alarms of the panel itself carry the line “HOME - close message” in their text instead of a device line. The alarms of the sensors do not."},{"p":"one","t":"How alarms work","h":"Closing an alarm","u":"en/one/how-alarms-work.html#closing-an-alarm","x":"What you do\nWhat happens\nTouch the red “Close” button in the window, or press “HOME”\nThis alarm closes. If more alarms are waiting, the next one opens. While the window is open, “HOME” is the only lit button.\nHold “HOME”\nEvery open alarm window closes at once and the panel returns to the home screen.\nClosing a window does not repair the fault. The alarms of a sensor that are still active are listed on its status screen — see The sensor status screen."},{"p":"one","t":"How alarms work","h":"Alarms are hidden at the higher access levels","u":"en/one/how-alarms-work.html#alarms-are-hidden-at-the-higher-access-levels","x":"At the “Agronomist” and “Service” levels alarm windows are not shown. They are not lost: they wait. When you return to “Operator”, the panel drops the ones that have gone away and shows the ones that are still true, one after another. See Access levels."},{"p":"one","t":"How alarms work","h":"Not alarms: SLOWER and FASTER","u":"en/one/how-alarms-work.html#not-alarms-slower-and-faster","x":"When the travel speed leaves the permitted range, no alarm window opens. The home screen shows “SLOWER” or “FASTER” instead, and keeps showing it while you correct the speed. See Indicators."},{"p":"one","t":"How alarms work","h":"How the alarm topics are written","u":"en/one/how-alarms-work.html#how-the-alarm-topics-are-written","x":"Every alarm has its own heading with the text exactly as on the screen; where the message has two lines on the panel, both lines are given. Under it: what the panel has detected, and what to check, in the order a mechanic checks it — the quick checks on the panel first, then the machine, then the sensor itself.\nNOTE\nIf the checks do not clear the fault, call the service support. The contact details are on the back cover of the printed manual."},{"p":"one","t":"Any device: connection lost or wrong device type","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-any-device.html","x":"The two alarms any sensor, actuator, controller or input device can raise — no answer from the device, or the wrong kind of device answering."},{"p":"one","t":"Any device: connection lost or wrong device type","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-any-device.html","x":"These two alarms are not tied to one kind of device. The name of the device in the alarm window tells you which one is affected. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Any device: connection lost or wrong device type","h":"“Connection lost”","u":"en/one/alarms-any-device.html#connection-lost","x":"What it means. The panel has received no answer from the device in three polls in a row. This applies to sensors, actuators, controllers and the input device alike.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe device is connected to the network.\nThe cable and the connector of the device.\nThe power supply of the device. It must meet the values in the device passport."},{"p":"one","t":"Any device: connection lost or wrong device type","h":"“Wrong device type!”","u":"en/one/alarms-any-device.html#wrong-device-type","x":"What it means. A device of another kind answers where the panel expects a device of this kind. The cause is either a wrong setting in the system profile or a wrong configuration of the device itself.\nWhat to do: call the service support. Do not try to correct it by changing the profile or the device configuration at random — see Device configuration.\nSEE ALSO\nThe status screen of a sensor shows the same two states as “No connection” and “Wrong device type!” in red — see The sensor status screen."},{"p":"one","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-rotation.html","x":"What to do when the fan or the dispenser shaft turns too slowly, too fast, or not at all."},{"p":"one","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-rotation.html","x":"The fan sensor and the dispenser shaft sensor use the same two messages. The device name in the alarm window tells you which one it is. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"“Low speed of rotation” / “High speed of rotation”","u":"en/one/alarms-rotation.html#low-speed-of-rotation-high-speed-of-rotation","x":"What it means. The fan or the dispenser shaft turns slower or faster than allowed.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe component being watched — the fan or the dispenser and its drive.\nThe settings of the sensor: for the fan “Minimum, rpm”, “Maximum, rpm” and “Pulses” — see Fan sensor; for the dispenser — see Dispenser sensor.\nDispenser only: the travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThe sensor itself is serviceable.\nDispenser only: run the calibration — see Calibrate the dispenser rotation."},{"p":"one","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"“No rotation”","u":"en/one/alarms-rotation.html#no-rotation","x":"What it means. The dispenser shaft does not turn. Only the dispenser shaft sensor raises this alarm.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe dispenser and its drive.\nThe settings of the sensor — see Dispenser sensor.\nThe sensor itself is serviceable."},{"p":"one","t":"Hopper empty","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-hopper.html","x":"What to do when the panel reports that a hopper is running empty."},{"p":"one","t":"Hopper empty","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-hopper.html","x":"The hopper sensor sits in the hopper at a fixed height. It reports when the product falls below it. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Hopper empty","h":"“Hopper empty”","u":"en/one/alarms-hopper.html#hopper-empty","x":"What it means. The level of the product in the hopper is very low. The hopper needs refilling.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nFill the hopper.\nIf the product stands above the height at which the sensor is mounted and the alarm stays, check that the sensor is serviceable. Its status screen shows the level on its input as “0” or “1” — see The sensor status screen.\nThe hopper sensor has no settings of its own beyond the common ones — see Hopper, implement position and engine sensors."},{"p":"one","t":"Air pressure too high or too low","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-pressure.html","x":"What to do when the pressure sensor of the air system reports a pressure or vacuum outside the allowed range."},{"p":"one","t":"Air pressure too high or too low","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-pressure.html","x":"The pressure sensor measures the positive pressure or the vacuum of the air system. Both alarms look at the absolute value — the size of the pressure or vacuum, whichever it is. The sensor's limits are described in Pressure sensor. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Air pressure too high or too low","h":"“High pressure”","u":"en/one/alarms-pressure.html#high-pressure","x":"What it means. The measured pressure or vacuum is greater than allowed.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nReduce the speed of the fan or turbine.\nThe position of the regulating damper of the air intake, if there is one.\nThe tube to the pressure sensor is connected correctly, and the measuring line is free of blockages."},{"p":"one","t":"Air pressure too high or too low","h":"“Low pressure”","u":"en/one/alarms-pressure.html#low-pressure","x":"What it means. The measured pressure or vacuum is less than allowed.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nIncrease the speed of the fan or turbine.\nThe position of the regulating damper of the air intake, if there is one.\nThe air duct for leaks.\nThe tube to the pressure sensor is connected correctly, and the measuring line is free of blockages.\nNOTE\nThe liquid fertilizer pump controller has its own “High pressure” alarm for the liquid line. It is a different alarm — see Liquid fertilizer pump."},{"p":"one","t":"Application rate too low, too high, or not under control","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-rate.html","x":"What to do when the seed, dry fertilizer or liquid fertilizer sensors report a rate below or above the allowed band, or too little product to start control at all."},{"p":"one","t":"Application rate too low, too high, or not under control","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-rate.html","x":"The section sensors — seed, dry fertilizer and liquid fertilizer — compare the rate they measure with the target. How the window works: How alarms work.\nTIP\nCheck the travel speed first. Every rate on the panel is worked out from the distance travelled. If the speed on the screen is wrong, every rate is wrong by the same amount, and the sensors are not to blame."},{"p":"one","t":"Application rate too low, too high, or not under control","h":"“Not under control!” / “Check seeding rate”","u":"en/one/alarms-rate.html#not-under-control-check-seeding-rate","x":"What it means. Over the set number of section sensors, the rate of the product is below the minimum needed for the panel to start controlling the application at all.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThe target rate, or the minimum alarm rate — which one depends on the control mode of the group of sections.\nThere is enough product in the hoppers or in the tank.\nSeed or dry fertilizer sensor: “Fraction” in its settings. The value may have to be reduced. See Seed and fertilizer sensors.\nLiquid fertilizer sensor: “Pulses per liter” in its settings. The value may have to be reduced. See Liquid fertilizer.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?"},{"p":"one","t":"Application rate too low, too high, or not under control","h":"“Under allowable rate”","u":"en/one/alarms-rate.html#under-allowable-rate","x":"What it means. A section sensor — seed, dry fertilizer or liquid fertilizer — has measured too little product during work.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThere is enough product in the hoppers or in the tank.\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nSeed or dry fertilizer sensor: “Fraction”. The value may have to be reduced.\nLiquid fertilizer sensor: “Pulses per liter”. The value may have to be reduced.\nSeed sensor: its status on the status screen (“Information” in its context menu) — see The sensor status screen.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?"},{"p":"one","t":"Application rate too low, too high, or not under control","h":"“Over allowable rate”","u":"en/one/alarms-rate.html#over-allowable-rate","x":"What it means. A section sensor — seed, dry fertilizer or liquid fertilizer — has measured too much product during work.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nSeed or dry fertilizer sensor: “Fraction”. The value may have to be increased.\nLiquid fertilizer sensor: “Pulses per liter”. The value may have to be increased.\nSeed sensor: its status on the status screen.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?\nSEE ALSO\nHow far the rate may stand from the target before these alarms is set by “Rate error,%” of the sensor group — Seed and fertilizer sensors, Liquid fertilizer. Columns under the control line on the home screen are not guarded — Change the target rate."},{"p":"one","t":"Too many doubles or skips","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-seeding-quality.html","x":"What to do when a seed sensor of a precision planter counts more doubles or skips than allowed."},{"p":"one","t":"Too many doubles or skips","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-seeding-quality.html","x":"Doubles and skips are counted only in the “Precise” mode of seeding — on a precision planter. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Too many doubles or skips","h":"“High % of doubles”","u":"en/one/alarms-seeding-quality.html#high-of-doubles","x":"What it means. The share of doubles the sensor counts is higher than the set limit.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\n“Doubles error,%” in the settings of the seed sensor — is the limit sensible? See Seed and fertilizer sensors.\n“Fraction” in the settings of the seed sensor. The value may have to be increased.\nThe sensor's status on its status screen — see The sensor status screen.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?"},{"p":"one","t":"Too many doubles or skips","h":"“High % of skips”","u":"en/one/alarms-seeding-quality.html#high-of-skips","x":"What it means. The share of skips the sensor counts is higher than the set limit.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThere is enough seed in the hoppers.\n“Skips error,%” in the settings of the seed sensor — is the limit sensible?\n“Fraction” in the settings of the seed sensor. The value may have to be reduced.\nThe sensor's status on its status screen.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?"},{"p":"one","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-tramline.html","x":"What to do when the tramline actuator jams, its position sensors contradict each other, or a shut-off section still delivers product."},{"p":"one","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-tramline.html","x":"The tramline actuator shuts the sections of the tramline bout. Two gate position sensors tell the panel where its shutter stands. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"“Stall”","u":"en/one/alarms-tramline.html#stall","x":"What it means. The tramline actuator has jammed.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe gate position sensors of the actuator are serviceable.\nThe mechanism of the tramline actuator is serviceable."},{"p":"one","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"“Illegal state”","u":"en/one/alarms-tramline.html#illegal-state","x":"What it means. Both gate position sensors have been triggered at the same time. With a serviceable installation this cannot happen.\nWhat to check: the gate position sensors are serviceable."},{"p":"one","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"“Seeding to tramline”","u":"en/one/alarms-tramline.html#seeding-to-tramline","x":"What it means. A section that should be shut off for the tramline still delivers too much product. The seed sensor, the dry fertilizer sensor or the liquid fertilizer sensor of that section has measured it in the tramline control mode.\nSeed sensor or dry fertilizer sensor — check, in this order:\nThe metering unit and the flow cut-off mechanism are serviceable.\n“Tolerable sowing to tramline,%” in the settings of the group of section sensors. The value may have to be increased. See Seed and fertilizer sensors.\n“Fraction” in the settings of the group of the alarming sensor. The value may have to be increased.\nThe sensor's status on its status screen — see The sensor status screen.\nLiquid fertilizer sensor — check, in this order:\nThe metering unit and the flow cut-off mechanism are serviceable.\n“Tolerable leak to tramline,%” in the settings of the group of section sensors. The value may have to be increased. See Liquid fertilizer.\nSEE ALSO\nWhich sections belong to which actuator is set in “Actuators to sensors mapping” — Seeding machine parameters. The rhythm: Tramline rhythm."},{"p":"one","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-sensor-state.html","x":"What to do when an optical seed or fertilizer sensor reports a blocked channel, too much light, a fault of its own, or outdated firmware."},{"p":"one","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-sensor-state.html","x":"The seed and dry fertilizer sensors count the product optically: an emitter shines across the channel, a receiver sees each grain. These alarms report the state of the sensor itself, not the rate. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"“Clogged”","u":"en/one/alarms-sensor-state.html#clogged","x":"What it means. The seed or dry fertilizer sensor has detected that its optical channel is blocked.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nRemove the blockage.\nCheck for a build-up of dust in the channel.\nCheck the power supply of the sensor. It must meet the values in the device passport."},{"p":"one","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"“Light struck”","u":"en/one/alarms-sensor-state.html#light-struck","x":"What it means. The seed or fertilizer sensor has detected an extremely high level of light in its optical channel.\nWhat to do: keep direct sunlight out of the optical channel of the sensor."},{"p":"one","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"“Broken”","u":"en/one/alarms-sensor-state.html#broken","x":"What it means. The seed or dry fertilizer sensor has detected a fault of its own. It may have to be replaced.\nWhat to do: check the power supply of the sensor. It must meet the values in the device passport. If the power supply is in order, the sensor may have to be replaced — call the service support."},{"p":"one","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"“Old firmware! Wrong S&D calc!”","u":"en/one/alarms-sensor-state.html#old-firmware-wrong-sd-calc","x":"What it means. A seed sensor with outdated firmware is in use. It counts, but its skips and doubles cannot be trusted.\nWhat to do: call the service support to have the firmware of the sensor updated.\nSEE ALSO\nThe status screen of the sensor shows the same states — “Clogged”, “Light struck”, “Faulty” — in red: The sensor status screen."},{"p":"one","t":"Tractor engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-tractor.html","x":"What to do when one of the on-board engine sensors — oil pressure, battery charge, fuel level, air filter — raises an alarm."},{"p":"one","t":"Tractor engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-tractor.html","x":"All on-board engine sensors share one alarm text: “FAILURE”. The name of the sensor above the text tells you which one has triggered. How the window works: How alarms work. The settings named below are described in Hopper, implement position and engine sensors."},{"p":"one","t":"Tractor engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“Emergency oil pressure” — “FAILURE”","u":"en/one/alarms-tractor.html#emergency-oil-pressure-failure","x":"What it means. The low oil pressure sensor has triggered.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe low oil pressure sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” in the settings of the low oil pressure sensor.\nIf an ignition key sensor is in the profile: is its monitoring switched on?\nIf an ignition key sensor is in the profile: is it serviceable?"},{"p":"one","t":"Tractor engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“No battery charge” — “FAILURE”","u":"en/one/alarms-tractor.html#no-battery-charge-failure","x":"What it means. The battery is not being charged.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe battery charge sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” in the settings of the battery charge sensor.\nIf an ignition key sensor is in the profile: is its monitoring switched on?\nIf an ignition key sensor is in the profile: is it serviceable?"},{"p":"one","t":"Tractor engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“Low fuel” — “FAILURE”","u":"en/one/alarms-tractor.html#low-fuel-failure","x":"What it means. Little fuel is left in the tank.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe fuel level sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” in the settings of the fuel level sensor.\n“Signal delay, sec” in the settings of the fuel level sensor. The default is 15 seconds."},{"p":"one","t":"Tractor engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“Air filter” — “FAILURE”","u":"en/one/alarms-tractor.html#air-filter-failure","x":"What it means. The air filter is clogged.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe air filter sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” in the settings of the air filter sensor.\n“Signal delay, sec” in the settings of the air filter sensor. The default is 5 seconds."},{"p":"one","t":"Product rate by weight too low, too high, or impossible","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html","x":"What to do when a product dosed by weight — by an electric motor or a weight-controlled dispenser — misses its target rate, or cannot reach it at all."},{"p":"one","t":"Product rate by weight too low, too high, or impossible","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html","x":"These alarms are raised by the system as a whole, not by one device. They exist for each of the four products separately: the number in the message is the number of the product. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Product rate by weight too low, too high, or impossible","h":"“Rate of Product 1” / “is too low!” — or — “is too high!”","u":"en/one/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html#rate-of-product-1-is-too-low-or-is-too-high","x":"The same for Product 2, 3 and 4.\nWhat it means. The product is applied below or above its target rate in kilograms per hectare.\nProduct metered by an electric motor — check, in this order:\nThe target rate of the product.\nThe speed limits in force, on the status screen of the speed sensor — see The sensor status screen.\nThe settings of the electric motor that drives the dispenser shaft — see Dispenser electric motor.\nThe metering unit.\nThe rotation sensor of the electric motor is serviceable.\nThe working load of the motor. Below 20 % or above 80 %, try a metering roller of another size: a larger one if too little is applied, a smaller one if too much.\nCalibrate the metering roller — see Calibrate a metering motor by weight.\nDispenser sensor with “Weight control” switched on — check, in this order:\nThe target rate of the product.\nThe settings of the dispenser shaft rotation sensor — see Dispenser sensor.\nThe metering unit.\nThe dispenser sensor is serviceable.\nCalibrate the metering roller — see Calibrate the dispenser by weight."},{"p":"one","t":"Product rate by weight too low, too high, or impossible","h":"“Product dosage is” / “impossible! Check product” / “rate and calibration!”","u":"en/one/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html#product-dosage-is-impossible-check-product-rate-and-calibration","x":"What it means. With the current settings and target rate, the product cannot be applied by weight at all.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe target rate of the product.\n“Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” of the speed sensor — see Speed sensor.\nThe settings of the electric motors that apply the products by weight, above all “Minimum, rpm” and “Maximum, rpm”.\nCalibrate the metering rollers of the electric motors.\nIf that does not help: fit metering rollers of another size.\nTIP\nThe calibration screen of a metering motor shows the speed window the current roller allows. If it reads “Can not provide dosage!”, no calibration will help — the roller does not suit the rate. See Calibrate a metering motor by weight."},{"p":"one","t":"User is Agronomist: alarms will not be shown","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-access-level.html","x":"Why the panel warns that alarms are hidden, and how to get them back."},{"p":"one","t":"User is Agronomist: alarms will not be shown","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-access-level.html","x":"How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"User is Agronomist: alarms will not be shown","h":"“User is Agronomist!” / “Alarms will not be shown!”","u":"en/one/alarms-access-level.html#user-is-agronomist-alarms-will-not-be-shown","x":"What it means. The conditions for controlling the product application are met — the machine is working — while the “Agronomist” access level is active. At this level the panel does not show alarm windows. The message appears only once for the whole time you work at this level.\nWhat to do: set the access level back to “Operator”:\nPress “MENU” on the home screen and select “Settings”.\nSelect “User” and choose “Operator” with “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\nPress “MENU”. Going down a level needs no PIN.\nSwitching the panel off and on does the same: after every power-on the panel starts at “Operator”.\nNOTE\nExpect a short run of alarms on the way back. The alarms raised while the level was high have waited. The panel drops those that have gone away and shows the ones that are still true. It is the backlog, not a new fault. See Access levels."},{"p":"one","t":"Travel speed sensor failed, or speed limits contradict","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-speed-sensor.html","x":"What to do when one of two speed sensors stops giving pulses, or when the minimum and maximum travel speed are set the wrong way round."},{"p":"one","t":"Travel speed sensor failed, or speed limits contradict","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-speed-sensor.html","x":"How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Travel speed sensor failed, or speed limits contradict","h":"“Speed sensor #1 failed” / “Speed sensor #2 failed”","u":"en/one/alarms-speed-sensor.html#speed-sensor-1-failed-speed-sensor-2-failed","x":"What it means. The profile says the machine has two travel speed sensors, but the panel sees movement from only one of them. The number names the silent sensor. This alarm cannot appear on a machine with one speed sensor.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nCheck that both speed sensors are serviceable, and repair what you find.\nIf one sensor cannot be repaired now: change the system profile so that only one speed sensor is used — see The profile."},{"p":"one","t":"Travel speed sensor failed, or speed limits contradict","h":"“Error in settings of Min/Max”","u":"en/one/alarms-speed-sensor.html#error-in-settings-of-minmax","x":"What it means. The minimum and the maximum travel speed set for the speed sensor contradict each other. The panel cannot decide whether the current speed is allowed.\nWhat to do: check “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” in the settings of the speed sensor. The minimum must be below the maximum. See Speed sensor.\nNOTE\n“SLOWER” and “FASTER” are not alarms. When the speed leaves the permitted range, no alarm window opens: the home screen shows “SLOWER” or “FASTER” and keeps showing it while you correct the speed. See Indicators."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html","x":"What to do when the controller of a dispenser electric motor reports a jam, a short circuit, over-current, a wrong supply voltage, overheating or an unknown fault."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html","x":"These alarms come from the controller that drives an electric motor of a dispenser. The name in the alarm window tells you which motor. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Motor jammed!”","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html#motor-jammed","x":"What it means. The controller does not see rotation while the motor should be running.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe metering unit is serviceable and turns freely.\nThe rotation sensor of the motor is serviceable, and so is its connection to the controller — the cable and the connector."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Short circuit”","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html#short-circuit","x":"What it means. The controller has detected a short circuit.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe cable connection: the cables and the connector are intact.\nReplace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Over current”","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html#over-current","x":"What it means. The current has gone above the limit.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe motor and the seeding mechanism for jamming.\nThe cable connection: the cables and the connector are intact.\nReplace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Under voltage”","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html#under-voltage","x":"What it means. The supply voltage of the motor is below the allowed value. The motor cannot run.\nMeasure the supply voltage first.\nIf it is too low:\nThe alternator is serviceable.\nThe tension of the alternator belts.\nThe power harness is connected firmly to the battery or the alternator terminals, as the required supply voltage demands.\nThe cable connections make reliable electrical contact.\nIf the supply voltage is in order: replace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Over voltage”","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html#over-voltage","x":"What it means. The supply voltage of the motor is above the allowed value. The motor cannot run.\nMeasure the supply voltage first.\nIf it is too high:\nThe alternator is serviceable.\nThe power harness is connected firmly to the battery or the alternator terminals, as the required supply voltage demands.\nThe cable connections make reliable electrical contact.\nIf the supply voltage is in order: replace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Over temperature”","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html#over-temperature","x":"What it means. The motor has overheated.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nLet the motor cool down.\nReplace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“FAILURE”","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html#failure","x":"What it means. The controller has detected an unusual fault that needs the service support.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nRead the fault code: context menu of the motor → “Information” → line “Last fault”. See Read the motor fault code.\nReplace the controller with a serviceable one to test it.\nGive the code and the number of the motor to the service support."},{"p":"one","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Device in bootloader!”","u":"en/one/alarms-motor-controller.html#device-in-bootloader","x":"What it means. The controller has stayed in its firmware update mode and is not running its working program. It cannot drive the motor.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nSwitch the power off and on.\nIf the message returns, the firmware update of the controller was not completed. Call the service support.\nSEE ALSO\nThe liquid fertilizer pump controller raises the same electrical alarms — Liquid fertilizer pump."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-pump-controller.html","x":"What to do when the liquid fertilizer pump controller reports too much pressure, a failed pressure sensor, or a rate it cannot hold."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"","u":"en/one/alarms-pump-controller.html","x":"The pump controller is built on the same electronics as the controller of a metering motor. Besides the alarms on this page it raises all the electrical alarms of the metering motor controller — “Short circuit”, “Over current”, “Under voltage”, “Over voltage”, “Over temperature”, “FAILURE” and “Device in bootloader!” — with the same meaning and the same actions: see Metering motor alarms. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"“High pressure”","u":"en/one/alarms-pump-controller.html#high-pressure","x":"What it means. The measured liquid pressure is greater than allowed.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nReduce the travel speed.\nIn the settings of the pump controller, increase “Max pressure, Bar” — see Liquid fertilizer."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"“Pressure sensor failure!”","u":"en/one/alarms-pump-controller.html#pressure-sensor-failure","x":"What it means. The signal from the pressure sensor has gone well outside its 4–20 mA working range.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe connection of the sensor to the controller — the cable and the connector are intact.\nThe sensor is serviceable. If it has failed, replace it.\nThe status screen of the pump controller shows the pressure and the raw sensor signal in mA — see The sensor status screen."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"“Rate low”","u":"en/one/alarms-pump-controller.html#rate-low","x":"What it means. The pump controller cannot reach the set rate.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed and is reasonably steady.\nThe working load of the pump. Above 90 %: drive more slowly.\nLoad under 90 %, or jumping strongly: increase “Product rate error,%” in the “Settings” menu — see The Settings menu.\nThe pump is serviceable."},{"p":"one","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"“Rate high”","u":"en/one/alarms-pump-controller.html#rate-high","x":"What it means. The pump controller applies more than the set rate.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed and is reasonably steady.\nThe working load of the pump. Under 10 %: drive faster.\nLoad above 10 %, or jumping strongly: increase “Product rate error,%” in the “Settings” menu.\nThe pump is serviceable."},{"p":"one","t":"Read the fault code of an electric motor","h":"","u":"en/one/motor-fault-codes.html","x":"Where the panel shows the numeric fault code of a metering motor controller, and what each code means."},{"p":"one","t":"Read the fault code of an electric motor","h":"","u":"en/one/motor-fault-codes.html","x":"When a metering motor raises “FAILURE”, its controller also records a numeric code. Read it out and give it to the service support together with the number of the motor.\nOn the home screen, open the context menu of the faulty motor: hold its tile, or hold “MENU”, step to the motor with “UP” / “DOWN” and press “MENU”.\nSelect “Information” and press “MENU”. The status screen of the motor opens.\nRead the line “Last fault”. The status of the motor reads “Faulty” while any of its alarms is active.\nThe same code is shown as “Error code” on the Motors screen — see Motors screen and Motor controller diagnostics."},{"p":"one","t":"Read the fault code of an electric motor","h":"The codes","u":"en/one/motor-fault-codes.html#the-codes","x":"The panel shows only the number. The descriptions come from the motor controller itself.\nCommand and parameter errors\nThe controller refused something the panel asked of it.\nCode\nDescription\n-250\nInvalid command\n-251\nUnexpected command\n-252\nInvalid command syntax\n-253\nInvalid command arguments\n-260\nInvalid number of arguments\n-261\nSpecified index is greater than FFFFh\n-262\nSpecified sub-index is greater than FFh\n-300\nParameter does not exist\n-301\nMemory write operation failed\n-302\nRestore operation failed\n-306\nParameter cannot be written\n-307\nParameter cannot be read\n-308\nParameter value out of range\n-316\nVersion of the stored parameter set does not match the firmware version\n-318\nStored parameter set has an invalid check sum\n-322\nParameter cannot be set: the axis is in motion\n-325\nParameter cannot be set: the positioning mode is inactive\n-340\nParameter cannot be set: a limit switch is active\n-343\nParameter cannot be set: a stop or halt command is being executed\n-350\nParameter not contained in the specified index\n-351\nParameter not contained in the specified sub-index\nDevice errors\nThe controller or the motor itself is in trouble.\nCode\nDescription\n-1000\nLow supply voltage\n-1001\nExcessive supply voltage\n-1002\nLow electronics supply voltage\n-1003\nExcessive electronics supply voltage\n-1020\nOvertemperature of the device\n-1021\nOvertemperature of the heat sink\n-1060\nShort circuit of a motor lead: between motor leads, motor and ground, or motor and power supply\n-1061\nMotor overcurrent\n-4000\nFollowing-difference error in the motion sequence\n-5100\nLimit switch active"},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html","x":"What each of the seven buttons and each touch gesture does — on the home screen, in menus, in windows, on the number pad and on every working screen."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html","x":"The seven buttons, left to right: HOME, MINUS, UP, MENU, DOWN, PLUS, BACK.\nThe panel lights only the buttons that do something on the screen in front of you. A dark button does nothing there. Where the buttons are: The panel and its seven buttons."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"Kinds of press and touch","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html#kinds-of-press-and-touch","x":"Press or gesture\nHow\nShort press\nPress a button and release.\nLong press (hold)\nHold the button until the panel reacts. Some buttons do something else when held.\nTouch and hold\nKeep a finger on one spot of the screen until the panel reacts.\nDouble tap\nTwo quick taps with one finger.\nTwo-finger tap\nOne tap with two fingers at once.\nSwipe\nA long movement of one finger across the screen. A short one is not recognised.\nTwo-finger swipe\nThe same with two fingers."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"Home screen","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html#home-screen","x":"Button or gesture\nWhat it does\n“HOME”\nNext working screen: the Table.\n“HOME”, held\nLocks or unlocks product application. One short beep confirms.\n“MENU”, or two-finger tap\nOpens the main menu.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nThe first press selects a rate field (it blinks); the next ones raise or lower its target.\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nMove to the previous or the next rate field. Nothing if the machine has only one.\n“BACK”\nReleases a blinking rate field; otherwise opens the tramline bout menu, if the tramline is on.\nDouble tap\nSets the alarm level from the rate being applied. Only while applying.\nTouch and hold a device\nOpens its context menu.\nTouch and hold a rate field, then swipe up or down\nSelects the field and changes its target.\nTouch and hold a kg/ha indicator\nOpens the number pad for that product's target rate.\nSwipe left / right\nTable / second home screen."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"Second home screen","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html#second-home-screen","x":"Button or gesture\nWhat it does\n“MENU”, or touch the settings symbol\nOpens the main menu.\n“HOME”, or swipe left\nBack to the home screen.\n“HOME”, held, or touch and hold the hand\nLocks or unlocks product application.\nTouch and hold the rate, then swipe up or down\nChanges the seeding rate.\nTouch a red or green chevron\nTakes a motor out of or into control.\nTouch and hold a motor\nOpens its context menu."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"Table","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html#table","x":"Button\nGesture\nWhat it does\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nLong two-finger swipe left or right\nNext or previous table.\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nSwipe up or down\nScroll the lines.\n“MENU”\nTwo-finger tap\nAsks “Zero readings?”.\n“HOME”\nSwipe left\nStatistics.\n“BACK”\nSwipe right\nHome screen."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"Statistics","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html#statistics","x":"Button\nGesture\nWhat it does\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nSwipe up or down\nScroll the report.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nTwo-finger swipe left or right\nShow another field.\n“MENU”\nTwo-finger tap\nMenu of the screen.\n“HOME”\nSwipe left\nMotors, or the home screen if there is none.\n“BACK”\n—\nHome screen.\n—\nSwipe right\nTable."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"Motors","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html#motors","x":"Button\nGesture\nWhat it does\n“HOME” or “BACK”\nSwipe left\nHome screen.\n—\nSwipe right\nStatistics.\n—\nTouch and hold a tile\nContext menu of that motor."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"In a menu","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html#in-a-menu","x":"Button\nWhat it does\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nMove the selection up and down the list.\n“MENU”\nEnters the selected item. For a value in square brackets, opens the number pad.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nChange the value of the selected item by one step. For a value in square brackets, they open the number pad.\n“BACK”\nLeaves the menu, one level up."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"Number pad","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html#number-pad","x":"Button or touch\nWhat it does\nTouch the digits\nType the value.\nTouch “OK”, or “MENU” (lit green)\nAccept the value.\n“BACK” (lit red), or touch outside the pad\nLeave without changing anything.\nTouch and hold the backspace\nClear the value.\nTouch and hold . −\nSwitch the minus sign, where allowed."},{"p":"one","t":"Buttons and gestures on every screen","h":"Windows","u":"en/one/buttons-and-gestures.html#windows","x":"Window\nButtons\nConfirmation\n“PLUS” or touch “Yes” — confirm. “MINUS” or touch “No” — refuse. “BACK” — cancel.\nInformation\nCloses by itself, or with a touch, “PLUS” or “BACK”.\nAlarm\n“HOME” or touch “Close” — close this alarm. “HOME” held — close and return to the home screen. The other buttons do nothing."},{"p":"one","t":"Terms","h":"","u":"en/one/terms.html","x":"The words this manual uses for rates, control, sensors, tramlines and devices — what each means on the Hectare One."},{"p":"one","t":"Terms","h":"Rates and control","u":"en/one/terms.html#rates-and-control","x":"Term\nMeaning\nRate\nThe target intensity the operator sets. The panel compares the actual intensity against it and reports a deviation that is too large.\nRate error\nHow far the actual intensity may stand from the rate before that becomes an alarm. Set per sensor group, in per cent (“Rate error,%”).\nProduct application control\nThe panel does not judge the sowing at every moment: it starts watching once the conditions are met — the implement is lowered and moving. Until then a silent section is not a fault.\nMinimum alarm rate\nThe lowest intensity still counted as normal — the control line of the diagram. Below it the section is reported, whatever the neighbours do. See The section diagram.\nPath interval\nThe stretch of the run over which skips, doubles and the other figures are gathered before they are redrawn. Set by “Recalculation,m”, which the panel renames “Recalculation,sec” when the seeding mode counts in seconds instead of metres.\nManual lock\nStopping all product application by hand, with a long press of “HOME”. A yellow hand on the home screen shows the lock is on. See Lock application by hand.\nControl (of a sensor)\nWhether the panel watches a sensor. A sensor out of control is shown with ! and reports nothing. See Take a sensor into or out of control."},{"p":"one","t":"Terms","h":"Seeding control modes","u":"en/one/terms.html#seeding-control-modes","x":"Mode\nMeaning\n“Precise”\nSeeds per metre, with skips and doubles — row-crop planters.\n“Flow”\nSeeds per second against an alarm level — grain drills.\n“Hybrid”\nSeeds per metre, with a rate error as in “Precise”; the alarm level can be set automatically as in “Flow”."},{"p":"one","t":"Terms","h":"Sensors","u":"en/one/terms.html#sensors","x":"Term\nMeaning\nSection control sensor\nThe common name for a seed, dry fertilizer or liquid fertilizer sensor. One per section of the implement.\nSeed sensor\nCounts the seed passing one section. On a row-crop planter it also separates doubles and skips; on a grain drill it measures intensity only — the seeds arrive too close together to be told apart.\nDry fertilizer sensor\nThe same device, watching a granular fertilizer line.\nLiquid fertilizer sensor\nA flow meter counting the volume through one section.\nSpeed sensor\nMeasures the travel speed. Distance, area and every rate are calculated from it.\nDispenser sensor\nWatches that the dispenser shaft turns.\nFan sensor\nWatches the fan speed against a set minimum and maximum.\nPressure sensor\nWatches positive pressure or vacuum, depending on how it is plumbed.\nHopper sensor\nReports when the product has fallen below the point where the sensor sits.\nWork position sensor\nTells the panel whether the implement is lowered into work or raised for transport.\nMarker sensors\nReport which mechanical marker is down, so that the tramline bout can advance by itself."},{"p":"one","t":"Terms","h":"Tramline","u":"en/one/terms.html#tramline","x":"Term\nMeaning\nTramline\nA driving lane left unsown, made by shutting off one or several sections on chosen bouts.\nRhythm\nThe repeating sequence of bouts, with the bouts marked on which particular sections must not sow. See Set up the tramline rhythm.\nBout\nOne pass of the implement across the field. The rhythm counts them and returns to the first when it runs out.\nTramline actuator\nThe device that shuts the sections off. One actuator controller carries three things: the actuator itself and both marker sensors."},{"p":"one","t":"Terms","h":"Devices","u":"en/one/terms.html#devices","x":"Term\nMeaning\nHUB8 (input device)\nAn eight-channel hub for sensors that have no controller of their own. It watches them and reports each to the panel as if it were a device.\nElectric motor controller\nDrives one or two dispenser motors, or the liquid fertilizer pump. Motors and pump share one controller design — which is why a machine cannot have a pump without also having the controller a motor would use.\nSCM\nSection Control Module — the interface through which a third-party navigation system can shut sections off.\nmicroSD\nThe memory card, for reports, profiles and firmware updates. FAT16 or FAT32 only.\nProfile\nThe list of what the machine carries. The panel draws its screens from it. See Profile."},{"p":"one","t":"Terms","h":"Access levels","u":"en/one/terms.html#access-levels","x":"Level\nWhat it is for\nOperator\nThe level the panel starts at after every power-on. Everyday work. Only at this level are alarm windows shown.\nAgronomist\nThe commissioning settings and resetting the statistics. Needs a PIN, which is different for every panel and is given in the device passport.\nService\nThe profile, the readdressing of devices, the “Reset to defaults” items. Used by the service department.\nDetails: Access levels."},{"p":"one","t":"Before you contact support","h":"","u":"en/one/before-you-contact-us.html","x":"What to check, what to write down and which photos to take before you ask for help, and whom to ask first."},{"p":"one","t":"Before you contact support","h":"","u":"en/one/before-you-contact-us.html","x":"Most faults can be found with the manual. If you still need help, a complete message gets an answer faster."},{"p":"one","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Check first","u":"en/one/before-you-contact-us.html#check-first","x":"The check after installation was passed, or you know which step failed.\nYou have looked up the text of the alarm in the alarm topics (How alarms work).\nThe faulty device answers: “Link:” on its status screen (The sensor status screen), and the cable, the connector and the power supply are in order (Any device: connection lost or wrong device type)."},{"p":"one","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Write down","u":"en/one/before-you-contact-us.html#write-down","x":"The exact text of the alarm window, and the kind and number of the device named in it (How alarms work).\nThe firmware version — where to read it: Check after installation, step 7.\nThe machine and the number of rows.\nWhat was done before the fault appeared.\nThe result of the check after installation, if you have recorded it."},{"p":"one","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Take photos","u":"en/one/before-you-contact-us.html#take-photos","x":"The sensor status screen of the faulty device (The sensor status screen).\nThe alarm window.\nHow the devices are connected."},{"p":"one","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Whom to contact","u":"en/one/before-you-contact-us.html#whom-to-contact","x":"Your dealer or the partner who installed the system. Start here.\nAgriwelt: support@agriwelt.com. Attach the notes and the photos."},{"p":"one","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Alarm topics","u":"en/one/before-you-contact-us.html#alarm-topics","x":"How alarms work · Any device: connection lost or wrong device type · Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken · Travel speed sensor failed, or speed limits contradict · Work without the speed sensor"},{"p":"five","t":"What the Hectare 5 system does","h":"","u":"en/five/overview.html","x":"The seeding control system with the Hectare 5 panel in one page — what it watches, what it drives, and what it records."},{"p":"five","t":"What the Hectare 5 system does","h":"","u":"en/five/overview.html","x":"The seeding control system with the Hectare 5 panel watches sowing and the application of dry and liquid fertilizer on seed drills and planters. Where the machine has dispenser electric motors or a liquid fertilizer pump, it also drives them, so that the set rate is actually applied.\nThe Hectare 5 panel. It is operated by touch only.\nThe panel sits in the cab and is operated with the fingers on its screen — it has no buttons. Everything else is on the implement: one seed sensor per section, and whatever the machine needs besides. What your machine carries is entered in the profile; the screens show exactly that."},{"p":"five","t":"What the Hectare 5 system does","h":"Seed","u":"en/five/overview.html#seed","x":"Applies seed to the rate the operator sets, through the dispenser electric motors.\nWatches the actual sowing against that rate and reports a section that falls behind.\nReports a blocked or empty tube by its number.\nCounts seeds, doubles and skips in the “Precise” mode — on row-crop planters."},{"p":"five","t":"What the Hectare 5 system does","h":"Dry and liquid fertilizer","u":"en/five/overview.html#dry-and-liquid-fertilizer","x":"Applies liquid fertilizer to the rate through the electric pump.\nWatches dry and liquid lines and reports a section that falls behind or blocks, by number."},{"p":"five","t":"What the Hectare 5 system does","h":"The machine around the product","u":"en/five/overview.html#the-machine-around-the-product","x":"Hopper level, dispenser shaft rotation, fan speed, air pressure or vacuum, travel speed — each against its own limits.\nThe sensors of an autonomous fan-drive engine: oil pressure, battery charge, ignition, fuel level, air filter.\nWorking or transport position of the implement, and the markers."},{"p":"five","t":"What the Hectare 5 system does","h":"Tramline","u":"en/five/overview.html#tramline","x":"Creates the tramline with actuators or by stopping dispenser motors.\nAdvances the bout by itself from the marker sensors or the work position sensor.\nKeeps watching the sections that are shut off, so that a section sowing into the tramline is still reported."},{"p":"five","t":"What the Hectare 5 system does","h":"Speed from the built-in GPS receiver","u":"en/five/overview.html#speed-from-the-built-in-gps-receiver","x":"The panel has a GPS receiver of its own. It can take the travel speed from the satellites instead of a wheel sensor, and it shows how many satellites it sees. See GPS."},{"p":"five","t":"What the Hectare 5 system does","h":"Records","u":"en/five/overview.html#records","x":"Treated area, split into sown, not sown and unknown.\nDetailed statistics of the work, kept for five fields.\nReports written to the memory card.\nNOTE\nThe system applies one product in the “Precise” mode (seeds per metre), up to four products by mass (kilograms per hectare) and one liquid product (litres per hectare).\nSEE ALSO\nThe panel · Quick start · Terms"},{"p":"five","t":"The panel and its touch screen","h":"","u":"en/five/front-panel.html","x":"What the Hectare 5 panel is made of from the operator's side — a touch screen without buttons — and what else is built into it."},{"p":"five","t":"The panel and its touch screen","h":"","u":"en/five/front-panel.html","x":"The Hectare 5 has no buttons. Everything is done with the fingers on the screen: taps, touch-and-hold, swipes, and taps with two or three fingers. What each gesture does on each screen is in Touch zones and gestures.\nThe Hectare 5 panel with the home screen. The screen texts in this picture are not in English."},{"p":"five","t":"The panel and its touch screen","h":"The screen","u":"en/five/front-panel.html#the-screen","x":"800 × 480 colour screen with a touch surface. It recognises up to three fingers at once — which is how the panel tells a tap from a two-finger or a three-finger tap.\nBrightness is set by hand, in steps of 10 %, in the Settings menu. There is no automatic brightness. See Set the screen brightness.\nSound. The panel clicks on every touch it recognises, if “Buttons sound” is set to “Yes” in the Settings menu. A gesture that the screen in front of you does not use gives no click. Alarms have their own sound, and the panel beeps when seeding starts and stops."},{"p":"five","t":"The panel and its touch screen","h":"Built into the panel","u":"en/five/front-panel.html#built-into-the-panel","x":"Part\nWhat you see of it\nmicroSD card slot\nA card symbol on the home screen while a card is ready. Reports are written to the card. See Memory card.\nClock with its own battery\nThe time of day at the top right of the home screen. A weak clock battery shows as BAT. See Clock.\nGPS receiver\nA satellite symbol in the right-hand column of the home screen, with the number of satellites. It can serve as the speed source. See GPS.\nTIP\nA touch the panel does not expect is ignored, not misread. Each screen listens only to its own gestures. If nothing happens, check the gesture — a swipe too short, a finger lifted too early — before you suspect the panel.\nHow the screens, menus and windows work: Menus, context menus, number pad and windows."},{"p":"five","t":"Switching on","h":"","u":"en/five/switching-on.html","x":"What the panel does after power-on, which state it starts in, and what to wait for before switching off."},{"p":"five","t":"Switching on","h":"","u":"en/five/switching-on.html","x":"When the panel gets power, a start screen with the logo appears. The start screen stays until every device connected to the panel has answered — usually a few seconds, at most about fifteen — and then the home screen opens.\nThe start screen.\nNOTE\nIf the start screen stays about fifteen seconds, the panel has been waiting for a device that does not answer. Nothing is broken yet — but look at the home screen and the alarms as soon as it appears. See How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Switching on","h":"What state the panel starts in","u":"en/five/switching-on.html#what-state-the-panel-starts-in","x":"Access level: Operator. The panel starts at the Operator level after every power-on, whatever level was set before. Switching off and on is therefore also the quickest way back to Operator — the only level at which alarm windows are shown. See Access levels.\nThe ordinary home screen. The second home screen is never opened by itself; you open it by hand.\nApplication locked, if the speed is imitated. If speed imitation is switched on in the speed sensor and the machine has dispenser electric motors or a liquid fertilizer pump, the panel starts with product application locked by hand. Unlock it when you are ready: Lock product application by hand.\nThe firmware version and the serial number can be read at any time: two-finger tap on the home screen, then “About” in the main menu. The window closes with “Close”."},{"p":"five","t":"Switching on","h":"Before switching off","u":"en/five/switching-on.html#before-switching-off","x":"Settings are stored by themselves a few seconds after your last change. Wait a few seconds after changing a setting before you switch the panel off.\nThe statistics are written every five minutes and when the machine stops. Switch the panel off only after you have stopped moving — otherwise the last stretch of work is lost from the statistics."},{"p":"five","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"","u":"en/five/interface-basics.html","x":"The five things everything on the panel is done through — working screens, the main menu, the context menu of a device, the number pad, and the windows that ask or tell — and how each is worked by touch."},{"p":"five","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"","u":"en/five/interface-basics.html","x":"Everything on the panel happens through five things: the working screens, the main menu, the context menu of a device, the number pad and the windows that ask or tell you something. All of them are worked with the fingers; the gestures are listed in Touch zones and gestures. The working screens have their own topic: Working screens."},{"p":"five","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"The main menu","u":"en/five/interface-basics.html#the-main-menu","x":"Tap the home screen with two fingers. The main menu opens over the home screen, with its first item selected. On the second home screen, a tap on the settings symbol does the same.\nSelected item. Black text on a blue band. Here “Fill the disks with seeds” — see Fill the disks.\n“Settings”. The settings of the system and of the machine. See The Settings menu.\nArrow head. The item opens another menu.\n“Service”. Commissioning and service items. Shown from the Agronomist level up.\n“About”. A window with the firmware version and the serial number of the panel; “Close” closes it.\nThe main menu of a planter with dispenser motors in the “Precise” mode, standing still, at the Agronomist level.\nWorking a menu by touch:\nTap an item to select it — the blue band moves to it.\nTap the selected item again to enter it: it opens its menu, runs its action or opens the number pad. Touch and hold an item does both at once.\nSwipe right or left anywhere over the menu to raise or lower the value of the selected item. A short stroke changes it by one step; a longer one goes on stepping as the finger travels. The swipe always changes the item on the blue band, not the one under your finger. Lists of words go round: after the last word comes the first.\nSwipe up or down to scroll a long menu.\nTap outside the menu, on the darkened screen, to leave it. A tap with three fingers does the same.\nAn item is read by what stands on its right. The marks - and + appear around the value of the selected item only.\nWhat you see on the right\nWhat it means\nAn arrow head >\nThe item opens another menu. Tap it again.\nA value between - and +\nSwipe right to raise it, left to lower it, right there in the list.\nA value between -[** and **]+\nA swipe, or a second tap, opens the number pad.\n!\nThe item writes over stored values: it zeroes the statistics, saves or loads settings, sets a device, or resets values to their defaults. Tap it again.\nNothing\nThe item performs an action. Tap it again.\nThe Seeding machine parameters menu: the width in square brackets opens the number pad; the modes change in the list; the two calibrations are actions.\nSettings are stored by themselves a few seconds after the last change; there is nothing to save."},{"p":"five","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"The context menu of a device","u":"en/five/interface-basics.html#the-context-menu-of-a-device","x":"Every sensor, motor and indicator on the home screen has its own menu. Touch and hold the device — its icon or its column on the diagram — for more than half a second, and its context menu opens. A short tap does nothing. On the Motors screen and on the second home screen the same works for a motor tile.\nThe context menu of a seed sensor.\nThe first item, “Information”, opens the status screen of the device; there a swipe right or left shows the next or the previous device, and a swipe up or a three-finger tap returns. The second item switches the control: “Disable control” or “Enable control” — see Take a sensor into or out of control. Below them stand the parameters; which ones you see depends on the access level (Access levels). All parameters: Sensor settings."},{"p":"five","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"Entering numbers","u":"en/five/interface-basics.html#entering-numbers","x":"When a value is written between square brackets, changing it opens the number pad.\nName of the parameter. Here “Width, cm”.\nValue. What you have typed so far.\nBackspace. Deletes the last digit. Touch and hold it to clear the whole value.\nDigits. 1 to 9.\nPoint key. Adds the decimal point — only for a value that has decimals.\nZero.\n“OK”. Accepts the value.\nThe number pad. A tap outside the pad leaves without changing anything.\nA value outside the permitted range is not refused: the panel sets the nearest limit instead."},{"p":"five","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"Windows that ask, and windows that tell","u":"en/five/interface-basics.html#windows-that-ask-and-windows-that-tell","x":"A window is a box in the middle of the screen with a coloured strip along its top — without a word in it — and the message under the strip.\nWindow\nWhat it is\nHow it closes\nBlue strip, buttons below\nThe panel asks. Nothing happens until you answer.\nTouch “Yes” (green) or “No” (red). A touch outside the box cancels: nothing is changed.\nBlue strip, no buttons\nThe panel tells you what happened.\nSome close by themselves after about two seconds, others with a touch inside the box.\nRed strip, no buttons\nThe panel reports an error, for example “Invalid PIN”.\nAs above.\nRed strip and a red bar with “FAILURE!”\nAn alarm.\nSee below.\nA question window ignores touches during the first half second, so that the touch that opened it does not answer it.\nA question window: the blue strip, the question, Yes and No."},{"p":"five","t":"Menus, context menus, number pad and windows","h":"Alarm windows","u":"en/five/interface-basics.html#alarm-windows","x":"An alarm window says what happened, on what kind of device, and which devices — their numbers are listed when several identical sensors report the same fault, for example #1-4,7. While it is open, the panel sounds the alarm.\nAn alarm window with the Close button.\nTouch “Close” in the window. This alarm closes and the sound stops; the next alarm in the queue appears. Nothing else closes an alarm window — touches elsewhere are ignored.\nIMPORTANT\nClosing the window stops the sound, not the fault. Alarms are closed one at a time, and they are shown only at the Operator level. What each alarm means: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"","u":"en/five/quick-start.html","x":"The order of getting the machine ready, what to check before the field, what happens when you move off, and what to watch while working."},{"p":"five","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"","u":"en/five/quick-start.html","x":"Your machine may not have every step — skip what it does not have. The main menu opens with a two-finger tap on the home screen; a device's context menu with a touch and hold on the device (Interface basics)."},{"p":"five","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"Before the field","u":"en/five/quick-start.html#before-the-field","x":"Calibrate the speed sensor. Area, rates and deviations are all calculated from the speed; a wrong speed makes every other figure wrong. Standing still, touch and hold the speed field and choose “Speed calibration” — at the Operator level. The same item is under “Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters”, at the Agronomist level. See Speed sensor.\nCalibrate the metering rollers — on machines that apply products by weight. From the context menu of each dispenser (“Dispenser calibration(weight)”, shown when its weight control is on) or of each dispenser motor, at the Operator level, standing still. See Sensor settings.\nCheck the tramline rhythm, if the machine makes tramlines. See Set up the tramline rhythm.\nCheck the sensor parameters — the list is below.\nCheck each section: is it under control, does it apply product. See The four states of a section.\nChoose the field on the Statistics screen: two-finger tap, “Select field”. For a new field, erase its statistics first with “Zero statistics”. See Statistics screen.\nSet the rates — seeding, dry fertilizer, liquid fertilizer. See Change the target rate.\nCheck the access level. It must be “Operator”: “Settings” → “User”.\nSeveral of these settings need the Agronomist level — “Seeding machine parameters” among them. Raise the level (Enable the Agronomist level), do what needs it, drop back to Operator, do the rest.\nIMPORTANT\nStep 8 is the one that ruins a day. From the Agronomist level upwards the panel shows no alarm windows but one: a blocked tube is not reported. The one that still comes through is “User is Agronomist! Alarms will not be shown!” — raised once, when seeding starts at that level. Faults that are still present are reported once you are back at Operator. Check the level before every start, not once a season.\nSensor parameters to check\nSpeed sensor — “Min speed, km/h”, “Max speed, km/h”.\nDispenser sensor — “Period, s”, for each sensor.\nFan sensor — “Minimum, rpm”, “Maximum, rpm”, for each sensor.\nPressure sensor — “Minimum, Pa”, “Maximum, Pa”, for each sensor.\nLiquid fertilizer pump — “Pulses per liter”, “Max pressure, Bar”.\nSeed and dry fertilizer sensors — “Fraction”, “Correction”, “Recalculation,m” or “Recalculation,sec”; in the “Precise” or “Hybrid” mode also “Rate error,%”.\nElectric motors, “Precise” mode — “Holes in disk”.\nElectric motors, dosing by weight — for each motor, “Weight, gr” and “Rotations”. The mass calibration fills both in.\nDetails: Sensor settings."},{"p":"five","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"Moving off","u":"en/five/quick-start.html#moving-off","x":"Put the implement into the working position.\nPlanters with dispenser motors: fill the disks — main menu, “Fill the disks with seeds”. Without it the first metres of every row are empty. See Fill the disks.\nGrain drills: nothing to start. The metering motors begin turning by themselves as soon as the product application control starts — the moment you move off.\nDrive.\nWARNING\nFilling the disks and starting the motors turn machinery while the machine stands still. Make sure nobody is near the metering units, the dispenser shafts or the fan.\nWhen the control starts. The manual lock must be off (Lock product application by hand). If a work position sensor is fitted and under control, the control begins once you move with the implement lowered; without such a sensor, as soon as you move. Alarms start after the delay set in “Settings” → “Alarm delay”, 2 to 20 seconds.\nSound\nWhat it means\nOne short beep\nThe panel sees the machine seeding. From now on the sections are watched.\nThree short beeps\nThe seeding has stopped — at the headland, or the columns have sunk under the control line.\nOn grain drills in the “Flow” or “Hybrid” mode, let the panel find the alarm level itself: Set the alarm level automatically."},{"p":"five","t":"Quick start — getting ready and moving off","h":"While working","u":"en/five/quick-start.html#while-working","x":"Watch four things: the speed against the tractor, the fan and the pressure inside their range, the actual rate against the target, and that no sensor is alarming. The Table screen shows how each section is doing right now; the Statistics screen how the whole field has gone.\nIf the speed sensor fails, see Work without the speed sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"","u":"en/five/check-after-installation.html","x":"A step-by-step check to run once after mounting — every device answers, every seed sensor sits in its own row, the counting, work position, speed and the other sensors work — and what to record."},{"p":"five","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"","u":"en/five/check-after-installation.html","x":"Run this check once, after the system is mounted and before the first field. Each step says what to do, what the screen must show, and where to go if it does not. Your machine may not have every device — skip the steps for what it does not have.\nStart at the “Operator” level: it is the level at which alarm windows are shown (Access levels). The panel starts at that level after every power-on.\nCheck that every device answers. Switch the panel on and wait for the home screen (Switching on).\nThe profile must match the machine: the number of seed sensors equals the number of rows, and the other devices are the ones the machine has (The profile). The machine settings are in Seeding machine parameters; the sensor parameters to check are listed in Quick start.\nNo alarm window “Connection lost”, no red indicator with a ×, no white indicator with !, no red indicator with ? (Indicators).\nOpen the status screen of a device: touch and hold it, then “Information”. Swipe left or right through every device of the list. “Link:” must read “Normal” on each (The sensor status screen).\nA seed or fertilizer sensor must show Status: Working.\nIf it does not: Connection lost or wrong device · Sensor clogged or broken · a device that is out of control: Take a sensor into or out of control.\nCheck that every seed sensor sits in its own row. The machine stands still. Open the Table screen with a long swipe to the left on the home screen. Tap it with two fingers and touch “Yes” to the question “Zero readings?” (Zero the readings). Then drop a few seeds by hand through the seed tube of row 1, one after another. Instead of seeds you may pass any small object, for example a long cable tie, through the seed tube several times — each pass is counted. Do the same for row 2, row 3 and so on, up to the last row.\nOnly the line of the row you dropped into reacts. In the “Precise” mode, switch to the table of the seed counters with a long two-finger swipe — the count of that row grows (The Table screen). Read the count on that table, which shows the number of seeds, not on the view with seeds per metre: that view shows values only while the machine moves. In the other modes, watch sds/s of that row.\nA line of another row reacts: that sensor is connected in the wrong order. Correct the order of the connections and repeat the drop.\nNo line reacts: see step 1 for that sensor.\nIf it does not: Connection lost or wrong device · Sensor clogged or broken.\nCheck the counting. Zero the readings again, as in step 2. Drop a known number of seeds, for example 20, one by one through the seed tube of one row. Read the count of that row on the table of the seed counters (“Precise” mode), the table that shows the number of seeds. On a precision planter it matches the number you dropped to within one seed, provided the “Fraction” of the seed sensors is set for the size of the seed. Write both numbers down. Repeat for one more row. If the figure is clearly higher — up to twice the number — the fraction is set too small for this seed: a large seed, for example maize, is then counted as two. Set the fraction by the table in Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors.\nIf it does not: write down both figures and see Before you contact support.\nCheck the work position sensor. Lower the machine into the working position, then raise it into the transport position.\nThe implement position indicator is green when lowered, yellow and blinking when raised (Indicators).\nOn the status screen of the sensor, “Position: Working” and “Position: Transporting” follow the machine.\nIf both are the wrong way round, check the setting “Transporting position signal” (Hopper, work position and engine sensors).\nCheck the speed. Calibrate the speed sensor first if you have not (Calibrate the speed sensor). Then drive.\nThe speed field shows a white figure. It matches the speed of the tractor.\nIt does not show XX.X, !!.! or ????.\nOn the status screen of the speed sensor, the frequency F= changes while you drive.\nIf it does not: Speed sensor alarms · Travel speed sensor · Work without the speed sensor.\nCheck the other sensors the machine has. One short check each:\nHopper. The hopper icon is full and green with product in the hopper. Hopper empty.\nFan. Start the fan. The fan icon is green and shows the speed in rpm. Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms.\nAir pressure. The pressure icon is green and shows the pressure in kPa while the fan runs. Air pressure.\nDispenser shaft. While the shaft turns, the metering unit icon turns. Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms.\nMarkers. Lower and raise the markers. The marker icon shows the arm or arms down or up (Indicators).\nDispenser motors and tramline actuators. Open the Motors screen. Every tile shows its value; none has a red × (The Motors screen).\nEngine sensors. The engine indicators the machine has are green; the ignition key is green with the ignition on (Indicators).\nRecord the result.\nTwo-finger tap on the home screen, then “About”. Write down the firmware version and the serial number. Close the window with “Close”.\nWrite down the date and the machine.\nPhotograph the status screen of the sensors — at least the screens that show a device with its link and its state.\nKeep the record and the photos. If you need help later, they are the first thing support asks for (Before you contact support)."},{"p":"five","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"If a step fails","u":"en/five/check-after-installation.html#if-a-step-fails","x":"Step\nGo to\n1. A device does not answer\nConnection lost or wrong device\n1. A seed sensor reports a fault\nSensor clogged or broken\n1. A device is out of control\nTake a sensor into or out of control\n1. The profile does not match the machine\nThe profile\n2, 3. A row does not react, reacts in another row, or the count is wrong\nConnection lost or wrong device, then Before you contact support\n4. The position does not follow the machine\nHopper, work position and engine sensors\n5. No speed or a wrong speed\nSpeed sensor alarms, Calibrate the speed sensor, Work without the speed sensor\n6. Hopper\nHopper empty\n6. Fan, dispenser shaft\nFan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms\n6. Air pressure\nAir pressure\nAny other fault\nHow alarms work"},{"p":"five","t":"About the pictures in this manual","h":"","u":"en/five/about-the-pictures.html","x":"Which pictures of this manual are still to come, why the text stands without them, and what on a bench picture belongs to the bench and not to your machine."},{"p":"five","t":"About the pictures in this manual","h":"","u":"en/five/about-the-pictures.html","x":"The screens in this manual are being shot on a Hectare 5 panel on a test bench. Until a frame is taken, its place carries the words Picture to follow. The text around it describes the screen in words — top row, right-hand column, rate field — so that every topic can be used without its picture.\nThe photograph of the panel is a product picture. The texts on its screen are not in English; the layout of the home screen is the same."},{"p":"five","t":"About the pictures in this manual","h":"The words on your screen","u":"en/five/about-the-pictures.html#the-words-on-your-screen","x":"This manual quotes every label exactly as the English panel shows it — including spellings that look unusual, such as “Factical” and “Setuped” on the Statistics screen, or ks/Ha for thousands of seeds per hectare. Look for the words as printed here."},{"p":"five","t":"About the pictures in this manual","h":"What belongs to the bench","u":"en/five/about-the-pictures.html#what-belongs-to-the-bench","x":"A bench panel shows a few things your panel will not. When the frames arrive, read them with this table.\nWhat you see in a picture\nWhy it is there\nWhat your panel shows instead\nA blue time in the clock slot, then TRIAL, then blank\nThe bench panel runs on a trial licence; the blue figure is the time left.\nThe time of day. See Clock.\nIMITAT in yellow in the clock slot\nThe bench runs in an imitation mode of the service menu.\nThe time of day.\nA yellow speed figure, or IMITATION in the speed field\nThe speed is simulated, not measured — there is no wheel turning on a bench.\nThe measured speed in white. See Speed.\nColumns that move although the machine stands still\nThe seed flow is imitated on the bench.\nColumns that move only while seeding.\nRed hoppers, a red oil can or battery\nThe bench has no product and no engine.\nGreen icons while everything is in order.\nA red actuator symbol in the right-hand column\nActuators named in the bench profile are not fitted, so the panel reports them as faulty.\nNothing — the symbol is not shown while every actuator is in order. See Actuator failure.\nA red × in place of an icon\nA device named in the bench profile is not connected.\nThe icon of the device.\nZeros on the Statistics screen\nThe bench statistics were empty when the frame was taken.\nYour figures."},{"p":"five","t":"The home screen","h":"","u":"en/five/home-screen.html","x":"What every part of the home screen is — speed, indicators, area, rates, the section diagrams — on a drill standing still and on a planter at work, and what you can do from it by touch."},{"p":"five","t":"The home screen","h":"","u":"en/five/home-screen.html","x":"The home screen is the main working screen. It shows the diagrams of the section control sensors, the state indicators of the devices and the key figures of the work. What it shows follows the profile: every group of sensors and devices entered there gets its icons, its columns, its rate field.\nThe screen has four zones. The travel speed stands large at the top left. The indicator rows run along the top, with the clock at the top right. A column of indicators runs down the right-hand edge. The lower part holds the diagrams — one column per section — each with its rate field on the right."},{"p":"five","t":"The home screen","h":"A grain drill with seed and fertilizer","u":"en/five/home-screen.html#a-grain-drill-with-seed-and-fertilizer","x":"Travel speed. In km/h. Messages about the speed replace the figure. Speed\nHoppers. One icon per hopper sensor, numbered. Red — the level is below the sensor. Hopper\nMetering units. Rotation of the dispenser shafts; the icon turns while the shaft turns. Metering unit\nFan. The fan icon and its speed in rpm. Fan\nEngine sensors. Oil pressure, battery charge and ignition of the fan-drive engine. Engine sensors\nClock. The time of day; the slot also carries messages. Clock\nMemory card. Shown while a microSD card is ready. Memory card\nArea. Hectares treated on the active field. Area\nProduct rate by weight. The rate of a product, in kg/ha. Product rate by weight\nSeed diagram. One column per seed sensor; the white line is the control line. The section diagram\nRate field. Measured rate on top, target below, here in seeds per second. Change the target rate\nFertilizer diagram. The fertilizer sections, with their own rate field.\nRight-hand column. Implement position, manual lock, satellites, actuator failure — whichever the machine has. Indicators\nSection numbers. The number of each section, under its column.\nA drill with seed and fertilizer rows, standing still: targets set, actual rates zero."},{"p":"five","t":"The home screen","h":"A planter at work","u":"en/five/home-screen.html#a-planter-at-work","x":"Travel speed. Speed\nHopper. Green — product above the sensor.\nPressure. Vacuum or positive pressure in kPa. Pressure\nArea. Hectares treated on the active field.\nPopulation. The actual seeding rate in thousands of seeds per hectare. Population\nSection column. Green — the row sows within its tolerance. Column colours\nSkips bar. Yellow hatching on the left of a column: the share of skips in that row.\nDoubles bar. Blue hatching on the right of a column: the share of doubles.\nRate line. The target rate across all rows.\nTolerance ticks. Upper and lower limit of the permitted deviation, in every column.\nRate field. Measured seeds per metre on top, target below.\nImplement position. Green — lowered, in work. Implement position\nAn eight-row planter in the “Precise” mode, seeding.\nWhat each indicator means in all its states is in Indicators; the diagram itself — line, ticks, colours — in The section diagram."},{"p":"five","t":"The home screen","h":"What you can do from the home screen","u":"en/five/home-screen.html#what-you-can-do-from-the-home-screen","x":"To do this\nDo this\nOpen the main menu\nTap with two fingers\nChange the target rate or the alarm level\nTouch and hold the diagram, then swipe up or down — Change the target rate\nSet the target of a product by weight\nTouch and hold its kg/ha indicator; the number pad opens\nLet the panel set the alarm level\nDouble tap, while applying — Set the alarm level\nLock or unlock product application\nTouch and hold an empty place of the right-hand column — Lock application by hand\nOpen the settings of a device\nTouch and hold its icon or column\nGo to the next working screen\nLong swipe left — Working screens\nOpen the second home screen\nLong swipe right — Second home screen\nA long swipe runs across more than a third of the screen. All gestures on all screens: Touch zones and gestures."},{"p":"five","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"","u":"en/five/section-diagram.html","x":"How to read the columns of the home screen in the \"Precise\" and the \"Flow\" mode, and what the control line means."},{"p":"five","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"","u":"en/five/section-diagram.html","x":"Each column of the diagram is one section — one seed or fertilizer sensor. Its height is the flow through that section. How the rest is drawn depends on the seeding control mode: “Precise” (seeds per metre, with skips and doubles) or “Flow” (seeds per second against an alarm level). The rate field stands at the right end of each diagram: the measured figure on top, the target or alarm level below it, the unit under them."},{"p":"five","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"“Precise” mode","u":"en/five/section-diagram.html#precise-mode","x":"Target rate. Grey, in seeds per metre (s/m). This is the figure you change by touch.\nMeasured rate. White — the average of the rows now.\nScale of the bars. The share of skips or doubles at which a bar reaches the top — “Scale S&D,%” in the settings.\nRate line. The target, drawn across all rows.\nUpper tick. The upper limit of the tolerance, in each column.\nLower tick. The lower limit of the tolerance.\nColumn. Green — the row sows within the tolerance.\nSkips bar. Yellow hatching on the left edge of a column. The taller it is, the more skips in that row.\nDoubles bar. Blue hatching on the right edge of a column. The taller it is, the more doubles.\nSection number.\nAn eight-row planter seeding; some rows skip, some double."},{"p":"five","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"“Flow” mode","u":"en/five/section-diagram.html#flow-mode","x":"Alarm level. Grey, in seeds per second (s/s). The level under which a section counts as losing flow.\nMeasured rate. White — the average of the rows now.\nControl line. The alarm level, drawn across all rows.\nColumn above the line. Green — the row works.\nEmpty part of a column. Dark grey above the green: the higher the green reaches, the more flow.\nSection numbers. Fertilizer sections continue after the seed sections.\nFertilizer diagram. The same, for the fertilizer sensors.\nFertilizer rate field. Measured on top, alarm level below, in fertilizer per second (f/s).\nA drill with seed and fertilizer rows, seeding. Every column stands above its line.\nTo change the target or the alarm level, touch and hold the diagram until its target figure lights up, then swipe up or down — one step per swipe. A tap outside the diagrams ends it. See Change the target rate."},{"p":"five","t":"The section diagram — columns, line and colours","h":"The line: rows under it are not under control","u":"en/five/section-diagram.html#the-line-rows-under-it-are-not-under-control","x":"The line is the boundary of a partial blockage, not a decoration. A column above the line is a row that works; a column that sinks under it is a row that is losing flow — long before it blocks completely.\n“NOT UNDER CONTROL”. The rows of this diagram are not watched.\nColumns under the line. Every row delivers less than the level.\nControl line. Dragged above the real flow.\nAlarm level. Higher than the machine sows.\nMeasured rate. Lower than the alarm level.\nA drill with the alarm level set too high. The diagrams have dropped out of control.\n“NOT UNDER CONTROL” opens no alarm window; at the Operator level the panel sounds the alarm instead. Rows under the line are not under control on purpose: the panel refuses to guard a level that was set above what the machine really delivers. Lower the level until the columns stand above the line, or let the panel set it with a double tap: Set the alarm level automatically.\nWhether the machine is seeding at all, the panel decides by the share of rows that are seeding — the sensor setting “Percent of sowing,%”, 20 % by default. On the headland the rows drop out of control; on the next pass they come back, with or without a work position sensor. The panel says so with sound: three short beeps when the seeding stops, one short beep when it starts again.\nIMPORTANT\nRead the columns against the line, not against each other. Above the line the section works. Down at the line it does not — whatever the neighbours do.\nWhat every colour and symbol of a column means, and the four states of a section: Column colours and section states."},{"p":"five","t":"Column colours and the four states of a section","h":"","u":"en/five/section-states.html","x":"What each colour and symbol of a column on the section diagram means, and the four combinations of control and product application a section can be in."},{"p":"five","t":"Column colours and the four states of a section","h":"","u":"en/five/section-states.html","x":"How the diagram is built — rate line, ticks, control line — is in The section diagram. This page lists what a single column can look like."},{"p":"five","t":"Column colours and the four states of a section","h":"Column colours","u":"en/five/section-states.html#column-colours","x":"What you see\nWhat it means\nGreen column\nThe row sows normally.\nBlue column\nThe row sows more than the target allows (“Precise” and “Hybrid”).\nYellow column\nThe row sows less than the target allows (“Precise” and “Hybrid”).\nBackground blinking dark red, no column\nThe row sows almost nothing while the machine is seeding — a blocked or empty tube.\nWhite column with !\nThe control of this section is switched off. Nothing about it is watched.\nRed ×\nThe sensor does not answer.\nRed ?, column blinking red\nA device of the wrong kind answers in place of this sensor.\nYellow ?\nThe sensor has just received its settings, or it counts seeds but the panel has no speed to judge them by.\nGrey hatching over the whole column\nThe section is shut off for the tramline, or its product application is switched off.\nGreen hatching at the bottom of a grey column\nA small flow in a shut-off section, still within what is tolerated.\nRed hatching in a grey column\nThe shut-off section sows into the tramline.\nA diagram with a section out of control, a silent sensor and a shut-off section."},{"p":"five","t":"Column colours and the four states of a section","h":"The four states of a section","u":"en/five/section-states.html#the-four-states-of-a-section","x":"Each section has two independent switches: whether the panel watches it (control), and whether the machine applies through it (product application).\nControl\nApplication\nWhat it means\nWhat the column shows\non\non\nNormal work.\nThe normal column.\non\noff\nThe row deliberately does not sow — a tramline row — and the panel knows it.\nGrey hatching.\noff\non\nThe row sows, but nobody is checking it.\nWhite with !\noff\noff\nThe section is out of the work altogether.\nWhite with !\nBoth switches are items of the sensor's context menu — touch and hold the column of the section:\n“Disable control” / “Enable control”, at the Operator level. See Take a sensor into or out of control.\n“Product application”, at the Agronomist level. See Access levels.\nIMPORTANT\nA white column with ! looks quiet, but it is blind. Whatever happens in that row — a blocked tube, a broken sensor — the panel reports nothing."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"","u":"en/five/indicators.html","x":"Every indicator of the home screen with all its states — what you see, what it means, what to do."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"","u":"en/five/indicators.html","x":"Where each indicator sits is shown in The home screen. This page says what each one means, state by state. Touch and hold any indicator to open the context menu of its device."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"How to read any indicator","u":"en/five/indicators.html#how-to-read-any-indicator","x":"Almost every indicator follows the same rule. Learn it once and you can read an indicator you have never seen.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nGreen icon\nNormal. The device answers and its value is inside the limits.\nRed icon, blinking\nAlarm. The device answers, but the value has left the limits. An alarm window follows after the alarm delay.\nIcon alternating with a red ×\nNo answer from the device. The value is shown as XXXX.\nIcon alternating with a white [!]\nThe device is in the profile, but its control is switched off. The value is shown as !!!!.\nIcon alternating with a red ?\nA device of the wrong kind answers in its place. The value is shown as ????.\nYellow icon\nNot a fault. A state you have chosen or that needs your attention: implement raised, markers up, ignition off, application locked by hand.\nIMPORTANT\nA switched-off indicator is not a quiet one — it is a blind one. White with !!!! means the panel does not watch that device at all. Nothing will be reported about it, however wrong it goes."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Speed","u":"en/five/indicators.html#speed","x":"The travel speed in km/h, large, at the top left. Messages about the speed replace the figure and blink about once a second, so that the figure stays readable.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nA number in white, e.g. 10.0\nNormal.\n“SLOWER” on red\nYou drive faster than the maximum set for the speed sensor. Slow down.\n“FASTER” on red\nYou drive slower than the minimum. Speed up.\nXX.X in red, blinking\nNo answer from the device that carries the speed sensor.\n???? in red\nA device of the wrong kind answers in place of the speed sensor.\n!!.! in white\nThe speed sensor is not under control.\nA yellow number alternating with “IMITATION”\nThe speed is simulated from the setting of the speed sensor, not measured. See Work without the speed sensor.\nINCREASE IMITATION / DECREASE IMITATION\nThe simulated speed itself is outside the permitted range. Change the setting, not the travel speed.\n“Sensor” with a number\nOne of two speed sensors has failed.\n“Settings”\nThe minimum and the maximum speed contradict each other.\n“SLOWER” and “FASTER” are not alarm windows. They stay in the speed field for as long as the speed is wrong, because you have to see them while correcting it. Speed from the built-in receiver: GPS."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Clock","u":"en/five/indicators.html#clock","x":"The clock slot at the top right is shared by several messages.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nThe time in white\nNormal.\nBAT in red, for about a second in every four\nThe clock battery is low. The clock still runs; between the flashes the time is shown in red. Have the battery replaced.\nXX:XX in red, blinking\nThe clock chip does not answer. Everything the panel records carries a wrong date and time.\nIMITAT in yellow\nThe imitation mode of the service menu is on.\nBlue hours:minutes, then TRIAL, then blank\nA trial period is running; the blue figure is the time left.\nThe clock is set in “Settings” → “Change Date&Time”: tap each line and swipe it to its value, then tap “Set” twice — once to select it, once to store the time. Leaving without “Set” keeps the old time."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Memory card","u":"en/five/indicators.html#memory-card","x":"A card symbol next to the clock, or in the right-hand column. Shown while a microSD card is in the slot and ready to be written to."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Hopper","u":"en/five/indicators.html#hopper","x":"A full green hopper is normal. An empty red one, blinking, means the level has fallen below the sensor: refill."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Metering unit","u":"en/five/indicators.html#metering-unit","x":"The rotation of the dispenser shaft. While the shaft turns, the icon turns — the fastest way to see that the metering unit really runs. Red means no rotation, or not the speed required."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Fan","u":"en/five/indicators.html#fan","x":"The fan speed in revolutions per minute (rpm). Green is normal; red — above the maximum or below the minimum."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Pressure","u":"en/five/indicators.html#pressure","x":"The vacuum or the positive air pressure in the distribution system, in kPa. Green is normal; red — outside the permitted range."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Engine sensors","u":"en/five/indicators.html#engine-sensors","x":"The sensors of the engine that drives the fan.\nIndicator\nGreen\nRed or yellow\nOil pressure\nNormal\nRed: the oil pressure has fallen below the permitted level.\nBattery charge\nNormal\nRed: the battery is not being charged.\nIgnition\nGreen key: ignition on\nYellow key: ignition off. Not a fault, and never an alarm window.\nFuel level\nNormal\nRed: little fuel left in the tank.\nAir filter\nNormal\nRed: the air filter is clogged; clean or replace it.\nIMPORTANT\nOil pressure and battery charge are watched only while the ignition is on. Fuel level and air filter are watched always."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Area","u":"en/five/indicators.html#area","x":"The area treated on the active field, in hectares."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Population","u":"en/five/indicators.html#population","x":"In the “Precise” mode: the actual seeding rate in thousands of seeds per hectare."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Product rate by weight","u":"en/five/indicators.html#product-rate-by-weight","x":"Shown when products are applied by weight; one indicator per product, with its number. It shows one figure, in kg/ha.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nIcon green, figure white\nNormal. While the actual rate stays inside the permitted deviation, the figure shown is the target.\nIcon red, blinking\nThe actual rate is outside the permitted deviation from the target.\nField lit\nYou have just set the target.\nTo set the target, touch and hold the indicator. The number pad opens with the caption Product and its number; type the rate, 10.0 to 500.0 kg/ha, and touch “OK”."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Dosing by weight is not possible","u":"en/five/indicators.html#dosing-by-weight-is-not-possible","x":"A red blinking product icon in the right-hand column. The panel has worked out that the target cannot be held at any travel speed: the lowest speed at which the metering rollers can deliver the rate is already at or above the highest.\nWhat to do. Check the target rates first — one product asking too much closes the window for all of them. Then check the metering rollers. The speed limits the panel calculated are on the “Information” screen of the speed sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Markers","u":"en/five/indicators.html#markers","x":"What you see\nWhat it means\nGreen, both arms down\nBoth markers are lowered.\nGreen, one arm down\nOnly that marker is lowered.\nYellow, both arms up\nBoth markers are raised. On a machine that changes the tramline bout by the markers, the bout changes now.\nA machine with one marker has one icon that shows that marker down or up."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Implement position","u":"en/five/indicators.html#implement-position","x":"Green — lowered, in work. Yellow, blinking — raised, in transport, not applying."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Tramline bout","u":"en/five/indicators.html#tramline-bout","x":"Shown only if the tramline is switched on. The large number is the current bout, the small one above it the number of bouts in the rhythm.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nTramline icon grey\nThe bout changes by itself at the end of every pass.\nTramline icon yellow\nThe bout does not change by itself: the machine has no marker or work position sensor that answers and is under control.\nA yellow symbol blinking in place of the icon\nThe bout could change by itself, but automatic change is switched off.\nHow the bout is counted: Set up the tramline rhythm."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Manual lock","u":"en/five/indicators.html#manual-lock","x":"A yellow hand, blinking, in the right-hand column: product application is locked by hand. The machine applies nothing, however it moves. Not shown while application is allowed. It appears on machines with dispenser electric motors or a liquid fertilizer pump. See Lock product application by hand."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Actuator failure","u":"en/five/indicators.html#actuator-failure","x":"One icon for all electric motors and tramline actuators together, in the right-hand column. Not shown at all while everything is in order.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nRed, blinking\nAt least one motor or actuator is in an alarm state.\nAlternating with white [!]\nThe control of at least one actuator is switched off.\nWhich one and why is on the Motors screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Indicators of the home screen","h":"Liquid fertilizer pump","u":"en/five/indicators.html#liquid-fertilizer-pump","x":"Indicator\nWhat it shows\nRate\nIn L/min when the machine has liquid section sensors, otherwise in L/Ha. Green, red, × and [!] as usual. While the rate stays inside the permitted deviation, the figure shown is the target.\nLoad, %\nHow hard the pump works. Yellow at 99 % and above: the pump is at its limit — drive slower, or the rate falls below the target. ---- blinking red: the controller gives no load figure.\nPressure, Bar\nOnly if the pressure sensor is switched on in the pump settings. Red: the liquid pressure is too high. ---- blinking red: the pressure sensor is broken or gives no reading."},{"p":"five","t":"The built-in GPS receiver","h":"","u":"en/five/gps.html","x":"What the satellite symbol on the home screen tells you, how the panel can take the travel speed from GPS, and what happens when the signal is poor."},{"p":"five","t":"The built-in GPS receiver","h":"","u":"en/five/gps.html","x":"The Hectare 5 has a GPS receiver of its own. The panel uses it in two ways: it shows how many satellites it receives, and — if it is set to — it takes the travel speed from the satellites instead of from a wheel sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"The built-in GPS receiver","h":"The satellite symbol","u":"en/five/gps.html#the-satellite-symbol","x":"On a machine with a speed sensor in the profile, a satellite symbol stands in the right-hand column of the home screen, with the number of satellites in use beside it.\nWhat you see\nWhat it means\nSatellite grey\nThe receiver works, but the speed is not taken from it. The number shows what it receives.\nSatellite green\nThe speed comes from GPS; more than 6 satellites — a good signal.\nSatellite yellow\nThe speed comes from GPS; 4 to 6 satellites. Usable, watch it.\nSatellite red\nThe speed comes from GPS; fewer than 4 satellites. The speed cannot be trusted.\nThe satellite symbol in its four states — grey, green, yellow, red — with the number of satellites in use."},{"p":"five","t":"The built-in GPS receiver","h":"Speed from GPS","u":"en/five/gps.html#speed-from-gps","x":"Whether the speed comes from the receiver is the setting “GPS internal” in the context menu of the speed sensor (touch and hold the speed field). It needs the Agronomist level. See Speed sensor.\nWhen GPS is the speed source and fewer than four satellites are received, the panel raises the alarm “Poor GPS signal quality”. Everything the panel calculates from the speed — area, rates, deviations, the drive of the dispenser motors — then depends on a weak signal. Move into the open, or switch back to the wheel sensor.\nThe alarm of a weak GPS signal.\nIMPORTANT\nUnder trees, buildings or a shed roof the receiver loses satellites. Start the work only when the satellite symbol is green or yellow."},{"p":"five","t":"The built-in GPS receiver","h":"The GPS screen","u":"en/five/gps.html#the-gps-screen","x":"On the second home screen, a tap on the GPS symbol opens a screen with the receiver's own data: the speed it measures, the date and time it receives (UTC, not the local time of the panel), and the number of satellites in use and in view. Below them runs a log of the receiver for the service engineer. The texts on this screen are in English in every panel language. Leave it with a long swipe up.\nThe GPS screen opened from the second home screen."},{"p":"five","t":"The second home screen","h":"","u":"en/five/second-home-screen.html","x":"The second home screen — what is on it, how to open and close it, and what its chevrons, tiles and symbols do when touched."},{"p":"five","t":"The second home screen","h":"","u":"en/five/second-home-screen.html","x":"The second home screen shows the work in large figures and the dispenser motors as a row of tiles. It suits a machine whose seeding rate is held by dispenser electric motors, where the panel watches the motors and the main working units rather than a diagram of sections.\nOpen it from the home screen with a long swipe to the right. Close it with a long swipe to the left — it returns to the home screen. It is not part of the circle of working screens, and the panel never opens it by itself.\nSeeding rate. In seeds per metre.\nTravel speed. In km/h; yellow while it is simulated. Touch and hold it for the context menu of the speed sensor. Speed\nSettings symbol. A tap opens the main menu.\nGPS symbol. A tap opens the GPS screen. GPS\nRed chevrons. A tap takes the outermost motor on that side out of control.\nGreen chevrons. A tap takes the next motor towards that side back into control.\nTarget rate. Shown in the “Precise” mode. Touch and hold it until it lights up, then swipe up or down to change it.\nMotor tiles. Number and value of each motor; the icon alternates with the state sign — STOP, or a red × with XXX. Touch and hold a tile for its context menu.\nStop symbol. Touch and hold it to lock or unlock product application. A short beep confirms.\nThe second home screen of a machine with dispenser motors, standing still.\nOn a machine with a fan, a pressure sensor or a pump, the unit value stands at the left of the top line — one at a time. A tap shows the next one; touch and hold opens its context menu.\nEach motor icon alternates, about once a second, between the picture of the motor and the sign of its state. What the states mean: Motors screen.\nThe chevrons are always on this screen. On a machine without dispenser motors they have nothing to act on."},{"p":"five","t":"The second home screen","h":"What you can do here","u":"en/five/second-home-screen.html#what-you-can-do-here","x":"To do this\nDo this\nOpen the main menu\nTap the settings symbol\nChange the seeding rate\nTouch and hold the rate, then swipe up or down. It stays within the permitted limits. A tap elsewhere ends it.\nSet the target of a product by weight\nTouch and hold its kg/ha tile; type the rate on the number pad\nTake motors out of or into control\nTap the red or green chevrons. Confirm with “Yes” if the panel asks.\nOpen the settings of a motor\nTouch and hold the motor tile\nLock or unlock product application\nTouch and hold the stop symbol. A short beep confirms.\nSee the receiver's data\nTap the GPS symbol\nReturn to the home screen\nLong swipe left\nNOTE\nTaking a motor out of control is the same as on the Motors screen — a motor out of control is neither driven nor watched. See Take a sensor into or out of control."},{"p":"five","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"","u":"en/five/working-screens.html","x":"The four working screens in their circle, the second home screen beside it, and the swipes that get you from one to another — including the way back home from Table and Statistics."},{"p":"five","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"","u":"en/five/working-screens.html","x":"The panel has four working screens in a circle, and a fifth that stands apart.\nScreen\nWhat it is for\nHome screen\nThe diagrams of the sections, the indicators of the devices, the speed, the area and the rates. The panel starts here.\nTable\nThe readings of every section in figures, as a list. Several tables, depending on the control mode.\nStatistics\nThe results of the work by field: area, path, quantities, quality.\nMotors\nThe dispenser electric motors, and the tramline actuators if the machine has them. Present only if the profile holds at least one motor or tramline actuator.\nSecond home screen\nLarge figures and the motor tiles. Outside the circle."},{"p":"five","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"The circle","u":"en/five/working-screens.html#the-circle","x":"A long swipe to the left — one finger, across more than a third of the screen — moves to the next screen, always in the same direction, and returns to the home screen from the last one:\nHome screen → Table → Statistics → Motors → Home screen.\nWithout motors and tramline actuators in the profile, there is no Motors screen and Statistics returns straight to the home screen.\nIMPORTANT\nTable and Statistics have no way back. A swipe to the right does nothing there, and neither does a three-finger tap. To return to the home screen, keep swiping left: from the Table through Statistics (and Motors) to the home screen."},{"p":"five","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"Every way from one screen to another","u":"en/five/working-screens.html#every-way-from-one-screen-to-another","x":"From\nGesture\nTo\nHome screen\nLong swipe left\nTable\nHome screen\nLong swipe right\nSecond home screen\nTable\nLong swipe left\nStatistics\nStatistics\nLong swipe left\nMotors (home screen, if the machine has none)\nMotors\nLong swipe left\nHome screen\nMotors\nLong swipe right\nStatistics\nSecond home screen\nLong swipe left\nHome screen\nA short swipe is not taken as a move to another screen; on Table and Statistics a short swipe up or down scrolls instead."},{"p":"five","t":"The working screens and how to move between them","h":"Gestures on the working screens","u":"en/five/working-screens.html#gestures-on-the-working-screens","x":"Screen\nWhat it does\nGesture\nHome screen\nOpens the main menu\nTap with two fingers\nHome screen\nSets the alarm level from the real rate\nDouble tap with one finger, while applying\nTable\nScrolls the rows\nSwipe up or down\nTable\nNext or previous table\nLong swipe right or left with two fingers\nTable\nAsks whether to zero the readings\nTap with two fingers\nStatistics\nScrolls the report, one line per swipe\nSwipe up or down\nStatistics\nShows another field\nSwipe right or left with two fingers\nStatistics\nOpens the menu of the screen\nTap with two fingers\nMotors\nOpens the context menu of a motor\nTouch and hold its tile\nThe full list per screen, menus and windows included: Touch zones and gestures."},{"p":"five","t":"The Table screen","h":"","u":"en/five/table-screen.html","x":"The readings of every section in figures — which tables your machine has, what each column means, and how to read the colours."},{"p":"five","t":"The Table screen","h":"","u":"en/five/table-screen.html","x":"The Table screen shows the readings of every section as figures, one line per section. It answers the question the diagram cannot: which section exactly, and by how much. Open it from the home screen with a long swipe to the left. It has no way back: a further long swipe left takes you on to the Statistics screen and round the circle to the home screen (Working screens)."},{"p":"five","t":"The Table screen","h":"A planter in the “Precise” mode","u":"en/five/table-screen.html#a-planter-in-the-precise-mode","x":"#. The number of the section.\nsds/m. Seeds per metre, measured now.\nQ.%. Quality — the share of seeds placed singly, as intended.\nDb%. Doubles — two seeds where one was intended.\nSk%. Skips — a gap where a seed was intended.\nks/Ha. The applied rate in thousands of seeds per hectare (in the second table: seeds per metre).\n%. The deviation of this section from the rate that was set.\nTable number. This table and how many there are, in the corner. Shown only when there is more than one.\nSection number on yellow. Something in this line has drifted towards its limit.\nValue on yellow. The figure that drifted.\nTotals. The same columns for the whole machine.\nThe first of three tables of an eight-row planter, while seeding.\nA value on yellow is drifting towards its limit; on red it is beyond it. A whole line in grey with ! is a section taken out of control; a line in red with × is a sensor that does not answer, with ? a device of the wrong kind.\nNOTE\nRead the deviation column first. A section 20 % below the others has a problem the average will not show — a partly blocked outlet, a worn disk, a sensor that needs cleaning."},{"p":"five","t":"The Table screen","h":"A drill in the “Flow” mode","u":"en/five/table-screen.html#a-drill-in-the-flow-mode","x":"#. The number of the section.\nsds/s. Seeds per second. Dashes while nothing is counted.\nDeviation scale. From −50 % through 0 to +50 %.\nDeviation bar. How far this section stands from the rate that was set.\n%. The same deviation as a figure.\nTable number. Here table 1 of 2 — the second holds the fertilizer sections.\nLowest deviation. The section furthest below the rate.\nHighest deviation. The section furthest above the rate.\nRecalculation interval. The stretch over which the figures are gathered — “Recalculation,m” of the sensors.\nThe seed table of a drill with seed and fertilizer rows, standing still."},{"p":"five","t":"The Table screen","h":"Which tables your machine has","u":"en/five/table-screen.html#which-tables-your-machine-has","x":"Table\nShown when\nSeed counters — seeds per second or per metre, with the deviation bars\nAlways.\nRate per area (thousands per hectare) and rate per distance (seeds per metre)\nOnly in the “Precise” seeding control mode.\nDry fertilizer\nThe machine has fertilizer sensors and the fertilizer control mode is not “Precise”.\nLiquid fertilizer\nThe machine has liquid fertilizer sensors.\nIn the “Precise” mode a planter has three seed tables: rate per area (1/3), rate per distance (2/3), and the seed counters (3/3) — the count of seeds each section has sown, beside the deviation bars, with the total for the machine at the bottom.\nThe seed counter table: seeds per section, deviation bars, total."},{"p":"five","t":"The Table screen","h":"Working with the screen","u":"en/five/table-screen.html#working-with-the-screen","x":"What you want\nGesture\nNext or previous table\nLong swipe right or left with two fingers\nScroll the lines\nSwipe up or down\nZero the readings\nTap with two fingers — Zero the readings\nGo on to Statistics\nLong swipe left\nOn large machines the rows do not fit on one page: swipe up and down to scroll them. A line that drifts turns yellow while you watch."},{"p":"five","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"","u":"en/five/statistics-screen.html","x":"The report of the work on each field, block by block and line by line — and the menu that chooses the field, saves the report and erases it."},{"p":"five","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"","u":"en/five/statistics-screen.html","x":"The Statistics screen is the report of the work: how far you drove, how much you covered, how much you sowed, and how evenly. It is kept separately for each of five fields and survives switching off. Open it from the Table screen with a long swipe to the left. It has no way back: a further long swipe left goes on to the Motors screen, or to the home screen on a machine without motors.\nThe report is one long list. Swipe up or down to scroll it, one line per swipe."},{"p":"five","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"The head and the work on the field","u":"en/five/statistics-screen.html#the-head-and-the-work-on-the-field","x":"Field number. Field # and the number of the field whose report you see. A two-finger swipe right or left shows the next or the previous field.\n(A). This is the active field — the one the panel writes to now.\nDate and time. Today's date and the time of day.\nPath. Distance driven, in km.\nArea. Area covered, in Ha.\nOperating time. Hours, minutes and seconds of work.\nMovement speed. The average travel speed.\nStarted / Ended. When the work on this field began and ended.\nSeeding. The next block — see below.\nThe head of the report of an eight-row planter in the “Precise” mode."},{"p":"five","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"The seeding block","u":"en/five/statistics-screen.html#the-seeding-block","x":"“Seeds sown (x1000)”. The total, in thousands of seeds.\n“Quality”. The share of seeds placed singly, as intended. Judge the work by this number.\n“Doubles”. Two seeds where one was intended.\n“Skips”. A gap where a seed was intended.\n“Not sown”. The share of the run when nothing came out at all.\n“Factical” / “Setuped”. The two column headings: what was actually applied, and what was set.\n“Population,ks/Ha”. In thousands of seeds per hectare; the deviation in brackets.\n“Rate, s/m”. In seeds per metre; the target on the right.\n“Sown” / “Not sown” / “Unknown”. The field split three ways, in hectares.\nBar. The same split as a coloured bar: green sown, red not sown, grey unknown.\n“Seed sensors”. The next block — section by section.\n“Unknown” is the area driven while the panel could not judge — not a fault, but not proof of work either.\nNOTE\nA large deviation is not always a fault of the machine. Before changing anything on the drill, check that the speed the panel sees matches the real one — a wrong speed moves every rate figure with it."},{"p":"five","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"Section by section","u":"en/five/statistics-screen.html#section-by-section","x":"“Seed sensors”. One block per section follows.\nSection. The section number, and the seeds it counted.\nq d s n. Quality, doubles, skips and not sown for this section, in per cent.\nSown / not sown / unknown. The share of each for this section, and the bar.\nPopulation. Thousands per hectare for this section, with its deviation.\nSpacing. Seeds per metre for this section, with its deviation.\nThis is where an uneven drill shows itself. A section with a lower quality than the rest, or a longer red part of its bar, points at that row: a blocked outlet, a worn disk, a sensor that needs cleaning."},{"p":"five","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"Blocks that appear on other machines","u":"en/five/statistics-screen.html#blocks-that-appear-on-other-machines","x":"Block\nWhat it holds\n“Work without alarms control”\nThe part of the work the panel was not watching — driven with the control switched off or locked.\n“Speed control”\nHow far and how long you drove outside the permitted speed range.\n“Fertilizing”, liquid fertilizer\nFertilized / not fertilized / unknown, if the machine applies them.\nProduct\nFor a product applied by weight: the quantity applied, and the actual against the set rate in kg/ha, on the line Factical: … Setuped: …."},{"p":"five","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"The menu of the screen","u":"en/five/statistics-screen.html#the-menu-of-the-screen","x":"Tap the screen with two fingers.\nThe menu of the Statistics screen.\nItem\nWhat it does\n“Select field”\nMakes the field shown the active one — the panel writes to it from now on. Shown only while you look at a field that is not active. If that field was worked with a different set of products by weight, the panel asks to erase its data first.\n“Statistics”\nSwitches the report between “Short” and “Full”. Not shown on a machine without a speed sensor.\n“Mileage”\nPath in km and area in ha over the whole life of the system. These totals cannot be reset. Without a speed sensor, the accumulated running time instead.\n“Save to SD-card”\nWrites one field, or all of them, to the memory card. Shown while a card is ready.\n“Zero statistics”\nErases the statistics of the field. At the Operator level the panel asks for the PIN of a higher level first; above Operator it asks “Are you sure?”.\nIMPORTANT\nThe statistics are also erased by something else. Changing the seeding control mode to or from “Precise”, or changing “Width, cm”, asks for confirmation and, if you confirm, erases the statistics of every field. Save what you need to the memory card first."},{"p":"five","t":"The Motors screen","h":"","u":"en/five/motors-screen.html","x":"The dispenser electric motors and the tramline actuators of the machine, one tile each — how to read a tile and how to reach a motor's settings."},{"p":"five","t":"The Motors screen","h":"","u":"en/five/motors-screen.html","x":"The Motors screen shows the dispenser electric motors of the machine, one tile each, and the tramline actuators if the machine has any. Look here when a row stops sowing and you want to know whether the motor is the reason.\nIt exists on any machine whose profile holds at least one electric motor or one tramline actuator. Reach it from the Statistics screen with a long swipe to the left. Leave it with a long swipe to the left to the home screen, or a long swipe to the right back to Statistics.\nMotor 1, STOP. Yellow, blinking — the motor is serviceable, but product application is not running.\nValue of motor 1. The driven shaft speed in rpm.\nMotor 2, red ×. No connection to this motor.\nXXX. No value, because the motor does not answer.\nThe Motors screen of a machine with two dispenser motors and no tramline actuators."},{"p":"five","t":"The Motors screen","h":"Reading a tile","u":"en/five/motors-screen.html#reading-a-tile","x":"The colour of the symbol is the state. The value beside it is the driven shaft speed in rpm. On the second home screen the same tiles show, in the “Precise” control mode, the seeds per metre measured by the seed sensor of that row.\nSymbol\nValue\nState\nMotor, green\nthe figure\nNormal. The motor is under control and driven.\nSTOP, yellow, blinking against the motor\nthe figure\nThe motor is serviceable, but product application is switched off. Nothing is wrong with it — the system is simply not applying.\nMotor, red\nthe figure\nThe motor is in an alarm state. Read the alarm for the reason.\n×, red\nXXX\nNo connection. Check the cable and the connector first.\n!, outlined\n!!!\nThe motor is taken out of control. It is neither driven nor watched.\nNOTE\nYellow is not a fault. A screen full of yellow STOP signs means application is off — at the headland, or locked by hand. Red is a fault; yellow is a state you chose."},{"p":"five","t":"The Motors screen","h":"Tramline actuators","u":"en/five/motors-screen.html#tramline-actuators","x":"Actuator symbol\nState\nPulled in or pulled out, green\nThe actuator has reached its position.\nPulled in or pulled out, yellow\nThe actuator is moving to that position.\nAlternating between in and out, yellow\nThe panel cannot tell the actuator's position.\nBlinking red\nThe actuator is in an alarm state.\nBlinking red × or ?\nNo connection, or a device of the wrong kind.\nBlinking white\nThe actuator is taken out of control."},{"p":"five","t":"The Motors screen","h":"Working with the screen","u":"en/five/motors-screen.html#working-with-the-screen","x":"What you want\nGesture\nThe settings of one motor or actuator\nTouch and hold its tile\nBack to the Statistics screen\nLong swipe right\nOn to the home screen\nLong swipe left\nThe touch and hold opens the context menu of the motor you pressed. Its “Information” item shows the motor's state, and for a controller fault the code of the last fault. Here you see every motor of the machine at once and can choose the one that misbehaves."},{"p":"five","t":"Change the target rate","h":"","u":"en/five/set-the-target-rate.html","x":"Raise or lower the rate the panel holds and guards, from the home screen, while seeding — by touch."},{"p":"five","t":"Change the target rate","h":"","u":"en/five/set-the-target-rate.html","x":"The rate field at the right end of each diagram shows two numbers: the measured rate on top, the target below it. In the “Flow” mode the target is the alarm level of the diagram — see The section diagram."},{"p":"five","t":"Change the target rate","h":"On a diagram","u":"en/five/set-the-target-rate.html#on-a-diagram","x":"On the home screen, touch and hold the diagram whose rate you want to change, for more than half a second. Its target figure lights up.\nThe seed diagram with its target figure lit for editing.\nSwipe up over the screen to raise the target, down to lower it — one step per swipe. The target stays within its permitted limits.\nTap the screen outside the diagrams to finish. The figure stops being lit and the new target is in force.\nTo change another diagram — fertilizer after seed, say — touch and hold that diagram instead."},{"p":"five","t":"Change the target rate","h":"For a product applied by weight","u":"en/five/set-the-target-rate.html#for-a-product-applied-by-weight","x":"Touch and hold the kg/ha indicator of the product. The number pad opens with the caption Product and the number of the product.\nType the target, from 10.0 to 500.0 kg/ha, and touch “OK” (Entering numbers). A tap outside the pad leaves the target as it was."},{"p":"five","t":"Change the target rate","h":"On the second home screen","u":"en/five/set-the-target-rate.html#on-the-second-home-screen","x":"Touch and hold the rate until it lights up, then swipe up or down. A tap elsewhere ends it. See Second home screen.\nIMPORTANT\nColumns under the line are not guarded. If the target is set far above what the machine really delivers, the columns sink under the control line and the panel shows “NOT UNDER CONTROL”. On a grain drill, let the panel find the level: Set the alarm level automatically."},{"p":"five","t":"Lock product application by hand","h":"","u":"en/five/lock-application-by-hand.html","x":"Stop all product application with one touch and hold, and start it again — what the lock does and how to see that it is on."},{"p":"five","t":"Lock product application by hand","h":"","u":"en/five/lock-application-by-hand.html","x":"The manual lock stops all product application: the dispenser motors and the pump do not run, however the machine moves. Use it to cross a field already sown, to drive on the road with the implement lowered, or when you work without the speed sensor.\nThe lock exists on machines with dispenser electric motors or a liquid fertilizer pump.\nOn the home screen, touch and hold an empty place in the right-hand column of indicators — not on an icon — until the panel beeps once.\nCheck the right-hand column. A yellow hand blinks there: application is locked.\nThe right-hand column of the home screen with the yellow hand.\nTo unlock, touch and hold the same place again. One short beep; the hand disappears.\nA touch and hold on an icon of the column opens the context menu of that device instead.\nOn the second home screen, touch and hold the stop symbol — the same lock, the same beep.\nIMPORTANT\nThe lock is announced by the screen only. Look for the yellow hand before you start a pass. A locked machine drives a whole field and sows nothing."},{"p":"five","t":"Lock product application by hand","h":"What the lock changes elsewhere","u":"en/five/lock-application-by-hand.html#what-the-lock-changes-elsewhere","x":"The motor tiles on the Motors screen show a yellow STOP — serviceable, not applying.\nThe product application control does not start when you move off — the lock must be off for the control to start (Quick start).\nThe work done while locked is counted in the statistics as “Work without alarms control” (Statistics)."},{"p":"five","t":"Lock product application by hand","h":"When the panel locks by itself","u":"en/five/lock-application-by-hand.html#when-the-panel-locks-by-itself","x":"Switching speed imitation on locks product application. A panel that is switched on with imitation already on starts locked as well. Unlock by hand when you are ready to apply: Work without the speed sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Set the alarm level automatically","h":"","u":"en/five/set-the-alarm-level.html","x":"Let the panel set the alarm level of the diagrams from the rate the machine really sows, with one double tap — grain drills in the \"Flow\" or \"Hybrid\" mode."},{"p":"five","t":"Set the alarm level automatically","h":"","u":"en/five/set-the-alarm-level.html","x":"On a grain drill the seeds arrive too close together to be counted one by one, so the panel guards each row against an alarm level: the line on the diagram. Set it too high and every row sinks under the line — “NOT UNDER CONTROL”. Set it too low and a half-blocked row goes unnoticed. The panel can set it for you from what the machine really sows.\nThis works in the “Flow” and “Hybrid” modes, for the seed and the dry fertilizer diagrams, and for liquid fertilizer in the “Flow” mode. In the “Precise” mode the double tap changes nothing.\nStart seeding at your normal speed, with product flowing through every row.\nDouble tap the home screen with one finger — two quick taps on the same place.\nThe panel beeps once and lights the rate field: the new alarm level is set from the rate being sown now — half of it in the “Flow” mode, one tenth of it in the “Hybrid” mode.\nThe diagrams after the double tap: the columns above the line, the rate field lit.\nCheck that every column stands above its line. The light goes out by itself after a few seconds.\nThe double tap works only while product is being applied. Standing still, nothing happens.\nTIP\nRepeat it after a change of seed lot or speed. The level follows the rate at the moment of the tap. You can also correct it by hand: touch and hold the diagram, then swipe up or down — see Change the target rate.\nWhat the line means and why rows under it are not watched: The section diagram."},{"p":"five","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"","u":"en/five/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html","x":"Stop the panel from watching one sensor — a section that is shut off, a broken sensor — and take it back later."},{"p":"five","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"","u":"en/five/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html","x":"A sensor out of control is not watched: the panel raises no alarm about it, whatever it reports. Take a sensor out of control when its section is out of work on purpose or when the sensor is broken and you must finish the field. Take it back as soon as you can."},{"p":"five","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"Take a sensor out of control","u":"en/five/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html#take-a-sensor-out-of-control","x":"On the home screen, touch and hold the sensor — its column on the diagram, or its icon. Its context menu opens.\nThe context menu of a seed sensor.\nTap “Disable control” to select it, and tap it again.\nThe first time in a session, the panel asks “Are you sure that you want disable control?”. Touch “Yes”. A touch outside the window cancels.\nThe question before control is switched off.\nThe column turns white with ! (an icon alternates with a white [!]). The sensor is no longer watched."},{"p":"five","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"Take it back into control","u":"en/five/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html#take-it-back-into-control","x":"Do the same and choose “Enable control”. The first time in a session the panel asks “Are you sure that you want enable control?”; confirm with “Yes”. A seed sensor taken back into control shows a yellow ? for a few seconds while it receives its settings.\nIMPORTANT\nA switched-off sensor is blind, not quiet. Nothing about it is reported, however wrong it goes."},{"p":"five","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"When the statistics must be erased","u":"en/five/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html#when-the-statistics-must-be-erased","x":"For some sensors the panel first asks to erase the statistics of the active field: it says “Need to erase data, for selected field!” and asks again.\n“Yes” erases the statistics of the active field and changes the control. At the Operator level the panel first asks for the PIN of a higher level; a wrong PIN shows “Invalid PIN” and nothing changes.\n“No” leaves both as they are.\nThe speed sensor cannot be taken out of control — its context menu has no such item. Motors are taken out of control the same way, from their tile on the Motors screen, or with the chevrons on the second home screen. What the four combinations of control and product application mean: The four states of a section."},{"p":"five","t":"Zero the readings of the Table screen","h":"","u":"en/five/zero-the-readings.html","x":"Set the seed and fertilizer counters of the Table screen to zero — for a new field or a new seed lot — without touching the field statistics."},{"p":"five","t":"Zero the readings of the Table screen","h":"","u":"en/five/zero-the-readings.html","x":"The Table screen keeps counting from the moment its readings were last zeroed. Zero them when you start a new field or a new seed lot, so that the counters and deviations describe only the new work.\nOpen the Table screen: a long swipe to the left on the home screen.\nTap the screen with two fingers. The panel asks “Zero readings?”.\nThe question before zeroing: Yes in green, No in red.\nTouch “Yes”. The counters of every seed, dry fertilizer and liquid fertilizer sensor are set to zero.\n“No”, or a touch outside the window, leaves everything as it was.\nTo get back to the home screen afterwards, keep swiping left — through Statistics (and Motors) to the home screen. The Table screen has no way back (Working screens).\nOnly the counters of the Table screen are zeroed. The field statistics are not touched; they are erased with “Zero statistics” in the menu of the Statistics screen.\nIMPORTANT\nZeroing cannot be undone. Do it when you start something new, not to tidy the screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"","u":"en/five/fill-the-disks.html","x":"Turn the seed disks of a planter with dispenser motors until every disk holds seed, so that sowing starts from the first metre — how to run it and read the result."},{"p":"five","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"","u":"en/five/fill-the-disks.html","x":"This applies only to planters with seed dispenser electric motors in the “Precise” control mode. Without motors in the profile, or in another mode, the menu item is not shown.\nWhen the disks are driven by electric motors, they stand empty until the motors run. Drive off with empty disks and the first metres of every row get nothing: each disk has to pick up seeds and carry them round to the drop point first. Filling turns the disks while the machine stands still, so that sowing starts from the first metre.\nWARNING\nThe disks turn while the machine stands still. Before you start, make sure nobody is near the metering units, the dispenser shafts or the drive. The disks start together and each keeps turning for up to about twenty seconds. From the cab the metering units cannot be seen.\nStop the machine. The item is shown only while the machine stands still.\nOn the home screen, tap with two fingers. The main menu opens with “Fill the disks with seeds” already selected; tap it once more.\nThe main menu with Fill the disks with seeds selected.\nThe panel asks “Are you sure?”. Touch “Yes”. “No”, or a touch outside the window, cancels.\nThe question before the disks turn.\nWatch the tiles. Each motor turns its disk until the seed sensor of its row registers the first seed. At the end the panel beeps three times and shows “Operation complete.”\nTouch “Close” to return to the home screen.\nTo stop before the end, touch “Cancel”: the motors stop at once and go back to the seeding control."},{"p":"five","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"Reading the screen","u":"en/five/fill-the-disks.html#reading-the-screen","x":"Green tile. Done: the seed sensor registered a seed, the disk is filled.\nDark red tile. Failed: the disk could not be filled. The motor number is bright red.\nReason letters. Why the tile failed — see the table below.\n“Operation complete.” Every motor has finished.\n“Close”. Returns to the home screen. While the operation runs, this button reads “Cancel”.\nThe end of the operation on an eight-motor planter.\nTile\nState\nDark grey\nWaiting its turn. Up to eight motors run at the same time; the rest wait.\nYellow\nRunning: the disk is turning.\nGreen\nDone.\nDark red\nFailed.\nEach motor turns its disk at 4 rpm of the driven shaft. If no seed is registered within 1.25 revolutions — about 19 seconds — that motor is stopped and marked as failed. A motor already in an alarm state is marked as failed at once. With the fertilizer control mode also in “Precise”, the fertilizer motors are run the same way.\nNOTE\nWhile the disks are being filled, alarm windows are held back. The panel raises its access level for the duration of the operation and returns to your level at the end."},{"p":"five","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"Letters on a failed tile","u":"en/five/fill-the-disks.html#letters-on-a-failed-tile","x":"The colour of the letter matters as much as the letter: red means a fault, white means taken out of control.\nLetter\nMeaning\nm red\nA fault of the dispenser electric motor during the operation.\nm white\nThe motor is taken out of control and cannot run.\ng red\nThe seed sensor registered no seed while the disk was turning.\ns red\nA fault of the seed sensor during the operation.\ns white\nThe seed sensor is taken out of control.\nIf the seed sensor is out of control, the panel cannot check the disk. A serviceable motor still turns it for about one and a quarter turns, so in practice the disk does get filled — but the panel reports that motor as failed, because it never saw a seed."},{"p":"five","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"","u":"en/five/tramline-rhythm.html","x":"What a tramline rhythm is, worked out for your drill and sprayer, how to set it up on the panel by touch, and how the panel counts the bouts."},{"p":"five","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"","u":"en/five/tramline-rhythm.html","x":"A tramline is the lane the sprayer will drive in later. You make it now, while sowing, by shutting off one or two sections on certain bouts so that nothing is sown where the sprayer wheels will go. Which bouts those are depends on the width of the drill against the width of the sprayer. That pattern, repeating across the field, is the rhythm."},{"p":"five","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"How a rhythm works — an example","u":"en/five/tramline-rhythm.html#how-a-rhythm-works-an-example","x":"A 6-metre drill and an 18-metre sprayer: every third bout must carry the lane. The rhythm is three bouts long, and actuator 1 shuts its sections off on one of them.\nBout\nActuator 1\nWhat is sown\n1\nshuts its sections off\nAll rows except the tramline rows — the lane is made.\n2\nopen\nAll rows.\n3\nopen\nAll rows.\n1 again\nshuts off\nThe next lane, 18 m further.\nIMPORTANT\nGet the rhythm wrong and the field looks perfect until the sprayer arrives and has nowhere to drive. Check the bout count against the sprayer, not against the drill."},{"p":"five","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"What the panel allows","u":"en/five/tramline-rhythm.html#what-the-panel-allows","x":"Rhythms stored\nThree. One is active at a time.\nActuators in one rhythm\nFour. On a machine that makes tramlines by stopping dispenser motors, the motors take their place.\nCut-off bouts per actuator\nFour different bouts of the same rhythm.\nBouts in a rhythm\n1 to 50."},{"p":"five","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"Setting it up","u":"en/five/tramline-rhythm.html#setting-it-up","x":"The tramline items sit under “Seeding machine parameters”, which needs the Agronomist level: Enable the Agronomist level. The “Tramline” item is shown only on a machine that can make tramlines.\nOn the home screen tap with two fingers. Tap “Settings”, and tap it again.\nTap “Seeding machine parameters”, and tap it again.\nTap “Tramline”, and tap it again.\nThe Tramline menu.\nTap “Tramline” and swipe right to “Yes”. Then tap “Tramline rhythm settings” twice. The rhythm form opens with rhythm 1 selected.\nOn “Rhythm”, swipe right or left to choose the rhythm number. Tap with two fingers and choose “Select”. The word “Active” appears beside the number — without it the rhythm is stored but not used.\nSwipe down to “Bouts”, tap with two fingers and set the number of bouts in the rhythm.\nSwipe to the framed cell under the activation heading and tap with two fingers. Choose the number of the actuator, then the bout on which it must shut its sections off.\nIf the pattern needs it, add more bouts to the same actuator — up to four — with the framed cell at the end of its row, or another actuator with the cell on the empty row below.\nTap with three fingers. The rhythm is saved and the Tramline menu returns. Tap outside the menus until the home screen returns. The tramline indicator shows the current bout and the number of bouts.\nA touch and hold anywhere on the form saves the rhythm without leaving it. Return to the Operator level before you work — above it, no alarms are shown."},{"p":"five","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"The rhythm form","u":"en/five/tramline-rhythm.html#the-rhythm-form","x":"“Rhythm”. The number of the rhythm; a swipe right or left steps through the three.\n“Active”. This rhythm is the one in use.\n“Bouts”. The number of bouts in the rhythm.\nActivation heading. “Tramline actuators activation” on a machine that makes tramlines with actuators; “Tramline motors activation” on one that stops dispenser motors. A machine with both shows both headings.\nFramed cell. Adds an actuator (or motor) and its cut-off bout.\nA new rhythm: one bout, nothing shut off yet.\nIn the form, a swipe up or down moves between lines, a swipe right or left along a line, and a two-finger tap opens the selected cell. “Reset rhythm settings”, in the menu of the “Rhythm” line, returns the selected rhythm to its defaults."},{"p":"five","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"How the bout changes","u":"en/five/tramline-rhythm.html#how-the-bout-changes","x":"The panel counts the bouts by itself. Something on the machine must tell it that a pass has ended:\nOn the machine\nWhat ends a bout\nMarker sensors in the profile\nAt least one marker sensor under control and answering. The change of the marker position ends the bout.\nNo markers\nThe work position sensor, under control and answering. Raising the implement out of work ends the bout.\nNeither\nThe bout does not change.\nWhen the bout cannot change by itself, the tramline icon on the home screen is yellow (Tramline bout). Check the indicator on the home screen before you start a pass."},{"p":"five","t":"Set the screen brightness","h":"","u":"en/five/screen-brightness.html","x":"Make the screen brighter or dimmer — one item in the Settings menu, set by hand."},{"p":"five","t":"Set the screen brightness","h":"","u":"en/five/screen-brightness.html","x":"On the home screen tap with two fingers. Tap “Settings”, and tap it again.\n“Brightness,%” is the first item. Tap it, then swipe right to brighten or left to dim, in steps of 10, from 0 to 100.\nThe Settings menu with Brightness,% selected.\nTap outside the menu to leave it. The new brightness stays in force and is stored by itself.\nThere is no automatic brightness: the screen stays at the value you set, day and night. Dim it for night work and raise it again in the morning. 0 is the dimmest setting, not off — the screen stays lit.\nThe same menu holds “Volume” — the loudness of the alarm sound — and “Buttons sound”, the click on every touch. Everything else in the Settings menu: The Settings menu."},{"p":"five","t":"Work without the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/work-without-speed-sensor.html","x":"When the speed sensor fails in the field — take the speed from GPS, or finish the work on a simulated speed and control the application by hand."},{"p":"five","t":"Work without the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/work-without-speed-sensor.html","x":"Symptom. The speed field shows XX.X in red, or the speed stays at zero although the machine moves. Without a speed, the panel cannot calculate area, rates or deviations, and the dispenser motors do not know how fast to turn.\nCause. The speed sensor or the device that carries it does not answer, or the wheel no longer gives pulses.\nWhat to do. Two ways, both through the context menu of the speed sensor — touch and hold the speed field. Both items need the Agronomist level: Enable the Agronomist level."},{"p":"five","t":"Work without the speed sensor","h":"Take the speed from GPS","u":"en/five/work-without-speed-sensor.html#take-the-speed-from-gps","x":"Set “GPS internal” to “Yes”. The panel takes the travel speed from its own receiver. Watch the satellite symbol in the right-hand column: green or yellow is usable, red is not. See GPS."},{"p":"five","t":"Work without the speed sensor","h":"Work on a simulated speed","u":"en/five/work-without-speed-sensor.html#work-on-a-simulated-speed","x":"WARNING\nWith speed imitation on, the panel drives the dispenser motors and the pump as if the machine were moving — also while it stands. Switch imitation off before you leave the cab, and lock application whenever you stop.\nTouch and hold the speed field. Its context menu opens.\nSet “Imitation, km/h” to the speed you will drive, up to 20 km/h. The panel tells you “Settings of Min and Max speed switched off!” — the speed limits are not watched while the speed is simulated.\nThe panel locks product application by itself: the yellow hand appears in the right-hand column.\nReturn to the Operator level, so that alarms are shown again.\nThe speed field now shows a yellow figure alternating with “IMITATION” (Speed).\nControl the application with the manual lock: touch and hold an empty place of the right-hand column to start applying on a pass, and again to stop at the headland. See Lock product application by hand.\nDrive at the speed you set. The rates are right only as long as the real speed matches it.\nWhen the sensor is repaired, set “Imitation, km/h” back to “No” — swipe left until the value reads “No” — and set the speed limits again.\nNOTE\nA panel switched on with imitation still set starts locked. If nothing is applied after a restart, look for the yellow hand.\nDetails of the speed sensor settings: Speed sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Access levels","h":"","u":"en/five/access-levels.html","x":"The three access levels of the Hectare 5, what each one opens, and what happens to the alarms while a higher level is set."},{"p":"five","t":"Access levels","h":"","u":"en/five/access-levels.html","x":"Not every setting is open to everyone. The panel has three access levels. After every power-on it starts at the lowest one, “Operator”.\nLevel\nWhat it opens\n“Operator”\nEveryday work: brightness, volume, language, alarm delay, the clock, the target rates and alarm levels, taking a sensor into or out of control, the mass calibrations. Resetting the statistics asks for a PIN at this level.\n“Agronomist”\nThe commissioning settings: “Seeding machine parameters”, “Product rate error,%”, most sensor parameters, the speed and dispenser calibrations, and the “Service” menu with the profile and the device configuration. Needs a PIN.\n“Service”\nEverything above, plus the reset-to-defaults items, “Reset settings”, “Save factory defaults”, the serial number, the trial period and “Imitation mode”. For the service department. Needs a PIN.\nEvery settings table in this manual names the level an item needs. An item above your level is not shown in the menu at all — if an item named here is missing, check the level first."},{"p":"five","t":"Access levels","h":"Switch the level","u":"en/five/access-levels.html#switch-the-level","x":"The level is changed in one place only: “Settings” → “User”. Going up a level asks for the PIN of that level; going down does not. Step by step: Enable the Agronomist level.\nAsk your dealer or the service support for the PIN.\nIMPORTANT\nAt “Agronomist” and “Service” no alarm windows are shown. When seeding starts at such a level, the panel warns once in a red window — see User is Agronomist. Return to “Operator” before you work, or switch the panel off and on."},{"p":"five","t":"Access levels","h":"What happens to the alarms while the level is high","u":"en/five/access-levels.html#what-happens-to-the-alarms-while-the-level-is-high","x":"The alarms are not lost while they are hidden — they wait. When you lower the level to “Operator”, the panel drops the ones that have gone away in the meantime and shows the ones that are still true, one window after another — also those you had already closed before you raised the level. After an hour of commissioning at “Agronomist”, expect a short run of alarm windows on the way back down. It is the backlog, not a new fault. How the windows work: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Enable the Agronomist level","h":"","u":"en/five/enable-agronomist-level.html","x":"The steps, by touch, to raise the access level to \"Agronomist\" or \"Service\" before changing settings, and to return to \"Operator\" afterwards."},{"p":"five","t":"Enable the Agronomist level","h":"","u":"en/five/enable-agronomist-level.html","x":"Most set-up work — sensor parameters, calibrations, the profile, device configuration — needs the “Agronomist” level. The “Service” level is raised the same way. What each level opens: Access levels.\nYou need the PIN of the level you want. A PIN opens only its own level: choose “Agronomist” and type the Agronomist PIN.\nOn the home screen tap with two fingers. The main menu opens.\nThe main menu.\nTap “Settings”, and tap it again. The “Settings” menu opens.\nThe Settings menu at the Operator level.\nFind “User” near the end of the menu — swipe up if it is not in view — and tap it. Swipe right until it reads “Agronomist” (or “Service”).\n“User” set to Agronomist.\nTap “User” again. The number pad opens with “Enter your PIN:”. Type the PIN and tap “OK”.\nThe number pad asking for the PIN.\nA short window names the new level, and the “Settings” menu returns with the items of that level — for example “Seeding machine parameters”. Tap outside the menu to leave.\nA wrong PIN shows “Invalid PIN” in a red window, and the number pad opens again. To give up, tap outside the number pad; the level stays as it was.\nIMPORTANT\nAt “Agronomist” and “Service” no alarm windows are shown. When you have finished, return to “Operator”: repeat steps 1–3, swipe left to “Operator” and tap “User” again — no PIN is asked going down. Switching the panel off and on does the same.\nWhen you come back down, the alarms that are still true are shown one after another. See Access levels."},{"p":"five","t":"The profile","h":"","u":"en/five/profile.html","x":"What the profile tells the panel, how to check it, what each profile item allows, when the panel refuses a profile, and how it is saved."},{"p":"five","t":"The profile","h":"","u":"en/five/profile.html","x":"The profile tells the panel which devices, and how many of each, are on the machine. A panel usually arrives with the profile already set. Check it before you set anything else: a device missing from the profile is never watched, and a device in the profile that is not fitted raises “Connection lost”."},{"p":"five","t":"The profile","h":"Check the profile on the home screen","u":"en/five/profile.html#check-the-profile-on-the-home-screen","x":"Switch the panel on and wait until the home screen appears. Then look at it:\nNo “Connection lost” alarm — the panel has found every device the profile declares.\nNo white indicators with an exclamation mark — no device is left out of control.\nNo red indicators with an “X” — every device answers.\nWhat the indicators mean: Indicators. How to take a device into control or out of it: Take a sensor into or out of control.\nNOTE\nA profile is sometimes supplied with devices that are not in the delivery, so that the system can be extended later. Those indicators stay out of control on purpose. Do not switch their control on."},{"p":"five","t":"The profile","h":"Open the profile","u":"en/five/profile.html#open-the-profile","x":"The profile needs the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level). Tap the home screen with two fingers, then tap “Service” twice and “Profile” twice. Change the selected item with a swipe right or left.\nThe top of the Profile menu."},{"p":"five","t":"The profile","h":"The profile items","u":"en/five/profile.html#the-profile-items","x":"Item\nRange\n“Seed sensors”\n2 … 120. The menu lets you set 1, but such a profile is refused when it is saved.\n“Fertilizer sensors”\n0 … 60; seed and fertilizer sensors together not more than 120.\n“Liq.fert.flow sensors”\n0 … 60 — liquid fertilizer section sensors.\n“Hoppers”\n0 … 9\n“Dispensers”\n0 … 9 — dispenser shaft sensors.\n“Speed”\n“No” / “Control panel” (sensor wired to the panel) / “Input device” (one wheel sensor on an input device) / “Input device (2)” (two wheel sensors on an input device).\n“Fans”\n0 … 2\n“Pressure sensors”\n0 … 3\n“Work position sensor”\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Markers”\n“No” / “One” / “Both”\n“No battery charge”, “Emergency oil pressure”, “Ignition”, “Low fuel”, “Air filter”\nThe engine sensors — “No” / “Yes” each.\n“EMD of the seeds”\n0 … 60 — seed dispenser electric motors.\n“EMD of the fertilizers”\n0 … 30 — fertilizer dispenser electric motors; both motor groups together not more than 60.\n“Tramline actuators”\n0 … 4\n“Liq.fert.pump controller”\n0 … 1\nAt the end of the menu follow “Save settings”, “Load settings”, the two memory card items and “Save profile” — see below and Save and load settings."},{"p":"five","t":"The profile","h":"When the panel refuses a profile","u":"en/five/profile.html#when-the-panel-refuses-a-profile","x":"The panel checks the whole profile, not only the item you changed. It refuses a profile when:\nthe hopper, dispenser and engine sensors together come to more than 12;\nthey come to more than 10 while the pressure and fan sensors together come to more than four;\nthe pressure and fan sensors together come to more than five;\nthere are fertilizer motors but no seed motors, or fertilizer motors but no fertilizer sensors;\nthere are electric motors or a liquid fertilizer pump but no speed sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"The profile","h":"Save the profile","u":"en/five/profile.html#save-the-profile","x":"After changing the profile, tap “Save profile” at the end of the menu, and tap it again.\nIf nothing was changed, the panel shows “No changes”. If the profile breaks one of the rules above, it shows “Invalid profile!” in red and saves nothing. Otherwise it asks “Are you sure?”.\nThe question before the profile is saved.\nTap “Yes”. The panel shows “Wait...”, then “Saved” and “Restart system”, and restarts by itself.\nA tap on “No”, or outside the question window, leaves the saved profile as it was."},{"p":"five","t":"Save and load settings","h":"","u":"en/five/save-and-load-settings.html","x":"Keep a copy of the settings inside the panel and bring it back, and write the profile to a memory card."},{"p":"five","t":"Save and load settings","h":"","u":"en/five/save-and-load-settings.html","x":"The panel can keep a copy of its settings and bring it back later — useful before you try something out. With a memory card in the slot it can also write the profile to the card. All these items are at the end of the “Profile” menu (The profile) and need the “Agronomist” level.\nItem in the “Profile” menu\nWhat it does\n“Save settings”\nKeeps a copy of the current settings inside the panel.\n“Load settings”\nBrings that copy back.\n“Save to SD-card”\nWrites the profile to the memory card. Shown only while a card is in the slot and ready.\n“Load from SD-card”\nShows the list of the profiles on the card. Shown only while a card is ready.\n“Save factory defaults”\nStores the current settings as the factory values. “Service” level only."},{"p":"five","t":"Save and load settings","h":"Keep and restore the settings","u":"en/five/save-and-load-settings.html#keep-and-restore-the-settings","x":"Raise the level (Enable the Agronomist level), tap the home screen with two fingers and open “Service” → “Profile” — tap each item twice.\nSwipe up to the end of the menu. Tap “Save settings” or “Load settings”, and tap it again. The panel asks “Are you sure?”.\nThe question before the settings are saved.\nTap “Yes”. The panel answers “Saved” or “Loaded”.\nThe panel confirms with “Saved”.\nIf there is no copy to bring back, “Load settings” ends with “FAILURE” in a red window."},{"p":"five","t":"Save and load settings","h":"Write the profile to the memory card","u":"en/five/save-and-load-settings.html#write-the-profile-to-the-memory-card","x":"Tap “Save to SD-card” twice. There is no question: the panel writes the profile and answers “Saved”. If the card cannot be written, it answers “FAILURE”; if the card has gone, “FAILURE” and “Insert SD-card”.\nThe files are named automatically, P0001.pfl and onwards, in the PROFILES folder of the card.\nKeep the card for its own panel: a profile file written by one firmware version is very likely not readable by another."},{"p":"five","t":"Save and load settings","h":"The list of profiles on the card","u":"en/five/save-and-load-settings.html#the-list-of-profiles-on-the-card","x":"“Load from SD-card” opens a list of the profile files found on the card. A file the panel cannot read is shown in red. A tap with three fingers closes the list. With no profile files on the card, the item answers “FAILURE”."},{"p":"five","t":"The Settings menu","h":"","u":"en/five/settings-menu.html","x":"Every item of the panel's own \"Settings\" menu — brightness, volume, language, touch sound, diagram settings, date and time, alarm delay, access level — with its range, default and access level."},{"p":"five","t":"The Settings menu","h":"","u":"en/five/settings-menu.html","x":"The “Settings” menu holds the settings of the panel itself. Open it from the home screen: tap with two fingers, then tap “Settings” twice. The settings of the individual sensors are elsewhere — see Sensor settings.\n“Brightness,%” — the backlight. The selected item shows its value between - and +; swipe right or left to change it.\n“Volume” — the loudness of the alarm sound.\n“Language” — the language of the screens.\n“Buttons sound” — a click on every touch the screen takes.\n“Seeding diagram settings” — how the seed diagram is drawn. Below.\n“Change Date&Time” — the clock. Below.\n“Alarm delay” — how long a fault must last before the alarm window opens.\n“User” — the access level. Access levels.\nThe “Settings” menu of a planter with a speed sensor in the “Precise” mode, at the “Operator” level, with “Brightness,%” selected.\nIMPORTANT\nThe menu does not always have the same items. The access level, the machine in the profile and the modes of the diagrams change it. If an item named here is missing, check those three before looking for a fault."},{"p":"five","t":"The Settings menu","h":"The items","u":"en/five/settings-menu.html#the-items","x":"Item\nRange and default\nLevel\n“Brightness,%”\n0 … 100, in steps of 10; default 100. 0 is the dimmest setting, not off. See Screen brightness.\nOperator\n“Volume”\n0 … 100, in steps of 10; default 100.\nOperator\n“Language”\nEnglish, Ukrainian, Polish, Hungarian, Romanian, Bulgarian.\nOperator\n“Buttons sound”\n“No” / “Yes”; default “Yes”.\nOperator\n“Seeding diagram settings”, “Fertil. diagram settings”, “Liq.fert. diagram settings”\nSubmenus. Each is shown only when the machine has a speed sensor, its own sensors are in the profile and its diagram is not in the “Flow” mode.\nOperator\n“Change Date&Time”\nSubmenu.\nOperator\n“Alarm delay”\n2 … 20 s; default 3 s. See How alarms work.\nOperator\n“Product rate error,%”\n3 … 20 %; default 5 %. How far a product dosed by weight, or the liquid fertilizer pump, may stand from its target before an alarm. Shown only when a product is dosed by weight or a pump controller is in the profile.\nAgronomist\n“Seeding machine parameters”\nSubmenu: width, modes, tramline, calibrations. Hidden when the machine has neither a speed sensor nor a tramline actuator. Seeding machine parameters\nAgronomist\n“User”\n“Operator” / “Agronomist” / “Service”. Enable the Agronomist level\nOperator\n“Reset settings”\nReturns the settings to the factory values.\nService\nOn a machine with two groups of seed sensors, the two diagram items read “Seeding gr.A diagram” and “Seeding gr.B diagram”.\nThe settings are stored by themselves a few seconds after the last change. Wait a moment before you switch the panel off."},{"p":"five","t":"The Settings menu","h":"Diagram settings","u":"en/five/settings-menu.html#diagram-settings","x":"The diagram settings of the seed sensors.\nThere are three of these menus — seed, dry fertilizer, liquid fertilizer. The liquid fertilizer menu has only “Grid” and “Reset to defaults”.\nItem\nWhat it does\n“Skips & Doubles”\n“Yes” shows the skip and double bars over the last stretch of the path. “Precise” mode only. Default “Yes”.\n“Grid”\n“No” — only the rate line; “Partial” — only the tolerance limits; “Full” — both. Default “Full”.\n“Scale S&D,%”\nThe height of the skip and double bars, 5 … 50 % in steps of 5; default 20. “Precise” mode only.\n“Reset to defaults”\nReturns this menu to the factory values. Service level only."},{"p":"five","t":"The Settings menu","h":"Change Date and Time","u":"en/five/settings-menu.html#change-date-and-time","x":"Tap “Change Date&Time”, and tap it again. The fields “Year”, “Month”, “Day”, “Hours”, “Minutes” and “Seconds” open, with “Set” at the end.\nThe date and time fields.\nTap a field and swipe right or left until it shows the value you need — one step per swipe. The days follow the month.\nTap “Set”, and tap it again. The clock takes the new values and the “Settings” menu returns.\nLeaving the fields with a tap outside the menu, without “Set”, keeps the old time."},{"p":"five","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"","u":"en/five/seeding-machine-parameters.html","x":"The working width, the modes of seeding and fertilizing, the tramline, the relation of sections to actuators and motors, and the two calibrations that need the machine to move."},{"p":"five","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"","u":"en/five/seeding-machine-parameters.html","x":"“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters”. The item needs the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level) and is hidden on a machine with neither a speed sensor nor a tramline actuator. Which items it holds depends on the profile.\nThe Seeding machine parameters menu of a planter: the width in square brackets, the two modes, the two calibrations.\nItem\nValues\n“Width, cm”\n100 … 2500; default 560. Number pad.\n“Mode of seeding”\n“Flow” / “Hybrid” / “Precise”; default “Flow”.\n“Mode of fertilizers”\n“Flow” / “Hybrid” / “Precise”.\n“Mode of liq.fert.”\n“Flow” / “By area”.\n“Tramline”\nSubmenu.\n“Relation of sensors to actuators”\nOpens the mapping screen.\n“Speed calibration”\nStarts the speed calibration.\n“Dispenser calibration”\n“ALL”, or the number of one dispenser; starts the rotation calibration.\n“Reset to defaults”\nReturns the width and the modes to the factory values. Service level only."},{"p":"five","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Width, cm","u":"en/five/seeding-machine-parameters.html#width-cm","x":"The working width of the implement. The worked area is calculated from it, so it must be entered. Tap the item twice, or swipe on it: the number pad opens. Type the width and tap “OK”; a tap outside the pad keeps the old value. The item is hidden without a speed sensor.\nIf the statistics already hold work, the panel first asks to erase them — the area of the old width and of the new one cannot be added together."},{"p":"five","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Mode of seeding, Mode of fertilizers","u":"en/five/seeding-machine-parameters.html#mode-of-seeding-mode-of-fertilizers","x":"Mode\nUnit\nFor\n“Flow”\nseeds per second\ngrain drills\n“Hybrid”\nseeds per metre\ngrain drills\n“Precise”\nseeds per metre, with skips and doubles counted\nprecision planters\nThe fertilizers can be set to “Precise” only while the seeding is “Precise”. On a machine with two groups of seed sensors the two items read “Mode of seeding gr.A” and “Mode of seeding gr.B”; group B can be “Precise” only while group A is, so set group B to “Flow” or “Hybrid” before you take group A out of “Precise”.\nIMPORTANT\nSwitching between “Precise” and the other two modes demands a reset of the statistics if any field has work recorded in it. The panel asks first; “No” keeps the old mode."},{"p":"five","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Liquid fertilizer mode","u":"en/five/seeding-machine-parameters.html#liquid-fertilizer-mode","x":"“Flow” (litres per minute) or “By area” (litres per hectare). Shown only when there is no liquid fertilizer pump controller in the profile — with a controller the panel works by area."},{"p":"five","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Relation of sensors to actuators","u":"en/five/seeding-machine-parameters.html#relation-of-sensors-to-actuators","x":"Which section sensors belong to which tramline actuator or dispenser motor. When an actuator shuts its sections off, their sensors switch to tramline monitoring. The item is shown only where there is something to relate: tramline actuators, or dispenser motors that are not one to one with the section sensors.\nThe mapping screen: one line for the actuators, one for the motors.\nA mapping is a range of sections, “Diapason start” to “Diapason end” — equal numbers mean a single section. Up to four ranges can be attached to one actuator. On a machine with dispenser motors the screen also has “Motors to sensors mapping”.\nOn the screen, a swipe up or down moves between the lines, a swipe right or left along a line, and a two-finger tap opens the menu of the selected cell — to add a range or to “Remove diapason”. Touch and hold anywhere on the screen to apply the mapping."},{"p":"five","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Tramline","u":"en/five/seeding-machine-parameters.html#tramline","x":"A submenu with two items: “Tramline” (“No” / “Yes”) and “Tramline rhythm settings”. Shown only on a machine that can make tramlines. Setting up a rhythm: Set up the tramline rhythm."},{"p":"five","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"Speed calibration and Dispenser calibration","u":"en/five/seeding-machine-parameters.html#speed-calibration-and-dispenser-calibration","x":"The two calibrations that need the machine to move. Both are hidden without a speed sensor. Select “Dispenser calibration” and swipe right or left to choose the dispenser — or “ALL” for all of them at once — then tap it again to start.\nCalibrate the speed sensor\nCalibrate the dispenser rotation"},{"p":"five","t":"Sensor settings","h":"","u":"en/five/sensor-settings.html","x":"How to reach the settings of one sensor by touch, what its context menu holds, and which calibrations exist and where."},{"p":"five","t":"Sensor settings","h":"","u":"en/five/sensor-settings.html","x":"Every sensor, actuator and motor on the machine has its own settings and its own status screen. Both are reached through its context menu. The parameters of each kind of sensor are in the topics that follow this one."},{"p":"five","t":"Sensor settings","h":"Reach a sensor","u":"en/five/sensor-settings.html#reach-a-sensor","x":"Touch and hold the device for more than half a second:\non the home screen — its icon, or its column on a diagram;\non the Motors screen or the second home screen — the tile of a motor or a tramline actuator.\nThe context menu opens. A short tap does nothing. A touch and hold on a kg/ha indicator, on the target of a diagram or on an empty place of the right-hand column does something else — see Touch zones and gestures.\nThe context menu of a fan sensor at the Agronomist level."},{"p":"five","t":"Sensor settings","h":"The context menu","u":"en/five/sensor-settings.html#the-context-menu","x":"Item\nWhat it does\nLevel\n“Information”\nOpens the status screen of this device. The sensor status screen\nOperator\n“Disable control” / “Enable control”\nTakes the device out of monitoring, or back in. Not offered for the speed sensor. Take a sensor into or out of control\nOperator\n“Registers”\nA raw view of the device's registers, for the service department. Not part of any procedure. For a motor or the pump controller the item reads “Electric motors” and opens the diagnostic screen of the controllers.\nAgronomist\nThe device's own items\nFor example “Speed calibration” for the speed sensor.\nas stated\nThe parameters\nWhich ones you see depends on the access level. Change the selected one with a swipe right or left.\nas stated\n“Reset to defaults”\nReturns this device's parameters to the factory values.\nService\nTap outside the menu to leave it. Changed values are stored by themselves."},{"p":"five","t":"Sensor settings","h":"The status screen","u":"en/five/sensor-settings.html#the-status-screen","x":"“Information” opens the status screen of the device: its identity, its link, its live figures and its active alarms. How to read it: The sensor status screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Sensor settings","h":"Calibrations","u":"en/five/sensor-settings.html#calibrations","x":"Four calibrations exist. They are not variants of one procedure: each writes different parameters and is reached a different way.\nWhat it puts right\nWhere\nLevel\nMachine\nTravel speed — rewrites “Factor, %” of the speed sensor. Calibrate the speed sensor\n“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters” → “Speed calibration”, or “Speed calibration” in the context menu of the speed sensor\nAgronomist in the menu, Operator in the context menu\ndrives 100 m\nDispenser rotation — “Wheel, pulses” against “Dispenser pulses”. Calibrate the dispenser rotation\n“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters” → “Dispenser calibration”\nAgronomist\nwheel and dispenser shaft turned together\nDispenser mass — “Weight, gr” and “Weight, pulses”. Calibrate the dispenser by weight\ncontext menu of the dispenser sensor\nOperator\nstanding\nMetering roller mass — “Weight, gr” and “Rotations” of one motor. Calibrate a metering motor by weight\ncontext menu of the dispenser electric motor\nOperator\nstanding\nThe context-menu items “Speed calibration” and the motor calibration are offered only while the machine stands still. The two mass calibrations are at the “Operator” level because they have to be repeated whenever the product changes."},{"p":"five","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"","u":"en/five/sensor-status-screen.html","x":"How to read the status screen of a sensor — channel, address, type, link state, live figures and active alarms — what each kind of sensor shows on it, and how to move through it by touch."},{"p":"five","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"","u":"en/five/sensor-status-screen.html","x":"Reached through “Information” in the context menu of a device (Sensor settings). It shows what the panel knows about one device."},{"p":"five","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"The screen","u":"en/five/sensor-status-screen.html#the-screen","x":"“Channel #” — which device of the whole list this is.\nThe marks either side of the channel number. Swipe left for the previous device, right for the next one. The screen stays open.\n“Address:” — the address the device answers on. Not given in this manual.\n“Type:” — the kind of device and its number, here hopper sensor 1.\n“Link:” — the state of the communication. Table below.\nThe figures of this kind of device — here the level on the input of a hopper sensor. Table below.\n“Alarm 1 from 1” — how many alarms of this device are active.\nThe name of the alarm — the first of the active alarms.\nThe status screen of a hopper sensor whose hopper is empty.\nSome devices show one more line, “Unit:” — the unit the device is connected to, for example “Control panel” for the travel speed sensor.\nGesture\nWhat it does\nSwipe right / left\nNext / previous device — the screen stays open.\nSwipe up, or three-finger tap\nBack to where you came from."},{"p":"five","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"Link states","u":"en/five/sensor-status-screen.html#link-states","x":"State\nMeaning\n“Normal”\nThe device answers.\n“Not controlled”\nThe device is out of control: neither the communication nor anything else is watched.\n“No connection”\nThe device does not answer. Red.\n“Wrong device type!”\nSomething answers, but not the kind of device the profile expects. Red.\n“IMITATION”\nSpeed sensor only: the speed is simulated, not measured. Yellow.\nThe figures are drawn only while the device is under control, answers, and is of the expected kind. A device out of control shows its identity and nothing else."},{"p":"five","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"What each kind of sensor adds","u":"en/five/sensor-status-screen.html#what-each-kind-of-sensor-adds","x":"The status screen of a speed sensor wired to the panel.\nDevice\nWhat the screen shows\nSpeed sensor\nThe pulse frequency F= in Hz; with two speed sensors F1= and F2=. Under it the two limits “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h”. On a machine that doses a product by weight with electric motors, the limits are those the motors can hold, narrowed by the two settings; a limit the settings cannot meet is written in red as an inequality. Without such motors a limit set to zero is shown as ----, switched off.\nHopper sensor\nPin: and the level on the input, \"0\" or \"1\".\nDispenser shaft sensor\nF= in Hz, to three decimals.\nFan sensor\nF= in Hz.\nPressure sensor\nP= in kPa.\nSeed sensor, dry fertilizer sensor\nStatus: and one of eight states — below.\nLiquid fertilizer flow sensor\nStatus: Working, or Working:Tramline in the tramline mode; under it Flow= in L/min.\nWork position sensor\n“Position: Working” or “Position: Transporting”, and Pin: with the level on the input.\nIgnition, oil pressure, battery charge, fuel level, air filter\nPin: and the level on the input, \"0\" or \"1\".\nMarker sensor\nOnly the common lines.\nTramline actuator\nStatus: Pulled in, Pulled out, Working while the shutter moves, or Unknown.\nDispenser electric motor\nStatus: Working, or Faulty while any alarm of the motor is active; then “Last fault:” with the controller's code, if there has been one (codes). A universal controller adds IN1[ ] IN2[ ] IN3[ ] with the levels on its three inputs.\nLiquid fertilizer pump controller\nStatus: Working or Faulty. With a fault, “Last fault:” and the code. Without one: Flow= in L/min, Load= in per cent, and Pressure sensor: No if there is none, otherwise Pressure sensor= in Bar with the raw signal in mA in brackets. Dashes stand for a value the controller cannot give."},{"p":"five","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"The eight states of a seed or fertilizer sensor","u":"en/five/sensor-status-screen.html#the-eight-states-of-a-seed-or-fertilizer-sensor","x":"Status\nMeaning\nWorking\nNormal.\nWorking:Tramline\nNormal; the sensor is in the tramline control mode for the current bout, or its product application is switched off.\nConfiguring\nThe sensor has received new settings and is applying them.\nCalibration in progress\nThe sensor runs its initial calibration. It takes a few seconds.\nClogged\nThe optical channel is blocked — most likely a blocked seed tube. Red.\nLight struck\nToo much light falls into the optical channel; the sensor may miscount. Red.\nFaulty\nThe sensor reports a fault of its own. Red.\nUnknown\nAnother fault that cannot be identified. Red."},{"p":"five","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/speed-sensor.html","x":"The parameters of the travel speed sensor — pulses, wheel diameter, factor, speed limits, imitation, the built-in GPS receiver — and what setting the imitation does by itself."},{"p":"five","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/speed-sensor.html","x":"Everything the panel counts — area, rates, deviations — is worked out from the distance travelled. A speed sensor that is out by a tenth makes every other figure on the panel wrong by a tenth. Set its parameters first, then calibrate it (Calibrate the speed sensor).\nTouch and hold the speed field on the home screen to open the context menu of the speed sensor (Sensor settings). All its parameters need the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level). The menu also offers “Speed calibration” directly, under “Information”, while the machine stands still.\nThe context menu of the speed sensor at the Agronomist level. A limit that is switched off reads “No”.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\n“Pulses”\nThe number of pulses the sensor gives per turn of the wheel.\n1 … 1000; 6\n“Count period”\nThe number of pulses the panel averages over before it works out the speed afresh; a sudden change of speed is taken at once. Hidden when the sensor is on an input device.\n1 … 1000; 1\n“Diameter, cm”\nThe wheel diameter.\n10 … 200; 60\n“Factor, %”\nA correction of the distance per wheel turn: the panel counts the wheel circumference times this factor. 100 takes the circumference as it is. The speed calibration rewrites it. Number pad.\n1 … 10000; 100\n“Seeding criteria”\n“Yes” — the section alarms are watched only while the machine moves; “No” — also while it stands. Hidden, and always “Yes”, in the “Precise” mode and on a machine with dispenser motors or a pump.\n“No” / “Yes”; “Yes”\n“Min speed, km/h”\nBelow this speed the home screen shows “FASTER”. “No” (0) switches the check off.\n0 … 99; “No”\n“Max speed, km/h”\nAbove this speed the home screen shows “SLOWER”. “No” (0) switches the check off.\n0 … 99; “No”\n“Imitation, km/h”\nA simulated speed. “No” (0) — the real sensor is used.\n0 … 20; “No”\n“GPS”\nTake the speed from an external receiver. Shown only when one is in use.\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Width, cm”\nThe track between the two wheels of a two-sensor installation. Shown only with “Input device (2)” in the profile.\n0 … 10000; 280\n“GPS internal”\nTake the speed from the receiver built into the panel.\n“No” / “Yes”; “No”\nThe minimum must be below the maximum, otherwise the panel raises “Error in settings of Min/Max” (Speed sensor alarms). With “GPS internal” on, the satellite symbol on the home screen shows the reception, and fewer than four satellites raise “Poor GPS signal quality” — see GPS.\nIMPORTANT\nSetting “Imitation, km/h” to anything but zero changes two other things by itself. The panel switches “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” off — they mean nothing against a simulated speed — and says so in a window. And it locks the product application, so that nothing is dosed while the machine stands. The lock is the yellow hand on the home screen; to release it, touch and hold an empty place of the right-hand column (Lock product application by hand).\nThe imitated speed is also held inside the range the dosing allows: a value outside it is pulled back to the nearest limit as you set it. While the speed is imitated, the status screen of the sensor shows “IMITATION” in yellow. Working without a speed sensor: Work without the speed sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"The status screen","u":"en/five/speed-sensor.html#the-status-screen","x":"The status screen shows the pulse frequency F= in Hz (with two sensors F1= and F2=) and the two limits in force. See The sensor status screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/calibrate-speed.html","x":"Drive a marked 100 m so that the panel corrects the factor of the travel speed sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/calibrate-speed.html","x":"The calibration measures a known distance and corrects “Factor, %” of the speed sensor so that the panel's distance matches it. Before you start, set “Pulses” and “Diameter, cm” of the sensor (Travel speed sensor) — the calibration corrects the distance per turn; it cannot invent the pulses.\nThere are two ways in:\n“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters” → “Speed calibration” — at the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level);\n“Speed calibration” in the context menu of the speed sensor, while the machine stands still. At the “Operator” level the panel first asks for the PIN of the Agronomist level; at the “Agronomist” level it asks “Are you sure?” — tap “Yes”.\nMark out 100 m on level ground and put the machine at the start.\nTap “Speed calibration”, and tap it again; coming from the context menu, answer the PIN or the question first. The panel shows “Pass 100m and press STOP” with a single “STOP” button.\nThe speed calibration window.\nDrive the marked distance and stop at the end.\nTap “STOP”. The panel shows “Done” and closes the window by itself.\nThe panel confirms the calibration.\nThe distance is fixed at 100 m; the panel does not ask for it. It waits half a second after “STOP” before reading the counter and checks the connection once more in that moment — a sensor that drops out on the last metre is reported as a lost connection, not as a bad result.\nNOTE\nA tap outside the window ends the calibration without a result. Keep your hand off the screen while driving, and tap only “STOP”."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"When it does not work","u":"en/five/calibrate-speed.html#when-it-does-not-work","x":"The panel shows\nMeaning\n“No speed sensor”\nThe profile has no speed sensor. The calibration does not start.\n“Connection lost with the sensor! Calibration canceled!”\nThe sensor does not answer.\n“Sensor not controlled! Calibration canceled!”\nThe sensor is out of control. Take it into control (Take a sensor into or out of control).\n“FAILURE! The tractor did not move!”\nThe counter did not move: the sensor gave no pulses at all — not an inaccurate reading, none. Check the sensor and its cable.\n“Calibration canceled!”\nThe window was closed some other way than with “STOP”. Nothing has changed."},{"p":"five","t":"Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors","h":"","u":"en/five/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html","x":"The parameters shared by a group of optical seed or dry fertilizer sensors — fraction, sensitivity, rate error, skips and doubles, percent of sowing, tramline tolerance — and the per-sensor switch for product application."},{"p":"five","t":"Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors","h":"","u":"en/five/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html","x":"Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors are the same kind of device and share one parameter set. Almost everything here is common to the whole group: change it on one sensor and it changes for all sensors of the group. Only two things are set per sensor — whether it is under control (“Disable control” / “Enable control”) and “Product application”. Touch and hold the column of any sensor of the group to open its context menu (Sensor settings).\nThe context menu of a seed sensor, top part.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\nLevel\n“Product application”\nPer sensor. “No” — the row deliberately applies nothing, and the panel watches it as a shut-off tramline row. Not offered when every sensor of the group has its own dispenser motor.\n“No” / “Yes”\nAgronomist\n“Sensitivity”\nThe sensitivity of the optical channel. Shown only for the sensor types that use it.\n1 … 120; 80\nAgronomist\n“Fraction”\nThe sensor's filter for the size of the product particles. The rate, skip and double alarms tell you in which direction to change it.\n1 … 100; 10\nOperator\n“Correction”\nA correction of the detection window of the optical channel. Shown only for the sensor type that uses it.\n100 … 3300, steps of 10; 300\nOperator\n“Rate error,%”\nHow far the actual rate may stand from the target before an alarm. “Hybrid” and “Precise” modes only.\n5 … 95, steps of 5; 50\nAgronomist\n“Doubles error,%”\nThe share of doubles that raises an alarm. “No” (0) switches the alarm off. “Precise” mode only.\n0 … 95; “No”\nAgronomist\n“Skips error,%”\nThe share of skips that raises an alarm. “No” (0) switches the alarm off. “Precise” mode only.\n0 … 95; “No”\nAgronomist\n“Recalculation,m” / “Recalculation,sec”\nThe stretch the skips and doubles are counted over — in metres on a machine with a speed sensor, in seconds without one.\n10 … 50; 20\nOperator\n“Percent of sowing,%”\nThe share of the seed sensors under control that must see seed before the panel decides that seeding has started. Seed sensors only; hidden on a machine with dispenser motors or a pump.\n5 … 80, steps of 5; 20\nAgronomist\n“Toler.sowing to tramline,%”\nHow much a section that should be shut off for the tramline may still deliver.\n0 … 50; 20\nAgronomist\n“Group start address”\nFertilizer sensors only. Set at commissioning — see Device configuration.\n—\nAgronomist\nTIP\n“Fraction” is the first setting to look at when the rate alarms do not match reality. Most rate, skip and double alarms name it, with the direction to change it: Rate alarms, Doubles and skips, Tramline alarms.\nThe target rates and the alarm level are not menu items: they are changed while you work, on the home screen — see Change the target rate. The status screen of these sensors shows one of eight states — see The sensor status screen. If a sensor reports “Clogged”, “Light struck” or “Broken”: Sensor clogged or broken."},{"p":"five","t":"Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors","h":"Fraction for the crop","u":"en/five/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html#fraction-for-the-crop","x":"Go by the size of the seed:\nCrop\n“Fraction”\nRapeseed\n1 – 5\nWheat\n5 – 10\nMaize\n10 – 30\nSunflower\n10 – 30\nOn pneumatic drills set 1 whatever the crop. Too high a fraction makes small seeds disappear from the count — the panel then reports too low a rate.\nIMPORTANT\nChange “Correction” only after talking to the service. It is needed in rare cases only; a wrong value makes the sensors miss seed or count dust."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer sensors and pump controller","h":"","u":"en/five/liquid-fertilizer.html","x":"The parameters of the liquid fertilizer section sensors and of the liquid fertilizer pump controller, with their ranges and defaults."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer sensors and pump controller","h":"","u":"en/five/liquid-fertilizer.html","x":"Liquid fertilizer is watched by flow sensors in the sections; on some machines a pump controller also drives the pump to the rate. Touch and hold the liquid diagram, or the pump indicator, to open the context menu (Sensor settings)."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer sensors and pump controller","h":"Liquid fertilizer section sensors","u":"en/five/liquid-fertilizer.html#liquid-fertilizer-section-sensors","x":"Parameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\nLevel\n“Pulses per liter”\nHow many pulses the flow sensor gives per litre. Number pad. Hidden when a pump controller is in the profile.\n1 … 60000; 5000\nAgronomist\n“Rate error,%”\nHow far the actual rate may stand from the target before an alarm.\n5 … 95, steps of 5; 50\nAgronomist\n“Tolerable leak to tramline,%”\nHow much a section shut off for the tramline may still deliver.\n0 … 50; 20\nAgronomist\n“Group start address”\nSee Device configuration.\n—\nAgronomist\nThe two target rates of these sensors — one for the “Flow” mode, one for the “By area” mode — have no menu item. They are set on the home screen, like every other target rate (Change the target rate). The mode itself: Seeding machine parameters."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer sensors and pump controller","h":"Liquid fertilizer pump controller","u":"en/five/liquid-fertilizer.html#liquid-fertilizer-pump-controller","x":"Parameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\nLevel\n“Pulses per liter”\nThe pulses of the pump's flow measurement per litre; the panel works out the volume pumped from it. Number pad.\n100 … 10000, steps of 10; 3000\nAgronomist\n“Pressure sensor”\nThe input of the controller the 4–20 mA pressure sensor of the line is wired to; “No” — none.\n“No”, 1, 2; “No”\nAgronomist\n“Pressure boundary, Bar”\nThe pressure the sensor reports at the top of its range (20 mA). Set it from the data sheet of the sensor.\n0.0 … 50.0; 16.0\nAgronomist\n“Max pressure, Bar”\nAbove it the controller raises “High pressure”. 0.0 switches the alarm off.\n0.0 … 50.0; 0.0\nOperator\n“Section control D.I.”\nAn input of the controller that shuts the pump off: “Shut off(0)” when the input reads 0, “Shut off(1)” when it reads 1; “No” — not used.\n“No” / “Shut off(0)” / “Shut off(1)”\nAgronomist\n“Start delay,mS”\nThe shortest time, in milliseconds, between two starts of the pump.\n0 … 500, steps of 5; 30\nService\n“Group start address”\nSee Device configuration.\n—\nAgronomist\nThe two pressure values are entered with one decimal place. With a pump controller, the liquid rate is always worked by area.\nThe status screen of the pump controller shows the flow, the pump load and the line pressure — see The sensor status screen. Alarms: Liquid fertilizer pump, Rate alarms."},{"p":"five","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/dispenser-sensor.html","x":"The parameters of the dispenser shaft sensor — rotation control against the wheel, or weight control by the mass applied — with ranges and defaults."},{"p":"five","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/dispenser-sensor.html","x":"The dispenser shaft sensor counts the turns of a dispenser driven from the ground wheel. The panel can watch it two ways: by its rotation against the speed sensor, or by the mass of product it applies. Touch and hold the metering unit icon on the home screen to open its context menu (Sensor settings).\nThe context menu of a dispenser sensor with both controls switched off.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\nLevel\n“Period, s”\nHow long the shaft may give no pulse before it counts as stopped — and how long after the start of seeding or moving the panel waits before it expects rotation.\n1 … 60; 10\nOperator\n“Rotation speed control”\nWatch the shaft rotation against the travel speed.\n“No” / “Yes”; “No”\nAgronomist\n“Wheel, pulses”\nSet by the rotation calibration. Shown with rotation control on.\n1 … 65535; 100\nAgronomist\n“Dispenser pulses”\nSet by the rotation calibration. Shown with rotation control on.\n1 … 65535; 100\nAgronomist\n“Maximum error, %”\nHow far the shaft speed may stand from the speed the travel speed asks for before “Low speed of rotation” or “High speed of rotation”. Shown with rotation control on.\n1 … 50; 10\nAgronomist\n“Weight control”\nWatch the dispenser by the mass applied instead of by its rotation.\n“No” / “Yes”; “No”\nAgronomist\n“Type of product”\nWhich of the four products this dispenser meters. Shown with weight control on.\n“Product 1” … “Product 4”\nAgronomist\n“Weight, gr” / “Weight, pulses”\nBoth set by the calibration by weight. Shown with weight control on. Number pad.\n1 … 65535 each\nAgronomist\nWithout a speed sensor in the profile, only “Period, s” is offered — both kinds of control need the travel speed. With “Weight control” switched on, the context menu also holds “Dispenser calibration(weight)”.\nTIP\nPrefer weight control to rotation control. Rotation control notices that the shaft has stopped, not that the product has. “Weight control” watches the kilograms per hectare applied and catches an empty or bridged hopper whose dispenser still turns."},{"p":"five","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"Calibrations","u":"en/five/dispenser-sensor.html#calibrations","x":"Rotation — for “Rotation speed control”: Calibrate the dispenser rotation.\nMass — for “Weight control”; repeat it whenever the product changes: Calibrate the dispenser by weight.\nThe status screen shows the shaft pulse frequency F= in Hz, to three decimals. Its alarms: Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms, and with weight control Dosing by weight."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate the dispenser rotation","h":"","u":"en/five/calibrate-dispenser-rotation.html","x":"Turn the drive wheel and the dispenser shaft together so that the panel learns how many shaft pulses belong to the wheel pulses."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate the dispenser rotation","h":"","u":"en/five/calibrate-dispenser-rotation.html","x":"This calibration teaches the panel how many dispenser shaft pulses belong to the wheel pulses, so that it can tell a stopped or slipping shaft from a moving machine. It is the basis of “Rotation speed control” of the dispenser sensor (Dispenser shaft sensor). It writes “Wheel, pulses” and “Dispenser pulses”.\nBefore you start: the speed sensor and every dispenser taking part must be under control and answering. You need the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level). Have everything ready to turn the wheel and the dispenser shafts: once started, the calibration runs until the panel has its result.\nOpen “Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters”. Tap “Dispenser calibration” and swipe right or left to the number of the dispenser — or to “ALL” for all of them at once.\nTap “Dispenser calibration” again. The panel shows “Calibration” and a progress bar.\nThe progress bar of the dispenser calibration.\nTurn the drive wheel and the dispenser shaft together — by hand, or by moving the machine. The bar fills as the counters rise.\nKeep turning until the bar reaches 100 %. That happens when the speed sensor and every dispenser taking part have each given at least 100 pulses. The bar follows whichever is furthest behind — a dispenser that does not turn holds it at zero.\nAt 100 % the panel beeps once and shows “Stop rotation!”. Stop everything.\nThe panel waits until no counter has changed for two seconds, then writes the result and shows “Done”.\nLet the calibration run to the end. If any sensor taking part is out of control when you start, or stops answering on the way, the calibration ends with “Sensor not controlled! Calibration canceled!” or “Connection lost with the sensor! Calibration canceled!”, and nothing is written.\nNOTE\nThe stored numbers are not the ones you watched. If the counts do not fit the parameter range, the panel halves both numbers together, again and again, until they fit. The ratio is kept; only the two stored figures come out smaller than what was counted."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate the dispenser by weight","h":"","u":"en/five/calibrate-dispenser-weight.html","x":"Turn a ground-driven dispenser into a container and weigh the product, so that the panel can watch the rate in kilograms per hectare."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate the dispenser by weight","h":"","u":"en/five/calibrate-dispenser-weight.html","x":"This calibration stores how many grams the dispenser delivers per shaft pulse — “Weight, gr” and “Weight, pulses” of the dispenser sensor. Repeat it whenever the product changes. It is done standing, at the “Operator” level; the panel raises itself to “Agronomist” for the length of the calibration and puts the level back on exit.\nYou need: a container that holds 2 to 60 kg of the product, and scales.\nThe item is only there when “Weight control” is switched on for this dispenser (Dispenser shaft sensor). Every step is a tap on a button of the screen.\nTouch and hold the metering unit icon on the home screen. Tap “Dispenser calibration(weight)”, and tap it again. The first screen lists the target rates of the products in use, in kg/ha; the product of this dispenser is marked.\nThe first screen of the calibration by weight.\nTap “Calibrate dispenser N”. If the dispenser cannot be calibrated, a red line stands there instead with the reason: “Not controlled”, “No connection” or “FAILURE”. “Exit” leaves.\nThe second screen gives the preparation. Weigh the empty container and write the weight down.\nFill the hopper with the product and place the empty container under the dispenser.\nTurn the rotor until its cells are filled. This matters more than it looks: a rotor with empty cells gives short measure for the first turns, and that error lands in the stored figure.\nTap “Go to calibration”. “Cancel” returns to the first screen instead. The third screen counts “Impulses” and turns a rotor icon as they arrive.\nEmpty the container and place it under the dispenser again.\nTurn the dispenser rotor and fill the container as far as possible — not less than 2 kg and not more than 60 kg.\nTap “Complete”. The button appears only after more than ten pulses. “Cancel” asks “Are you sure?” before throwing the measurement away.\nWeigh the filled container. The number pad asks to “Enter the difference of the filled and empty container in grams”. Type the weight of the product alone — 2000 to 60000 g; the pad offers 10000 — and tap “OK”. The panel stores “Weight, gr” and “Weight, pulses”.\nA weight typed outside 2000 … 60000 g is not refused: the panel takes the nearest limit. Weigh carefully and stay inside the range.\nNOTE\nA dispenser alarm during the calibration does not stop it — only a lost connection does. The measurement is mechanical, and the panel keeps out of the way until the container is weighed. A dispenser that is already in alarm cannot start a calibration."},{"p":"five","t":"Dispenser electric motor","h":"","u":"en/five/dispenser-motor.html","x":"The parameters of an electric metering motor — holes in the disk, gear ratio, direction, jam rollback, product, calibration figures, speed window and calibration button — with ranges, defaults and when each is shown."},{"p":"five","t":"Dispenser electric motor","h":"","u":"en/five/dispenser-motor.html","x":"An electric motor drives a dispenser at the speed the panel works out from the target rate and the travel speed. Touch and hold its tile on the Motors screen or on the second home screen to open its context menu (Sensor settings).\nThe context menu of a metering motor, top part.\nWhich items you see depends on the mode of its group: a motor of a group in the “Precise” mode drives a seed disk; in “Flow” or “Hybrid” it meters a product by weight.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\nLevel\n“Holes in disk”\nThe number of holes in the seed disk. “Precise” mode only.\n1 … 200; 27\nOperator\n“Numerator” / “Denominator”\nTogether: the gear ratio between the motor and the dispenser shaft.\n1 … 500 each; 10 / 10\nAgronomist\n“Rotation direction”\nThe direction the motor turns.\n“Forward” / “Backward”; “Forward”\nAgronomist\n“Start delay,mS”\nThe shortest time, in milliseconds, between the starts of two motors, so that they do not all start at once.\n0 … 500, steps of 5; 30\nService\n“Section control D.I.”\nAn input of the controller that shuts the section off: “Shut off(0)” when the input reads 0, “Shut off(1)” when it reads 1.\n“No” / “Shut off(0)” / “Shut off(1)”; “No”\nAgronomist\n“Rollback when jammed”\n“Yes” — when the rotor jams, the motor turns backwards for a moment instead of only stopping.\n“No” / “Yes”; “No”\nAgronomist\n“Group start address”\nSet at commissioning — see Configure the motor controllers.\n—\nAgronomist\nIn the “Flow” and “Hybrid” modes the menu also holds:\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\nLevel\n“Dispenser calibration(weight)”\nStarts the calibration by weight. Shown only while the machine stands still. Calibrate a metering motor by weight\n—\nOperator\n“Type of product”\nWhich of the four products this motor meters.\n“Product 1” … “Product 4”\nAgronomist\n“Weight, gr” / “Rotations”\nBoth set by the calibration by weight. Number pad.\n1 … 65535 each; 10000 / 100\nAgronomist\n“Minimum, rpm” / “Maximum, rpm”\nThe shaft speed window the rate control may use, with one decimal place. Number pad.\n0.1 … 500.0 each; 0.1 / 500.0\nAgronomist\n“Button input”\nThe input of the motor controller used as a calibration button; “No” — none.\n“No”, 1 … 3; “No”\nAgronomist\n“Activation signal”\nWhich state of that input counts as pressed.\n0 / 1; 1\nAgronomist\n“Minimum, rpm” and “Maximum, rpm” are not a safety limit but the window the rate control works in. The speed window shown on the calibration screen is worked out from them together with the roller figures. “Button input” and “Activation signal” let one person run the motor from the dispenser during the calibration — see Calibrate a metering motor by weight.\nThe status screen shows “Working” or “Faulty” and the last fault code (Read the fault code of an electric motor). Alarms: Metering motor alarms, Dosing by weight. The controller itself is set up in Configure the motor controllers."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate a metering motor by weight","h":"","u":"en/five/calibrate-motor-weight.html","x":"Let the electric motor turn the metering roller into a container and weigh the product, so that the panel knows how much one turn delivers."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate a metering motor by weight","h":"","u":"en/five/calibrate-motor-weight.html","x":"This calibration stores how much product one turn of the metering roller delivers — “Weight, gr” and “Rotations” of one motor. Repeat it whenever the product or the roller changes. It is done at the “Operator” level, on a motor whose group is in the “Flow” or “Hybrid” mode.\nYou need: the machine standing still, a container that holds 1 to 60 kg of the product, and scales.\nIMPORTANT\nThe item appears only while the machine is standing still. In motion the panel removes it from the context menu without a message. And while the calibration is open, every electric motor on the machine — seed and fertilizer — is held at zero speed: nothing meters. Leaving the calibration returns them all to automatic rate control.\nTouch and hold the tile of the motor on the Motors screen or the second home screen. Tap “Dispenser calibration(weight)”, and tap it again.\nThe first screen shows the target rates and, under “Product dosage speed limitation:”, the “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” the current settings allow. If it reads “Can not provide dosage!” in red, the roller fitted cannot deliver the rate at any speed — calibration will not repair that; fit another roller. Otherwise tap “Calibrate electric motor N”. “Exit” leaves.\nThe first screen of the motor calibration.\nThe second screen prepares the rotor. Weigh the empty container and write the weight down. Fill the hopper and place the empty container under the dispenser.\nTap “Fill the rotor cells”. The motor turns the driven shaft two turns and stops by itself. “Stop motor” interrupts it. Repeat until the cells are full.\nTap “Go to calibration”. The third screen shows “Rotations” to one decimal.\nEmpty the container and place it under the dispenser again.\nTap “Calibration”: the motor runs. Tap “Stop motor” to stop it. Run and stop as often as needed — the counter keeps a running total. Fill the container as far as possible, not less than 1 kg and not more than 60 kg.\nTap “Complete”. It appears once more than one full turn has been made. “Cancel” asks “Are you sure?” before throwing the measurement away.\nWeigh the filled container. The number pad asks for the difference of the filled and empty container in grams — 1000 to 60000 g; the pad offers 10000. Type it and tap “OK”. The panel stores “Weight, gr” and “Rotations” for this motor.\nDuring the rotor filling the motor runs at the middle of its “Minimum, rpm” … “Maximum, rpm” window, but not faster than 60 rpm."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate a metering motor by weight","h":"Run the motor from a button at the dispenser","u":"en/five/calibrate-motor-weight.html#run-the-motor-from-a-button-at-the-dispenser","x":"If “Button input” of the motor is set to an input (1 … 3) and “Activation signal” matches the button (Dispenser electric motor), a button on that input runs the motor on the third screen: the motor runs while the button is held and stops when it is released. One person can stand at the dispenser with the container instead of walking to the cab between runs. With such a button set up, the motor runs only while the button is held. The screen gives no hint that a button is set up."},{"p":"five","t":"Calibrate a metering motor by weight","h":"When the calibration stops by itself","u":"en/five/calibrate-motor-weight.html#when-the-calibration-stops-by-itself","x":"The calibration ends with a failure if the motor stops answering, if the motor raises an alarm, or if ten seconds pass without pulses from the rotation sensor — then the panel shows “No data from rotation sensor”. A jammed or unplugged rotation sensor ends the run rather than quietly storing zero."},{"p":"five","t":"Fan sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/fan-sensor.html","x":"The parameters of the fan speed sensor — the allowed speed band and the pulses per turn — with ranges and defaults."},{"p":"five","t":"Fan sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/fan-sensor.html","x":"The fan sensor watches the speed of the fan of a pneumatic drill or planter. Touch and hold the fan icon on the home screen to open its context menu (Sensor settings).\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\nLevel\n“Minimum, rpm”\nBelow it the panel raises “Low speed of rotation”.\n500 … 9000, steps of 500; 3000\nOperator\n“Maximum, rpm”\nAbove it the panel raises “High speed of rotation”.\n500 … 9000, steps of 500; 5000\nOperator\n“Pulses”\nHow many pulses one turn of the fan gives.\n1 … 60; 2\nAgronomist\nThe context menu of a fan sensor at the Agronomist level.\nThe home screen shows the fan speed in rpm beside the icon; the status screen shows the pulse frequency F= in Hz. Alarms: Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms."},{"p":"five","t":"Pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/pressure-sensor.html","x":"The parameters of the air pressure sensor — the smoothing filter and the allowed pressure band — with ranges, defaults and when the band is watched."},{"p":"five","t":"Pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/pressure-sensor.html","x":"The pressure sensor measures the positive pressure or the vacuum of the air system. Touch and hold the pressure icon on the home screen to open its context menu (Sensor settings).\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\nLevel\n“Filter”\nHow strongly the sensor smooths its reading.\n1 … 16; 1\nAgronomist\n“Minimum, Pa”\nThe lower limit of the allowed band. “No” (0) switches the low-pressure check off.\n0 … 10000, steps of 100; “No”\nOperator\n“Maximum, Pa”\nThe upper limit of the allowed band. “No” (0) switches the high-pressure check off.\n0 … 10000, steps of 100; “No”\nOperator\nThe band is watched only while the machine seeds, or moves with the implement in work. Both limits apply to the size of the pressure or vacuum, whichever it is. The limits are set in pascals; the home screen and the status screen show the pressure P= in kilopascals (1 kPa = 1000 Pa).\nAlarms: Air pressure. Giving a new pressure sensor its address: Configure a pressure sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Marker sensors","h":"","u":"en/five/marker-sensors.html","x":"The parameters of the marker sensors — which marker a sensor watches and which input state means \"lowered\"."},{"p":"five","t":"Marker sensors","h":"","u":"en/five/marker-sensors.html","x":"The marker sensors tell the panel which marker is down. The profile declares “No”, “One” or “Both” (The profile). Touch and hold the marker icon on the home screen to open the context menu of a marker sensor (Sensor settings).\nParameter\nWhat it does\nValues, default\nLevel\n“Connected marker”\nWhich marker the single sensor watches. Hidden when the profile has both markers.\n“Left” / “Right”; “Left”\nAgronomist\n“Signal \"down\"”\nWhich state of the input means the marker is lowered.\n0 / 1; 0\nAgronomist\nThe status screen of a marker sensor shows only the common lines. On a machine that makes tramlines, the change of the marker position ends a bout — see Set up the tramline rhythm.\nNOTE\nThe marker sensors are wired to the tramline actuator controller. One such controller carries the actuator and both marker sensors — see Configure a tramline actuator controller."},{"p":"five","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"","u":"en/five/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html","x":"The settings of the two-state sensors — hopper level, work position of the implement, and the five sensors of the fan-drive engine."},{"p":"five","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"","u":"en/five/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html","x":"These sensors report one of two states and nothing more. Touch and hold the icon of any of them on the home screen to open its context menu (Sensor settings). Their status screen shows Pin: and the level on the input, \"0\" or \"1\"."},{"p":"five","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"Hopper sensor","u":"en/five/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html#hopper-sensor","x":"The hopper sensor has no parameters of its own — its context menu holds only the common items.\nAlarm: Hopper empty."},{"p":"five","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"Work position sensor","u":"en/five/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html#work-position-sensor","x":"The work position sensor tells the panel whether the implement is lowered for work or raised for transport. The status screen shows “Position: Working” or “Position: Transporting”.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nValues, default\nLevel\n“Transporting position signal”\nWhich state of the input means the transport position.\n0 / 1; 0\nAgronomist\n“Alarms criteria”\n“Yes” — while the implement is raised, the section alarms are not watched. “No” — the position does not affect the alarms. Hidden, and always “Yes”, on a machine with dispenser motors or a pump.\n“No” / “Yes”; “Yes”\nAgronomist\nOn a machine without markers that makes tramlines, raising the implement ends a bout — see Set up the tramline rhythm."},{"p":"five","t":"Hopper, work position and engine sensors","h":"Engine sensors","u":"en/five/hopper-position-engine-sensors.html#engine-sensors","x":"“No battery charge”, “Emergency oil pressure”, “Ignition”, “Low fuel” and “Air filter” are five separate inputs, each switched on in the profile on its own (The profile).\nThe context menu of the oil pressure sensor.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange, default\nLevel\n“Activation signal”\nWhich state of the input is the one worth reporting.\n0 / 1; 1\nAgronomist\n“Signal delay, sec”\nHow long that state must hold before it counts.\n1 … 30; 15 s for the fuel level, 5 s for the air filter, 1 s for the others\nAgronomist\nOil pressure and battery charge are watched only while the ignition is on; fuel level and air filter always. Alarms: Tractor engine alarms."},{"p":"five","t":"Tramline actuator","h":"","u":"en/five/tramline-actuator.html","x":"The two parameters of the tramline actuator, where the rest of its settings live, and what its status screen shows."},{"p":"five","t":"Tramline actuator","h":"","u":"en/five/tramline-actuator.html","x":"The tramline actuator shuts the sections of the tramline bout. It has two parameters of its own, in its context menu — touch and hold its tile on the Motors screen (Sensor settings):\nParameter\nValues\nLevel\n“Cutoff position”\n“Pulled out” / “Pulled in” — the position of the actuator in which its sections are shut off. Default “Pulled out”.\nAgronomist\n“Switch”\n“when seeding” / “always” — when the actuator is switched. Default “when seeding”.\nAgronomist\nThe rest of its settings live elsewhere:\nWhat\nWhere\nThe address and the marker channels of its controller\nConfigure a tramline actuator controller\nThe rhythm — which bout shuts which actuator\nSet up the tramline rhythm\nWhich sections belong to the actuator\n“Relation of sensors to actuators” — Seeding machine parameters\nThe status screen of the actuator shows Status: Pulled in, Pulled out, Working while the shutter is moving, or Unknown. Alarms: Tramline alarms.\nNOTE\nOne actuator controller carries three things: the actuator itself and both marker sensors of the bout. That is why a machine with markers and a tramline needs only one such controller."},{"p":"five","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"","u":"en/five/device-configuration.html","x":"What device configuration is, the preparation it needs, and the three-item pattern every configuration menu follows."},{"p":"five","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"","u":"en/five/device-configuration.html","x":"When a system is commissioned, or a sensor is replaced, each new device must be given its address — the number by which the panel finds it on the sensor network. That is done in “Service” → “Sensors Configuration”, one device at a time.\nWARNING\nFor authorised service engineers. A device given the wrong address disappears from the panel or takes the place of another one. Follow these topics or the instructions of an Agriwelt GmbH engineer, and do not try menu items at random."},{"p":"five","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"Prepare","u":"en/five/device-configuration.html#prepare","x":"Disconnect every sensor from the network connector of the panel.\nConnect only the one device that is to be given a new address.\nSwitch the panel on. Alarms about lost communication appear — close them all with “Close” (How alarms work).\nRaise the access level to “Agronomist” — see Enable the Agronomist level.\nTap the home screen with two fingers, then tap “Service” twice and “Sensors Configuration” twice. The menu offers four kinds of device: “Seed sensor”, “Pressure sensor”, “Concentrator-8” and “Tramline actuator”.\nThe Sensors Configuration menu."},{"p":"five","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"The pattern","u":"en/five/device-configuration.html#the-pattern","x":"All four menus work the same way:\nItem\nWhat it is\n“Sensor address” / “Device address”\nWhich device the menu talks to. Leave it at “AUTO” and the panel talks to the single device that answers. Tapping the selected item again starts a search; each further tap steps to the next device found.\n“New address”\nThe address the device is to take. Set it with a swipe right or left.\n“Set”\nWrites it. Tap it, and tap it again. “Done” means the device has accepted it.\nWhat “Set” can answer:\nMessage\nMeaning\n“Done”\nThe device has taken the new address.\n“More than one device in network!”\nWith “AUTO”, more than one device answers. Disconnect all but one.\n“No connection”\nNo device answers. Check the cable and the power of the device.\n“Address: … is used!”\nThe address you chose belongs to another device already. Choose another.\nCAUTION\nThe search on the address item is not a harmless look. It talks to the devices on the network. Use it only with a single device on the cable, never on an assembled machine. With an address typed by hand instead of “AUTO”, the panel writes to whatever answers on that address.\nAfterwards, every time: disconnect the device and connect it again, return to the home screen and check that it appears where it should. Then set the level back to “Operator” — or switch the panel off and on, which does the same."},{"p":"five","t":"Device configuration — before you start","h":"The devices","u":"en/five/device-configuration.html#the-devices","x":"Device\nTopic\nSeed sensor\nConfigure a seed sensor\nPressure sensor\nConfigure a pressure sensor\nHUB8 input device — “Concentrator-8” on the screen\nConfigure a HUB8\nTramline actuator controller\nConfigure a tramline actuator controller\nElectric motor controllers — their own way in\nConfigure the motor controllers"},{"p":"five","t":"Configure a seed sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-seed-sensor.html","x":"Give a new or replaced seed sensor the number of its row."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure a seed sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-seed-sensor.html","x":"For a seed sensor the new address is simply the number of the row — the same number the operator sees under the column on the home screen. Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this sensor on the cable, “Agronomist” level.\nOpen “Service” → “Sensors Configuration” → “Seed sensor” — tap each item twice. The menu has three items: “Sensor address”, “New address” and “Set”.\nThe Seed sensor configuration menu.\nLeave “Sensor address” at “AUTO”.\nTap “New address” and swipe right or left to the row number.\nTap “Set”, and tap it again. The panel answers “Done” and, below it, “Found #” with the number the sensor had until now.\nThe panel confirms with “Done”. The number after “Found #” is left out of the picture.\nDisconnect the sensor and connect it again. On the home screen, check that it appears under its row.\nIf the panel answers “More than one device in network!”, another device is still on the cable. If it answers “No connection”, the sensor does not answer — check its cable and power.\nWhen all sensors are done, set the level back to “Operator”, or switch the panel off and on. The parameters of the seed sensors: Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure a pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-pressure-sensor.html","x":"Give a new or replaced air pressure sensor its address."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure a pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-pressure-sensor.html","x":"Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this sensor on the cable, “Agronomist” level. The menu has the same three items as every configuration menu. The address values are not printed in this manual: take them from the data sheet of the system.\nOpen “Service” → “Sensors Configuration” → “Pressure sensor” — tap each item twice. The menu shows “Sensor address”, “New address” and “Set”.\nLeave “Sensor address” at “AUTO”.\nTap “New address”. The panel proposes the value for the first pressure sensor by itself; each further sensor takes the next one. Check the value against the data sheet and correct it with a swipe right or left if needed.\nTap “Set”, and tap it again. The panel answers “Done”.\nDisconnect the sensor and connect it again. On the home screen, check that its indicator appears.\nWhen all sensors are done, set the level back to “Operator”, or switch the panel off and on. Its parameters: Pressure sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure a HUB8","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-hub8.html","x":"Tell a HUB8 input device — \"Concentrator-8\" on the screen — what is wired to each of its eight channels."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure a HUB8","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-hub8.html","x":"The HUB8 carries several devices at once. On the panel it is called “Concentrator-8”. Its menu is longer than the others: after the device address come its eight channels, each with a type and a number. Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this HUB8 on the cable, “Agronomist” level.\nItem\nWhat it is\n“Device address”\nWhich HUB8 the menu talks to. Leave it at “AUTO”.\n“Channel 1 type” … “Channel 8 type”\nWhat is wired to the channel: “Seed sensors” or “Input device”.\n“Channel 1 address” … “Channel 8 address”\nWhich device of that kind it is: for a seed sensor, the number of its row; for an input device, the value from the data sheet of the system.\n“Set”\nWrites all channels to the HUB8.\nThe Concentrator-8 menu with its first channels.\nThe type and the number together make the device appear on the home screen in the right place.\nOpen “Service” → “Sensors Configuration” → “Concentrator-8” — tap each item twice.\nLeave “Device address” at “AUTO”.\nFor each channel that carries a device, tap its type item — “Channel 1 type” for channel 1 — and swipe to the type, then tap its address item — “Channel 1 address” — and swipe to the number. Leave a channel that carries nothing as it is.\nSwipe up to “Set” at the end of the menu, tap it, and tap it again. The panel answers “Done”.\nDisconnect the HUB8 and connect it again. On the home screen, check that each device appears in its place.\nTwo channels of the same type cannot have the same number: “Set” then refuses with “Invalid profile!” and writes nothing.\nWhen all devices are done, set the level back to “Operator”, or switch the panel off and on."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure a tramline actuator controller","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-tramline-actuator.html","x":"Tell an actuator controller which tramline actuator it drives and which marker sensors are wired to it."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure a tramline actuator controller","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-tramline-actuator.html","x":"The actuator controller carries three things at once — the tramline actuator and the two marker sensors of the bout — and each has its own line. That is why one controller serves an actuator and both markers. Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this controller on the cable, “Agronomist” level.\nItem\nWhat it is\n“Device address”\nWhich controller the menu talks to. Leave it at “AUTO”.\n“Actuator”\nThe number of the tramline actuator this controller drives, or “No” if it drives none.\n“Input device, channel 1”\nThe first marker sensor, or “No”.\n“Input device, channel 2”\nThe second marker sensor, or “No”.\n“Set”\nWrites the three lines to the controller.\nThe configuration menu of a tramline actuator controller, all lines still at “No”.\nOpen “Service” → “Sensors Configuration” → “Tramline actuator” — tap each item twice.\nLeave “Device address” at “AUTO”.\nSet “Actuator”, “Input device, channel 1” and “Input device, channel 2”: tap each line and swipe right or left to its value.\nTap “Set”, and tap it again. The panel answers “Done”.\nDisconnect the controller and connect it again. On the home screen, check that the actuator and the markers appear.\n“Set” refuses with “Invalid profile!”, and writes nothing, when all three lines are at “No”, or when both marker channels carry the same number.\nWhen all devices are done, set the level back to “Operator”, or switch the panel off and on. The actuator's own settings: Tramline actuator; the markers: Marker sensors."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-motor-controllers.html","x":"Give a metering motor controller its address, choose the kind of controller, and set its parameters."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"","u":"en/five/configure-motor-controllers.html","x":"The controllers that drive the metering motors are not addressed from “Sensors Configuration”. They have their own way in, their own choice of controller kind and their own parameters.\nWARNING\nFor authorised service engineers. As with every device on the cable: one controller connected at a time. The panel writes to whichever controller answers."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"Open the motor controller menu","u":"en/five/configure-motor-controllers.html#open-the-motor-controller-menu","x":"Raise the level to “Agronomist” (Enable the Agronomist level).\nTap the home screen with two fingers, tap “Service” twice, then “Electric motors” twice. The diagnostic screen of the controllers opens on motor 1. The same screen opens from “Electric motors” in the context menu of a motor.\nThe Electric motors screen of motor 1, page “Registers”.\nTap the screen with two fingers. Two items appear: “Change address” and “Configuration”.\nThe menu of the Electric motors screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"Change the address","u":"en/five/configure-motor-controllers.html#change-the-address","x":"Tap “Change address”, and tap it again. Three items open.\n“Motor address” — the address the controller answers on now. Leave it as found.\n“New address” — the address it is to take. Take it from the data sheet of the system; the values are not printed in this manual.\nTap “Set”, and tap it again, to write it."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"Choose the controller kind","u":"en/five/configure-motor-controllers.html#choose-the-controller-kind","x":"“Configuration” opens the choice of controller kind, offered by number. The panel reads the kind back from the controller and opens on the one that answers. Choosing another kind by hand is how a replacement board is set up — not a way to change what a motor is.\nKind\nWhat it is\n“1)universal”\nThe universal controller: two DC channels or one stepper channel on the same board.\n“2)DC motor”\nA DC motor controller.\n“3)STEPPER fw0.006+”\nA stepper controller, firmware 0.006 and later.\n“4)BGxxCI”\nA BG45CI motor with the controller built in, on the older board.\nIf the controller that answers does not suit the menu, the panel says so: “Wrong controller type!”, “Old firmware version!” or “Wrong controller work mode!”."},{"p":"five","t":"Configure the motor controllers","h":"Set the parameters","u":"en/five/configure-motor-controllers.html#set-the-parameters","x":"Every configuration menu ends with “Set”, and “Set” carries a value of its own — the address group: “Motor address” (this controller only), “ALL”, “All-Seeding” or “All-Fertilizer”. Nothing reaches the controller until “Set” is entered — the items above it change the numbers the panel holds, not those the controller runs on. “Set” answers “Done”, “FAILURE” or “No connection”; a group that has no motors in the profile is answered with “EM of dispensers is missing in profile”.\nKind\nWhat it is given\nSTEPPER\n“Pulses” 1 … 8192 — the pulses of one turn of the motor shaft; “Motor type” from a list; “Set”.\nBGxxCI\n“Pulses” 1 … 8192; “Set”. Nothing else — the controller carries the rest.\nDC motor\n“Pulses”, “Motor type”, the regulator terms “PID kP”, “PID kI”, “PID kD” (0 … 65535, read as hundredths), “TURBINA” 0/1, and two speed–time pairs “TIME1” / “SPEED1”, “TIME2” / “SPEED2”; “Set”.\nuniversal\nThe largest set, in three menus: “Work mode”, “Inputs setup” and “Parameters”.\nThe “Parameters” menu of the universal controller:\nParameter\nRange\n“Motor type”\n0 … 255, from the controller's own table\n“Pulses”\n1 … 8192 per shaft turn\n“Acceleration,Ms” / “Deceleration,Ms”\n50 … 65535 — the ramp up and down\n“Minimum, rpm” / “Maximum, rpm”\n0 … 65535 — the shaft speed window\n“PID kP”, “PID kI”, “PID kD”\n0 … 65535, hundredths\n“!! Temp,*C”\n0 … 200 — the temperature at which the controller alarms\n“I_peak,A” / “I_peak,mS”\nthe peak current, in tenths of an ampere, and how long it may be drawn\n“I_push,A” / “I_push,Sec”\nthe breakaway current and its time\n“I_hilo,A” / “I_hilo,Sec”\nthe sustained-current threshold and its time\n“Set”\nwrites all of it, at the chosen address group\nIMPORTANT\nThe current and temperature limits protect the motor. They are the controller's own protection thresholds: set them from the data sheet of the motor, not by trying values until the alarms stop.\nThe four kind names and the items “PID kP”, “PID kI”, “PID kD”, “TURBINA”, “TIME1”, “SPEED1”, “TIME2”, “SPEED2”, “!! Temp,*C”, “I_peak,A”, “I_push,A”, “I_hilo,A” and their time partners read the same in every language of the panel.\nReading what the controller reports: Motor controller diagnostics."},{"p":"five","t":"Motor controller diagnostics","h":"","u":"en/five/motor-controller-diagnostics.html","x":"How to read what a metering motor controller reports about itself on the Electric motors screen — speed, error code, voltages, temperature, current, status bits and fault counters — and how to move through it by touch."},{"p":"five","t":"Motor controller diagnostics","h":"","u":"en/five/motor-controller-diagnostics.html","x":"The Electric motors screen shows, live, what a motor controller reports about itself. Open it with a two-finger tap on the home screen → “Service” → “Electric motors”, or with “Electric motors” in the context menu of a motor — both at the “Agronomist” level. The working view of the motors is a different screen: Motors screen.\n“Mode” — tap it to switch the page: “Registers”, then “Statistics”.\n“Motor #” with a number — which motor is shown, here motor 1. Swipe right for the next number, left for the previous one.\n“Registers” — the name of the page. A universal controller has two register pages, “Registers-1” and “Registers-2”.\nThe status word — sixteen bits of the controller; a lit (yellow) cell is a bit that is set.\nThe legend of the bits — the number and the name of each bit. Table below.\n“Speed” / “Target speed” — what the shaft is doing against what the panel asked for. A gap that does not close is the first sign of a load the motor cannot carry.\n“Error code” / “Act.posit.” — the controller's own error number (codes), and the position it counts itself at.\n“Volt.mot.,mV” / “Volt.elec.,mV” — the voltage at the motor and at the electronics, in millivolts. A motor voltage far below the electronics voltage means the supply sags under load.\n“Temperat.,C” / “Current,mA” — the controller's temperature and the current it draws. Compare the current with “I_hilo,A” set for this motor (Configure the motor controllers).\nThe “Registers” page of a motor, standing.\nGesture\nWhat it does\nTap “Mode”\nNext page.\nSwipe right / left\nNext / previous controller number. The numbers run from 1 to 127 and go round; a number without a controller shows nothing useful.\nTwo-finger tap\nThe menu: “Change address”, “Configuration” — see Configure the motor controllers.\nThree-finger tap, or long swipe up\nClose the screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Motor controller diagnostics","h":"The status bits","u":"en/five/motor-controller-diagnostics.html#the-status-bits","x":"Bit\nName\nBit\nName\n0\nEnable\n8\nToggle\n1\nError\n9\nCmdTgl\n2\nWarn.\n10\nCmdErr\n3\nMoving\n11\nStpHalt\n4\nReach\n12\nLimCur\n5\nLimit\n13\nLimVel\n6\nFolErr\n14\nLimPos\n7\nHomDone\n15\nLimSVel\nThe legend is drawn in English on every panel, whatever its language: it is a bit map of the controller, not a message to the operator."},{"p":"five","t":"Motor controller diagnostics","h":"The fault counters","u":"en/five/motor-controller-diagnostics.html#the-fault-counters","x":"The Statistics page of a motor.\nThe “Statistics” page shows the faults this motor has collected on the active field — they are part of the field statistics, not of the motor:\nCounter\nWhat it counts\n“Jammed”\nThe rotor stalled and the controller had to give up or back off.\n“Disconnects”\nThe controller stopped answering.\n“Other”\nEverything else the controller reported.\n“In failure”\nThe distance in metres this motor spent in a fault state.\nEach counter is white at zero and red once it is not. A red figure does not mean the motor is faulty now — it means it was, somewhere on this field, and the seed placed there is worth a look. To read another field, make it the active field first (Statistics screen)."},{"p":"five","t":"The Service menu","h":"","u":"en/five/service-menu.html","x":"Every item of the \"Service\" menu, its access level, and which ones an engineer should leave alone."},{"p":"five","t":"The Service menu","h":"","u":"en/five/service-menu.html","x":"Tap the home screen with two fingers, then tap “Service” twice. The menu itself is shown from the “Agronomist” level up; which items you see depends on the level (Access levels).\nThe Service menu at the Service level.\nItem\nLevel\nWhat it is\n“Profile”\nAgronomist\nWhich devices the machine has, and how many. The profile\n“Sensors Configuration”\nAgronomist\nGiving devices their address. Device configuration\n“Registers”\nAgronomist\nA raw register view. Not part of any procedure.\n“Electric motors”\nAgronomist\nThe diagnostic screen and the configuration of the metering motor controllers. Configure the motor controllers\n“Serial number”\nService\nThe serial number of the panel, entered once at manufacture.\n“Imitation mode”\nService\n“No” / “Yes” — the panel works from simulated data. See the warning below.\n“Count seeds always”\nAgronomist\n“No” / “Yes” — “Yes” shows the seed counters in the “Flow” and “Hybrid” modes too; otherwise they are shown in the “Precise” mode only.\n“Enter key”\nAgronomist\nEnters the licence key that takes the panel out of the demonstration state. Shown only while the panel is in that state.\n“Set trial”\nService\nStarts the demonstration period.\n“Trial, hours”\nService\n1 … 999 — how long that period lasts.\nFour further items — the tracker protocol, two switches for logging to the memory card and the display type — exist in the firmware but are set never to be shown. They cannot be reached at any level.\nWARNING\n“Imitation mode” is not a test aid to be left on. With it on, the panel works from figures it makes up: every number on every screen and every entry in the statistics is invented. The clock slot of the home screen shows IMITAT in yellow while it is on. Switch it off before the machine goes to work — and check it if a panel reports a plausible field it never sowed.\n“Imitation mode” is shown in English whatever language the panel is set to."},{"p":"five","t":"How alarms work","h":"","u":"en/five/how-alarms-work.html","x":"When the Hectare 5 raises an alarm, what the alarm window shows, how it is closed by touch, which faults sound without a window, and how to use the alarm topics of this manual."},{"p":"five","t":"How alarms work","h":"","u":"en/five/how-alarms-work.html","x":"An alarm is a window that opens over the working screen, with the alarm sound. It tells you that one device — a sensor, an actuator, a motor controller — or the system as a whole has found a fault. The alarm topics in this section are grouped by the device that raises the alarm. Look for the words you see on the screen, not for a description of them."},{"p":"five","t":"How alarms work","h":"When the panel raises an alarm","u":"en/five/how-alarms-work.html#when-the-panel-raises-an-alarm","x":"A fault is not reported the moment it appears. The condition must hold without a break for the time set in “Alarm delay” before the alarm window opens.\nSetting\nValue\nWhere it is set\n“Settings” menu, item “Alarm delay”, at the “Operator” level. See The Settings menu.\nRange\n2 to 20 seconds, in steps of 1 second.\nDefault\n3 seconds. A value outside the range is reset to the default by itself.\nA short delay makes the panel react quickly, but also to brief disturbances — a single bump, a momentary gap in the seed flow. A long delay keeps the screen quiet, but a real fault can run for up to twenty seconds before you are told.\nTIP\nIf the system alarms too often on a rough field, raise “Alarm delay” first. Change the sensor settings only when a longer delay does not help.\nTwo cases do not follow the delay:\nLost communication is counted in polls, not in seconds. “Connection lost” appears after three polls in a row without an answer from the device.\nRight after a sensor has been set up, its alarms are not processed for a short blocking period. This prevents a burst of alarms while a device is being configured."},{"p":"five","t":"How alarms work","h":"The alarm window","u":"en/five/how-alarms-work.html#the-alarm-window","x":"“FAILURE!” — the title of every alarm window, on a red bar, whatever the fault. It is not the fault itself.\nThe alarm text — the fault. Look it up under this text in the alarm topics.\nThe kind of device that raises the alarm — here a hopper sensor. The same text can come from different devices; this line tells you which.\nThe number of the device — here hopper 2. When several devices of the same kind raise the same alarm, they share one window and are listed together, for example #3-5,8.\n“Close” — closes this alarm. Below.\nThe alarm window of an empty hopper.\nAn over-current alarm of a motor adds a line with the current the controller measured, in amperes. The alarms of the panel itself — for example “User is Agronomist!” — have no device line; their text starts with the line “HOME - close message”. They are closed like every other alarm, with “Close”."},{"p":"five","t":"How alarms work","h":"Closing an alarm","u":"en/five/how-alarms-work.html#closing-an-alarm","x":"Tap “Close” in the window. This alarm closes and the sound stops. If more alarms are waiting, the next one opens. Nothing else closes an alarm window — taps elsewhere, swipes and the three-finger tap are ignored while it is open.\nClosing a window does not repair the fault. The alarms of a device that are still active are counted on its status screen — see The sensor status screen."},{"p":"five","t":"How alarms work","h":"Alarms are hidden at the higher access levels","u":"en/five/how-alarms-work.html#alarms-are-hidden-at-the-higher-access-levels","x":"At the “Agronomist” and “Service” levels no alarm windows are shown — apart from the one warning that says so, User is Agronomist — and the faults without a window stay silent too. The alarms are not lost: they wait. When you return to “Operator”, the panel drops the ones that have gone away and shows the ones that are still true, one after another. See Access levels."},{"p":"five","t":"How alarms work","h":"Alarms without a window","u":"en/five/how-alarms-work.html#alarms-without-a-window","x":"Two faults are not shown as a window, because you have to see the working screen while you correct them. At the “Operator” level the alarm sound plays while they last, as long as no alarm window is open:\n“SLOWER” or “FASTER” — the travel speed is outside the limits set for the speed sensor. The speed field shows the word. See Indicators.\n“NOT UNDER CONTROL” — rows run under the control line of their diagram. See The section diagram."},{"p":"five","t":"How alarms work","h":"How the alarm topics are written","u":"en/five/how-alarms-work.html#how-the-alarm-topics-are-written","x":"Every alarm has its own heading with the text exactly as on the screen; where the message has two lines on the panel, both lines are given. Under it: what the panel has detected, and what to check, in the order a mechanic checks it — the quick checks on the panel first, then the machine, then the device itself.\nNOTE\nIf the checks do not clear the fault, call the service support."},{"p":"five","t":"Any device: connection lost or wrong device type","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-any-device.html","x":"The two alarms any sensor, actuator, controller or input device can raise — no answer from the device, or the wrong kind of device answering."},{"p":"five","t":"Any device: connection lost or wrong device type","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-any-device.html","x":"These two alarms are not tied to one kind of device. The name of the device in the alarm window tells you which one is affected. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Any device: connection lost or wrong device type","h":"“Connection lost”","u":"en/five/alarms-any-device.html#connection-lost","x":"What it means. The panel has received no answer from the device in three polls in a row. This applies to sensors, actuators, controllers and input devices alike.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe device is connected to the network.\nThe cable and the connector of the device.\nThe power supply of the device. It must meet the values in the device passport.\nThe device is meant to be there: a device in the profile that is not fitted raises this alarm too. Leave such a device out of control (Take a sensor into or out of control)."},{"p":"five","t":"Any device: connection lost or wrong device type","h":"“Wrong device type!”","u":"en/five/alarms-any-device.html#wrong-device-type","x":"What it means. A device of another kind answers where the panel expects a device of this kind. The cause is either a wrong setting in the system profile or a wrong configuration of the device itself.\nWhat to do: call the service support. Do not try to correct it by changing the profile or the device configuration at random — see Device configuration.\nSEE ALSO\nThe status screen of a device shows the same two states as “No connection” and “Wrong device type!” in red — see The sensor status screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-rotation.html","x":"What to do when the fan or the dispenser shaft turns too slowly, too fast, or not at all."},{"p":"five","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-rotation.html","x":"The fan sensor and the dispenser shaft sensor use the same two messages. The device name in the alarm window tells you which one it is. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"“Low speed of rotation” / “High speed of rotation”","u":"en/five/alarms-rotation.html#low-speed-of-rotation-high-speed-of-rotation","x":"What it means. The fan or the dispenser shaft turns slower or faster than allowed. For the fan the limits are “Minimum, rpm” and “Maximum, rpm”; for the dispenser shaft the panel compares it with the speed the travel speed asks for, within “Maximum error, %”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe component being watched — the fan or the dispenser and its drive.\nThe settings of the sensor: for the fan “Minimum, rpm”, “Maximum, rpm” and “Pulses” — see Fan sensor; for the dispenser — see Dispenser shaft sensor.\nDispenser only: the travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThe sensor itself is serviceable.\nDispenser only: run the calibration — see Calibrate the dispenser rotation."},{"p":"five","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"“No rotation”","u":"en/five/alarms-rotation.html#no-rotation","x":"What it means. The dispenser shaft does not turn while the machine seeds or moves. Only the dispenser shaft sensor raises this alarm. The shaft counts as stopped when no pulse has come for “Period, s”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe dispenser and its drive.\nThe settings of the sensor, above all “Period, s” — see Dispenser shaft sensor.\nThe sensor itself is serviceable."},{"p":"five","t":"Hopper empty","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-hopper.html","x":"What to do when the panel reports that a hopper is running empty."},{"p":"five","t":"Hopper empty","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-hopper.html","x":"The hopper sensor sits in the hopper at a fixed height. It reports when the product falls below it. On the home screen the hopper icon turns from full green to empty red. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Hopper empty","h":"“Hopper empty”","u":"en/five/alarms-hopper.html#hopper-empty","x":"What it means. The level of the product in the hopper is very low. The hopper needs refilling.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nFill the hopper.\nIf the product stands above the height at which the sensor is mounted and the alarm stays, check that the sensor is serviceable. Its status screen shows the level on its input as \"0\" or \"1\" — see The sensor status screen.\nThe hopper sensor has no settings of its own beyond the common ones — see Hopper, work position and engine sensors."},{"p":"five","t":"Air pressure too high or too low","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-pressure.html","x":"What to do when the pressure sensor of the air system reports a pressure or vacuum outside the allowed range."},{"p":"five","t":"Air pressure too high or too low","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-pressure.html","x":"The pressure sensor measures the positive pressure or the vacuum of the air system. Both alarms look at the size of the pressure or vacuum, whichever it is, and only while the machine seeds or moves with the implement in work. The limits are described in Pressure sensor. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Air pressure too high or too low","h":"“High pressure”","u":"en/five/alarms-pressure.html#high-pressure","x":"What it means. The measured pressure or vacuum is greater than “Maximum, Pa”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nReduce the speed of the fan or turbine.\nThe position of the regulating damper of the air intake, if there is one.\nThe tube to the pressure sensor is connected correctly, and the measuring line is free of blockages."},{"p":"five","t":"Air pressure too high or too low","h":"“Low pressure”","u":"en/five/alarms-pressure.html#low-pressure","x":"What it means. The measured pressure or vacuum is less than “Minimum, Pa”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nIncrease the speed of the fan or turbine.\nThe position of the regulating damper of the air intake, if there is one.\nThe air duct for leaks.\nThe tube to the pressure sensor is connected correctly, and the measuring line is free of blockages.\nNOTE\nThe liquid fertilizer pump controller has its own “High pressure” alarm for the liquid line. It is a different alarm — see Liquid fertilizer pump."},{"p":"five","t":"Application rate too low or too high","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-rate.html","x":"What to do when the seed, dry fertilizer or liquid fertilizer sensors report a rate below or above the allowed band, and what NOT UNDER CONTROL means."},{"p":"five","t":"Application rate too low or too high","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-rate.html","x":"The section sensors — seed, dry fertilizer and liquid fertilizer — compare the rate they measure with the target. How the window works: How alarms work.\nMode of the group\n“Under allowable rate”\n“Over allowable rate”\n“Flow”\nThe flow falls below the alarm level — the control line of the diagram.\nNot raised.\n“Hybrid”, “Precise”\nThe rate falls below the target by more than “Rate error,%”.\nThe rate rises above the target by more than “Rate error,%”.\nTIP\nCheck the travel speed first. Every rate per metre or per hectare is worked out from the distance travelled. If the speed on the screen is wrong, every rate is wrong by the same amount, and the sensors are not to blame."},{"p":"five","t":"Application rate too low or too high","h":"“Under allowable rate”","u":"en/five/alarms-rate.html#under-allowable-rate","x":"What it means. A section sensor — seed, dry fertilizer or liquid fertilizer — has measured too little product during work.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThere is enough product in the hoppers or in the tank.\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nSeed or dry fertilizer sensor: “Fraction”. The value may have to be reduced. See Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors.\nLiquid fertilizer sensor: “Pulses per liter”. The value may have to be reduced. See Liquid fertilizer.\nSeed sensor: its status on the status screen (“Information” in its context menu) — see The sensor status screen.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?"},{"p":"five","t":"Application rate too low or too high","h":"“Over allowable rate”","u":"en/five/alarms-rate.html#over-allowable-rate","x":"What it means. A section sensor — seed, dry fertilizer or liquid fertilizer — has measured too much product during work.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nSeed or dry fertilizer sensor: “Fraction”. The value may have to be increased.\nLiquid fertilizer sensor: “Pulses per liter”. The value may have to be increased.\nSeed sensor: its status on the status screen.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?"},{"p":"five","t":"Application rate too low or too high","h":"“NOT UNDER CONTROL” — no window","u":"en/five/alarms-rate.html#not-under-control-no-window","x":"When rows run under the control line, the home screen shows “NOT UNDER CONTROL” in red and, at the “Operator” level, the alarm sound plays — but no alarm window opens. The rows under the line are not guarded.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThe alarm level of the diagram: is it above the rate the machine really applies? Lower it, or let the panel set it — Set the alarm level.\nThere is enough product in the hoppers or in the tank.\n“Fraction” of the seed or fertilizer sensors, or “Pulses per liter” of the liquid sensors — the value may have to be reduced.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?\nWhat the line means: The section diagram."},{"p":"five","t":"Too many doubles or skips","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-seeding-quality.html","x":"What to do when a seed sensor of a precision planter counts more doubles or skips than allowed."},{"p":"five","t":"Too many doubles or skips","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-seeding-quality.html","x":"Doubles and skips are counted only in the “Precise” mode of seeding — on a precision planter. Each alarm is raised only if its limit is set: “Doubles error,%” and “Skips error,%” at “No” switch it off. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Too many doubles or skips","h":"“High % of doubles”","u":"en/five/alarms-seeding-quality.html#high-of-doubles","x":"What it means. The share of doubles the sensor counts is higher than “Doubles error,%”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\n“Doubles error,%” in the settings of the seed sensors — is the limit sensible? See Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors.\n“Fraction” in the settings of the seed sensors. The value may have to be increased.\nThe sensor's status on its status screen — see The sensor status screen.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?"},{"p":"five","t":"Too many doubles or skips","h":"“High % of skips”","u":"en/five/alarms-seeding-quality.html#high-of-skips","x":"What it means. The share of skips the sensor counts is higher than “Skips error,%”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThere is enough seed in the hoppers.\n“Skips error,%” in the settings of the seed sensors — is the limit sensible?\n“Fraction” in the settings of the seed sensors. The value may have to be reduced.\nThe sensor's status on its status screen.\nThe metering unit: is it serviceable, and is it adjusted correctly?\nThe stretch over which the shares are counted is “Recalculation,m” of the seed sensors. How the skips and doubles look on the diagram: The section diagram."},{"p":"five","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-tramline.html","x":"What to do when the tramline actuator jams, its position sensors contradict each other, or a shut-off section still delivers product."},{"p":"five","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-tramline.html","x":"The tramline actuator shuts the sections of the tramline bout. Two gate position sensors tell the panel where its shutter stands. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"“Stall”","u":"en/five/alarms-tramline.html#stall","x":"What it means. The tramline actuator has jammed.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe gate position sensors of the actuator are serviceable.\nThe mechanism of the tramline actuator is serviceable."},{"p":"five","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"“Illegal state”","u":"en/five/alarms-tramline.html#illegal-state","x":"What it means. The actuator reports a position that cannot be: both gate position sensors have triggered at the same time. With a serviceable installation this cannot happen.\nWhat to check: the gate position sensors are serviceable."},{"p":"five","t":"Tramline: actuator stalled or product in the tramline","h":"“Seeding to tramline”","u":"en/five/alarms-tramline.html#seeding-to-tramline","x":"What it means. A section that should be shut off for the tramline still delivers too much product. The seed sensor, the dry fertilizer sensor or the liquid fertilizer sensor of that section has measured it in the tramline control mode.\nSeed sensor or dry fertilizer sensor — check, in this order:\nThe metering unit and the flow cut-off mechanism are serviceable.\n“Toler.sowing to tramline,%” in the settings of the group of section sensors. The value may have to be increased. See Seed sensors and dry fertilizer sensors.\n“Fraction” in the settings of the group of the alarming sensor. The value may have to be increased.\nThe sensor's status on its status screen — see The sensor status screen.\nLiquid fertilizer sensor — check, in this order:\nThe metering unit and the flow cut-off mechanism are serviceable.\n“Tolerable leak to tramline,%” in the settings of the group of section sensors. The value may have to be increased. See Liquid fertilizer.\nA sensor whose “Product application” is set to “No” is watched the same way — as a shut-off row.\nSEE ALSO\nWhich sections belong to which actuator is set in “Relation of sensors to actuators” — Seeding machine parameters. The rhythm: Set up the tramline rhythm."},{"p":"five","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-sensor-state.html","x":"What to do when an optical seed or fertilizer sensor reports a blocked channel, too much light, a fault of its own, or outdated firmware."},{"p":"five","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-sensor-state.html","x":"The seed and dry fertilizer sensors count the product optically: an emitter shines across the channel, a receiver sees each grain. These alarms report the state of the sensor itself, not the rate. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"“Clogged”","u":"en/five/alarms-sensor-state.html#clogged","x":"What it means. The seed or dry fertilizer sensor has detected that its optical channel is blocked.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nRemove the blockage.\nCheck for a build-up of dust in the channel.\nCheck the power supply of the sensor. It must meet the values in the device passport."},{"p":"five","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"“Light struck”","u":"en/five/alarms-sensor-state.html#light-struck","x":"What it means. The seed or fertilizer sensor has detected an extremely high level of light in its optical channel.\nWhat to do: keep direct sunlight out of the optical channel of the sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"“Broken”","u":"en/five/alarms-sensor-state.html#broken","x":"What it means. The seed or dry fertilizer sensor has detected a fault of its own. It may have to be replaced. Its status screen shows “Faulty”.\nWhat to do: check the power supply of the sensor. It must meet the values in the device passport. If the power supply is in order, the sensor may have to be replaced — call the service support."},{"p":"five","t":"Seed or fertilizer sensor clogged, light-struck or broken","h":"“Old firmware! Wrong S&D calc!”","u":"en/five/alarms-sensor-state.html#old-firmware-wrong-sd-calc","x":"What it means. A dry fertilizer sensor with outdated firmware is in use while the fertilizers are in the “Precise” mode. It counts, but its skips and doubles cannot be trusted.\nWhat to do: call the service support to have the firmware of the sensor updated — or set “Mode of fertilizers” to “Flow” or “Hybrid” (Seeding machine parameters).\nSEE ALSO\nThe status screen of the sensor shows the same states — “Clogged”, “Light struck”, “Faulty” — in red: The sensor status screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-tractor.html","x":"What to do when one of the engine sensors — oil pressure, battery charge, fuel level, air filter — raises an alarm."},{"p":"five","t":"Engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-tractor.html","x":"All engine sensors share one alarm text: “FAILURE”. The name of the sensor under the text tells you which one has triggered. Oil pressure and battery charge are watched only while the ignition is on; fuel level and air filter always. How the window works: How alarms work. The settings named below are described in Hopper, work position and engine sensors."},{"p":"five","t":"Engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“FAILURE” — “Emergency oil pressure”","u":"en/five/alarms-tractor.html#failure-emergency-oil-pressure","x":"What it means. The low oil pressure sensor has triggered.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe low oil pressure sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” in the settings of the low oil pressure sensor.\nIf an ignition sensor is in the profile: is its control switched on?\nIf an ignition sensor is in the profile: is it serviceable?"},{"p":"five","t":"Engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“FAILURE” — “No battery charge”","u":"en/five/alarms-tractor.html#failure-no-battery-charge","x":"What it means. The battery is not being charged.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe battery charge sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” in the settings of the battery charge sensor.\nIf an ignition sensor is in the profile: is its control switched on?\nIf an ignition sensor is in the profile: is it serviceable?"},{"p":"five","t":"Engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“FAILURE” — “Low fuel”","u":"en/five/alarms-tractor.html#failure-low-fuel","x":"What it means. Little fuel is left in the tank.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe fuel level sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” in the settings of the fuel level sensor.\n“Signal delay, sec” in the settings of the fuel level sensor. The default is 15 seconds."},{"p":"five","t":"Engine: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“FAILURE” — “Air filter”","u":"en/five/alarms-tractor.html#failure-air-filter","x":"What it means. The air filter is clogged.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe air filter sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” in the settings of the air filter sensor.\n“Signal delay, sec” in the settings of the air filter sensor. The default is 5 seconds.\nThe ignition sensor itself never raises an alarm window: a yellow key on the home screen only says the ignition is off (Indicators)."},{"p":"five","t":"Product rate by weight too low, too high, or impossible","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html","x":"What to do when a product dosed by weight — by an electric motor or a weight-controlled dispenser — misses its target rate, or cannot reach it at all."},{"p":"five","t":"Product rate by weight too low, too high, or impossible","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html","x":"These alarms are raised by the system as a whole, not by one device. They exist for each of the four products separately: the number in the message is the number of the product. How far a product may stand from its target is “Product rate error,%” in the “Settings” menu (The Settings menu). How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Product rate by weight too low, too high, or impossible","h":"“Rate of Product 1” / “is to low!” — or — “is to high!”","u":"en/five/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html#rate-of-product-1-is-to-low-or-is-to-high","x":"The same for Product 2, 3 and 4. The panel writes the message in this spelling.\nWhat it means. The product is applied below or above its target rate in kilograms per hectare by more than “Product rate error,%”.\nProduct metered by an electric motor — check, in this order:\nThe target rate of the product.\nThe speed limits in force, on the status screen of the speed sensor — see The sensor status screen.\nThe settings of the electric motor that drives the dispenser shaft — see Dispenser electric motor.\nThe metering unit.\nThe rotation sensor of the electric motor is serviceable.\nThe working load of the motor. Below 20 % or above 80 %, try a metering roller of another size: a larger one if too little is applied, a smaller one if too much.\nCalibrate the metering roller — see Calibrate a metering motor by weight.\nDispenser sensor with “Weight control” switched on — check, in this order:\nThe target rate of the product.\nThe settings of the dispenser shaft sensor — see Dispenser shaft sensor.\nThe metering unit.\nThe dispenser sensor is serviceable.\nCalibrate the dispenser — see Calibrate the dispenser by weight."},{"p":"five","t":"Product rate by weight too low, too high, or impossible","h":"“Product dosage is” / “impossible! Check product” / “rate and calibration!”","u":"en/five/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html#product-dosage-is-impossible-check-product-rate-and-calibration","x":"What it means. With the current settings and target rate, the product cannot be applied by weight at all: the lowest speed at which the metering rollers can deliver the rate is already at or above the highest. On the home screen a red product icon blinks in the right-hand column.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe target rates of the products — one product asking too much closes the window for all of them.\n“Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” of the speed sensor — see Travel speed sensor.\nThe settings of the electric motors that apply the products by weight, above all “Minimum, rpm” and “Maximum, rpm”.\nCalibrate the metering rollers of the electric motors.\nIf that does not help: fit metering rollers of another size.\nTIP\nThe calibration screen of a metering motor shows the speed window the current roller allows. If it reads “Can not provide dosage!”, no calibration will help — the roller does not suit the rate. See Calibrate a metering motor by weight."},{"p":"five","t":"User is Agronomist: alarms will not be shown","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-access-level.html","x":"Why the panel warns that alarms are hidden, and how to get them back by touch."},{"p":"five","t":"User is Agronomist: alarms will not be shown","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-access-level.html","x":"How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"User is Agronomist: alarms will not be shown","h":"“User is Agronomist!” / “Alarms will not be shown!”","u":"en/five/alarms-access-level.html#user-is-agronomist-alarms-will-not-be-shown","x":"What it means. Seeding has started while the “Agronomist” or “Service” access level is set. At these levels the panel does not show alarm windows; this warning is the only exception. It appears once for the whole time you work at that level. Like every alarm of the panel itself, its text starts with the line “HOME - close message”; close it with “Close”.\nThe warning window, with the Close button.\nWhat to do: set the access level back to “Operator”:\nTap “Close” in the warning.\nTap the home screen with two fingers, then tap “Settings” twice.\nSwipe up to “User” and tap it. Swipe left until it reads “Operator”.\nTap “User” again. Going down a level needs no PIN.\nSwitching the panel off and on does the same: after every power-on the panel starts at “Operator”.\nNOTE\nExpect a short run of alarms on the way back. The alarms raised while the level was high have waited. The panel drops those that have gone away and shows the ones that are still true. It is the backlog, not a new fault. See Access levels."},{"p":"five","t":"Travel speed sensor failed, speed limits contradict, or poor GPS signal","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-speed-sensor.html","x":"What to do when one of two speed sensors stops giving pulses, when the minimum and maximum travel speed are set the wrong way round, or when the built-in receiver sees too few satellites."},{"p":"five","t":"Travel speed sensor failed, speed limits contradict, or poor GPS signal","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-speed-sensor.html","x":"How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Travel speed sensor failed, speed limits contradict, or poor GPS signal","h":"“Speed sensor #1 failed” / “Speed sensor #2 failed”","u":"en/five/alarms-speed-sensor.html#speed-sensor-1-failed-speed-sensor-2-failed","x":"What it means. The profile says the machine has two travel speed sensors, but the panel sees movement from only one of them. The number names the silent sensor. This alarm cannot appear on a machine with one speed sensor.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nCheck that both speed sensors are serviceable, and repair what you find.\nIf one sensor cannot be repaired now: change the system profile so that only one speed sensor is used — see The profile."},{"p":"five","t":"Travel speed sensor failed, speed limits contradict, or poor GPS signal","h":"“Error in settings of Min/Max”","u":"en/five/alarms-speed-sensor.html#error-in-settings-of-minmax","x":"What it means. The minimum and the maximum travel speed set for the speed sensor contradict each other. The panel cannot decide whether the current speed is allowed.\nWhat to do: check “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” in the settings of the speed sensor. The minimum must be below the maximum. See Travel speed sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Travel speed sensor failed, speed limits contradict, or poor GPS signal","h":"“Poor GPS signal quality”","u":"en/five/alarms-speed-sensor.html#poor-gps-signal-quality","x":"What it means. The speed is taken from the receiver built into the panel (“GPS internal” is “Yes”), and it sees fewer than four satellites. The speed it gives cannot be trusted. The satellite symbol on the home screen is red.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nGive the panel a clear view of the sky; move off from under trees, buildings or a shed roof.\nWatch the number beside the satellite symbol: it shows how many satellites the receiver uses. Wait until it reaches four or more.\nIf the reception stays poor, take the speed from the wheel sensor: set “GPS internal” to “No” — see GPS and Travel speed sensor.\nNOTE\n“SLOWER” and “FASTER” are not alarm windows. When the speed leaves the permitted range, the speed field shows “SLOWER” or “FASTER” and, at the “Operator” level, the alarm sound plays until you correct the speed. See Indicators."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html","x":"What to do when the controller of a dispenser electric motor reports a jam, a short circuit, over-current, a wrong supply voltage, overheating, an unknown fault or a stuck firmware update."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html","x":"These alarms come from the controller that drives an electric motor of a dispenser. The name in the alarm window tells you which motor; the Motors screen shows which tile is in trouble (Motors screen). How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Motor jammed!”","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html#motor-jammed","x":"What it means. The controller does not see rotation while the motor should be running.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe metering unit is serviceable and turns freely.\nThe rotation sensor of the motor is serviceable, and so is its connection to the controller — the cable and the connector.\nIf jams are frequent, “Rollback when jammed” lets the motor back off and try again — see Dispenser electric motor."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Short circuit”","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html#short-circuit","x":"What it means. The controller has detected a short circuit.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe cable connection: the cables and the connector are intact.\nReplace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Over current”","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html#over-current","x":"What it means. The current has gone above the limit. The window adds a line with the current the controller measured, in amperes.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe motor and the seeding mechanism for jamming.\nThe cable connection: the cables and the connector are intact.\nReplace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Under voltage”","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html#under-voltage","x":"What it means. The supply voltage of the motor is below the allowed value. The motor cannot run.\nMeasure the supply voltage first.\nIf it is too low:\nThe alternator is serviceable.\nThe tension of the alternator belts.\nThe power harness is connected firmly to the battery or the alternator terminals, as the required supply voltage demands.\nThe cable connections make reliable electrical contact.\nIf the supply voltage is in order: replace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Over voltage”","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html#over-voltage","x":"What it means. The supply voltage of the motor is above the allowed value. The motor cannot run.\nMeasure the supply voltage first.\nIf it is too high:\nThe alternator is serviceable.\nThe power harness is connected firmly to the battery or the alternator terminals, as the required supply voltage demands.\nThe cable connections make reliable electrical contact.\nIf the supply voltage is in order: replace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Over temperature”","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html#over-temperature","x":"What it means. The motor controller has overheated.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nLet the motor cool down.\nReplace the controller with a serviceable one to test it."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“FAILURE”","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html#failure","x":"What it means. The controller has detected an unusual fault that needs the service support.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nRead the fault code: touch and hold the motor tile → “Information” → line “Last fault:”. See Read the fault code of an electric motor.\nReplace the controller with a serviceable one to test it.\nGive the code and the number of the motor to the service support."},{"p":"five","t":"Metering motor: jammed, electrical faults, bootloader","h":"“Device in bootloader!”","u":"en/five/alarms-motor-controller.html#device-in-bootloader","x":"What it means. The controller has stayed in its firmware update mode and is not running its working program. It cannot drive the motor.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nSwitch the power off and on.\nIf the message returns, the firmware update of the controller was not completed. Call the service support.\nSEE ALSO\nThe liquid fertilizer pump controller raises the same electrical alarms — Liquid fertilizer pump."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-pump-controller.html","x":"What to do when the liquid fertilizer pump controller reports too much pressure, a failed pressure sensor, or a rate it cannot hold."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"","u":"en/five/alarms-pump-controller.html","x":"The pump controller is built on the same electronics as the controller of a metering motor. Besides the alarms on this page it raises the electrical alarms of the metering motor controller — “Short circuit”, “Over current”, “Under voltage”, “Over voltage”, “Over temperature”, “FAILURE” and “Device in bootloader!” — with the same meaning and the same actions: see Metering motor alarms. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"“High pressure”","u":"en/five/alarms-pump-controller.html#high-pressure","x":"What it means. The measured liquid pressure has reached “Max pressure, Bar”.\nWhat to do, in this order:\nReduce the travel speed.\nIn the settings of the pump controller, check “Max pressure, Bar” and, if the line allows it, increase it — see Liquid fertilizer."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"“Pressure sensor failure!”","u":"en/five/alarms-pump-controller.html#pressure-sensor-failure","x":"What it means. The signal from the pressure sensor has fallen well below its 4–20 mA working range.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe connection of the sensor to the controller — the cable and the connector are intact.\n“Pressure sensor” in the settings of the pump controller names the input the sensor is really wired to.\nThe sensor is serviceable. If it has failed, replace it.\nThe status screen of the pump controller shows the pressure and the raw sensor signal in mA — see The sensor status screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"“Rate low”","u":"en/five/alarms-pump-controller.html#rate-low","x":"What it means. The pump controller cannot reach the set rate: the actual rate is below the target by more than “Product rate error,%”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed and is reasonably steady.\nThe working load of the pump. Above 90 %: drive more slowly.\nLoad under 90 %, or jumping strongly: increase “Product rate error,%” in the “Settings” menu, at the “Agronomist” level — see The Settings menu.\nThe pump is serviceable."},{"p":"five","t":"Liquid fertilizer pump: pressure and rate alarms","h":"“Rate high”","u":"en/five/alarms-pump-controller.html#rate-high","x":"What it means. The pump controller applies more than the set rate: the actual rate is above the target by more than “Product rate error,%”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe travel speed on the panel matches the real speed and is reasonably steady.\nThe working load of the pump. Under 10 %: drive faster.\nLoad above 10 %, or jumping strongly: increase “Product rate error,%” in the “Settings” menu.\nThe pump is serviceable."},{"p":"five","t":"Read the fault code of an electric motor","h":"","u":"en/five/motor-fault-codes.html","x":"Where the panel shows the numeric fault code of a metering motor controller, and what each code means."},{"p":"five","t":"Read the fault code of an electric motor","h":"","u":"en/five/motor-fault-codes.html","x":"When a metering motor raises “FAILURE”, its controller also records a numeric code. Read it out and give it to the service support together with the number of the motor.\nLong swipe left on the home screen until the Motors screen appears (Working screens). Touch and hold the tile of the faulty motor; its context menu opens. A motor tile on the second home screen works the same way.\nTap “Information”, and tap it again. The status screen of the motor opens.\nRead the line “Last fault:”. The status of the motor reads “Faulty” while any of its alarms is active.\nThe same code is shown as “Error code” on the Electric motors screen, at the “Agronomist” level — see Motor controller diagnostics."},{"p":"five","t":"Read the fault code of an electric motor","h":"The codes","u":"en/five/motor-fault-codes.html#the-codes","x":"The panel shows only the number. The descriptions come from the motor controller itself.\nCommand and parameter errors\nThe controller refused something the panel asked of it.\nCode\nDescription\n-250\nInvalid command\n-251\nUnexpected command\n-252\nInvalid command syntax\n-253\nInvalid command arguments\n-260\nInvalid number of arguments\n-261\nSpecified index is greater than FFFFh\n-262\nSpecified sub-index is greater than FFh\n-300\nParameter does not exist\n-301\nMemory write operation failed\n-302\nRestore operation failed\n-306\nParameter cannot be written\n-307\nParameter cannot be read\n-308\nParameter value out of range\n-316\nVersion of the stored parameter set does not match the firmware version\n-318\nStored parameter set has an invalid check sum\n-322\nParameter cannot be set: the axis is in motion\n-325\nParameter cannot be set: the positioning mode is inactive\n-340\nParameter cannot be set: a limit switch is active\n-343\nParameter cannot be set: a stop or halt command is being executed\n-350\nParameter not contained in the specified index\n-351\nParameter not contained in the specified sub-index\nDevice errors\nThe controller or the motor itself is in trouble.\nCode\nDescription\n-1000\nLow supply voltage\n-1001\nExcessive supply voltage\n-1002\nLow electronics supply voltage\n-1003\nExcessive electronics supply voltage\n-1020\nOvertemperature of the device\n-1021\nOvertemperature of the heat sink\n-1060\nShort circuit of a motor lead: between motor leads, motor and ground, or motor and power supply\n-1061\nMotor overcurrent\n-4000\nFollowing-difference error in the motion sequence\n-5100\nLimit switch active"},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html","x":"What each touch gesture does — on the home screen, the second home screen, the working screens, in menus, on the number pad and in windows."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html","x":"The Hectare 5 has no buttons; every action is a gesture on the screen. Each screen listens only to its own gestures and ignores the rest. With “Buttons sound” set to “Yes”, every gesture the screen takes is confirmed with a click."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Kinds of touch","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#kinds-of-touch","x":"Gesture\nHow\nTap\nOne finger, touch and lift within half a second.\nTouch and hold\nOne finger kept on one spot for more than half a second. The panel reacts while the finger is still down.\nDouble tap\nTwo quick taps with one finger.\nTwo-finger tap\nOne tap with two fingers at once.\nThree-finger tap\nOne tap with three fingers at once.\nSwipe\nA short stroke of one finger across the screen.\nLong swipe\nA stroke of one finger across more than a third of the screen.\nTwo-finger swipe, long two-finger swipe\nThe same with two fingers."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Home screen","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#home-screen","x":"Gesture\nWhat it does\nTwo-finger tap\nOpens the main menu.\nTouch and hold a device — icon or column\nOpens its context menu.\nTouch and hold a diagram, then swipe up or down\nLights the diagram's target and changes it, one step per swipe.\nTap outside the diagrams\nEnds the change of a target.\nTouch and hold a kg/ha indicator\nOpens the number pad for that product's target rate.\nTouch and hold an empty place of the right-hand column\nLocks or unlocks product application. One short beep confirms. Machines with dispenser motors or a pump only.\nDouble tap\nSets the alarm level from the rate being applied. Only while applying.\nLong swipe left\nTable (Statistics, if there is no Table).\nLong swipe right\nSecond home screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Second home screen","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#second-home-screen","x":"Gesture\nWhat it does\nTap the settings symbol\nOpens the main menu.\nTap the GPS symbol\nOpens the GPS screen; a long swipe up leaves it.\nTap a red or green chevron\nTakes a motor out of or into control.\nTap the unit value\nShows the next unit — fan, pressure, pump.\nTouch and hold the rate, then swipe up or down\nChanges the seeding rate.\nTouch and hold the stop symbol\nLocks or unlocks product application.\nTouch and hold a motor, the speed or a unit value\nOpens its context menu.\nLong swipe left\nBack to the home screen."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Table","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#table","x":"Gesture\nWhat it does\nLong two-finger swipe right or left\nNext or previous table.\nSwipe up or down\nScroll the lines.\nTwo-finger tap\nAsks “Zero readings?”.\nLong swipe left\nStatistics. There is no gesture back."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Statistics","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#statistics","x":"Gesture\nWhat it does\nSwipe up or down\nScroll the report, one line per swipe.\nTwo-finger swipe right or left\nShow the next or the previous field.\nTwo-finger tap\nMenu of the screen.\nLong swipe left\nMotors, or the home screen if there is none. There is no gesture back."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Motors","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#motors","x":"Gesture\nWhat it does\nLong swipe left\nHome screen.\nLong swipe right\nStatistics.\nTouch and hold a tile\nContext menu of that motor or actuator."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Status screen of a device","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#status-screen-of-a-device","x":"Opened with “Information” in a context menu.\nGesture\nWhat it does\nSwipe right or left\nThe next or the previous device.\nSwipe up, or three-finger tap\nBack."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Tramline rhythm form","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#tramline-rhythm-form","x":"Gesture\nWhat it does\nSwipe up or down\nMove between lines.\nSwipe right or left\nMove along a line; on “Rhythm”, step through the rhythms.\nTwo-finger tap\nOpens the selected cell.\nTouch and hold\nSaves the rhythm without leaving.\nThree-finger tap\nSaves the rhythm and returns to the Tramline menu."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"In a menu","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#in-a-menu","x":"Gesture\nWhat it does\nTap an item\nSelects it.\nTap the selected item again\nEnters it: opens its menu, runs its action, or opens the number pad.\nTouch and hold an item\nSelects and enters it at once.\nSwipe right or left\nRaises or lowers the value of the selected item by one step. For a value in square brackets, opens the number pad.\nSwipe up or down\nScrolls the list.\nTap outside the menu, or three-finger tap\nLeaves the menu."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Number pad","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#number-pad","x":"Touch\nWhat it does\nTap the digits\nType the value.\nTap “OK”\nAccept the value.\nTap outside the pad\nLeave without changing anything.\nTouch and hold the backspace\nClear the value."},{"p":"five","t":"Touch zones and gestures on every screen","h":"Windows","u":"en/five/buttons-and-gestures.html#windows","x":"Window\nTouch\nQuestion — blue strip, buttons\nTap “Yes” — confirm. Tap “No” — refuse. Tap outside the window — cancel.\nInformation — blue or red strip, no buttons\nCloses by itself after about two seconds, or with a tap inside the window.\nAlarm — red bar “FAILURE!”\nTap “Close” — close this alarm. No other touch works."},{"p":"five","t":"Terms","h":"","u":"en/five/terms.html","x":"The words this manual uses for gestures, rates, control, sensors, tramlines and devices — what each means on the Hectare 5."},{"p":"five","t":"Terms","h":"Gestures","u":"en/five/terms.html#gestures","x":"Term\nMeaning\nTap\nOne finger, touch and lift at once.\nTouch and hold\nOne finger kept on one spot for more than half a second.\nLong swipe\nA stroke of one finger across more than a third of the screen. It moves between the working screens.\nTwo-finger tap\nOne tap with two fingers at once. On the home screen it opens the main menu.\nAll gestures: Touch zones and gestures."},{"p":"five","t":"Terms","h":"Rates and control","u":"en/five/terms.html#rates-and-control","x":"Term\nMeaning\nRate\nThe target intensity the operator sets. The panel compares the actual intensity against it and reports a deviation that is too large.\nRate error\nHow far the actual intensity may stand from the rate before that becomes an alarm. Set per sensor group, in per cent (“Rate error,%”). For products by weight and the pump the setting is “Product rate error,%”.\nProduct application control\nThe panel does not judge the sowing at every moment: it starts watching once the conditions are met — the implement is lowered and moving. Until then a silent section is not a fault.\nAlarm level\nIn the “Flow” mode, the lowest intensity still counted as normal — the control line of the diagram. Below it the section is reported, whatever the neighbours do. See The section diagram.\nPath interval\nThe stretch of the run over which skips, doubles and the other figures are gathered before they are redrawn. Set by “Recalculation,m”, which the panel renames “Recalculation,sec” when the seeding mode counts in seconds instead of metres.\nShare of sowing rows\nThe share of rows that must sow for the panel to decide that the machine is seeding — “Percent of sowing,%”, 20 % by default.\nManual lock\nStopping all product application by hand, with a touch and hold on an empty place of the right-hand column of the home screen. A yellow hand shows the lock is on. See Lock product application by hand.\nControl (of a sensor)\nWhether the panel watches a sensor. A sensor out of control is shown with ! and reports nothing. See Take a sensor into or out of control.\nImitation\nA simulated travel speed, set in the speed sensor, used when the speed sensor has failed. See Work without the speed sensor."},{"p":"five","t":"Terms","h":"Seeding control modes","u":"en/five/terms.html#seeding-control-modes","x":"Mode\nMeaning\n“Precise”\nSeeds per metre, with skips and doubles — row-crop planters.\n“Flow”\nSeeds per second against an alarm level — grain drills.\n“Hybrid”\nSeeds per metre, with a rate error as in “Precise”; the alarm level can be set automatically as in “Flow”."},{"p":"five","t":"Terms","h":"Sensors","u":"en/five/terms.html#sensors","x":"Term\nMeaning\nSection control sensor\nThe common name for a seed, dry fertilizer or liquid fertilizer sensor. One per section of the implement.\nSeed sensor\nCounts the seed passing one section. On a row-crop planter it also separates doubles and skips; on a grain drill it measures intensity only — the seeds arrive too close together to be told apart.\nDry fertilizer sensor\nThe same device, watching a granular fertilizer line.\nLiquid fertilizer sensor\nA flow meter counting the volume through one section.\nSpeed sensor\nMeasures the travel speed. Distance, area and every rate are calculated from it. On the Hectare 5 the speed can also come from the built-in GPS receiver.\nDispenser sensor\nWatches that the dispenser shaft turns.\nFan sensor\nWatches the fan speed against a set minimum and maximum.\nPressure sensor\nWatches positive pressure or vacuum, depending on how it is plumbed.\nHopper sensor\nReports when the product has fallen below the point where the sensor sits.\nWork position sensor\nTells the panel whether the implement is lowered into work or raised for transport.\nMarker sensors\nReport which mechanical marker is down, so that the tramline bout can advance by itself."},{"p":"five","t":"Terms","h":"Tramline","u":"en/five/terms.html#tramline","x":"Term\nMeaning\nTramline\nA driving lane left unsown, made by shutting off one or several sections on chosen bouts.\nRhythm\nThe repeating sequence of bouts, with the bouts marked on which particular sections must not sow. See Set up the tramline rhythm.\nBout\nOne pass of the implement across the field. The rhythm counts them and returns to the first when it runs out.\nTramline actuator\nThe device that shuts the sections off. A machine can have up to four."},{"p":"five","t":"Terms","h":"Devices","u":"en/five/terms.html#devices","x":"Term\nMeaning\nHUB8 (input device; in the menus “Concentrator-8”)\nAn eight-channel hub for sensors that have no controller of their own. It watches them and reports each to the panel as if it were a device.\nElectric motor controller\nDrives one or two dispenser motors, or the liquid fertilizer pump.\nmicroSD\nThe memory card, for reports.\nProfile\nThe list of what the machine carries. The panel draws its screens from it. See Profile."},{"p":"five","t":"Terms","h":"Access levels","u":"en/five/terms.html#access-levels","x":"Level\nWhat it is for\n“Operator”\nThe level the panel starts at after every power-on. Everyday work. Only at this level are alarm windows shown.\n“Agronomist”\nThe commissioning settings, the machine profile and the address set-up of the devices. Needs a PIN.\n“Service”\nThe reset items and the service settings. Used by the service department; needs its own PIN.\nThe level is chosen in “Settings” → “User”. Details: Access levels."},{"p":"five","t":"Before you contact support","h":"","u":"en/five/before-you-contact-us.html","x":"A checklist — what to try, what to write down and which photos to attach — so that your question can be answered at once, and whom to contact first."},{"p":"five","t":"Before you contact support","h":"","u":"en/five/before-you-contact-us.html","x":"Most faults can be found without help. Go through this list first. If you still need help, the notes and photos below let support answer at once instead of asking you again."},{"p":"five","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Checklist","u":"en/five/before-you-contact-us.html#checklist","x":"The check after installation was done, and every step passed.\nYou read the alarm window and its topic: How alarms work. Alarms are shown at the “Operator” level only (Access levels).\nYou went through the topic of that alarm — for example Connection lost or wrong device, Sensor clogged or broken, Speed sensor alarms, Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms, Air pressure, Hopper empty, Motor controller alarms.\nYou did not change the profile or the device configuration to try things out (Device configuration)."},{"p":"five","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Write down","u":"en/five/before-you-contact-us.html#write-down","x":"The exact text of the alarm window, word for word, and the name of the device in it.\nThe firmware version and the serial number: two-finger tap on the home screen, then “About” (Switching on).\nThe machine and the number of rows.\nThe status of the device: on its status screen, “Link:”, Status: and, for a motor, “Last fault:” with its code (The sensor status screen, Motor fault codes).\nWhat was done before the fault — what you changed, mounted, connected or replaced, and when the fault first appeared."},{"p":"five","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Photograph","u":"en/five/before-you-contact-us.html#photograph","x":"The sensor status screen of the device that is wrong.\nThe alarm window.\nHow the devices are connected — the cables and the connectors of the device and of the panel, so that the connections can be followed.\nThe result of the check after installation, if you have it."},{"p":"five","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Whom to contact","u":"en/five/before-you-contact-us.html#whom-to-contact","x":"First your dealer or installation partner. They know your machine and how it was mounted.\nThen Agriwelt: support@agriwelt.com. Send the notes and the photos in the first message."},{"p":"mini","t":"What the Hectare Mini system does","h":"","u":"en/mini/overview.html","x":"The seeding control system with the Hectare Mini panel in one page — what it watches, what it drives, what it records, and who to ask."},{"p":"mini","t":"What the Hectare Mini system does","h":"","u":"en/mini/overview.html","x":"The seeding control system with the Hectare Mini panel is made by Agriwelt GmbH. It watches sowing and fertilizing on seed drills and planters of any make, in real time, and on machines with electric dispenser motors it also drives the metering so that the set rate is applied.\nThe panel sits in the cab: a 320 × 240 colour screen and seven touch buttons around it — see The panel and its seven buttons. Everything else is on the machine: one sensor per seed line, and whatever the machine needs besides."},{"p":"mini","t":"What the Hectare Mini system does","h":"Seed","u":"en/mini/overview.html#seed","x":"Shows the seed flow in every seed line, live, as a column on the section diagram.\nRaises an alarm when the flow in a line falls off or stops, with the number of the line.\nCompares the seeding with the target rate the operator sets (the “Hybrid” and “Precise” modes) and raises an alarm when it deviates more than allowed.\nCounts seeds, doubles and skips at every coulter (the “Precise” mode).\nSplits the treated area into the area sown and the area not sown."},{"p":"mini","t":"What the Hectare Mini system does","h":"Fertilizer","u":"en/mini/overview.html#fertilizer","x":"Shows the flow of dry fertilizer in every line and raises an alarm, by line number, when it falls off or stops.\nWatches liquid fertilizer lines, where the machine has them."},{"p":"mini","t":"What the Hectare Mini system does","h":"The machine around the seed","u":"en/mini/overview.html#the-machine-around-the-seed","x":"Hopper level, dispenser shaft rotation, fan speed, air pressure at the fan outlet, travel speed — each against its own limits.\nOptional: the shaft speed against the travel speed, and the weight of seed applied by the dispensers.\nWorking or transport position of the drill.\nThe sensors of a separate fan-drive engine — oil pressure, battery charge, ignition, fuel level, air filter. They are optional and meant for drill combinations with their own diesel engine."},{"p":"mini","t":"What the Hectare Mini system does","h":"Driving the metering and the tramline","u":"en/mini/overview.html#driving-the-metering-and-the-tramline","x":"On machines with electric dispenser motors: applies up to three products to the rate in kg/ha, or the seed to the rate in seeds per metre — see The rate control screen and The Motors screen.\nMakes tramlines with tramline actuators or by stopping motors — see Set up the tramline rhythm."},{"p":"mini","t":"What the Hectare Mini system does","h":"Speed","u":"en/mini/overview.html#speed","x":"The travel speed comes from a wheel or radar sensor, or from the GPS receiver built into the panel. When GPS is set up, it is used while it has a valid fix, and the sensor takes over by itself when it loses the signal — see GPS reception."},{"p":"mini","t":"What the Hectare Mini system does","h":"Records","u":"en/mini/overview.html#records","x":"Detailed statistics for each field, kept over switch-off — see The Statistics screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"What the Hectare Mini system does","h":"Contact","u":"en/mini/overview.html#contact","x":"Agriwelt GmbH · Hackhofergasse 1 · 1190 Wien, Austria · phone +43 664 156 4910 · info@agriwelt.com · www.agriwelt.com. This is also the address wherever this manual sends you to the technical service.\nSEE ALSO\nQuick start · Commissioning · Terms"},{"p":"mini","t":"The panel and its seven buttons","h":"","u":"en/mini/front-panel.html","x":"What the Hectare Mini panel consists of, how it is mounted, and what each of the seven buttons does with a short and a long press."},{"p":"mini","t":"The panel and its seven buttons","h":"","u":"en/mini/front-panel.html","x":"The Hectare Mini panel has a 320 × 240 colour screen and seven buttons on the frame around it. The buttons are touch areas, not mechanical keys: a short touch is a short press; a touch held for about half a second is a long press.\nPower button. Touch-sensitive, at the top edge of the housing.\nBracket. Lets you mount the panel on a flat surface at any angle, horizontally or vertically. For mounting on a tube, an additional bracket is available.\nThe seven buttons. Three on the left, three on the right, one under the screen — see the table below.\nScreen. It also takes touches in some places, for example the digits of the number pad.\nCable entry. One harness brings the speed sensor, the sensor network and the power supply to the panel.\nThe panel from the front and from the side, and its harness."},{"p":"mini","t":"The panel and its seven buttons","h":"The seven buttons","u":"en/mini/front-panel.html#the-seven-buttons","x":"Button\nShort press\nLong press\n“HOME”\nMoves on to the next working screen. In an alarm window: closes the alarm.\nOn the home screen and the rate control screen: locks or unlocks product application, if the machine has dispenser motors or a liquid fertilizer pump. In an alarm window: closes it and returns to the home screen.\n“BACK”\nLeaves a menu, a window or a mode, one step back. On the home screen: opens the tramline bout menu, if the tramline is on.\nOn the home screen, while seeding: sets the alarm level from the flow being applied — see Set the alarm level.\n“MINUS”\nLowers the selected value. In the sensor setup mode: takes the selected sensor out of control.\n—\n“MENU”\nOpens the menu of the screen, or enters the selected menu item. In the sensor setup mode: opens the context menu of the selected device.\nOn the home screen, the rate control screen and the Motors screen: switches on the sensor setup mode.\n“PLUS”\nRaises the selected value. In the sensor setup mode: takes the selected sensor into control.\n—\n“DOWN”\nSelects the next item, field or device.\n—\n“UP”\nSelects the previous item, field or device.\n—\nNOTE\nHolding a button does not repeat it. One touch is one step, however long you hold it — a held button either does its long-press function or nothing. To change a value by ten steps, touch ten times. Where a value is large, the number pad is quicker.\nIf “Buttons sound” is set to “Yes” in the Settings menu, the panel beeps at every touch it accepts.\nWhat every button does on every screen: Buttons on every screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Switching on and off","h":"","u":"en/mini/switching-on.html","x":"What the Hectare Mini shows after power-on, which screen and access level it starts with, and when it is safe to switch off."},{"p":"mini","t":"Switching on and off","h":"","u":"en/mini/switching-on.html","x":"After the power is switched on, a splash screen with the logo appears. It stays until the panel has found every device connected to it — a few seconds — and then the panel changes to its first working screen.\nThe splash screen with the logo.\nThe logo on the splash screen depends on the supplier of the system and may differ from the picture.\nNOTE\nIf the splash screen stays much longer than usual, the panel is waiting for a device that does not answer. Look at the alarms as soon as the working screen appears — see How alarms work."},{"p":"mini","t":"Switching on and off","h":"What state the panel starts in","u":"en/mini/switching-on.html#what-state-the-panel-starts-in","x":"Access level: Operator. After every power-on the panel is at the Operator level, whatever level was set before. Operator is the only level at which alarm windows are shown, so switching off and on is also the quickest way back to safe working. See Access levels.\nThe home screen — with one exception: if the rate control screen was open when the panel was switched off, it opens again. It is the only screen the panel remembers.\nWork paused, on a bench. If speed imitation is switched on and the machine has dispenser motors, the panel starts with product application locked — see Lock application by hand.\nThe version of the software, the serial number of the panel and its hardware identifier can be read at any time: press “MENU” on the home screen and choose the last item of the main menu. “BACK” or “Close” leaves the window."},{"p":"mini","t":"Switching on and off","h":"Switching off","u":"en/mini/switching-on.html#switching-off","x":"What\nWhen it is written\nSettings, target rates, alarm levels\nBy themselves, about one and a half seconds after the last change and the last touch of a button.\nStatistics of the active field\nEvery five minutes, and a few seconds after the machine stops.\nReadings of the Seeding table\nNever — they belong to the working session and start from zero after every power-on.\nIMPORTANT\nStop before you switch off, and wait a few seconds. Otherwise the last stretch of work is missing from the statistics, and a value changed just before may not be stored."},{"p":"mini","t":"Screens, menus, context menus and windows","h":"","u":"en/mini/interface-basics.html","x":"The things everything on the Hectare Mini is done through — the working screens, the menus, the context menu of a device, the windows that ask or tell, and the access levels."},{"p":"mini","t":"Screens, menus, context menus and windows","h":"","u":"en/mini/interface-basics.html","x":"Everything on the panel happens through the working screens, the menus, the context menu of a device and the windows that ask or tell you something. Numbers are typed on the number pad."},{"p":"mini","t":"Screens, menus, context menus and windows","h":"The working screens","u":"en/mini/interface-basics.html#the-working-screens","x":"“HOME” moves on from one working screen to the next, always in this order:\nScreen\nShown\nHome screen\nAlways.\nSeeding table\nAlways.\nStatistics\nAlways.\nMotors\nOnly if the machine has dispenser motors or tramline actuators, and only above the Operator access level.\nRate control\nAlways. One more “HOME” returns to the home screen.\n“BACK” returns to the home screen from the Seeding table, the Statistics and the Motors screen. The rate control screen is left only with “HOME”.\nThe home screen and the Statistics screen, two of the working screens."},{"p":"mini","t":"Screens, menus, context menus and windows","h":"Menus","u":"en/mini/interface-basics.html#menus","x":"“MENU” opens the menu of the screen you are on — on the home screen, the main menu.\nSelected item. Black text on a blue band. “UP” and “DOWN” move the band; at the end of the list it jumps to the other end.\nValue between − and +. Shown on the selected item: change it with “PLUS” and “MINUS”, right there in the list.\nValue of an item that is not selected. The − and + appear when you select it.\nArrow head. The item opens another menu. Press “MENU”.\nScroll bar. The list is longer than the screen.\nThe “Settings” menu, with “Language” selected.\nWhat you see on the right\nWhat it does\nAn arrow head\nOpens another menu. Press “MENU”.\nA value between - and +\n“PLUS” and “MINUS” change it one step at a time.\nA value between -[ and ]+\n“PLUS” and “MINUS” change it one step at a time; “MENU” opens the number pad to type it.\nNothing\nThe item performs an action. Press “MENU”.\nYou can also touch an item to select it and touch it again to open it. “BACK” leaves a menu, one level up."},{"p":"mini","t":"Screens, menus, context menus and windows","h":"The context menu of a device","u":"en/mini/interface-basics.html#the-context-menu-of-a-device","x":"Every sensor on the home screen, the rate control screen and the Motors screen has its own menu. Hold “MENU” until one device is shown on a white background — this is the sensor setup mode — then select the device with “UP” and “DOWN” and press “MENU”. How to work in this mode: Take a sensor into or out of control. The items of each context menu are in Sensor settings.\nThe context menu of the speed sensor."},{"p":"mini","t":"Screens, menus, context menus and windows","h":"Windows that ask, and windows that tell","u":"en/mini/interface-basics.html#windows-that-ask-and-windows-that-tell","x":"A question window has a yellow strip with “Confirmation”. The lines under the question name the two buttons that answer it — they differ from window to window, so read them. The green “Yes” and red “No” on the screen can also be touched. “BACK”, or a touch outside the window, always cancels.\nA question window: here PLUS answers Yes and MINUS answers No.\nAn information window has a blue strip with “Information”. It closes by itself, or with a touch or a button."},{"p":"mini","t":"Screens, menus, context menus and windows","h":"Alarm windows","u":"en/mini/interface-basics.html#alarm-windows","x":"Red strip. “FAILURE!” — the window reports a fault.\nHint. “HOME” closes the window.\nThe fault. Here the connection to a sensor is lost.\nType of the sensor. Here the fan.\nNumbers of the sensors. When several sensors of the same type have the same fault, all their numbers are listed.\n“Close”. Touch it to close the window.\nAn alarm window about the fan sensor.\nWhile the window is shown, the panel sounds the alarm signal and lights its alarm indicator. “HOME” or a touch of “Close” closes the window; “HOME” held closes it and returns to the home screen. The other buttons do nothing here. All alarms and what to do: How alarms work."},{"p":"mini","t":"Screens, menus, context menus and windows","h":"Access levels","u":"en/mini/interface-basics.html#access-levels","x":"Level\nWhat it opens\n“Operator”\nWhat you need for work. Active after every power-on. The only level at which alarm windows are shown.\n“Agronomist”\nHidden items and parameters of the machine and the sensors. Needs a code from the data sheet of your panel.\n“Service”\nThe profile and the rest of the commissioning work — for the service only. The configuration of seed and pressure sensors needs only the “Agronomist” level.\nThe level is chosen in “Settings” → “User”. How: Enable the Agronomist level. In this manual, a parameter that needs a higher level says so."},{"p":"mini","t":"The number pad","h":"","u":"en/mini/number-pad.html","x":"How to type a number on the Hectare Mini — what the window shows, which buttons work, and the traps of an empty field and of values outside the range."},{"p":"mini","t":"The number pad","h":"","u":"en/mini/number-pad.html","x":"Some values can be typed as a whole number instead of being stepped with “PLUS” and “MINUS”. In a menu such a value stands between -[ and ]+; press “MENU” on it and the number pad opens. The pad also opens by itself where steps make no sense — for the weight at the end of a motor calibration, for the number of holes in the disk of a dispenser motor, and for an access code.\nThe item Width, cm in a menu and the number pad it opens."},{"p":"mini","t":"The number pad","h":"What the pad shows","u":"en/mini/number-pad.html#what-the-pad-shows","x":"Name of the parameter, in light blue. In some windows the whole prompt, over several lines.\nThe number. It opens with the present value (empty if the value is 0) and grows to the left as you type.\nBackspace. A touch deletes the last character; touch and hold deletes the whole number.\nDigits. Touch them — the digits are typed on the screen only.\n.− key. Sets the decimal point. Touch and hold switches the minus sign, where the value may be negative.\n“OK”, in green. Accepts the number and closes the pad.\nThe number pad for the working width.\nA touched key does not light up. You see that the touch has arrived by the number, and you hear it if the button sound is on."},{"p":"mini","t":"The number pad","h":"Typing a number","u":"en/mini/number-pad.html#typing-a-number","x":"On a menu item whose value stands between -[ and ]+, press “MENU”. The pad opens.\nTouch and hold the backspace. The old value is cleared.\nTouch the digits. Each joins the number on the right.\nIf the value has tenths, touch .− and type the places after the point.\nCheck the number, then touch “OK” or press “MENU”. The pad closes and the menu shows the new value.\n“BACK” leaves the pad without changing anything. The other buttons and a touch outside the keys do nothing.\nIMPORTANT\nThe panel does not warn you. A number outside the permitted range is silently replaced by the nearest limit — for “Width, cm” a typed 50 becomes 100. An empty field is accepted as 0, not as the smallest permitted value. If you have cleared the field by mistake, press “BACK” and open the pad again."},{"p":"mini","t":"The number pad","h":"Rules of the pad","u":"en/mini/number-pad.html#rules-of-the-pad","x":"The point works only where the value has tenths, for example “Minimum, rpm” and “Maximum, rpm” of a dispenser motor. For whole numbers the .− key does nothing.\nOne point per number. A second touch of .− does nothing until you delete the first point.\nThe places after the point are limited to what the value knows. Type fewer and the panel adds zeros: with one place, 12 becomes 12.0.\nThe length is limited by the width of the field. When it is full, further digits do nothing."},{"p":"mini","t":"Quick start — before and during the first pass","h":"","u":"en/mini/quick-start.html","x":"What to check before you drive into the field with the Hectare Mini, how the panel starts watching by itself, and what to look at during the first pass."},{"p":"mini","t":"Quick start — before and during the first pass","h":"","u":"en/mini/quick-start.html","x":"This assumes the system has been commissioned — see Commissioning. Before the season the speed sensor must have been calibrated, and the dispensers too if their shaft speed or the seed weight is to be watched (Speed sensor, Sensor settings)."},{"p":"mini","t":"Quick start — before and during the first pass","h":"Before the field","u":"en/mini/quick-start.html#before-the-field","x":"Switch the panel on and wait for the home screen.\nPress “HOME” twice to open the Statistics screen. Check that the right active field is selected; on a new field, select an empty one or erase it — Start a new field.\nBack on the home screen, set the alarm level of the seed diagram with “PLUS” / “MINUS” — or, in the “Hybrid” and “Precise” modes, the target rate. “DOWN” moves to the fertilizer diagram. See Change the target rate.\nThe seed diagram with the alarm level in s/s and its control line.\nIf products are applied by weight, set their rates in kg/ha the same way.\nCheck the settings that change with the work — in the sensor setup mode (Take a sensor into or out of control), each sensor separately: “Period, s” of each dispenser shaft sensor; “Minimum, rpm” and “Maximum, rpm” of each fan; “Minimum, Pa” and “Maximum, Pa” of each pressure sensor; “Fraction” and “Correction” of the seed sensors and of the fertilizer sensors. All seed sensors share one set of settings, all fertilizer sensors another; every other sensor has its own.\nMake sure the access level is “Operator”. At a higher level no alarm windows are shown, and the panel warns you with an alarm if you start seeding. Switching off and on returns to Operator."},{"p":"mini","t":"Quick start — before and during the first pass","h":"Seeding","u":"en/mini/quick-start.html#seeding","x":"Just start. There is nothing to switch on: the panel starts watching the alarms by itself, with one short beep, once these conditions have held for the “Alarm delay” (3 s by default):\napplication is not locked by hand (Lock application by hand);\nwith an implement position sensor under control: the drill is in the working position;\nwith dispenser motors or a liquid pump: the drill moves, and the speed sensor is under control and shows a speed;\notherwise: at least “Percent of sowing,%” (20 % by default) of the lines under control are sowing — and, with “Seeding criteria” set at the speed sensor, the drill moves.\nWhen a condition fails, the watching stops at once with three short beeps. There is no separate alarm for a drill that moves while no seed arrives: then the watching does not start and the beep does not come.\nThe home screen during work."},{"p":"mini","t":"Quick start — before and during the first pass","h":"During the first pass, check","u":"en/mini/quick-start.html#during-the-first-pass-check","x":"the speed against the tractor's speedometer or a GPS navigator;\nthe fan speed and the pressure — within their limits, not red;\nthe seed columns above their line, and the seed rate against the target in the “Hybrid” and “Precise” modes;\nthe fertilizer columns above their line;\nthe product rate in kg/ha against the target;\nno red on any other indicator — Indicators.\nIf the speed sensor fails, the speed can be imitated in its settings until it is repaired (Speed sensor). When an alarm appears, stop and clear the fault — How alarms work. The quality of the last stretch is on the Seeding table; the result for the whole field on the Statistics screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Commissioning — setting up a new system","h":"","u":"en/mini/commissioning.html","x":"The order in which a new Hectare Mini system is set up on a machine — from connecting the devices to the calibrations — with a link to the details of every step."},{"p":"mini","t":"Commissioning — setting up a new system","h":"","u":"en/mini/commissioning.html","x":"This is the sequence for the dealer or service engineer when the system is installed. Each step links to the topic with the details. Most of the parameters need the Agronomist access level — Enable the Agronomist level.\nConnect all devices to the panel and check their power supply according to the wiring diagram in the data sheet of the system.\nCheck the profile. It must match the equipment of the machine — The profile. If it does not, set it up first.\nCheck the connections. Every sensor must answer and be under control — see Indicators for the red cross and the not-controlled sign.\nSet the general parameters in “Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters” (Settings menu): the working width, “Width, cm”; “Mode of seeding” — “Flow” for a grain drill; “Mode of fertilizers” — “Flow”.\nSet the sensors (Sensor settings):\nspeed sensor: “Diameter, cm” and “Pulses”; “Imitation, km/h” at “No”; then the speed calibration;\ndispenser shaft sensors: “Period, s”; if wanted, “Rotation speed control” or “Weight control”, each followed by its calibration;\nfans: “Pulses”, “Minimum, rpm”, “Maximum, rpm”;\npressure sensors: “Filter”, “Minimum, Pa”, “Maximum, Pa”;\nimplement position sensor: “Transporting position signal”;\nengine sensors: “Activation signal”;\nseed sensors, and separately fertilizer sensors: at least “Fraction” for the crop.\nSet up the tramline, if the machine makes tramlines — after the profile, never before: Set up the tramline rhythm.\nOn machines with dispenser motors: assign the products and calibrate every motor — Assign a product to a motor, Calibrate a motor by weight.\nIMPORTANT\nThe panel stores a change by itself about one and a half seconds after the last change and the last touch of a button. Wait a few seconds before switching off.\nEach sensor is set separately — except the seed sensors, which share one set of settings, and the fertilizer sensors, which share another.\nWhen everything is set, return to the “Operator” level (switching off and on does it) and go to Quick start."},{"p":"mini","t":"About this manual and its pictures","h":"","u":"en/mini/about-the-pictures.html","x":"How to read this manual — signal words, access levels, button presses — and what in the pictures belongs to the test bench and not to your machine."},{"p":"mini","t":"About this manual and its pictures","h":"How to read this manual","u":"en/mini/about-the-pictures.html#how-to-read-this-manual","x":"NOTE — extra information, for example a capability you may not know of.\nIMPORTANT, CAUTION, WARNING — information that costs work, a crop or a part if you ignore it.\nA parameter that is visible only above the “Operator” level says so — see Access levels.\nPress means a short touch of a button; hold means a touch held until the panel reacts, about half a second.\nLabels in quotes, such as “Settings”, are written exactly as the English panel shows them."},{"p":"mini","t":"About this manual and its pictures","h":"The pictures","u":"en/mini/about-the-pictures.html#the-pictures","x":"The screens are real frames from a Hectare Mini panel with firmware 2.2.8, mostly shot on a test bench instead of a machine in the field. Some belong to an earlier firmware; where the layout differs, the text describes 2.2.8. A few things in them belong to the bench:\nWhat you see in a picture\nWhy it is there\nWhat your panel shows instead\nA blue hours:minutes in the clock slot, or TRIAL\nThe bench panel runs on a trial licence.\nThe time of day — see Clock.\nYellow IMITAT in the clock slot\nThe bench runs in an imitation mode.\nThe time of day.\nA yellow speed figure, or IMITATION\nThe speed is imitated — no wheel turns on a bench.\nThe measured speed in white.\nRed hoppers, red dispenser shafts, a red fan with XXXX\nThe bench has no product, no shafts turning and no fan connected.\nGrey indicators while everything is in order.\nZeros on the Statistics screen and the Seeding table\nThe bench was standing still.\nYour figures.\nWhere an English picture is still missing, the page shows \"Picture to follow\"; the text says everything the picture would show. The German pages show the German frame in its place.\nNOTE\nThe question, information and alarm windows were brought to one shape with a coloured strip across the top in firmware 2.2.8: yellow for a question, blue for information, red for a fault."},{"p":"mini","t":"The home screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/home-screen.html","x":"What every part of the Hectare Mini home screen is — speed, indicators, area, population, the diagrams and rate fields — and what you can do from it."},{"p":"mini","t":"The home screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/home-screen.html","x":"The home screen is the main working screen. It shows the state of every device of the machine and, below, one diagram for the seed and one for the fertilizer. What it shows follows the profile: every device entered there gets its indicator. When space runs short, the panel makes the speed figure or the diagrams smaller, and on a crowded screen it leaves out the area or the population indicator."},{"p":"mini","t":"The home screen","h":"A planter with seed and fertilizer","u":"en/mini/home-screen.html#a-planter-with-seed-and-fertilizer","x":"Travel speed, in km/h. Messages about the speed replace the figure. Speed\nHoppers, numbered. Red — empty. Hopper\nDispenser shafts. Grey — in order; the icon turns while the shaft turns. Dispenser shaft\nClock. The time of day; it also carries messages. Clock\nMarkers. Yellow — both marker arms are up. Markers\nFan and its speed in rpm. Fan\nArea treated on the active field, in ha. Area\nPopulation — the seeding density in thousands of seeds per hectare, in the “Precise” mode. Population\nSeed diagram. One column per seed sensor. The section diagram\nScale of skips and doubles, in per cent (“Precise” mode).\nSeed rate field. Measured rate on top, target below, here in seeds per metre. Change the target rate\nSection numbers.\nFertilizer diagram, here in the “Flow” mode, with its control line.\nFertilizer rate field. Measured flow on top, alarm level below, in fertilizer units per second.\nA 12-row planter in the “Precise” mode with 12 fertilizer lines in the “Flow” mode, standing still."},{"p":"mini","t":"The home screen","h":"A grain drill on the bench","u":"en/mini/home-screen.html#a-grain-drill-on-the-bench","x":"Travel speed.\nHoppers. Red outline — empty.\nDispenser shafts. Red — alarm: the shaft does not turn.\nClock.\nFan. Red with XXXX — no connection to the fan sensor.\nArea.\nGPS reception. The satellite icon and the number of satellites used. GPS reception\nSeed column. Dark grey — no flow, the drill stands.\nControl line. The alarm level drawn across the diagram.\nRate field. Measured flow on top, alarm level below, in seeds per second.\nSection numbers.\nA 12-row grain drill in the “Flow” mode, standing still on the bench with no product and no fan.\nAll indicators and their states: Indicators. The diagram — columns, lines, colours: The section diagram."},{"p":"mini","t":"The home screen","h":"Two modes","u":"en/mini/home-screen.html#two-modes","x":"The home screen works in two modes:\nMonitoring mode — after switch-on. You watch the work and change the rates.\nSensor setup mode — hold “MENU”. One device is shown on a white background; “UP” and “DOWN” move to the next device, “PLUS” and “MINUS” take it into or out of control, “MENU” opens its context menu. “BACK” leaves the mode. See Take a sensor into or out of control.\nThe home screen in the sensor setup mode: the speed indicator is selected."},{"p":"mini","t":"The home screen","h":"What you can do from the home screen","u":"en/mini/home-screen.html#what-you-can-do-from-the-home-screen","x":"To do this\nDo this\nOpen the main menu\n“MENU”\nChange a target rate or alarm level\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”; “UP” / “DOWN” choose the field — Change the target rate\nLet the panel set the alarm level\nHold “BACK” while seeding — Set the alarm level\nLock or unlock product application\nHold “HOME” — Lock application by hand\nOpen the tramline bout menu\n“BACK” — Next tramline bout\nOpen the settings of a device\nHold “MENU”, select it, “MENU”\nGo to the next working screen\n“HOME” — Screens, menus and windows"},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"","u":"en/mini/indicators.html","x":"Every indicator of the Hectare Mini home screen in every state — speed, hopper, dispenser shaft, fan, pressure, engine sensors, implement position, markers, tramline, clock, area, population and product rates."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"","u":"en/mini/indicators.html","x":"Read the indicators by colour. On firmware 2.2.8 a device in order is drawn in grey; red, mostly blinking, is an alarm; yellow is a state you should know about but not a fault. Three states are the same for every sensor:\nWhat you see\nMeaning\nWhat to do\nA red cross, blinking, or XXXX in place of the value\nNo connection to the sensor.\nCheck its cable and connectors.\nA red question mark, blinking, or ???? in place of the value\nA device of another type answers at this place.\nHave the configuration checked by the service.\nA grey not-controlled sign, blinking, or !!!! in place of the value\nThe sensor is taken out of control. It is not watched.\nTake it back into control when the fault is repaired."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Speed","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#speed","x":"What you see\nMeaning\nThe travel speed in km/h.\nalternating with\nFaster than allowed. The red figure alternates with “SLOWER”.\nalternating with\nSlower than allowed. The red figure alternates with “FASTER”.\nRed figure alternating with “Sensor” 1 or 2, or with “Settings”\nOne of the two wheel sensors has failed, or the speed limits are set wrongly.\nNo connection to the speed sensor.\nalternating with\nSpeed imitation is on: the figure is yellow and alternates with “IMITATION”. Outside the limits, with INCREASE IMITATION or DECREASE IMITATION.\nThe speed is watched against its limits only if that control is switched on in the speed sensor settings. Where the speed comes from GPS: GPS reception."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Hopper","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#hopper","x":"What you see\nMeaning\nGrey\nProduct above the sensor.\nred, blinking\nThe hopper is empty."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Dispenser shaft","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#dispenser-shaft","x":"What you see\nMeaning\nGrey; turning while the shaft turns\nIn order.\nred, blinking\nThe shaft does not turn, or not at the speed required."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Fan","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#fan","x":"What you see\nMeaning\nGrey, with the speed in rpm\nIn order.\nRed, blinking, with the speed\nThe fan speed is outside its limits."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Pressure","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#pressure","x":"What you see\nMeaning\nGrey, with the pressure in kPa\nIn order.\nred\nThe pressure at the fan outlet is outside its limits."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Engine sensors","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#engine-sensors","x":"The sensors of a separate fan-drive engine. Oil pressure and battery charge are watched only while the ignition is on.\nWhat you see\nMeaning\nred\nLow oil pressure.\nred\nThe battery is not charging.\nred\nLittle fuel in the tank.\nred\nThe air filter is clogged.\nKey grey / key yellow\nIgnition on / ignition off.\nIn order, each of these icons is grey."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Implement position","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#implement-position","x":"What you see\nMeaning\nGrey\nThe drill is in the working position.\nyellow, blinking\nThe drill is in the transport position."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Markers","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#markers","x":"Grey arms show which marker is down and which is up. With two marker sensors, both arms yellow means both markers are up."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Tramline bout","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#tramline-bout","x":"Wheel tracks with the current bout on top and the number of bouts below — see Next tramline bout."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Clock","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#clock","x":"What you see\nMeaning\nWhite time\nThe time of day.\nRed time, with a red BAT for a moment every four seconds\nThe clock battery must be replaced.\nRed XX:XX, blinking\nThe clock is faulty.\nYellow IMITAT\nThe panel runs in an imitation mode — see About the pictures.\nBlue hours:minutes and TRIAL\nA trial period is active: the time left. It appears every 30 seconds and covers the hopper and shaft icons for a moment."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Area","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#area","x":"Hectares treated on the active field — shown only with a speed sensor, and only where the screen has room."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Population","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#population","x":"In the “Precise” mode: thousands of seeds per hectare, averaged over the seed sensors under control that answer. If seed and fertilizer are both in “Precise”, two indicators show, marked A (seed) and B (fertilizer); the area is then usually not shown."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"Product rate","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#product-rate","x":"One indicator per product applied by weight, with the product number, in kg/ha. Red and blinking: the rate deviates more than “Product rate error,%”. While it is within the tolerance, the panel shows the target figure."},{"p":"mini","t":"Indicators of the home screen and their states","h":"The column on the right","u":"en/mini/indicators.html#the-column-on-the-right","x":"What you see\nMeaning\nMotor symbol, yellow, blinking\nProduct application is locked by hand — see Lock application by hand.\nActuator symbol, red, blinking\nA dispenser motor or a tramline actuator is in alarm — see the Motors screen.\nActuator symbol alternating with the not-controlled sign\nA motor or an actuator is taken out of control.\nProduct symbol, red, blinking\nThe set rates cannot be applied at any speed — see Calibrate a motor by weight."},{"p":"mini","t":"The section diagram — columns, lines and colours","h":"","u":"en/mini/section-diagram.html","x":"How to read the seed and fertilizer diagrams of the Hectare Mini in the \"Flow\", \"Hybrid\" and \"Precise\" modes, and what \"NOT UNDER CONTROL\" means."},{"p":"mini","t":"The section diagram — columns, lines and colours","h":"","u":"en/mini/section-diagram.html","x":"Each column of a diagram is one seed or fertilizer line — one sensor. Its height is the flow through that line now. Next to the columns, the rate field shows the measured rate on top and the set value below it; while you change the set value it turns yellow for a few seconds. How the rest is drawn depends on the mode set in “Mode of seeding” and “Mode of fertilizers”: “Flow”, “Hybrid” or “Precise”. The fertilizer diagram always stands above the seed diagram and is usually run in “Flow”."},{"p":"mini","t":"The section diagram — columns, lines and colours","h":"“Flow” mode","u":"en/mini/section-diagram.html#flow-mode","x":"The seed diagram in the Flow mode with its marks.\nAlarm level — the lower figure of the rate field, in seeds per second (s/s). The flow under which a line counts as failing. “PLUS” and “MINUS” change it by 1.\nControl line — the white line across the diagram: the alarm level drawn.\nMeasured flow — the upper figure: the average of the lines now.\nGreen column — the flow is above the alarm level.\nYellow column — the flow is below the alarm level: alarm.\nNo column — no flow at all. While the drill is seeding and the alarms are watched, the background of that column blinks dark red over its full height."},{"p":"mini","t":"The section diagram — columns, lines and colours","h":"“Hybrid” mode","u":"en/mini/section-diagram.html#hybrid-mode","x":"As “Flow”, with three differences: the set value is a target rate in seeds per metre (s/m), changed by 1; lines for the permitted deviation can be drawn above and below it; and skips and doubles are not counted."},{"p":"mini","t":"The section diagram — columns, lines and colours","h":"“Precise” mode","u":"en/mini/section-diagram.html#precise-mode","x":"The seed diagram in the Precise mode with skips and doubles.\nTarget rate — the lower figure of the rate field, in seeds per metre (s/m), changed by 0.1. While you change it, the panel also shows it as a population in ks/Ha and as the spacing between seeds in centimetres.\nMeasured rate — the upper figure, averaged over the lines that have flow now.\nRate line and the upper and lower deviation lines. Which of them are drawn is set by “Grid”: the rate line with “No” or “Full”, the deviation lines with “Partial” or “Full” (Seeding diagram settings). The permitted deviation is “Rate error,%” of the seed sensors.\nGreen column — within the deviation; blue — above it; yellow — below it.\nYellow hatched bar left of a column — the skips in that line; blue hatched bar right of it — the doubles. Their height is measured against “Scale S&D,%”, shown at the top of the rate field while “Skips & Doubles” is “Yes”. On machines with many rows the columns are too narrow and the bars are not drawn.\nSkips, doubles and the quality are counted over the recalculation interval — the stretch of path set by “Recalculation,m” of the seed sensors."},{"p":"mini","t":"The section diagram — columns, lines and colours","h":"Marks on a column — all modes","u":"en/mini/section-diagram.html#marks-on-a-column-all-modes","x":"Mark\nMeaning\nWhite box with !\nThe sensor is taken out of control.\nRed cross\nNo connection to the sensor.\nGrey background, white number\nThe sensor selected in the sensor setup mode.\nLight grey hatching\nThe line is shut off for a tramline — see Next tramline bout."},{"p":"mini","t":"The section diagram — columns, lines and colours","h":"“NOT UNDER CONTROL”","u":"en/mini/section-diagram.html#not-under-control","x":"The panel starts watching the seeding alarms only when the drill is really seeding: when at least the share of lines set by “Percent of sowing,%” (20 % by default) sows within the set value — and, if “Seeding criteria” is set in the speed sensor, while the drill moves. Seed and fertilizer alarms start and stop together. One short beep says the watching has started; three short beeps, that it has stopped.\nIf the drill is seeding but the flow stays under the set value, the diagram shows “NOT UNDER CONTROL”. Either the flow has fallen during work — a real fault — or the alarm level or target rate is set higher than the machine delivers.\nIMPORTANT\nColumns under the line are not guarded. Set the level so that the columns stand above the line, or let the panel set it: Set the alarm level."},{"p":"mini","t":"GPS reception and the GPS information screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/gps-receiver.html","x":"How the Hectare Mini takes the travel speed from its built-in GPS receiver, what the colour of the satellite icon means, and how to read the GPS information screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"GPS reception and the GPS information screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/gps-receiver.html","x":"The panel has a GPS receiver built in. It gives the travel speed where the drill has no wheel or radar sensor, or alongside one. While the receiver answers and has a valid fix, the speed comes from GPS. If it falls silent or loses the signal, the sensor set under “Speed” in the profile takes over by itself — you do not have to switch anything.\nThe receiver is switched on by the profile item GPS: UBX for the built-in receiver (the normal setting) or NMEA; “No” switches it off and hides the satellite icon.\nIMPORTANT\nGPS needs a speed sensor in the profile. Without one, the GPS item snaps back to “No” when the profile is saved. On a drill without a drive wheel it is enough to set “Speed” to “Control panel” without connecting a sensor there — this takes no place on the sensor network. Then, if the GPS signal is lost, the panel shows no travel speed until reception returns."},{"p":"mini","t":"GPS reception and the GPS information screen","h":"The satellite icon","u":"en/mini/gps-receiver.html#the-satellite-icon","x":"The icon stands at the right edge of the home screen, with the number of satellites used in its lower right corner.\nThe satellite icon in its states: receiver silent, no fix, 3, 6 and 12 satellites.\nColour\nMeaning\nRed\nThe receiver does not answer, or it answers without a valid fix — normal indoors and just after switch-on. The number is not shown while the receiver is silent.\nGrey\nValid fix, fewer than 5 satellites.\nYellow\nValid fix, 5 to 7 satellites.\nGrey\nValid fix, 8 satellites or more. The two grey states differ only by the number."},{"p":"mini","t":"GPS reception and the GPS information screen","h":"The information screen","u":"en/mini/gps-receiver.html#the-information-screen","x":"To open it, hold “MENU” on the home screen, select the satellite icon with “UP” / “DOWN” and press “MENU”.\n“GPS signal:” “Yes” if the receiver answers, otherwise “No”.\nSatellites — the number seen and the number used in the fix.\nSpeed — the speed as the seeding uses it. Below a small standstill threshold it shows 0 km/h, so that a drill standing still does not count area.\nraw — the unfiltered speed, and whether the fix is valid.\nUTC — date and time from the receiver; zeros before the first valid fix.\nReception counters. With NMEA: bytes received, valid sentences, checksum errors and the baud rate found; with UBX: bytes, frames and errors.\nring drop — bytes lost in the receive buffer. A rising figure means the connection to the receiver is disturbed.\nBACK leaves the screen.\nThe GPS information screen, receiver set to NMEA, on the bench.\nThe screen only shows; nothing on it can be changed, and no access code is needed."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Seeding table screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/table-screen.html","x":"The readings of every seed line as figures, one row per line — which tables your machine has, what each column and colour means, and how to zero the readings."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Seeding table screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/table-screen.html","x":"The Seeding table answers what the diagram on the home screen cannot: which line exactly, and by how much. Open it with “HOME” from the home screen; the next “HOME” goes on to the Statistics screen, “BACK” returns to the home screen.\nThe figures belong to the working session: they count from switch-on and are lost at switch-off. The field statistics are kept apart — see The Statistics screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Seeding table screen","h":"What the table shows","u":"en/mini/table-screen.html#what-the-table-shows","x":"Row number — the seed line, in the order of the rows of the machine.\nsds/m — the rate measured now, in seeds per metre. ----- — the line is watched but not sowing.\nQ.% — quality: the share of seeds placed singly, as intended.\nDb% — doubles: two seeds where one was intended.\nSk% — skips: a gap where a seed was intended.\nPage indicator — this table and the number of tables, between the signs of “MINUS” and “PLUS”, the buttons that change the table. Absent if there is only one table.\nPopulation of the line, in thousands of seeds per hectare.\nAverages over all lines watched. With two groups of sensors, two such lines, A and B.\nThe first table of a 12-row planter in the “Precise” mode, standing still.\nColumns 3 to 7 belong to the last completed recalculation interval — the stretch set by “Recalculation,m” (or “Recalculation,sec” without a speed sensor), shown at the bottom right. Only column 2 is a live reading. After the machine stops, the figures of the last interval stay on the screen to be read at leisure. A further column, %, gives the deviation of each line from the target, with its sign; positive means more seeds and a closer spacing."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Seeding table screen","h":"Which tables your machine has","u":"en/mini/table-screen.html#which-tables-your-machine-has","x":"Table\nShown when\nRate per area (population, ks/Ha), Rate per distance (seeds per metre)\nSeed in the “Precise” mode.\nSeed counters\nAlways.\nDry fertilizer\nThere are fertilizer sensors and their mode is not “Precise”.\nLiquid fertilizer\nThere are liquid fertilizer sensors.\nA planter with seed in “Precise” and dry-fertilizer sensors has four tables. “PLUS” goes to the next one, “MINUS” to the previous one; the page indicator at the top right shows which one you are on.\nTable 1 of 4 — rate per area: seeds per metre, quality, doubles, skips and the population of every line.\nTable 2 of 4 — rate per distance: the same columns with seeds per metre in place of the population.\nTable 3 of 4 — seed counters: seeds counted per line and the bar of the deviation.\nTable 4 of 4 — dry fertilizer: the flow of every fertilizer line and its deviation.\nIf seed and fertilizer are both in “Precise”, the fertilizer lines appear in the seed tables as group B, in light yellow type.\nThe Seed counters table: seeds per line and a bar of the deviation from the target.\nOn the Seed counters table the bar runs between the permitted deviation “Rate error,%” either side of the target — not a fixed scale. In the “Flow” and “Hybrid” modes the scale is fixed at −50 % to +50 %. The left column then shows seeds per second instead of seeds per metre."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Seeding table screen","h":"Markers and colours","u":"en/mini/table-screen.html#markers-and-colours","x":"In the rate column\nMeaning\n-----\nWatched, not sowing now.\n! ---, whole row grey\nTaken out of control.\nX ---, whole row dark red\nNo connection to this sensor.\n? ---, whole row dark red\nA sensor of the wrong type answers here.\nWhile sowing, a cell turns yellow when it reaches more than half the permitted deviation and red when it exceeds it. The limits are “Rate error,%”, “Doubles error,%” and “Skips error,%” of the seed sensors; with the doubles or skips check off, “Scale S&D,%” is used. The row number turns yellow or red with its worst cell, so the line that stands out is found at a glance.\nTIP\nRead the deviation first. A line 20 % below the others has a problem the average will not show — a partly blocked outlet, a worn disk, a sensor that needs cleaning."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Seeding table screen","h":"Buttons","u":"en/mini/table-screen.html#buttons","x":"Button\nWhat it does\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nNext / previous table.\n“DOWN” / “UP”\nScroll one row. One touch is one row.\n“MENU”\nAsks “Zero readings?” — Zero the readings.\n“HOME”\nStatistics.\n“BACK”\nHome screen.\nThe table itself does not react to touch."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Seeding table screen","h":"Fertilizer tables and tramline rows","u":"en/mini/table-screen.html#fertilizer-tables-and-tramline-rows","x":"The Dry fertilizer table.\nThe Dry fertilizer table is built like the Seed counters table, with fert/m in the “Hybrid” mode or fert/s in “Flow”; the piece count is shown only with “Count seeds always” on. The Liquid fertilizer table shows per line the flow and the litres applied, with L/Ha or L/min by “Mode of liq.fert.”, and has no deviation bar.\nA line shut off for a tramline shows only its number and flow; the other columns are dashed. Here yellow and red warn that more seed falls into the tramline than “Toler.sowing to tramline,%” allows.\nA tramline row among the other rows."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/statistics-screen.html","x":"The report of the work on each field, block by block — and the menu that chooses the active field, shows the lifetime mileage and erases a field."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/statistics-screen.html","x":"The Statistics screen is the report of the work: how far you drove, how much you covered, what was sown and how evenly. It is kept separately for each field and survives switching off. Open it with “HOME” from the Seeding table (twice from the home screen); “BACK” returns to the home screen, “HOME” goes on to the next working screen.\nThe report is one long list. “UP” and “DOWN” scroll it one line per touch; “PLUS” and “MINUS” show the next or previous field. On the screen itself, a swipe up or down scrolls, a two-finger swipe changes the field and a two-finger tap opens the menu."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"The head and the work on the field","u":"en/mini/statistics-screen.html#the-head-and-the-work-on-the-field","x":"Field number, between the signs of “MINUS” and “PLUS”.\n(A) — this is the active field, the one the panel writes to now.\nPath — distance driven while seeding, in km.\nArea — area treated while seeding, in ha.\nOperating time — time spent seeding.\nMovement speed — the average speed while seeding.\nStarted / Ended — when the work on this field began and ended.\nSeeding — the area sown, not sown and unknown.\nSeed sensors — one block per sensor follows.\ns, n, ? — the share of sown, not sown and unknown area for this sensor.\nBar — the same shares in colour: green sown, red not sown, grey unknown.\nScroll bar — where you are in the report.\nThe report of field 1, the active one, in the short view.\nUnknown is area driven while a sensor had no connection or was out of control. In the “Flow” mode, not sown is the area where the sensor saw no flow; in “Precise” it is the area of one skip times the number of skips."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"Blocks you may also see","u":"en/mini/statistics-screen.html#blocks-you-may-also-see","x":"Block\nWhat it holds\nProducts by weight\nPer product: the weight applied, actual against target in kg/ha and in per cent, and the distance, share and number of deviations beyond “Product rate error,%”.\n“Work without alarms control”\nPath, area and share, time and speed driven while seeding without the alarms watched — a set value too high, or set high on purpose.\n“Speed control”\n“Path, slow” and “Path, fast”: distance and share driven outside the speed limits. Only with the speed control on.\n“Fertilizing”\nFertilized, not fertilized and unknown area.\nPer seed sensor, in the full view\nThe distance, share and number of times the line ran under the alarm level.\nIMPORTANT\nThe weight is right only after a calibration. Calibrate the dispenser again after changing the metering roller, adjusting the variator or gearbox, or changing the seed."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"In the “Precise” mode","u":"en/mini/statistics-screen.html#in-the-precise-mode","x":"The Seeding block in the Precise mode.\nThe “Seeding” block adds “Seeds sown (x1000)”; “Quality” (singles), “Doubles” and “Skips” while seeding, and “Not sown” — skips over stretches where nothing was sown — each as a share of all seeds and skips; and “Population,ks/Ha” and “Rate, s/m”, each in the columns “Factical” and “Setuped”, with the ratio in per cent. Each seed sensor adds its seeds, its quality, doubles and skips, its population and rate, and the distances it ran below and above the permitted deviation. “Seeds sown (x1000)” also appears in the other modes with “Count seeds always” on.\nThe actual rate counts only the path where seed flowed; the population counts the whole path. Their deviations can therefore differ."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Statistics screen","h":"The menu of the screen","u":"en/mini/statistics-screen.html#the-menu-of-the-screen","x":"Press “MENU”.\nThe menu of the Statistics screen.\nItem\nWhat it does\n“Select field”\nMakes the field shown the active one — see Start a new field. Shown only on a field that is not active.\n“Statistics”\n“Short” or “Full” view. Not shown without a speed sensor.\n“Mileage”\nPath and area over the whole life of the system; they cannot be reset. Without a speed sensor, the running time.\n“Save to SD-card”\nShown when a memory card is ready. Firmware 2.2.8 does not save the report yet and answers with a failure message.\n“Zero statistics”\nErases the field shown. At the Operator level the panel asks for the code of a higher level; above it, “Are you sure?”.\nThe lifetime mileage of the system."},{"p":"mini","t":"The rate control screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/rate-control-screen.html","x":"The screen for machines that apply products to a rate — what it shows, its three states, its colours, and how to reach the motors and their calibration from it."},{"p":"mini","t":"The rate control screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/rate-control-screen.html","x":"On the rate control screen you set the target rates of seed, dry fertilizer, liquid fertilizer and the products applied by weight, and watch the rate actually applied. It also shows the area, the speed, the hoppers, the fans and the implement position. It is the last working screen: “HOME” from the Statistics screen (or the Motors screen) brings you here, and one more “HOME” returns to the home screen. “BACK” does not leave this screen.\nIt is the only screen the panel remembers over switch-off: if it was open, it opens again after the next switch-on."},{"p":"mini","t":"The rate control screen","h":"What the screen shows","u":"en/mini/rate-control-screen.html#what-the-screen-shows","x":"Area worked on the active field, in ha. Only with a speed sensor.\nTravel speed, in km/h, with the same messages as on the home screen — see Speed. Only with a speed sensor.\nSettings symbol. Touch and hold it to open the main menu.\nProduct indicator — here product 1, in kg/ha. One for every product that has at least one metering unit under control applying it by weight.\nRate. The rate actually applied; while the indicator is selected, the target.\nWork pause — the hand. Dark grey: working; blinking yellow: application locked — see Lock application by hand.\nHopper, one for every hopper.\nThe rate control screen of a drill with one product applied by weight, standing still.\nDepending on the machine, more indicators appear: the target rate of liquid fertilizer in L/ha, the rate of seed and of dry fertilizer in the “Precise” mode, a fan indicator for every fan, and the implement position at the bottom right. If there is no room for all fans and hoppers, the panel drops first a fan, then hoppers; the home screen still shows them all."},{"p":"mini","t":"The rate control screen","h":"Three states","u":"en/mini/rate-control-screen.html#three-states","x":"Observation — nothing is selected. The indicators show the rates actually applied.\nRate change — “PLUS” or “MINUS”. The rate indicators can be selected; see Change the target rate.\nSensor setup — hold “MENU”. The settings symbol, the speed, the fans, the hoppers and the implement position can be selected; “PLUS” and “MINUS” take the selected sensor into or out of control, “MENU” opens its context menu — or, on the settings symbol, the main menu.\nIn both modes “UP” and “DOWN” move the selection, “BACK” leaves the mode, and “HOME” held locks or unlocks application."},{"p":"mini","t":"The rate control screen","h":"Colours of a rate indicator","u":"en/mini/rate-control-screen.html#colours-of-a-rate-indicator","x":"What you see\nMeaning\nSymbol and unit green, value white\nIn order; the value is the rate applied.\nSymbol, unit and value blinking red\nThe rate applied deviates from the target more than allowed.\nDark blue-green background, light blue symbol, yellow value\nSelected; the value is the target.\n!!!!, XXXX or ???? blinking red (liquid fertilizer only)\nNot under control / no connection / a device of the wrong type.\nIn the “Precise” mode the seed and fertilizer indicators show the population in ks/Ha while not selected, and the target in s/m while selected.\nThe rate of a product is averaged over each 10 m of travel. It counts as deviating when it is off by more than “Product rate error,%”: the alarm comes after two such stretches in a row and clears after two good stretches. Standing still, with application locked, or with no metering unit under control, the indicator shows 0.00 and the alarm is cleared."},{"p":"mini","t":"The rate control screen","h":"To the motors and their calibration","u":"en/mini/rate-control-screen.html#to-the-motors-and-their-calibration","x":"Touch and hold a product indicator — or select it in the rate change state and press “MENU”. A list of the motors of that product opens (“Motor” or “Fert.mot.” with the number). Select a motor and press “MENU”: “Calibration, product”, “Weight, gr” and “Rotations”. How to calibrate: Calibrate a motor by weight.\nThe list exists only for products whose motors work in “Flow” or “Hybrid”. If the machine is moving, the panel refuses: the speed must be zero for a calibration. A touch and hold on the liquid fertilizer indicator opens the context menu of the pump."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Motors screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/motors-screen.html","x":"One tile per dispenser motor and one symbol per tramline actuator — how to reach the screen, read the colours, take a motor out of control and open its settings."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Motors screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/motors-screen.html","x":"The Motors screen shows the dispenser electric motors of the machine, one tile each, and the tramline actuators. It is the screen to look at when a row stops sowing and you want to know whether the motor is the reason. It has no heading: it carries only numbered motor and actuator symbols — no rates, no area, no speed.\nIt exists if the profile holds at least one seed motor, one fertilizer motor or one tramline actuator, and it is shown only above the Operator level — enable the Agronomist level first (Enable the Agronomist level). Then it follows the Statistics screen: three presses of “HOME” from the home screen. “HOME” goes on to the rate control screen; “BACK” returns to the home screen.\nThe Motors screen of a planter with eight seed motors.\nThe seed motors stand in a bar at the top, numbered in the order of the profile. Below it a bar of fertilizer motors, if the machine has them — on a machine with more than twenty rows and no fertilizer motors, the second bar holds the second half of the seed motors. The tramline actuators stand below the bars. Beside each motor: the speed of the driven shaft in rpm; on machines with many motors it is left out so that all tiles fit."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Motors screen","h":"Reading a motor tile","u":"en/mini/motors-screen.html#reading-a-motor-tile","x":"The colour of the symbol is the state. Symbol and sign alternate once a second while the state is not normal.\nSymbol\nValue\nState\nMotor, green\nthe speed\nUnder control and driven.\nMotor and STOP, yellow\nthe speed\nIn order, but application is off — at the headland, or locked by hand. Not a fault.\nMotor, red\nthe speed\nAlarm. The alarm window names the reason.\nMotor and cross, red\nXXX\nNo connection to the motor controller. Check its power supply and its cable and connectors.\nMotor and ?, red\n???\nThe device answering is not a motor controller, or the controller stayed in its update mode.\nMotor struck through\n!!!\nTaken out of control: neither driven nor watched.\nThree tiles: green in order, red with XXX, struck through.\nNOTE\nYellow is not a fault. A screen of yellow STOP signs means application is switched off. Red is a fault; yellow is a state you chose."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Motors screen","h":"Reading a tramline actuator","u":"en/mini/motors-screen.html#reading-a-tramline-actuator","x":"Symbol\nState\nPulled in or pulled out, grey, steady\nIt has reached that position.\nPulled in or pulled out, yellow, steady\nIt is moving there.\nPulled in and pulled out alternating, yellow\nThe position is not certain.\nRed cross, blinking\nNo connection.\nRed question mark, blinking\nThe device answering is not a tramline actuator.\nActuator symbol, red, blinking\nThe actuator reports an alarm.\nStruck through, grey, blinking\nTaken out of control.\nThe position reached is grey, not green: only motors are green here."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Motors screen","h":"Selecting a motor","u":"en/mini/motors-screen.html#selecting-a-motor","x":"Hold “MENU” to switch on the selection; it stands on the first motor, on a white background. “UP” and “DOWN” move through a bar and on into the next bar and the actuators. Then:\n“MENU” opens the context menu of the selected motor or actuator — Motor context menu.\n“MINUS” / “PLUS” take it out of or into control — Take a sensor into or out of control.\n“BACK” leaves the selection; a second “BACK” returns to the home screen.\nThe Motors screen with motor 3 selected."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Motors screen","h":"The service screen of the motors","u":"en/mini/motors-screen.html#the-service-screen-of-the-motors","x":"The context menu item “Electric motors” (Service level) opens a screen for the service engineer: versions, motor type, target and actual speed, error code, voltages, temperature, current and sixteen state signs in the “Registers” view, and counters per field in the “Statistics” view. You do not need it for sowing.\nMode — “HOME” switches between the views.\nMotor number — “MINUS” and “PLUS” switch the motor.\nName of the view.\nField number — “UP” and “DOWN” switch it; (A) marks the active field.\nCounters — “Jammed”, “Disconnects” and “Other”. Above zero, red.\nIn failure — distance driven with the motor in failure.\nThe Statistics view of motor 1, field 1.\n“BACK” leaves the screen. If the number belongs to no motor of the profile, “No data” appears."},{"p":"mini","t":"Change the target rate or the alarm level","h":"","u":"en/mini/set-the-target-rate.html","x":"Raise or lower the alarm level, the target rate in seeds per metre or the product rate in kg/ha — on the home screen or on the rate control screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Change the target rate or the alarm level","h":"","u":"en/mini/set-the-target-rate.html","x":"Every diagram and every product applied by weight has a rate field: the measured value on top, the set value below. What the set value is depends on the mode — see The section diagram:\nMode\nThe set value\nOne step\n“Flow”\nAlarm level, seeds per second\n1 s/s\n“Hybrid”\nTarget rate, seeds per metre\n1 s/m\n“Precise”\nTarget rate, seeds per metre\n0.1 s/m (0.5 to 50.0)\nProduct by weight\nTarget rate, kg/ha\n0.1 kg/ha up to 50.0, above that 1 kg/ha (1.0 to 999.0)\nLiquid fertilizer\nTarget rate, L/ha\n0.1 L/ha up to 50.0, above that 1 L/ha"},{"p":"mini","t":"Change the target rate or the alarm level","h":"On the home screen","u":"en/mini/set-the-target-rate.html#on-the-home-screen","x":"Press “PLUS” or “MINUS” once. The rate field used last is selected and blinks; nothing has changed yet.\nChanging the target rate of a product in kg/ha on the home screen.\nIf the machine has more than one rate field — seed and fertilizer, products by weight — press “DOWN” or “UP” to move to the next or the previous one.\nPress “PLUS” to raise the value or “MINUS” to lower it, one step per touch. Holding the button does not repeat it.\nPress “BACK” to finish, or simply wait: five seconds after the last touch the field stops blinking. The new value is in force.\nIn the “Precise” mode the panel also shows, while you change the target, the same rate as a population in ks/Ha and as the spacing between seeds in centimetres."},{"p":"mini","t":"Change the target rate or the alarm level","h":"On the rate control screen","u":"en/mini/set-the-target-rate.html#on-the-rate-control-screen","x":"Press “PLUS” or “MINUS”. The target selected last is highlighted — dark blue-green background, yellow value; after switch-on, “Product 1”. The first press changes nothing.\nThe rate control screen with Product 2 selected for a change.\nMove to the target you need with “UP” and “DOWN”.\nSet the value with “PLUS” and “MINUS”.\nPress “BACK”. The highlight goes; the indicators show the applied rates again.\nComing down from above 50.0, a product or liquid rate stops exactly at 50.0; the next touch takes it below, in steps of 0.1. The seed target in the “Precise” mode is one number for all seed sensors: the home screen and the rate control screen change the same value, and there is no menu item for it.\nThe value is stored by itself about one and a half seconds after the last change; wait that long before switching off.\nIMPORTANT\nColumns under the line are not guarded. Set far above what the machine delivers, the columns sink under the line and the panel shows “NOT UNDER CONTROL”. On a grain drill, let the panel find the level: Set the alarm level."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set the alarm level of a grain drill","h":"","u":"en/mini/set-the-alarm-level.html","x":"Let the Hectare Mini set the alarm level from the flow being applied, with one long press — or set it by hand — so that a line that loses flow is reported."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set the alarm level of a grain drill","h":"","u":"en/mini/set-the-alarm-level.html","x":"In the “Flow” mode the panel watches every line against an alarm level in seeds per second: a line whose flow drops below it is reported. Set too high, the columns sink under the line and the diagram shows “NOT UNDER CONTROL”; set too low, a partly blocked line goes unnoticed. The simplest way is to let the panel set it from the flow it sees."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set the alarm level of a grain drill","h":"Let the panel set it","u":"en/mini/set-the-alarm-level.html#let-the-panel-set-it","x":"Start seeding as usual, at working speed, and wait until the panel gives one short beep — it is now watching the seeding.\nOn the home screen, hold “BACK”. The panel gives one short beep.\nLook at the rate fields. In the “Flow” mode each alarm level is now half the flow measured at that moment; in the “Hybrid” mode the target rate is set to the rate measured at that moment. The changed field is highlighted.\nThis works for the seed diagram, the fertilizer diagram and liquid fertilizer in “Flow”, all at once, but only while the panel watches the seeding. Standing still, or in the “Precise” mode, the long press does nothing.\nThe seed diagram with the alarm level in s/s and its control line."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set the alarm level of a grain drill","h":"Set it by hand","u":"en/mini/set-the-alarm-level.html#set-it-by-hand","x":"Press “PLUS” or “MINUS” on the home screen: the first press selects the seed rate field, further presses change it by 1 s/s. “DOWN” and “UP” move to the fertilizer rate field. Details: Change the target rate.\nTIP\nCheck after the first pass. Every column should stand clearly above its line. If several columns hover close to it, lower the level a little; if the diagram shows “NOT UNDER CONTROL” while the drill sows well, the level is too high."},{"p":"mini","t":"Lock product application by hand","h":"","u":"en/mini/lock-application-by-hand.html","x":"Stop the dispenser motors and the liquid pump with one long press, so that nothing turns while you work on the machine — and how the panel shows the lock."},{"p":"mini","t":"Lock product application by hand","h":"","u":"en/mini/lock-application-by-hand.html","x":"On a machine with dispenser electric motors, fertilizer motors or a liquid fertilizer pump, you can lock product application by hand. While it is locked, no dispenser motor turns, the pump stands still, and the panel expects no application: alarms about a rate that is too low do not occur. Use it whenever you work on the metering units, or to cross a field without sowing.\nWARNING\nThe lock is a function of the panel, not a safety switch. Before you put your hands into a metering unit, switch off the drive at the machine as its manual requires.\nOn the home screen or on the rate control screen, hold “HOME” until the panel gives one short beep.\nCheck the lock. On the home screen a yellow motor symbol blinks in the column at the right; on the rate control screen the hand at the bottom left turns yellow.\nThe work pause hand: dark grey while working, yellow while locked.\nTo release the lock, hold “HOME” again. One short beep; the symbol stops blinking and application resumes.\nOn every other machine the long press does nothing and the hand stays dark grey.\nOn the Motors screen a locked motor shows a yellow STOP — see The Motors screen. > NOTE > > On a bench with speed imitation and dispenser motors, the panel starts with application locked and the imitated speed stays at 0. Only releasing the lock with “HOME” starts the imitated travel."},{"p":"mini","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"","u":"en/mini/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html","x":"Switch the watching of a single sensor, motor or actuator off and on with the sensor setup mode — what the panel asks, and when it wants the statistics erased."},{"p":"mini","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"","u":"en/mini/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html","x":"A sensor taken out of control is no longer watched: it raises no alarms, and its indicator shows the blinking not-controlled sign. Do it only to keep working with a faulty sensor until it is repaired — and take it back into control afterwards.\nIMPORTANT\nA sensor out of control is blind, not quiet. Nothing about that line or device is watched any more. A motor out of control is not driven either: its row is not sown.\nOn the home screen, hold “MENU” until one device is shown on a white background. The panel is now in the sensor setup mode.\nThe home screen in the sensor setup mode, the speed indicator selected.\nSelect the sensor with “UP” and “DOWN”. In a diagram, the selected column gets a grey background and a white number.\nPress “MINUS” to take it out of control, or “PLUS” to take it back into control.\nThe panel asks whether you are sure. Press “MENU” for “Yes”, or “BACK” for “No”; the green “Yes” and red “No” on the screen can also be touched.\nThe question before the control of a sensor is switched off.\nPress “BACK” to leave the sensor setup mode.\nThe question comes only the first time while you stay in the sensor setup mode. After that, “PLUS” and “MINUS” act on every further sensor without asking, so a whole row of sensors can be switched quickly. After you leave the mode, the panel asks again.\nThe same works on the rate control screen (hold “MENU”) and, for motors and tramline actuators, on the Motors screen. The control of the speed sensor cannot be switched off."},{"p":"mini","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"When the statistics must be erased","u":"en/mini/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html#when-the-statistics-must-be-erased","x":"If the active field already holds data and the change alters the set of products being applied — possible with motors in the “Flow” and “Hybrid” modes — the panel first asks to erase the statistics of the active field. “Yes” erases them for good; at the Operator level it also asks for the code of a higher access level. To keep the data, select a field that is empty first — see Start a new field."},{"p":"mini","t":"Take a sensor into or out of control","h":"What the context menu offers","u":"en/mini/sensor-in-and-out-of-control.html#what-the-context-menu-offers","x":"In the sensor setup mode “MENU” opens the context menu of the selected device: its information screen and its parameters, depending on the access level. The parameters of every sensor are in Sensor settings."},{"p":"mini","t":"Zero the readings of the Seeding table","h":"","u":"en/mini/zero-the-readings.html","x":"Set the counters and interval figures of the Seeding table to zero — what is zeroed, what is not, and what else zeroes them."},{"p":"mini","t":"Zero the readings of the Seeding table","h":"","u":"en/mini/zero-the-readings.html","x":"The figures of the Seeding table — seed counters, quality, doubles, skips, rates, litres of liquid fertilizer — count from switch-on. Zero them when you start a new field or a new seed lot.\nOn the home screen, press “HOME”. The Seeding table opens.\nPress “MENU”. The panel asks “Zero readings?”.\nThe question before the readings are zeroed.\nPress “PLUS” for “Yes”, or touch “Yes”. “MINUS”, “No” or “BACK” cancels.\nOn “Yes” the readings of all sensors are zeroed — seed, dry fertilizer and liquid fertilizer — whichever table you are looking at. Only the readings of this screen: the field statistics are not touched.\nIMPORTANT\nZeroing cannot be undone. Do it at the start of a field or a lot, not to tidy the screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Zero the readings of the Seeding table","h":"What else zeroes them","u":"en/mini/zero-the-readings.html#what-else-zeroes-them","x":"Selecting another active field on the Statistics screen.\nErasing the statistics of the active field.\nSwitching the panel off — the readings belong to the working session.\nThe interval figures — quality, doubles, skips and the rates — are also renewed by the panel itself with every completed recalculation interval once the machine moves again. The seed counters stay."},{"p":"mini","t":"Start work on a new field","h":"","u":"en/mini/start-a-new-field.html","x":"Choose the field the panel writes its statistics to, and erase it if it is not empty — before the first pass on a new field."},{"p":"mini","t":"Start work on a new field","h":"","u":"en/mini/start-a-new-field.html","x":"The panel keeps the statistics of several fields. It writes to one of them, the active field, marked (A) on the Statistics screen. Before the first pass on a new field, make an empty field the active one.\nOn the home screen, press “HOME” twice. The Statistics screen opens with the active field.\nPress “PLUS” or “MINUS” until the field you want is shown. Fields without (A) are not active.\nPress “MENU”. The menu of the screen opens.\nThe menu of the Statistics screen.\nSelect “Select field” and press “MENU”. The item is shown only for a field that is not active.\nThe panel asks “Are you sure?”. Press “PLUS” for “Yes”. The field is now active, marked (A), and the readings of the Seeding table start from zero.\nIf the field already holds data from other products than the machine applies now, the panel first asks to erase that field; at the Operator level it also wants the code of a higher access level."},{"p":"mini","t":"Start work on a new field","h":"Erase a field that holds old data","u":"en/mini/start-a-new-field.html#erase-a-field-that-holds-old-data","x":"Show the field on the Statistics screen and press “MENU”.\nSelect “Zero statistics” and press “MENU”.\nAt the Operator level, type the code of the Agronomist level on the number pad. Above Operator, the panel asks “Are you sure?” — press “PLUS” for “Yes”.\nThe statistics of that field are erased for good. The lifetime totals under “Mileage” are not affected.\nIMPORTANT\nThe statistics can also be erased by other changes. Taking a motor into or out of control, or changing the product type of a motor, can demand that the statistics of the active field be erased first. Switch to an empty field before such changes if you want to keep the data."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"","u":"en/mini/fill-the-disks.html","x":"Turn the seed disks of a planter with dispenser motors until every disk holds seed, so that sowing starts from the first metre — how to run it and read the result."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"","u":"en/mini/fill-the-disks.html","x":"This applies only to planters with seed dispenser electric motors in the “Precise” mode. Without seed motors in the profile, or in another mode, the menu item is not shown.\nWhen the disks are driven by electric motors, they stand empty until the motors run. Drive off with empty disks and the first metres of every row get nothing: each disk first has to pick up seeds and carry them round to the drop point. Filling turns the disks while the machine stands still, so that sowing starts from the first metre.\nWARNING\nThe disks turn while the machine stands still. Before you start, make sure nobody is near the metering units, the dispenser shafts or the drive. Up to eight motors start at once, and each turns for up to about nineteen seconds. From the cab the metering units cannot be seen.\nStop the machine. While it moves, the item is hidden.\nOn the home screen, press “MENU”. Select the first item, “Fill the disks with seeds”, and press “MENU”. It is open at every access level.\nThe panel asks for confirmation. Press “PLUS” or touch “Yes”. “MINUS”, “No”, “BACK” or a touch beside the window cancels.\nThe question before the disks turn.\nWatch the tiles. Each motor turns its disk at 4 rpm of the driven shaft until the seed sensor of its row registers the first seed. While the operation runs, the bottom line tells you that “BACK” aborts it.\nThe operation running: tile 1 is done and green, the others are still turning.\nWhen every motor has finished, the panel gives three short beeps and shows “Operation complete.” Read the tiles at leisure, then press “BACK” to return to the main menu.\nTo stop early, press “BACK”: every motor stops at once, goes back to the seeding logic, and the screen closes. You can start again straight away. On this screen only “BACK” works; the other buttons and touches do nothing."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"Reading the tiles","u":"en/mini/fill-the-disks.html#reading-the-tiles","x":"The end of the operation: tile 1 green, tiles 2 to 4 failed.\nTile\nState\nDark grey, full height\nWaiting its turn — only eight motors run at once.\nYellow, upper part\nRunning: the disk is turning.\nGreen, upper part\nDone: the seed sensor registered a seed.\nDark red, full height, bright red number\nFailed: the disk could not be filled. The letters say why.\nA motor fails if no seed is registered within 1.25 turns of the shaft — about nineteen seconds. A motor that reports a fault is stopped and failed at once; a motor out of control does not turn and counts as failed. Extra motors without a seed sensor of their own turn their disks but are reported as failed, because no seed can be seen. Fertilizer motors take part only if they too run in “Precise”, each paired with the fertilizer sensor of the same number."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fill the disks with seeds","h":"Letters on a failed tile","u":"en/mini/fill-the-disks.html#letters-on-a-failed-tile","x":"The letters stand always in the same places — m left, g middle, s right — and red means a fault, white means out of control.\nA failed tile close up.\nLetter\nMeaning\nm red\nA fault of the motor during the operation.\nm white\nThe motor is out of control and cannot run.\ng red\nThe seed sensor saw no seed while the disk turned.\ns red\nA fault of the seed sensor during the operation.\ns white\nThe seed sensor is out of control, or the motor has no seed sensor paired with it.\nIf the seed sensor is out of control, a serviceable motor still turns the disk about one and a quarter turns, so the disk does get filled — but the panel reports a failure, because it never saw a seed."},{"p":"mini","t":"Assign a product to a dispenser motor","h":"","u":"en/mini/assign-product-to-motor.html","x":"Tell the panel which of its three products each dispenser motor applies, so that one target rate in kg/ha serves all motors of that product."},{"p":"mini","t":"Assign a product to a dispenser motor","h":"","u":"en/mini/assign-product-to-motor.html","x":"Every dispenser electric motor drives one dispenser and belongs to exactly one product. The panel keeps up to three products: “Product 1”, “Product 2” and “Product 3”. Several motors may apply the same product; its rate is then set once for all of them. In the factory state all motors are “Product 1”.\nThis needs the Agronomist level (Enable the Agronomist level) and motors in the “Flow” or “Hybrid” mode.\nCAUTION\nThe active field may have to be erased. If the active field already holds data, the panel demands that its statistics be erased before the product type changes; “Yes” erases them for good. Select an empty field first if you want to keep them — Start a new field.\nGo to the Motors screen and hold “MENU”. The first motor is selected.\nThe Motors screen with a motor selected.\nSelect the motor with “UP” and “DOWN”.\nPress “MENU”. The context menu opens.\nGo to “Type of product” with “DOWN”.\nChoose the product with “PLUS” and “MINUS”.\nType of product before and after the change.\nIf the panel asks to erase the statistics, press “MENU” for “Yes” or “BACK” for “No”.\nThe question before the product type is changed on a field with data.\nPress “BACK”. You are back on the Motors screen. Repeat from step 2 for every other motor.\nWhile you step through the values, a product that a dispenser shaft with weight control already applies is skipped — one product is applied either by motors or by such dispensers, not by both. If no other product is available, the value does not move; that is not a fault.\nThen set the rate of each product: Change the target rate, and calibrate each motor: Calibrate a motor by weight."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate a dispenser motor by weight","h":"","u":"en/mini/calibrate-motor-weight.html","x":"Tell the panel how much product a motor-driven dispenser delivers per shaft turn — what you need, the steps on the screen, and what the failure messages mean."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate a dispenser motor by weight","h":"","u":"en/mini/calibrate-motor-weight.html","x":"The calibration tells the panel how much product the dispenser delivers per turn of its shaft. Without it, the panel cannot turn the rate in kg/ha into a shaft speed. Calibrate every dispenser separately, and again whenever the product or the seed lot changes. It applies to motors in the “Flow” and “Hybrid” modes.\nYou need: scales, a container to put under the dispenser, and the product in the hopper.\nWARNING\nThe dispenser shaft turns while the machine stands still. Before you start, make sure nobody is near the dispensers, the shafts or the drive. From the cab they cannot be seen.\nStop the machine. While it moves, the calibration item is hidden.\nOpen the context menu of the motor — on the Motors screen, or from its product on the rate control screen. Select “Dispenser calibration(weight)” (from the rate control screen: “Calibration, product”) and press “MENU”.\nThe first screen shows the target rates of the products in use — the product of this motor between arrows — and under “Product dosage speed limitation:” the lowest and highest travel speed at which the rates can be applied. Press “PLUS”.\nThe first calibration screen with the speed limits.\nThe screen lists what to do before calibrating: weigh the empty container and note the weight, fill the hopper, put the container under the dispenser, fill the rotor cells. Press “MENU” to fill the rotor cells.\nThe list of actions before the calibration.\nThe motor turns the shaft and the “Rotations” counter runs. After two turns the panel stops the motor and returns. “BACK” stops it earlier.\nPress “UP”. The screen lists the steps of the calibration itself: empty the container, put it under the dispenser, calibrate, stop the motor, weigh. Empty the container and put it back.\nPress “MENU”. The motor runs and the dispenser fills the container. Collect as much as possible — at least 1 kg, at most 60 kg.\nPress “BACK” to stop the motor.\nAfter more than one shaft turn, the bottom line offers “MINUS” to complete the calibration. Press “MINUS”.\nThe calibration screen with the line for completing it.\nWeigh the full container. On the number pad, type the difference between full and empty, in grams, and press “MENU”. The panel stores “Weight, gr” and “Rotations” and returns to the first screen.\nThe number pad asking for the weight in grams.\nPress “BACK” to leave.\nTo discard the calibration instead of step 9, press “BACK”: the panel asks “Are you sure?” and discards on “PLUS”; “MINUS” returns to the calibration.\nCAUTION\nOverwrite the 10000 completely, and never accept an empty field. The pad opens at 10000 every time. Values below 1000 g or above 60000 g are silently set to the limit; an empty field writes unusable values without a word, and the dispenser will no longer apply the rate.\nThe context menu afterwards shows not your grams but an equivalent pair of numbers; only their ratio matters. It is stored by itself."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate a dispenser motor by weight","h":"Messages during the calibration","u":"en/mini/calibrate-motor-weight.html#messages-during-the-calibration","x":"Message\nMeaning and what to do\n“FAILURE” with a reason, e.g. “No connection” or “No data from rotation sensor”\nThe link was lost, the motor reported an alarm, or no shaft turns arrived for ten seconds. The motor is stopped, nothing is stored. “BACK” returns to the first screen; start again.\n“All electric motors not controlled.”\nNo motor is under control. Take the motor into control with “PLUS” on the Motors screen.\nThe motor name in red with “Not controlled”, “No connection” or “FAILURE”\nThe calibration cannot start until that state is cleared.\n“Can not provide dosage!”, limits in red\nThe rates contradict each other: no speed meets them all. Change a rate, or the shaft speed limits — each line names the product that sets it.\n“Run calibration on the console”\nA push-button on the motor controller is set up: the motor runs only while the button is held.\nThe speed must be zero (in English in every language)\nThe machine is moving. Stop it.\nThe failure screen of the calibration."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"","u":"en/mini/tramline-rhythm.html","x":"Tell the Hectare Mini on which bouts which actuator or motor shuts off — the limits, the rhythm editor, its dialogs, and the order that keeps your rhythm from being overwritten."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"","u":"en/mini/tramline-rhythm.html","x":"A tramline is the lane the sprayer will drive in later. You make it while sowing: the panel shuts off seed lines or motors on certain bouts — passes across the field. It counts the passes and starts again at the first after the last. The number of bouts and the list of which device shuts off on which bout make the rhythm. Example: four bouts, actuator 1 shutting off on bout 4 — every fourth pass leaves a tramline.\nLimit\nValue\nRhythms stored\nThree, one active at a time. Keep the patterns of the machines you work with.\nBouts in a rhythm\n1 to 50.\nTramline actuators\nFour at most; the profile item “Tramline actuators” sets how many.\nCut-off bouts per device\nFour.\nThe panel knows nothing more: there is no setting for a starting side or for the width of the tramline. Which seed lines an actuator physically shuts off is set in “Relation of sensors to actuators” (Settings menu); without it the panel counts the bouts but shuts off nothing.\nIMPORTANT\nSet up the profile first, the tramline afterwards. Saving a profile takes over its whole set of settings, all three rhythms included — a rhythm set up before is gone. And check the number of bouts against the sprayer, not the drill: a wrong rhythm looks perfect until the sprayer has nowhere to drive.\nThe item “Tramline” exists only if the machine has a tramline actuator, or as many seed motors as seed sensors, or as many fertilizer motors as fertilizer sensors. Setting up needs the Agronomist level (Enable the Agronomist level).\nPress “MENU”, then open “Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters” → “Tramline”. Set “Tramline” to “Yes” with “PLUS”.\nThe Tramline submenu.\nSelect “Tramline rhythm settings” and press “MENU”. The rhythm editor opens.\nChoose the rhythm number with “PLUS” / “MINUS”, press “MENU” and choose “Select”. (“Active”) appears beside the number. Without this the rhythm is stored but not used.\nPress “DOWN” to the number of bouts, press “MENU”, set “Bouts” with “PLUS” / “MINUS” and confirm with “MENU”.\nPress “DOWN” into the empty row of a section and press “MENU”. Set the device number and the “Bout” on which it shuts off; confirm with “MENU”.\nFor further cut-off bouts of the same device, press “PLUS” to the cell with the “MENU” sign at the end of its row and press “MENU” — up to four.\nPress “BACK”, or hold “HOME”. The rhythm is saved."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"The rhythm editor","u":"en/mini/tramline-rhythm.html#the-rhythm-editor","x":"The rhythm editor with a new rhythm: one bout, nothing shut off yet.\nRead it top down: “Rhythm” with its number between arrows and (Active) if it is in use; under it the number of bouts in a box; then up to three sections — “Tramline actuators activation”, “Tramline motors activation”, “Tramline fert. motors activation” (with both modes “Precise”: “Tramline motors gr.A activation” and “Tramline motors gr.B activation”). Each row names a device — “Actuator”, “Motor”, “Fert.mot.” — and the bouts on which it shuts off; a cell with the “MENU” sign adds a bout or a row. A selected cell is white on blue.\nButton\nWhat it does\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nOne row up or down, across the sections.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nOn the rhythm number: next / previous rhythm. In a row: one cell right / left. On the number of bouts: nothing — open it with “MENU”.\n“MENU”\nOpens the dialog of the selected cell.\n“BACK”\nSaves and returns to the “Tramline” menu.\n“HOME” held\nSaves and returns to the home screen. A short press does nothing here."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"The dialogs","u":"en/mini/tramline-rhythm.html#the-dialogs","x":"Rhythm number: “Select” makes it the one in use; “Reset rhythm settings” deletes its rows and sets its bouts to 1.\nNumber of bouts: lowering it deletes entries for bouts that fall away; a row left with no bout disappears. If the current bout was above the new number, counting restarts at 1.\nRow of a device: the device number and “Remove”. Numbers already used in the section are skipped — read the value, do not count presses.\nBout of a row: “Bout”, “Remove bout” and “Remove all bouts”. Bouts already in the row are skipped. If every bout is already entered, “Unused bouts are not found!” appears instead; any button closes it.\nCAUTION\nThe delete items sit right below the value. One “DOWN” too many and a “MENU” delete the row you are working on. “Reset rhythm settings” also discards unsaved changes to the other two rhythms and an unsaved “Select”."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set up the tramline rhythm","h":"What is stored where","u":"en/mini/tramline-rhythm.html#what-is-stored-where","x":"The three rhythms, the number of bouts, the current bout, “Tramline” and “Auto-switching” belong to the settings of the panel and are stored by themselves shortly after the last touch. The profile holds only how many devices there are; on opening, the editor clears rows for which the profile has no device. With “Tramline” at “No”, all actuators and motors return to sowing.\nWorking in the field: Next tramline bout."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline in the field — bout indicator and next bout","h":"","u":"en/mini/next-tramline-bout.html","x":"Read the tramline indicator, move to the next bout by hand or let the panel do it from the markers or the implement position, and recognise a line shut off for the tramline."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline in the field — bout indicator and next bout","h":"","u":"en/mini/next-tramline-bout.html","x":"As soon as “Tramline” is set to “Yes” (Set up the tramline rhythm), the home screen shows the tramline indicator: a wheel-track symbol, and beside it the current bout on top and, smaller, the number of bouts of the rhythm below. With markers in the profile it stands right of the marker indicator.\nThe home screen during a cut-off bout: the tramline indicator and hatched columns.\nSymbol\nMeaning\nGrey\nThe panel moves to the next bout by itself.\nYellow\nIt does not: “Auto-switching” is off, or the machine gives it nothing to switch by.\nYellow, alternating with a cross\nThe machine could switch by itself, but “Auto-switching” is off.\nThe columns of the lines shut off are hatched light grey; the flow inside is still shown, hatched: green while it stays below the limit, red above it — and then the panel reports “Seeding to tramline”. The limit is a share of the target rate, “Toler.sowing to tramline,%” of the seed sensor (Sensor settings). Over a sensor that does not answer or is out of control the hatching blinks."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline in the field — bout indicator and next bout","h":"Change the bout by hand","u":"en/mini/next-tramline-bout.html#change-the-bout-by-hand","x":"On the home screen, press “BACK”. The bout menu opens. If a rate field is blinking, the first “BACK” only stops the blinking; in the sensor setup mode “BACK” leaves the mode instead.\nThe bout menu.\nTo set a bout, select “Active bout” and change it with “PLUS” / “MINUS”, from 1 to the number of bouts. It is taken over when the menu closes.\nTo advance one bout, select “Next bout” and press “MENU”. The menu closes. This item is there only when the panel cannot switch by itself.\nWhere the panel can switch by itself, the menu offers “Auto-switching” instead: “MENU” toggles it between “Yes” and “No”, beeps and closes the menu.\nNo higher access level is needed for this."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline in the field — bout indicator and next bout","h":"When the panel switches by itself","u":"en/mini/next-tramline-bout.html#when-the-panel-switches-by-itself","x":"It needs something that tells it a bout has ended:\nMachine\nWhat ends a bout\nWith markers\nA change of the marker position. At least one marker sensor must be under control and answering.\nWithout markers\nLifting the drill out of work. The implement position sensor must be under control and answering.\nNeither\nNothing — the menu offers “Next bout” in place of “Auto-switching”.\nBesides, “Auto-switching” must be “Yes” and the bout must really have been driven: while the machine moves (without a speed sensor: while it sows), switching is blocked. It is released when the work stops; the next marker change — or lift — then advances exactly one bout.\nNOTE\nIf the bout does not advance at the headland, check in this order: is “Auto-switching” “Yes”; is the marker or the implement position sensor under control; does it answer. The first is a setting, the other two are the machine."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline in the field — bout indicator and next bout","h":"The actuator itself","u":"en/mini/next-tramline-bout.html#the-actuator-itself","x":"Two settings of a tramline actuator are in its context menu (Sensor settings), Agronomist level:\nItem\nValues\n“Cutoff position”\nThe position in which it shuts off: “Pulled out” (factory setting) or “Pulled in”.\n“Switch”\nWhen it moves: “when seeding” (factory setting) or “always”.\nTwo alarms concern the actuator: “Illegal state” — it reports both end positions at once; “Stall” — it does not reach the position asked. See Tramline alarms."},{"p":"mini","t":"Access levels","h":"","u":"en/mini/access-levels.html","x":"The three access levels of the Hectare Mini — Operator, Agronomist, Service — what each one opens, what happens to the alarms at the higher levels, and how to get back to Operator."},{"p":"mini","t":"Access levels","h":"","u":"en/mini/access-levels.html","x":"The panel hides every setting a driver does not need. What a menu shows depends on the access level chosen under “User” in the “Settings” menu. After every power-on the panel starts at “Operator”.\nLevel\nWho\nWhat it opens\n“Operator”\nThe driver\nEverything for work: the target rates, the fields, the statistics, “Alarm delay”, the date and time, the language. In the context menus: “Information” and the everyday sensor settings such as “Fraction” or “Minimum, rpm”.\n“Agronomist”\nThe farmer, the dealer\nAlso “Seeding machine parameters” (working width, modes, calibrations), the hidden parameters of every sensor, and in the main menu the “Service” menu with “Sensors Configuration” for seed and pressure sensors.\n“Service”\nThe service engineer\nAlso the profile, the configuration of HUB-8 concentrators and tramline actuators, the service screen of the motors, “Reset to defaults” in every context menu and “Reset settings”. The status screen of a sensor shows its address.\nThe list under “User” has a fourth value, “Debug”. It is for the manufacturer and has no use in the field.\nA raise to “Agronomist” or “Service” asks for the PIN of that level. The PINs are different for every panel: the dealer gives them for your panel. Going down asks nothing. How: Enable the Agronomist level.\nIn this manual a parameter or menu item says which level it needs. An item you do not see is above your level, or does not apply to the machine in the profile."},{"p":"mini","t":"Access levels","h":"What happens to the alarms while the level is high","u":"en/mini/access-levels.html#what-happens-to-the-alarms-while-the-level-is-high","x":"IMPORTANT\nAt “Agronomist” and “Service” the panel shows no alarm windows. A fault on a sensor raises no window and no alarm signal until the level is back at “Operator”.\nThe only window that still comes is “User is Agronomist! Alarms will not be shown!” — once per session, as soon as the panel sees seeding at the higher level. See User is Agronomist.\nWhen you go back down to “Operator”, the panel drops the alarms that have cleared in the meantime and shows the ones that are still true, one after another."},{"p":"mini","t":"Access levels","h":"Back to Operator","u":"en/mini/access-levels.html#back-to-operator","x":"In “Settings” set “User” to “Operator” with “MINUS” and press “MENU”, or\nswitch the panel off and on.\nDo one of the two after every commissioning or repair, before the machine goes back to work."},{"p":"mini","t":"Access levels","h":"What is in the Service menu","u":"en/mini/access-levels.html#what-is-in-the-service-menu","x":"The main menu shows “Service” from the “Agronomist” level on. What it holds:\nItem\nLevel\nWhat it is for\n“Profile”\nService\nThe list of devices of the machine. The profile\n“Sensors Configuration”\nAgronomist\nGives a new or replaced device its address. Device configuration\n“Electric motors”\nService\nThe service screen of the dispenser motors; only with motors in the profile.\n“Count seeds always”\nAgronomist\n“Yes”: the Seeding table and the Statistics screen count seeds also in the “Flow” and “Hybrid” modes, not only in “Precise”.\n“Enter key”\nAgronomist\nShown only on a panel with a time-limited licence: enter the licence key from the dealer.\nThe remaining items of this menu are for the service engineer and the manufacturer."},{"p":"mini","t":"Enable the Agronomist level","h":"","u":"en/mini/enable-agronomist-level.html","x":"The steps, with the screen of each, to raise the access level of the Hectare Mini to \"Agronomist\" or \"Service\" before changing settings, and to return to \"Operator\" afterwards."},{"p":"mini","t":"Enable the Agronomist level","h":"","u":"en/mini/enable-agronomist-level.html","x":"Sensor parameters, the working width, the calibrations and the device configuration need the “Agronomist” level. The “Service” level is raised the same way. What each level opens: Access levels.\nYou need the PIN of this panel for that level. It is different for every panel; the dealer gives it.\nOn the home screen press “MENU”. The main menu opens.\nThe main menu.\nSelect “Settings” with “UP” / “DOWN” and press “MENU”. The “Settings” menu opens.\nPress “DOWN” until “User” is selected — it is the last item. Press “PLUS” until it reads “Agronomist” (or “Service”).\n“User” set to Agronomist.\nPress “MENU”. The PIN pad opens with “Enter your PIN:”; the line under the prompt identifies this panel. Type the PIN on the number pad and touch “OK” or press “MENU”.\nThe PIN pad.\nThe panel shows the name of the new level for a moment and returns to the “Settings” menu, now with the items of that level — for example “Seeding machine parameters”.\nThe panel confirms the new level.\nPress “BACK” to leave the menu.\nIf the PIN is wrong — or is the PIN of another level than the one chosen in step 3 — the panel shows “Invalid PIN” for a moment and opens the PIN pad again. Type it again, or press “BACK” to give up; the level stays as it was.\nIMPORTANT\nAt “Agronomist” and “Service” no alarm windows are shown. When you have finished, go back to “Operator”: repeat steps 1–3, choose “Operator” with “MINUS” and press “MENU” — no PIN is asked going down. Switching the panel off and on does the same.\nWhen you come back down, the alarms that are still true are shown one after another — see Access levels."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the profile and the connections","h":"","u":"en/mini/check-profile-and-connections.html","x":"The first check after installing a Hectare Mini — does the profile match the machine, does every device answer, is every sensor under control — as a procedure with what the screen shows at each step."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the profile and the connections","h":"","u":"en/mini/check-profile-and-connections.html","x":"The panel watches only the devices its profile lists. Systems are usually delivered with the profile already set, but before you set any parameter, check that it matches the machine and that every device answers. Checking the profile needs the “Service” level, which asks for a PIN — the dealer gives it for your panel; see Enable the Agronomist level. The other two checks need no code."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the profile and the connections","h":"1. Check the profile","u":"en/mini/check-profile-and-connections.html#check-the-profile","x":"Connect all devices to the panel and power them as the wiring diagram in the data sheet of the system shows.\nSwitch the panel on. After the splash screen the home screen appears.\nRaise the access level to “Service”: “MENU” → “Settings” → “User” → “Service”, “MENU”, PIN — as in Enable the Agronomist level.\nPress “BACK”. The main menu returns, now with “Service”. Select “Service” and press “MENU”, then select “Profile” and press “MENU”. The profile menu opens.\nThe way to the profile: Settings with the Service level, the main menu, the Service menu, the profile menu.\nRead the profile menu, from the top. Each item is one kind of device with the number the panel expects, for example “Seed sensors”; “Speed” shows how the speed sensor is connected: “Control panel”, “Input device” or “Input device (2)”. “UP” and “DOWN” go through the list, which is longer than the screen. All items: The profile.\nThe profile menu.\nCompare the list with the machine and the wiring diagram. Change nothing here: “BACK” leaves the profile menu without saving.\nGo back to the “Operator” level: switch the panel off and on, as in Enable the Agronomist level. At the “Service” level the panel shows no alarm windows, and the next check needs them.\nIf the profile does not match the machine, set it up first: Set up the profile."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the profile and the connections","h":"2. Check that every device answers","u":"en/mini/check-profile-and-connections.html#check-that-every-device-answers","x":"Wait a few seconds on the home screen. If no window “Connection lost” appears, the panel has found every device of the profile.\n“Connection lost” for fan sensor 1.\nWhen several devices of one kind do not answer, one window lists all their numbers — here pressure sensors 1 to 3:\n“Connection lost” for three pressure sensors.\nIMPORTANT\nA device that does not answer is not watched. Check its connection against the wiring diagram and the profile once more. If you cannot find the cause, call the service — see Device does not answer."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the profile and the connections","h":"3. Check that every sensor is under control","u":"en/mini/check-profile-and-connections.html#check-that-every-sensor-is-under-control","x":"A sensor out of control is not watched at all. Its indicator shows the blinking grey not-controlled sign, or !!!! in place of the value; under a diagram column it shows a white box with ! — see Indicators.\nThe hopper sensor, the fan sensor and seed sensor 11 out of control.\nIf there is such a sensor, take it into control:\nOn the home screen hold “MENU” until the speed indicator has a white background. The panel is in the sensor setup mode. Release the button.\nThe sensor setup mode, the speed sensor selected.\nPress “DOWN” (or “UP”) until the sensor out of control is selected. A seed sensor is selected when its number under the column has a white background.\nThe fan sensor selected.\nPress “PLUS”. The panel asks whether you are sure; press “MENU” for “Yes”. “BACK” answers “No”.\nThe question before the control of a sensor changes.\nRepeat steps 2 and 3 for every sensor out of control. The question comes only the first time while you stay in this mode.\nPress “BACK” to leave the sensor setup mode.\nSwitching the control of a sensor on is what this manual calls taking a sensor into control. More: Take a sensor into or out of control."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the profile and the connections","h":"The result","u":"en/mini/check-profile-and-connections.html#the-result","x":"Every sensor must be under control, and no indicator may show a red cross — the sign of a device that does not answer.\nWhen everything is right, the home screen shows every device in its normal grey, with no red cross and no not-controlled sign:\nAll devices answer and are under control.\nThen go on with the settings: Commissioning."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html","x":"The checks to run once after mounting a Hectare Mini — every sensor in its own row, counting, work position, speed and the other sensors — with what the screen must show at each step and where to go when it does not."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html","x":"Run these checks once, when the system is mounted and before the first field. Each step says what to do and what the screen must show. If a step fails, the last column of the table at the end tells you where to go. Do not skip a step because the one before it was fine.\nYou need a few seeds of the crop and a helper. All checks are done at the “Operator” level, where the panel shows its alarm windows."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"1. The profile and the connections","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html#the-profile-and-the-connections","x":"Do the three checks of Check the profile and the connections. Passed means: the profile list matches the machine, no window “Connection lost” appears after a few seconds on the home screen, and no indicator shows a red cross or a not-controlled sign. The home screen then looks like this:\nAll devices answer and are under control.\nGo on only when this is so."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"2. Every seed sensor sits in its own row","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html#every-seed-sensor-sits-in-its-own-row","x":"Each seed sensor must stand under the number of its own row. Check it row by row:\nSwitch the panel on and press “HOME”. The Seeding table opens. Press “PLUS” until the Seed counters table is shown, with one row per seed line. Read the count on this table, which shows the number of seeds — not on the view with seeds per metre: that view shows values only while the machine moves.\nThe Seed counters table.\nPress “MENU” and “PLUS” for “Yes”, to zero the readings — Zero the readings of the Seeding table.\nDrop a few seeds by hand through the seed tube of row 1. Instead of seeds you may pass any small object, for example a long cable tie, through the seed tube several times — each pass is counted. Watch the table: the figures of row 1 must change.\nDo the same for row 2, then for every other row, one after the other. After each row, look at which row of the table reacted.\nWrite down every row where another row reacted, or none did.\nA sensor that reacts in another row is connected in the wrong order. A row that does not react at all needs a look at its sensor.\nIf the order is wrong, give the sensor the number of its row — Configure a seed sensor. If a row stays silent, open the status screen of its sensor (step 7 shows how): Status: must read “Working”. For any other state, see Seed sensor state; for a sensor that does not answer, see Device does not answer."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"3. Counting","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html#counting","x":"Zero the readings as in step 2 of the previous check.\nCount a known number of seeds, for example 20, and drop them through the seed tube of one row.\nRead the figure of that row on the Seed counters table. On a precision planter it matches the number you dropped to within one seed, provided the “Fraction” of the seed sensors is set for the size of the seed.\nIf the figure is clearly higher — up to twice the number — the fraction is set too small for this seed: a large seed, for example maize, is then counted as two. Set the fraction by the table in Seed and fertilizer sensors."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"4. The work position sensor","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html#the-work-position-sensor","x":"Skip this step if the profile has no work position sensor.\nLower the machine into the working position. The status screen of the work position sensor must read “Position: Working”.\nRaise the machine into the transport position. The status screen must read “Position: Transporting”, and on the home screen the implement indicator blinks yellow.\nLower and raise it once more. The state must follow every time.\nThe status screen of the work position sensor.\nIf the state is the wrong way round, or does not change, see Hopper and work position sensors: the parameter “Transporting position signal” must match what the sensor gives with the machine raised."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"5. Speed","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html#speed","x":"Drive the machine slowly in the working position.\nThe speed indicator on the home screen must show a speed, not zero and not a red cross.\nCompare it with the speedometer of the tractor or a GPS navigator, as in Quick start.\nIf no speed is shown, see Speed sensor alarms. If the speed is wrong by a constant share, calibrate the sensor — Calibrate the speed sensor."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"6. The other sensors of the profile","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html#the-other-sensors-of-the-profile","x":"Check only the sensors your profile lists. For each, open its status screen as in step 7 and look at the “Link:” line: it must read “Normal”.\nSensor\nWhat to do\nWhat the screen must show\nIf not\nFan\nStart the fan.\nOn the home screen a fan speed in rpm, not red. On the status screen F= in Hz.\nFan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms\nPressure\nStart the fan.\nP= in kPa on the status screen, no alarm window.\nPressure alarms\nHopper\nLook at the status screen with product in the hopper.\nPin: reads \"0\" while product covers the sensor.\nHopper empty\nDispenser shaft\nOpen the status screen.\nF= in Hz, and no alarm window “No rotation” while the machine sows or moves.\nFan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms"},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"7. Record the result","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html#record-the-result","x":"Write down, or take a photo of, what you need if you later have to ask for help:\nThe firmware version. On the home screen press “MENU” and choose the last item of the main menu. The window shows the version of the software, the serial number of the panel and its hardware identifier. “BACK” closes it.\nThe date of the check.\nA photo of the sensor status screen of every sensor that gave you trouble, and of one sensor that works. To reach it, hold “MENU” on the home screen until the speed indicator has a white background, select the sensor with “UP” / “DOWN”, press “MENU”, then select “Information” and press “MENU”. “BACK” leaves — The sensor status screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Check the system after installation","h":"If a step fails","u":"en/mini/check-after-installation.html#if-a-step-fails","x":"Step\nGo to\n1. Profile and connections\nSet up the profile, Device does not answer\n2. Sensor in the wrong row\nConfigure a seed sensor\n2. A row does not react\nSeed sensor state, Device does not answer\n3. Counting\nSeed and fertilizer sensors\n4. Work position\nHopper and work position sensors\n5. Speed\nSpeed sensor alarms, Calibrate the speed sensor\n6. Fan, dispenser shaft\nFan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms\n6. Pressure\nPressure alarms\n6. Hopper\nHopper empty\nStill not clear\nBefore you contact support"},{"p":"mini","t":"The profile","h":"","u":"en/mini/profile.html","x":"What the profile of the Hectare Mini is, every item of the profile menu with its values, and the rules a profile must keep before the panel accepts it."},{"p":"mini","t":"The profile","h":"","u":"en/mini/profile.html","x":"The profile tells the panel which devices the machine has and how many of each. It decides which indicators the home screen shows, which context menus exist and which devices the panel polls. A system is usually delivered with its profile set; change it only when the equipment of the machine changes. How to check it: Check the profile and the connections. How to change it: Set up the profile.\nThe profile counts what is watched and what is driven, not boxes. One input device can carry, for example, fan sensors and hopper sensors together; one actuator controller can carry an actuator and two marker sensors. Each of them is entered under its own item.\n“Seed sensors” — the selected item.\nThe value, here 12, between − and +: change it with “MINUS” and “PLUS”.\n“Fertilizer sensors”.\n“Liq.fert.flow sensors”.\n“Hoppers”.\n“Dispensers”.\n“Speed” — how the speed sensor is connected.\nIts value, here “Control panel”.\nScroll bar. The list goes on below the screen.\nThe first items of the profile menu."},{"p":"mini","t":"The profile","h":"The items","u":"en/mini/profile.html#the-items","x":"Every item needs the “Service” level, like the menu itself.\nItem\nWhat it states\nValues\n“Seed sensors”\nNumber of seed sensors.\n2 … 60\n“Fertilizer sensors”\nNumber of dry fertilizer sensors.\n0 … 60\n“Liq.fert.flow sensors”\nNumber of liquid fertilizer flow sensors.\n0 … 60\n“Hoppers”\nNumber of hopper level sensors.\n0 … 9\n“Dispensers”\nNumber of dispenser shaft sensors.\n0 … 9\n“Speed”\nWhether there is a travel speed sensor and where it is connected: “No”; “Control panel” — one sensor on the panel; “Input device” — one sensor on an input device; “Input device (2)” — two sensors on an input device.\nsee left\n“GPS”\nThe built-in GPS receiver as a speed source: “No”; “UBX” — the normal setting; “NMEA” — the receiver delivers its data in the NMEA 0183 format. While it has a valid fix, GPS takes priority over the sensor under “Speed”; without a fix the sensor takes over by itself. GPS reception\nsee left\n“Fans”\nNumber of fan speed sensors.\n0 … 2\n“Pressure sensors”\nNumber of pressure (vacuum) sensors.\n0 … 3\n“Work position sensor”\nWhether there is a sensor for the working and transport position of the implement.\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Markers”\nHow the marker arms are watched: “No”; “One” — one sensor; “Both” — one sensor per arm.\nsee left\n“No battery charge”\nBattery charge sensor of the fan-drive engine. Switch it off together with the ignition sensor.\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Emergency oil pressure”\nLow oil pressure sensor of the fan-drive engine. Switch it off together with the ignition sensor.\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Ignition”\nIgnition key sensor of the fan-drive engine.\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Low fuel”\nFuel level sensor of the fan-drive engine.\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Air filter”\nAir filter sensor of the fan-drive engine.\n“No” / “Yes”\n“EMD of the seeds”\nNumber of electric dispenser motors for seed.\n0 … 60\n“EMD of the fertilizers”\nNumber of electric dispenser motors for fertilizer.\n0 … 30\n“Tramline actuators”\nNumber of tramline actuators.\n0 … 4\n“Liq.fert.pump controller”\nWhether there is a pump controller for liquid fertilizer.\n0 / 1\n“Save profile”\nSaves the profile and restarts the panel.\n—\nIMPORTANT\n“GPS” works only with a speed sensor entered under “Speed”. If “Speed” is “No”, the panel sets “GPS” back to “No” by itself. On a drill without a drive wheel set “Speed” to “Control panel” — no sensor needs to be connected there."},{"p":"mini","t":"The profile","h":"Rules the profile must keep","u":"en/mini/profile.html#rules-the-profile-must-keep","x":"“Save profile” refuses a profile that breaks one of these rules and shows “Invalid profile!”:\nat least 2 seed sensors;\n“Hoppers”, “Dispensers” and the five engine sensors together: at most 12; above 10, “Fans” and “Pressure sensors” together may be at most 4;\n“Fans” and “Pressure sensors” together: at most 5;\nseed and fertilizer motors together: at most 60; fertilizer motors only together with seed motors and with fertilizer sensors;\ndispenser motors and a liquid pump controller need a speed sensor."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set up the profile","h":"","u":"en/mini/set-up-the-profile.html","x":"The steps to change the profile of the Hectare Mini — reach the profile menu at the Service level, enter the devices of the machine, save, and let the panel restart."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set up the profile","h":"","u":"en/mini/set-up-the-profile.html","x":"Set up the profile when the equipment of the machine changes — a device added, removed or of another kind. The items and their limits: The profile. The profile menu needs the “Service” level.\nWARNING\nChange the profile only at commissioning, by the service. A wrong profile makes the panel watch devices that are not there and ignore the ones that are.\nConnect all devices to the panel and power them as the wiring diagram in the data sheet of the system shows.\nSwitch the panel on. After the splash screen the home screen appears; what it shows depends on the present profile.\nThe home screen after start-up.\nRaise the access level to “Service”: “MENU” → “Settings” → “User” → “Service”, “MENU”, PIN — as in Enable the Agronomist level.\nPress “BACK”. The main menu returns, now with “Service”.\nSelect “Service” and press “MENU”. The “Service” menu opens with “Profile” as its first item.\nSelect “Profile” and press “MENU”. The profile menu opens.\nThe way to the profile: Settings with the Service level, the main menu, the Service menu, the profile menu.\nGo through the items with “UP” / “DOWN” and set each with “PLUS” / “MINUS” to the equipment of the machine.\nThe profile menu.\nSelect “Save profile”, the last item, and press “MENU”. The panel asks “Are you sure?” — press “PLUS” for “Yes”, “MINUS” for “No”.\nThe panel asks you to wait, then shows “Saved” and “Restart system”, and restarts by itself. The new profile is in force after the restart.\nSaving the profile: the item Save profile, the question, then the message Saved and Restart system.\n“BACK” leaves the profile menu; the device numbers you changed are not saved. “GPS” is the exception: it is stored at once, like a setting of the “Settings” menu."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set up the profile","h":"When the profile is not saved","u":"en/mini/set-up-the-profile.html#when-the-profile-is-not-saved","x":"The panel shows\nMeaning\n“No changes”\nThe profile is the same as the stored one. Nothing is saved, nothing restarts.\n“Invalid profile!”\nThe profile breaks a rule — too few seed sensors, too many small indicators, too many fans and pressure sensors, or motors without a speed sensor. See Rules the profile must keep. Correct it and save again.\nAfter the restart the panel is at the “Operator” level. Check the result as in Check the profile and the connections, then, if the machine makes tramlines, set up the tramline rhythm — always after the profile — Set up the tramline rhythm."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Settings menu","h":"","u":"en/mini/settings-menu.html","x":"Every item of the Settings menu of the Hectare Mini — sound, language, diagram settings, date and time, alarm delay, product rate error, machine parameters, user — with its access level, range and default."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Settings menu","h":"","u":"en/mini/settings-menu.html","x":"“MENU” on the home screen → “Settings”. The menu holds the general settings of the panel. Which items you see depends on the access level (Access levels) and on the machine in the profile.\n“Volume” — the selected item.\nIts value between − and +. “PLUS” and “MINUS” change it on the spot.\n“Language”.\n“Buttons sound”.\n“Change Date&Time” — the arrow head opens a submenu.\n“Alarm delay”, in seconds.\n“Seeding machine parameters” — a submenu, from the Agronomist level.\nScroll bar. “User” follows further down.\nThe “Settings” menu at the Agronomist level.\nThe panel stores every change by itself about one and a half seconds after the last change and the last touch of a button."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Settings menu","h":"The items","u":"en/mini/settings-menu.html#the-items","x":"Item\nLevel\nWhat it does\nRange · default\n“Volume”\nOperator\nLoudness of the alarm signal, in per cent.\n0 … 100, steps of 10 · 100\n“Language”\nOperator\nLanguage of the texts on the screen: English, Ukrainian, Polish, Hungarian, Romanian, Bulgarian or German.\n· English\n“Buttons sound”\nOperator\nA short beep at every touch the panel accepts.\n“No” / “Yes” · “Yes”\n“Seeding diagram settings”\nOperator\nHow the seed diagram of the home screen is drawn — below. Shown only with a speed sensor and “Mode of seeding” at “Hybrid” or “Precise”.\nsubmenu\n“Fertil. diagram settings”\nOperator\nThe same for the fertilizer diagram. Shown only with a speed sensor, fertilizer sensors and “Mode of fertilizers” at “Hybrid” or “Precise”.\nsubmenu\n“Liq.fert. diagram settings”\nOperator\n“Grid” of the liquid fertilizer diagram. Shown only with a speed sensor, liquid fertilizer sensors and “Mode of liq.fert.” at “By area”.\nsubmenu\n“Change Date&Time”\nOperator\nDate and time of the panel — Set the date and time.\nsubmenu\n“Alarm delay”\nOperator\nHow long a fault must last before the alarm window opens — How alarms work.\n2 … 20 s · 3 s\n“Product rate error,%”\nAgronomist\nPermitted deviation of the applied product weight from its target; beyond it the panel raises “Rate of Product 1 is to low!” or “Rate of Product 1 is to high!” (with the number of the product). Shown only when a product is dosed by weight, or with a liquid pump controller.\n3 … 20 % · 5 %\n“Seeding machine parameters”\nAgronomist\nWorking width, control modes, calibrations — Seeding machine parameters.\nsubmenu\n“User”\nOperator\nThe access level — Enable the Agronomist level.\n“Operator” … “Debug”\n“Reset settings”\nService\nReturns the values of this menu and of “Seeding machine parameters” to the factory values at once, without asking — including “Language” (English), “Width, cm”, the modes, the active field and the “GPS” setting of the profile.\n—\nIMPORTANT\nThe weight is calculated correctly only after a fresh dispenser calibration whenever the metering roller has been changed, the variator or gearbox of the dispenser drive has been adjusted, or another seed is in the hopper. See Calibrate the dispenser by weight."},{"p":"mini","t":"The Settings menu","h":"Diagram settings","u":"en/mini/settings-menu.html#diagram-settings","x":"The submenus “Seeding diagram settings” and “Fertil. diagram settings” hold the same items, each for its own diagram. When the fertilizer sensors run in “Precise”, they are treated as a second seed group, and the items are named “Seeding gr.A diagram” and “Seeding gr.B diagram”.\nItem\nLevel\nWhat it does\nValues · default\n“Skips & Doubles”\nOperator\nShows or hides the columns of skips and doubles over the last completed stretch. Only in “Precise”.\n“No” / “Yes” · “Yes”\n“Grid”\nOperator\n“No” — only the target rate line; “Partial” — only the limits of the permitted deviation; “Full” — both.\n· “Full”\n“Scale S&D,%”\nOperator\nFull height of a skips or doubles column, in per cent of the diagram height. Only in “Precise”.\n5 … 50 %, steps of 5 · 20 %\n“Reset to defaults”\nService\nReturns the three items above to their defaults.\n—\nWhat the diagram shows: The section diagram."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set the date and time","h":"","u":"en/mini/set-date-and-time.html","x":"Set the clock of the Hectare Mini by hand, or let it follow the GPS receiver with a time zone."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set the date and time","h":"","u":"en/mini/set-date-and-time.html","x":"The panel stamps the start and end of the work on every field with its clock, and shows the time on the home screen. “Change Date&Time” is in the “Settings” menu at the “Operator” level. What it opens depends on the “GPS” item of the profile."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set the date and time","h":"With GPS in the profile","u":"en/mini/set-date-and-time.html#with-gps-in-the-profile","x":"“Automatic” — whether the clock follows the GPS receiver.\nIts value, here “No”. Change it with “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\n“Enter date and time” — opens the menu for setting the clock by hand. Shown while “Automatic” is “No”.\nThe date and time menu of a panel with the built-in GPS receiver switched on.\n“Automatic” at “Yes”: the clock takes the time from the GPS receiver. The item “Time zone” appears instead of “Enter date and time” — set your offset from UTC with “PLUS” / “MINUS”, from −12:00 to +14:00 in whole hours.\n“Automatic” at “No”: select “Enter date and time” and press “MENU”, then go on as below from step 2."},{"p":"mini","t":"Set the date and time","h":"By hand","u":"en/mini/set-date-and-time.html#by-hand","x":"“MENU” → “Settings” → “Change Date&Time”, press “MENU”. Without GPS in the profile the menu opens with “Year”, “Month”, “Day”, “Hours”, “Minutes”, “Seconds” and “Set”.\nSelect an item with “UP” / “DOWN” and change it with “PLUS” / “MINUS”. “MENU” on a number — all items but “Month” — opens the number pad.\nSet all six items. After a change of the month, a day that the month does not have is pulled back to its last day.\nSelect “Set” and press “MENU”. The clock takes the new date and time, and the menu you came from returns.\n“BACK” leaves without changing the clock. Every item opens with the present date and time, so you only change what is wrong."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"","u":"en/mini/seeding-machine-parameters.html","x":"The submenu with the working width and the control modes of the Hectare Mini, what changing them does to the statistics, and where the two menu calibrations start."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"","u":"en/mini/seeding-machine-parameters.html","x":"“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters”, “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level). Set these first on a new machine — every figure of area and rate is worked out from them.\n“Width, cm” — the selected item.\nIts value between -[ and ]+: “PLUS” / “MINUS” step it, “MENU” opens the number pad.\n“Mode of seeding”.\nIts value, here “Flow”.\n“Speed calibration” — starts the calibration of the speed sensor.\nA machine with seed sensors only, a speed sensor and no dispenser sensors.\nItem\nWhat it does\nRange · default\n“Width, cm”\nThe working width of the machine. The area, and every rate per hectare, is worked out from it. Always enter it.\n100 … 2500 cm · 560\n“Mode of seeding”\nHow the seed sensors are controlled and shown: “Flow” — seeds per second, for grain drills; “Hybrid” — seeds per metre, possible on grain drills but not used in practice; “Precise” — seeds per metre with skips and doubles, for precision planters.\n· “Flow”\n“Mode of fertilizers”\nThe same three modes for the fertilizer sensors. Shown only with fertilizer sensors in the profile.\n· “Flow”\n“Mode of liq.fert.”\n“Flow” or “By area” for the liquid fertilizer sensors. Shown only with such sensors and no pump controller.\n· “By area”\n“Tramline”\nThe tramline settings — Set up the tramline rhythm. Shown only if the machine can make tramlines.\nsubmenu\n“Relation of sensors to actuators”\nWhich seed lines each tramline actuator or motor shuts off. Shown only when that is not one to one.\nscreen\n“Speed calibration”\nCalibrate the speed sensor.\n—\n“Dispenser calibration”\nCalibrate the dispenser rotation for “ALL” dispensers or for one, by its number. Shown only with dispenser sensors in the profile.\n“ALL”, 1 … number of dispensers\n“Reset to defaults”\n“Service” level. Returns the width and the modes to their defaults.\n—\nWithout a speed sensor in the profile the three mode items and “Speed calibration” are hidden: the panel can only work in “Flow”.\nThe target rate is set on the home screen, not here — Change the target rate. Its range follows the mode: “Flow” 1 to 1500 seeds per second, “Hybrid” 1 to 200 seeds per metre, “Precise” 0.5 to 50.0 seeds per metre."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding machine parameters","h":"What a change does to the statistics","u":"en/mini/seeding-machine-parameters.html#what-a-change-does-to-the-statistics","x":"IMPORTANT\nA new width or a change of mode to or from “Precise” erases the statistics of every field. If any field holds data, the panel asks first — for the width “Need to erase statistics!” with “PLUS” for “Yes”, for the mode a message that the statistics must be erased, with “MENU” to delete and “BACK” to cancel. “No” or “BACK” keeps the old value. Note the figures you need before you change.\nA change between “Flow” and “Hybrid” keeps the statistics.\nThe two modes depend on each other: the fertilizer sensors can run in “Precise” only while the seed sensors do. The panel refuses the other combinations with a message that names the item to change first. When both run in “Precise”, the fertilizer sensors become a second seed group, and the items read “Mode of seeding gr.A” and “Mode of seeding gr.B”.\nIf a product dosed by weight is then claimed both by a dispenser sensor and by a motor, the panel switches “Weight control” of the dispenser sensors off and says so in a “WARNING!” window."},{"p":"mini","t":"Sensor settings","h":"","u":"en/mini/sensor-settings.html","x":"How to reach the settings of one sensor of the Hectare Mini, what every context menu holds, which settings are shared by a group, and which calibrations exist."},{"p":"mini","t":"Sensor settings","h":"","u":"en/mini/sensor-settings.html","x":"Every sensor, motor and actuator has its own context menu: its status screen, its parameters and, for some, a calibration. The parameters of each kind of sensor are in the topics that follow this one."},{"p":"mini","t":"Sensor settings","h":"Reach a sensor","u":"en/mini/sensor-settings.html#reach-a-sensor","x":"On the home screen hold “MENU” until the speed indicator has a white background. The panel is in the sensor setup mode. Release the button.\nThe sensor setup mode, the speed sensor selected.\nSelect the sensor with “UP” / “DOWN”. A selected seed or fertilizer sensor shows its number under the column on a white background.\nPress “MENU”. The context menu of the selected sensor opens.\nChange a value with “UP” / “DOWN” and “PLUS” / “MINUS”, as in any menu. “BACK” closes the context menu; “BACK” once more leaves the sensor setup mode.\nWhat the context menu holds depends on the kind of sensor, on the machine and on the access level. A parameter you do not see is either above your level or does not apply — the topic of each sensor says which."},{"p":"mini","t":"Sensor settings","h":"The context menu","u":"en/mini/sensor-settings.html#the-context-menu","x":"“Information” — opens the status screen of this sensor. Every context menu starts with it. The sensor status screen\nAn action of this kind of sensor — here “Speed calibration”. A dispenser sensor has “Dispenser calibration(weight)” instead; seed and fertilizer sensors have “Product application”.\nA parameter — here “Pulses” of the speed sensor.\nIts value. Select the line; the value stands between − and + and changes with “MINUS” / “PLUS”.\nScroll bar. More parameters follow; the last item is “Reset to defaults”.\nThe context menu of the speed sensor at the “Agronomist” level.\n“Reset to defaults” returns the parameters of this sensor to the factory values. It needs the “Service” level and acts at once, without asking.\nThe context menu reached from the status screen with “MENU” has “Disable control” or “Enable control” as its first item instead of “Information” — see Take a sensor into or out of control."},{"p":"mini","t":"Sensor settings","h":"One set for a group, or one per sensor","u":"en/mini/sensor-settings.html#one-set-for-a-group-or-one-per-sensor","x":"Sensors\nSettings\nSeed sensors\nOne set for all of them. Change it on any seed sensor — it applies to every one. Only “Product application” is set per line.\nFertilizer sensors\nOne set for all fertilizer sensors, separate from the seed sensors. “Product application” per line.\nAll other sensors\nEach sensor has its own settings — set every dispenser, fan or pressure sensor separately."},{"p":"mini","t":"Sensor settings","h":"Calibrations","u":"en/mini/sensor-settings.html#calibrations","x":"What it puts right\nWhere\nLevel\nMachine\nTravel speed — “Factor, %” of the speed sensor, over 100 m. Calibrate the speed sensor\n“Seeding machine parameters” → “Speed calibration”, or the context menu of the speed sensor\nAgronomist; from the context menu also Operator with a PIN\ndrives 100 m\nDispenser rotation — “Wheel, pulses” against “Dispenser pulses”. Calibrate the dispenser rotation\n“Seeding machine parameters” → “Dispenser calibration”\nAgronomist\ndrive wheel turned\nDispenser by weight — “Weight, gr” and “Weight, pulses”. Calibrate the dispenser by weight\ncontext menu of the dispenser sensor → “Dispenser calibration(weight)”\nOperator\nstanding\nDispenser motor by weight. Calibrate a motor by weight\ncontext menu of the motor\nOperator\nstanding\nThe two weight calibrations are at the “Operator” level because they must be repeated whenever the product changes."},{"p":"mini","t":"Sensor settings","h":"The kinds of sensor","u":"en/mini/sensor-settings.html#the-kinds-of-sensor","x":"Speed sensor · Seed and fertilizer sensors · Dispenser sensor · Fan sensor · Pressure sensor · Hopper and work position sensors · Engine sensors · Marker sensors · Dispenser motors"},{"p":"mini","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/sensor-status-screen.html","x":"How to read the status screen of a Hectare Mini sensor — type, unit, link, live figures and active alarms — and what each kind of sensor shows on it."},{"p":"mini","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/sensor-status-screen.html","x":"“Information” in the context menu of a sensor opens its status screen (Sensor settings). It shows what the panel knows about this one sensor right now — the first place to look when an alarm is not clear.\nChannel selector — which sensor of the whole list this is. “MINUS” shows the previous sensor, “PLUS” the next; the screen stays open.\n“Type:” — the kind of sensor and its number.\n“Unit:” — where the sensor is connected, here “Control panel”. Sensors on the sensor network have no such line.\n“Link:” — the state of the communication. Table below.\nThe live figure of this kind of sensor — here the pulse frequency F= of the speed sensor, in Hz.\n“Min speed, km/h” in force. ---- means switched off.\n“Max speed, km/h” in force.\nThe status screen of a speed sensor connected to the panel, machine standing.\nAt the “Service” level a line with the address of the sensor in the sensor network is added at the top. When the sensor has active alarms, two red lines appear at the bottom: “Alarm 1 from 2” and the name of the alarm shown.\nButton\nAction\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nNext / previous sensor.\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nNext / previous active alarm of this sensor.\n“MENU”\nThe context menu of this sensor, with “Disable control” or “Enable control” as first item. At “Operator”, while the machine moves, “MENU” does nothing on the speed sensor.\n“BACK”\nBack to the screen you came from."},{"p":"mini","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"Link states","u":"en/mini/sensor-status-screen.html#link-states","x":"State\nMeaning\n“Normal”\nThe sensor answers.\n“Not controlled”\nThe sensor is out of control: neither its communication nor anything else is watched.\n“No connection”\nThe sensor does not answer. Red.\n“Wrong device type!”\nSomething answers, but not the kind of device the profile expects. Red.\n“IMITATION”\nSpeed sensor only: the speed is simulated, not measured. Yellow.\nThe live figures and the alarms are shown only while the sensor is under control, answers, and is of the right kind."},{"p":"mini","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"What each kind of sensor shows","u":"en/mini/sensor-status-screen.html#what-each-kind-of-sensor-shows","x":"Sensor\nLive figures\nSpeed sensor\nF= in Hz; with two sensors F1= and F2=. Then “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h”. With motors that dose by weight, the limits are those the motors can hold, narrowed by the two settings; a limit the settings cannot meet is written in red.\nSeed and fertilizer sensors\nStatus: and the state of the sensor — below.\nDispenser sensor\nF= in Hz, to three decimals.\nFan sensor\nF= in Hz.\nPressure sensor\nP= in kPa.\nHopper sensor\nPin: with the level on the input, \"0\" or \"1\".\nWork position sensor\n“Position: Working” or “Position: Transporting”, and Pin: with the level on the input.\nIgnition, oil pressure, battery charge, fuel level, air filter\nPin: with the level on the input.\nLiquid fertilizer flow sensor\nStatus: “Working”, or Working:Tramline in a tramline bout; Flow= in L/min.\nDispenser motor\nStatus: “Working” or “Faulty”; after a fault Last fault: with the code of the controller."},{"p":"mini","t":"The sensor status screen","h":"States of a seed or fertilizer sensor","u":"en/mini/sensor-status-screen.html#states-of-a-seed-or-fertilizer-sensor","x":"Status\nMeaning\n“Working”\nNormal. Working:Tramline — normal, in the tramline mode of this bout.\n“Configuring”\nThe sensor has received new settings from the panel and is applying them.\n“Calibration in progress”\nThe sensor runs its initial calibration; it takes a few seconds.\n“Clogged”\nSomething blocks the optical channel — the coulter or seed tube may be blocked. Red.\n“Light struck”\nToo much sunlight falls on the sensor; it cannot count. Red.\n“Faulty”\nThe sensor has failed and must be replaced. Red.\n“Unknown”\nAnother fault that cannot be identified. Red."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/speed-sensor.html","x":"Every parameter of the travel speed sensor of the Hectare Mini — pulses, count period, wheel diameter, factor, seeding criteria, speed limits, imitation — with its range and default."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/speed-sensor.html","x":"Area, rates, skips and doubles are all worked out from the distance travelled. A speed sensor that is out by a tenth makes every figure on the panel wrong by a tenth. Set “Pulses” and “Diameter, cm”, then calibrate (Calibrate the speed sensor) — the calibration sets “Factor, %”. The speed sensor cannot be taken out of control.\nOpen its context menu as in Sensor settings. Its parameters need the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level).\nThe context menu of the speed sensor.\n“Speed calibration”, right under “Information”, is shown only while the machine stands. At “Operator” it asks for the PIN of a higher level.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nRange · default\n“Pulses”\nPulses of the sensor per revolution of the shaft it sits on.\n1 … 1000 · 6\n“Count period”\nOver how many of the latest pulse intervals the panel averages the speed. The panel always takes at least 8 and at most 16, none older than two seconds: a higher value gives a steadier but slower figure, values below 8 act as 8. Shown only for a sensor connected to the panel.\n1 … 1000 · 1\n“Diameter, cm”\nDiameter of the drive wheel.\n10 … 200 · 60\n“Factor, %”\nGear ratio between the wheel and the shaft of the sensor, for a sensor on an intermediate shaft or on the dispenser shaft driven by gears or chain. 100 is a direct 1 : 1 drive. Best left to the calibration.\n1 … 10000 · 100\n“Seeding criteria”\n“Yes”: travel is part of the condition under which the panel starts to control the seeding — see When the panel controls the seeding. “No”: the travel speed is left out of that condition — for drills whose speed sensor sits before the clutch. Hidden, and always “Yes”, in the “Precise” mode and on machines with dispenser motors.\n“No” / “Yes” · “Yes”\n“Min speed, km/h”\nBelow this speed, while the panel is controlling, the speed indicator shows “FASTER”. “No” switches the check off.\n“No”, 1 … 99 · “No”\n“Max speed, km/h”\nAbove this speed, while the panel is controlling, the speed indicator shows “SLOWER”. “No” switches the check off.\n“No”, 1 … 99 · “No”\n“Imitation, km/h”\nA simulated speed, to keep working with a faulty sensor. “No” = the sensor is used.\n“No”, 1 … 20 · “No”\n“Width, cm”\nDistance between the centres of the two wheels. Shown only with “Input device (2)” in the profile.\n0 … 10000 · 280\nThe minimum must stay below the maximum: a value that would reach the other limit is not accepted. On a machine with dispenser motors the limits in force are also narrowed to the speeds the motors can dose; if a limit cannot be met, the panel raises “Error in settings of Min/Max” (Speed sensor alarms)."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"Working out the factor","u":"en/mini/speed-sensor.html#working-out-the-factor","x":"If you do not calibrate, the factor can be calculated: k = Na / Nt × 100 %. Na is the product of the numbers of teeth of all driven gears (the even-numbered ones), Nt the product of the numbers of teeth of all driving gears (the odd-numbered ones), counted from the gear on the shaft of the drive wheel.\nIf the tractor has a speedometer and the panel shows another speed: Knew = Kcurrent × Vtractor / Vpanel."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"Imitation","u":"en/mini/speed-sensor.html#imitation","x":"IMPORTANT\nSetting “Imitation, km/h” switches the speed limits off. “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” go to “No”, and the panel says “Settings of Min and Max speed switched off!”. On a machine with dispenser motors it also locks the product application: nothing is dosed until you release the lock by holding “HOME” (Lock application by hand).\nWith motors the imitated speed is also kept inside the range the dosing allows: a value outside it is pulled to the nearest limit. While the speed is imitated, the link line of the status screen reads “IMITATION” in yellow, and the speed indicator shows the imitation sign.\nThe speed can also come from the built-in GPS receiver. That is switched on in the profile, item “GPS”, not here — The profile, GPS reception."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed sensor","h":"The status screen","u":"en/mini/speed-sensor.html#the-status-screen","x":"It shows the pulse frequency F= in Hz and the two limits in force — see The sensor status screen.\nThe status screen of a speed sensor connected to the panel."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/calibrate-speed.html","x":"Drive a marked 100 m so that the Hectare Mini corrects the factor of the travel speed sensor — started from the Seeding machine parameters or from the context menu of the sensor."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/calibrate-speed.html","x":"The calibration measures the distance the panel counts over a marked 100 m and corrects “Factor, %” of the speed sensor. Do it before the first seeding and after any change to the wheel or its drive. Set “Pulses” and “Diameter, cm” first (Travel speed sensor) — the calibration corrects the factor; it cannot replace a wrong pulse figure.\nMark out 100 m on level ground, as close to field conditions as you can, and put the machine at the start."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"From the Seeding machine parameters","u":"en/mini/calibrate-speed.html#from-the-seeding-machine-parameters","x":"Raise the level to “Agronomist” (Enable the Agronomist level).\n“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters”, select “Speed calibration” and press “MENU”.\nThe way to the calibration: the Settings menu and the Seeding machine parameters, then the calibration window.\nThe panel shows “Pass 100m and press MENU” with a “STOP” button. Drive the marked distance and stop at the end.\nPress “MENU” or touch “STOP”. The panel shows “Done” and closes the window by itself."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"From the context menu of the speed sensor","u":"en/mini/calibrate-speed.html#from-the-context-menu-of-the-speed-sensor","x":"Hold “MENU” on the home screen, select the speed indicator, press “MENU”. The context menu opens.\nSelect “Speed calibration” and press “MENU”. The item is shown only while the machine stands.\nAt the “Operator” level the PIN pad opens: type the PIN of the Agronomist or Service level. At “Agronomist” the panel asks “Are you sure?” — press “PLUS” for “Yes”.\nThe calibration from the context menu: the item, the PIN request at the Operator level, the calibration window.\nGo on as in steps 3 and 4 above.\nThe PIN given here starts this one calibration; the access level stays as it was.\nWhile the calibration runs, product application is locked; it returns to its state before the calibration when the window closes. The panel waits half a second after “STOP” and checks the connection to the sensor once more before it takes the result."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the speed sensor","h":"When it does not work","u":"en/mini/calibrate-speed.html#when-it-does-not-work","x":"The panel shows\nMeaning\n“No speed sensor”\nThe profile has no speed sensor. The calibration does not start.\n“Connection lost with the sensor! Calibration canceled!”\nThe sensor does not answer — at the start, or at the moment of “STOP”.\n“Sensor not controlled! Calibration canceled!”\nThe speed sensor is out of control.\n“FAILURE! The tractor did not move!”\nThe sensor gave no pulses at all over the distance. Check the sensor, its gap to the target and its cable.\n“Calibration canceled!”\nThe window was closed with “BACK” or a touch outside. Nothing has changed.\n“Invalid PIN”\nThe PIN asked at the “Operator” level was wrong. The calibration does not start.\nIf the speed on the panel still differs from the real speed after the calibration, check “Pulses” and “Diameter, cm” and calibrate again."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed and fertilizer sensors","h":"","u":"en/mini/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html","x":"The settings of the seed sensors and the fertilizer sensors of the Hectare Mini — fraction for the crop, correction, error limits, recalculation path, percent of sowing — and when the panel starts to control the seeding."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed and fertilizer sensors","h":"","u":"en/mini/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html","x":"The seed sensors share one set of settings: change it on any seed sensor and it applies to all. The fertilizer sensors share a second set. Only “Product application” is set line by line. Open the context menu of any sensor of the group as in Sensor settings.\nThe context menu of a seed sensor.\nParameter\nLevel\nWhat it does\nRange · default\n“Product application”\nAgronomist\n“Yes”: this line sows and is watched. “No”: the line is treated as shut off, like a line in a tramline bout — seed through it raises “Seeding to tramline”. Not shown when every line of the group has its own motor.\n“No” / “Yes”\n“Sensitivity”\nAgronomist\nSensitivity of a seed sensor of an older generation. Shown only when such a sensor is connected.\n1 … 120 · 80\n“Fraction”\nOperator\nThe count filter of the sensors, set for the size of the seed — a filter against dust and chaff. Change it with the crop — table below.\n1 … 100 · 10\n“Correction”\nOperator\nAdapts the sensitivity to the bracket of the sensor. A higher value lowers the sensitivity and keeps dust and fine dirt out of the count. Shown only for the sensor generation that uses it.\n100 … 3300 · 300\n“Rate error,%”\nAgronomist\nPermitted deviation of the measured rate from the target; beyond it “Under allowable rate” or “Over allowable rate”. Only in “Hybrid” and “Precise”.\n5 … 95 % · 50 %\n“Doubles error,%”\nAgronomist\nAbove this share of doubles: “High % of doubles”. “No” switches the alarm off. Only in “Precise”.\n“No”, 1 … 95 % · “No”\n“Skips error,%”\nAgronomist\nAbove this share of skips: “High % of skips”. “No” switches the alarm off. Only in “Precise”.\n“No”, 1 … 95 % · “No”\n“Recalculation,m”\nOperator\nThe stretch over which skips, doubles and quality are collected for the diagram and the Seeding table. Without a speed sensor it is “Recalculation,sec”, in seconds. “MENU” on it lets the panel calculate it — Let the panel calculate the recalculation path.\n10 … 50 · 20\n“Percent of sowing,%”\nAgronomist\nThe share of sowing lines the panel needs before it starts to control the seeding — below. Seed sensors only; not shown on machines with dispenser motors.\n5 … 80 %, steps of 5 · 20 %\n“Toler.sowing to tramline,%”\nAgronomist\nHow much seed may still pass a line shut off for a tramline, in per cent of the target rate, before “Seeding to tramline”.\n0 … 50 % · 20 %\n“Group start address”\nService (seed), Agronomist (fertilizer)\nWhere the numbering of this group starts in the sensor network. Leave it at “AUTO” unless the service tells you otherwise.\n“AUTO”, …\nIn “Flow” mode a line is in alarm only when it stays below the alarm level; there is no upper limit. The target rates and alarm levels themselves are set on the home screen — Change the target rate."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed and fertilizer sensors","h":"Fraction for the crop","u":"en/mini/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html#fraction-for-the-crop","x":"Go by the size of the seed:\nCrop\n“Fraction”\nRapeseed\n1 – 5\nWheat\n5 – 10\nMaize\n10 – 30\nSunflower\n10 – 30\nOn pneumatic drills set 1 whatever the crop. Too high a fraction makes small seeds disappear from the count — the panel then reports too low a rate.\nIMPORTANT\nChange “Correction” only after talking to the service. It is needed in rare cases only; a wrong value makes the sensors miss seed or count dust."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed and fertilizer sensors","h":"When the panel controls the seeding","u":"en/mini/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html#when-the-panel-controls-the-seeding","x":"The panel starts to watch the seeding — and to raise seed alarms — only when seed flows within the target rate through at least “Percent of sowing,%” of the seed lines, and, if “Seeding criteria” of the speed sensor is “Yes”, the machine moves (Travel speed sensor). Until then the diagram shows “NOT UNDER CONTROL” — see Seeding alarms. With a work position sensor whose “Alarms criteria” is “Yes”, nothing is watched in the transport position (Hopper and work position sensors)."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed and fertilizer sensors","h":"The status screen","u":"en/mini/seed-and-fertilizer-sensors.html#the-status-screen","x":"Shows Status: with the state of the sensor — “Working”, “Clogged”, “Light struck” and the others: The sensor status screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Let the panel calculate the recalculation path","h":"","u":"en/mini/recalculation-path.html","x":"Let the Hectare Mini work out the best \"Recalculation,m\" for skips and doubles from the target rate and the holes in the disk — with or without dispenser motors."},{"p":"mini","t":"Let the panel calculate the recalculation path","h":"","u":"en/mini/recalculation-path.html","x":"“Recalculation,m” of the seed sensors is the stretch over which the panel collects skips, doubles and quality before it updates the diagram and the Seeding table (Seed and fertilizer sensors). A stretch that covers whole turns of the seed disk gives the steadiest figures. The panel can work it out from the target rate in seeds per metre and the number of holes in the disk.\nIMPORTANT\nCalculate the path again whenever the target rate has changed. The old path no longer matches whole turns of the disk.\nSet the target rate on the home screen first — Change the target rate. The calculation uses the target of the “Precise” mode.\nOpen the context menu of any seed sensor (Sensor settings), select “Recalculation,m” and press “MENU”.\nWith dispenser motors the panel asks whether the rate and the number of holes are set — “Calculate path?”. Press “PLUS” for “Yes”. It takes the holes from “Holes in disk” of the motor (The context menu of a motor).\nWithout motors it first reminds you that the seeding rate must already be set; close the message. Then type the number of holes in the disk in the “Holes in disk” pad (1 to 500) and touch “OK”.\nThe panel calculates the path and sets it: the longest stretch of whole disk turns, up to ten, that fits into 50 m, doubled while it is shorter than 25 m.\nIf even one turn of the disk is longer than 50 m, the panel shows “Calculated path to big. Maximum setuped.” and sets 50 m.\n“No” in step 3, or “BACK” on the pad, leaves the path as it was. You can always set “Recalculation,m” by hand with “PLUS” / “MINUS”, from 10 to 50 m.\nWithout a speed sensor the item is “Recalculation,sec” and the stretch is counted in seconds."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/dispenser-sensor.html","x":"The settings of a dispenser shaft sensor of the Hectare Mini — the period for \"No rotation\", the control of the shaft speed against the travel speed, and the control of the product weight — with ranges and defaults."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/dispenser-sensor.html","x":"A dispenser shaft sensor counts the turns of the metering shaft. At the least it tells you that the shaft has stopped. Calibrated, it also watches the shaft speed against the travel speed, or counts the weight of product applied. Each dispenser sensor has its own settings — set every one.\nOpen its context menu as in Sensor settings.\nThe context menu of a dispenser shaft sensor.\nParameter\nLevel\nWhat it does\nRange · default\n“Dispenser calibration(weight)”\nOperator\nStarts the calibration by weight — Calibrate the dispenser by weight. Shown only with “Weight control” at “Yes”.\n—\n“Period, s”\nOperator\nThe longest time between two pulses of the sensor. If the shaft gives no pulse for longer while the machine sows or moves, the panel raises “No rotation”.\n1 … 60 s · 10 s\n“Rotation speed control”\nAgronomist\n“Yes”: the shaft speed is watched against the travel speed, within “Maximum error, %”. “No”: only whether the shaft turns at all. Needs the rotation calibration.\n“No” / “Yes” · “No”\n“Wheel, pulses”\nAgronomist\nPulses of the speed sensor that go with “Dispenser pulses” of this sensor. Written by the rotation calibration. Shown with “Rotation speed control” at “Yes”.\n1 … 65535 · 100\n“Dispenser pulses”\nAgronomist\nPulses of this sensor that go with “Wheel, pulses”. Written by the rotation calibration.\n1 … 65535 · 100\n“Maximum error, %”\nAgronomist\nPermitted deviation of the shaft speed; beyond it, while the machine moves, “Low speed of rotation” or “High speed of rotation”.\n1 … 50 % · 10 %\n“Weight control”\nAgronomist\n“Yes”: the panel counts the weight of product applied per hectare from the turns of this shaft. Needs the calibration by weight.\n“No” / “Yes” · “No”\n“Type of product”\nAgronomist\nWhich product this dispenser applies: “Product 1”, “Product 2” or “Product 3”. Shown with “Weight control” at “Yes”.\n· “Product 1”\n“Weight, gr”\nAgronomist\nWeight of product that goes with “Weight, pulses” — together the output of the metering unit per turn. Written by the calibration by weight.\n1 … 65535 · 10000\n“Weight, pulses”\nAgronomist\nPulses of this sensor that go with “Weight, gr”.\n1 … 65535 · 100\nWithout a speed sensor in the profile the panel can only watch whether the shaft turns: the speed and weight items are hidden."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"Type of product","u":"en/mini/dispenser-sensor.html#type-of-product","x":"The panel follows up to three products by weight. Dispensers that apply the same product get the same number:\nMachine\nSetting\nOne dispenser sensor\n“Product 1”.\nTwo dispensers, the same seed\n“Product 1” on both.\nTwo dispensers, seed and dry fertilizer\n“Product 1” on the seed dispenser, “Product 2” on the fertilizer dispenser.\nThree dispensers, two for seed, one for fertilizer\n“Product 1” on the two seed dispensers, “Product 2” on the third.\nA product already dosed by an electric motor cannot be chosen here; the panel skips to the next free one. If no product is free, “Weight control” stays at “No”.\nIMPORTANT\nChanging “Type of product”, switching “Weight control” on or off, or taking a weight-controlled dispenser sensor into or out of control can erase the statistics of the active field. If the field already holds data, the panel asks first — “Need to erase statistics” for the active field; “MENU” for “Yes”, “BACK” for “No”. To keep the data, select an empty field first (Start a new field)."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dispenser shaft sensor","h":"The status screen","u":"en/mini/dispenser-sensor.html#the-status-screen","x":"Shows the pulse frequency F= in Hz, to three decimals — The sensor status screen.\nThe status screen of a dispenser shaft sensor."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the dispenser rotation","h":"","u":"en/mini/calibrate-dispenser-rotation.html","x":"Turn the drive wheel so that the Hectare Mini learns how many dispenser pulses go with how many wheel pulses — needed before \"Rotation speed control\" can watch the shaft speed."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the dispenser rotation","h":"","u":"en/mini/calibrate-dispenser-rotation.html","x":"With “Rotation speed control” at “Yes”, a dispenser sensor watches whether the shaft turns as fast as the travel speed demands (Dispenser shaft sensor). For that the panel must know the ratio between the pulses of the speed sensor and the pulses of the dispenser sensor. The calibration measures it and writes “Wheel, pulses” and “Dispenser pulses”.\nBefore you start: the speed sensor and the dispenser sensors to be calibrated must be under control and must answer. You need the “Agronomist” level (Enable the Agronomist level). Raise the machine so that the drive wheel can be turned, or be ready to move it.\n“Settings” → “Seeding machine parameters”. Select “Dispenser calibration”.\nChoose with “PLUS” / “MINUS” which dispensers to calibrate: “ALL”, or the number of one dispenser.\nPress “MENU”. The “Calibration” screen opens with a progress bar at 0 %.\nTurn the drive wheel. The bar fills as pulses come in from the wheel and from every dispenser being calibrated.\nAt 100 % — at least 100 pulses from each — the panel beeps and shows “Stop rotation!”. Stop turning.\nWhen no pulse has come for two seconds, the panel stores the values and shows “Done”; the “Seeding machine parameters” menu returns.\nScreen shown in German\nDispenser calibration: the menu, the progress while turning, and the message Stop rotation!\n“BACK” at any moment aborts with “Calibration canceled!”; nothing changes.\nThe panel shows\nMeaning\n“Sensor not controlled! Calibration canceled!”\nThe speed sensor or a dispenser sensor to be calibrated is out of control. Take it into control (Take a sensor into or out of control).\n“Connection lost with the sensor! Calibration canceled!”\nThe speed sensor or a dispenser sensor does not answer, at the start or during the calibration.\nThe more you turn before “Stop rotation!”, the better: the values are scaled down by themselves if the count grows beyond what they can hold.\nCalibrate again after any change to the drive between the wheel and the dispenser — sprockets, gearbox or variator."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the dispenser by weight","h":"","u":"en/mini/calibrate-dispenser-weight.html","x":"Turn the dispenser into a container and weigh it, so that the Hectare Mini knows how much product one turn of the shaft applies — needed for \"Weight control\"."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the dispenser by weight","h":"","u":"en/mini/calibrate-dispenser-weight.html","x":"With “Weight control” at “Yes”, the panel counts the product applied from the turns of the dispenser shaft (Dispenser shaft sensor). The calibration measures the weight per pulse and writes “Weight, gr” and “Weight, pulses”. It is done at the “Operator” level, standing.\nIMPORTANT\nCalibrate again after every change of the metering roller, of the variator or gearbox setting in the dispenser drive, and of the seed in the hopper. Otherwise the weight shown is wrong.\nYou need a container, scales and a way to turn the dispenser shaft by hand.\nOn the home screen hold “MENU”, select the dispenser sensor and press “MENU”. The context menu opens.\nSelect “Dispenser calibration(weight)” and press “MENU”. The calibration screen shows the target rates of the products in use — the product of this dispenser between arrows — and “PLUS - calibrate dispenser 1” with the number of the dispenser.\nPress “PLUS”. The panel lists what to do before the calibration: weigh the empty container and write the weight down; fill the hopper; put the empty container under the dispenser; turn the rotor until its cells are filled. Do all of it, then press “UP”.\nThe next screen lists the calibration itself and counts the “Impulses” from the sensor: empty the container, put it back under the dispenser, and turn the rotor, filling the container — as much as possible, between 2 kg and 60 kg.\nAs soon as more than ten pulses have been counted, the line “MINUS - complete calibration” appears. Go on turning until the container holds as much as you can manage — at least 100 pulses and 5 kg give a good result. Then stop.\nPress “MINUS”. The number pad opens: “Enter the difference of the filled and empty container in grams”.\nWeigh the full container, subtract the empty weight, type the difference in grams (2000 to 60000) and touch “OK”. The panel stores the values and returns to the first screen.\nPress “BACK” to leave.\nScreen shown in German\nCalibration by weight: the menu item, the first screen, the list before the calibration, the list with the pulse counter, the line MINUS - complete calibration, and the weight entry.\n“BACK” on the list before the calibration cancels it. “BACK” during the calibration asks “Are you sure?” — “PLUS” cancels, “MINUS” goes on. “BACK” on the number pad keeps the old values."},{"p":"mini","t":"Calibrate the dispenser by weight","h":"When it does not start or stops","u":"en/mini/calibrate-dispenser-weight.html#when-it-does-not-start-or-stops","x":"“PLUS” in step 3 works only while the sensor is under control, answers and has no alarm. Otherwise the first screen names the reason — “Not controlled”, “No connection” or “FAILURE”. If the sensor fails during the calibration, the panel shows “FAILURE” with the reason; “BACK” returns to the first screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fan sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/fan-sensor.html","x":"The settings of a fan speed sensor of the Hectare Mini — minimum and maximum fan speed and pulses per revolution — and when the panel raises its alarms."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fan sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/fan-sensor.html","x":"A fan sensor counts the revolutions of the fan of a pneumatic drill or planter. The home screen shows the fan speed in rpm. Each fan sensor has its own settings.\nOpen its context menu as in Sensor settings.\nThe context menu of a fan sensor.\nParameter\nLevel\nWhat it does\nRange · default\n“Minimum, rpm”\nOperator\nLowest permitted fan speed. Below it the panel raises “Low speed of rotation” — but only while the machine sows, or moves in the working position.\n500 … 9000 rpm, steps of 500 · 3000\n“Maximum, rpm”\nOperator\nHighest permitted fan speed. Above it the panel raises “High speed of rotation” — at any time, also standing.\n500 … 9000 rpm, steps of 500 · 5000\n“Pulses”\nAgronomist\nPulses of the sensor per revolution of the fan.\n1 … 60 · 2\nA fan speed that looks right but triggers alarms usually means a wrong “Pulses”: twice the pulses show half the speed.\nWhat sowing and moving mean for the panel — When the panel controls the seeding. Without a speed sensor the minimum is watched while the panel sees seeding. With a work position sensor whose “Alarms criteria” is “Yes”, the minimum is not watched in the transport position."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fan sensor","h":"The status screen","u":"en/mini/fan-sensor.html#the-status-screen","x":"Shows the pulse frequency F= in Hz — The sensor status screen. The fan speed in rpm is this frequency × 60 ÷ “Pulses”.\nThe status screen of a fan sensor.\nThe alarms of the fan and what to check: Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms."},{"p":"mini","t":"Pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/pressure-sensor.html","x":"The settings of a pressure (vacuum) sensor of the Hectare Mini — filter, minimum and maximum pressure — and when the panel raises its alarms."},{"p":"mini","t":"Pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/pressure-sensor.html","x":"A pressure sensor measures the vacuum (or overpressure) of the air system — for example at the outlet of the fan or in the seed meters of a vacuum planter. The panel works with the absolute value: a higher figure means more vacuum. Each pressure sensor has its own settings.\nOpen its context menu as in Sensor settings.\nThe context menu of a pressure sensor.\nParameter\nLevel\nWhat it does\nRange · default\n“Filter”\nAgronomist\nHow long the sensor measures and averages the pressure. Raise it if the figure jumps a lot.\n1 … 16 · 1\n“Minimum, Pa”\nOperator\nBelow this pressure — too little vacuum — the panel raises “Low pressure”. “No” switches the check off.\n“No”, 100 … 10000 Pa, steps of 100 · “No”\n“Maximum, Pa”\nOperator\nAbove this pressure — too much vacuum — the panel raises “High pressure”. “No” switches the check off.\n“No”, 100 … 10000 Pa, steps of 100 · “No”\nBoth limits are watched only while the machine sows, or moves in the working position — see When the panel controls the seeding. Without a speed sensor they are watched while the panel sees seeding.\nThe limits are set in pascals; the status screen shows the pressure in kilopascals: 1 kPa = 1000 Pa."},{"p":"mini","t":"Pressure sensor","h":"The status screen","u":"en/mini/pressure-sensor.html#the-status-screen","x":"Shows the measured pressure P= in kPa, only while the sensor answers — The sensor status screen.\nThe status screen of a pressure sensor that does not answer: the link line is red, no pressure is shown.\nThe alarms and what to check: Pressure alarms. A new or replaced pressure sensor must first get its address — Configure a pressure sensor."},{"p":"mini","t":"Hopper and work position sensors","h":"","u":"en/mini/hopper-and-work-position-sensors.html","x":"The hopper level sensor of the Hectare Mini, which has no settings, and the work position sensor with its transport signal and its alarm criterion."},{"p":"mini","t":"Hopper and work position sensors","h":"Hopper level sensor","u":"en/mini/hopper-and-work-position-sensors.html#hopper-level-sensor","x":"The hopper sensor reports when the product in the hopper falls below the height at which the sensor is mounted. It has no settings: its context menu holds only “Information” (and “Reset to defaults” at the Service level, which has nothing to reset).\nThe status screen shows Pin: with the level on its input: \"1\" — the hopper is empty at the sensor, and the panel raises “Hopper empty”; \"0\" — product covers the sensor. See Hopper alarms."},{"p":"mini","t":"Hopper and work position sensors","h":"Work position sensor","u":"en/mini/hopper-and-work-position-sensors.html#work-position-sensor","x":"The work position sensor tells the panel whether the implement is lowered in the working position or raised in the transport position. Open its context menu as in Sensor settings.\nThe context menu of the work position sensor.\nParameter\nLevel\nWhat it does\nValues · default\n“Transporting position signal”\nAgronomist\nThe level on the input that means transport position: 0 or 1. Set it to what the sensor gives with the implement raised.\n0 / 1 · 0\n“Alarms criteria”\nAgronomist\n“Yes”: in the transport position the panel does not watch the seeding, and the fan minimum, the pressure limits and the dispenser shafts are not watched on the move — no alarms at the headland. “No”: the position is only shown; it does not affect the alarms. Hidden, and always “Yes”, on machines with dispenser motors or a liquid pump controller.\n“No” / “Yes” · “Yes”\nOn a machine with dispenser motors, a work position sensor that does not answer counts as transport position — for safety, nothing is dosed. Without motors, a sensor that does not answer or is out of control counts as working position.\nThe status screen shows “Position: Working” or “Position: Transporting”, and Pin: with the level on the input — compare it with the real position when you set “Transporting position signal”. The position changes after a short filter, so a bump does not toggle it.\nThe status screen of the work position sensor.\nOn the home screen the implement indicator blinks yellow in the transport position — see Indicators."},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine sensors of the fan drive","h":"","u":"en/mini/engine-sensors.html","x":"The five sensors of a separate fan-drive engine on the Hectare Mini — ignition, emergency oil pressure, battery charge, low fuel, air filter — their two settings and how the ignition sensor gates the others."},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine sensors of the fan drive","h":"","u":"en/mini/engine-sensors.html","x":"Drills whose fan is driven by its own engine can watch that engine with up to five switch sensors on an input device. Each is entered in the profile on its own. All five have the same two settings and the same status screen; they differ in what the signal means.\nThe context menu of the ignition sensor.\nParameter\nLevel\nWhat it does\nRange · default\n“Activation signal”\nAgronomist\nThe level on the input, 0 or 1, that means the state in the table below.\n0 / 1 · 1\n“Signal delay, sec”\nAgronomist\nHow long the input must hold its new level before the panel accepts the change — against false triggering from vibration or a loose contact.\n1 … 30 s · 1 s; “Low fuel” 15 s, “Air filter” 5 s\nSensor\nThe activation signal means\nAlarm\n“Ignition”\nThe ignition of the fan-drive engine is switched on.\nnone — see below\n“Emergency oil pressure”\nThe oil pressure of the engine is too low.\nyes, only with the ignition on\n“No battery charge”\nThe battery is not being charged by the engine.\nyes, only with the ignition on\n“Low fuel”\nLittle fuel is left in the tank of the engine.\nyes\n“Air filter”\nThe air filter of the engine is dirty.\nyes\nThe defaults of “Signal delay, sec” for “Low fuel” and “Air filter” are set when the sensor is reset to its defaults."},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine sensors of the fan drive","h":"How the ignition sensor gates the others","u":"en/mini/engine-sensors.html#how-the-ignition-sensor-gates-the-others","x":"The ignition sensor raises no alarm of its own. It keeps the oil pressure and battery charge sensors quiet while the engine is off — with the ignition off, low oil pressure and no charging are normal. While the ignition sensor is under control, answers and signals the ignition off, these two stay quiet. If the ignition sensor is out of control or does not answer, they report whatever the state of the ignition.\nThat is why “Emergency oil pressure” and “No battery charge” should be in the profile only together with “Ignition”, and be taken out together with it.\nOn the home screen the ignition indicator is yellow while the ignition is off and grey while it is on — see Indicators."},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine sensors of the fan drive","h":"The status screen","u":"en/mini/engine-sensors.html#the-status-screen","x":"Shows Pin: with the present level on the input, \"0\" or \"1\" — compare it with the real state of the engine to set “Activation signal”. See The sensor status screen.\nThe status screen of the ignition sensor.\nThe alarms and what to check: Engine alarms."},{"p":"mini","t":"Marker sensors","h":"","u":"en/mini/marker-sensors.html","x":"The two settings of the marker arm sensors of the Hectare Mini — which arm a single sensor watches, and which signal means \"down\"."},{"p":"mini","t":"Marker sensors","h":"","u":"en/mini/marker-sensors.html","x":"Marker sensors tell the panel whether the marker arms are up or down. The home screen draws the two arms; both up are shown yellow — see Indicators. How many sensors watch the arms is set under “Markers” in the profile: “One” or “Both”.\nOpen the context menu of a marker sensor as in Sensor settings. Both settings need the “Agronomist” level.\nParameter\nWhat it does\nValues · default\n“Connected marker”\nWhich arm the single sensor watches: “Left” or “Right”. Shown only with “Markers” at “One”.\n· “Left”\n“Signal \"down\"”\nThe level on the input, 0 or 1, that means the arm is down. Set it to what the sensor gives with the arm lowered.\n0 / 1 · 0\nThe status screen of a marker sensor shows only the common lines — type, link and alarms (The sensor status screen). A marker sensor raises only the alarms every device can raise: “Connection lost” and “Wrong device type!” — see Device does not answer."},{"p":"mini","t":"Device configuration","h":"","u":"en/mini/device-configuration.html","x":"What device configuration on the Hectare Mini is for, and how to prepare it — only the one device on the harness, the Agronomist level, the Sensors Configuration menu."},{"p":"mini","t":"Device configuration","h":"","u":"en/mini/device-configuration.html","x":"Every device on the sensor network answers on its own address. A seed sensor's address is the number of its row. A new or replaced sensor must get the right address before the panel can find it. This is device configuration, in “Service” → “Sensors Configuration”.\nIMPORTANT\nThis work is for commissioning and for replacing a seed or fertilizer sensor. It is mostly done at the factory or by an authorised service engineer. A wrong address makes a sensor answer in the place of another. Do not try out unknown items of the “Service” menu; follow only this manual or the service engineer of Agriwelt GmbH."},{"p":"mini","t":"Device configuration","h":"Prepare","u":"en/mini/device-configuration.html#prepare","x":"Disconnect all sensors from the cable entry of the communication harness on the panel — the four-pin connector.\nThe cable entry of the communication harness: the four-pin connector, the harness and the power connection.\nConnect only the device that is to get a new address.\nSwitch the panel on. When it has started, it reports “Connection lost” for all the devices that are missing. Close the windows with “HOME”.\nRaise the level to “Agronomist” — Enable the Agronomist level. For a HUB-8 concentrator or a tramline actuator raise it to “Service”.\nPress “BACK” to return to the main menu. Select “Service” and press “MENU”.\nSelect “Sensors Configuration” and press “MENU”. The menu lists the devices that can be configured.\nThe way to the sensor configuration: Settings, the main menu, the Service menu, the Sensors Configuration menu.\nItem\nLevel\nHow\n“Seed sensor”\nAgronomist\nConfigure a seed sensor\n“Pressure sensor”\nAgronomist\nConfigure a pressure sensor\n“HUB8”\nService\nConfigure a HUB-8 concentrator\n“Tramline actuator”\nService\nFor the service engineer only."},{"p":"mini","t":"Device configuration","h":"The rules of every configuration","u":"en/mini/device-configuration.html#the-rules-of-every-configuration","x":"One device on the harness. With “Sensor address” at “AUTO” the panel takes the one device it finds. If more than one answers, it refuses with “More than one device in network!”; if none answers, it shows “No connection”.\nThe new address must be free. When the device was picked by its present address instead of “AUTO”, the panel also checks the new one: if another device already uses it, it shows “Address: … is used!” with the number, and changes nothing.\nAny other refusal reads “Error #” with a number. Disconnect and connect the device, and try again.\nCheck the result: disconnect the device, connect it again, and return to the home screen with “BACK”. The device must appear in its place, without a red cross.\nIMPORTANT\nWhen all devices are done, return to “Operator” — set “User” back in the “Settings” menu, or switch the panel off and on. Then reconnect all devices."},{"p":"mini","t":"Configure a seed sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/configure-seed-sensor.html","x":"Give a new or replaced seed sensor of the Hectare Mini the number of its row — how to hold an open sensor, the three menu items, and the check afterwards."},{"p":"mini","t":"Configure a seed sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/configure-seed-sensor.html","x":"For a seed sensor the new address is the number of its row — the number shown under its column on the home screen. Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this sensor on the harness, “Agronomist” level, “Sensors Configuration” menu open.\nThe address of a seed sensor can be changed only while the sensor works normally. A tube sensor is ready as soon as it is connected. An open sensor must sit on its coulter, or be held so that no direct light falls into the optical channel — the gap between the two glasses. In direct light the sensor reports a fault and does not take the new address.\nHolding an open seed sensor: a finger covers the optical channel between the glasses.\nIn “Sensors Configuration” select “Seed sensor” and press “MENU”. The menu has three items: “Sensor address”, “New address” and “Set”.\nLeave “Sensor address” at “AUTO”. If it shows something else, set it back with “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\nSelect “New address” and set the row number with “PLUS” / “MINUS”.\nSelect “Set” and press “MENU”. The panel answers “Done” and the address it found the sensor on.\nDisconnect the sensor and connect it again. Return to the home screen with “BACK” (twice from the “Service” menu). The sensor must appear under its row; the rows not plugged in show a red cross.\nThe home screen after re-addressing, with only one sensor on the harness: the other rows and devices crossed out in red.\nRepeat for every sensor to be addressed. When all are done, return to “Operator” — set “User” back in “Settings”, or switch the panel off and on — and reconnect all devices."},{"p":"mini","t":"Configure a seed sensor","h":"Searching for a sensor","u":"en/mini/configure-seed-sensor.html#searching-for-a-sensor","x":"Hold “MENU” on “Sensor address” to search the network for a seed sensor: the item then shows the present address of the sensor found. Hold “MENU” again to search for the next one. With a found address in place of “AUTO”, “Set” re-addresses exactly that sensor, and the panel checks that the new address is free. If nothing is found, the panel shows “No connection”.\nWhat the error messages mean: The rules of every configuration."},{"p":"mini","t":"Configure a pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/configure-pressure-sensor.html","x":"Give a new or replaced pressure sensor of the Hectare Mini its address — the three menu items and the check afterwards."},{"p":"mini","t":"Configure a pressure sensor","h":"","u":"en/mini/configure-pressure-sensor.html","x":"Each pressure sensor of the profile answers on its own address; the first pressure sensor has the first address of the range, the second the next, and so on. “New address” offers only the addresses of that range. Ask the service which one your sensor needs if you are not sure. Prepare first as in Device configuration: only this sensor on the harness, “Agronomist” level, “Sensors Configuration” menu open.\nIn “Sensors Configuration” select “Pressure sensor” and press “MENU”. The menu has three items: “Sensor address”, “New address” and “Set”.\nLeave “Sensor address” at “AUTO”.\nSelect “New address” and set the address with “PLUS” / “MINUS”. It opens at the first address of the range, the one of pressure sensor 1.\nSelect “Set” and press “MENU”. The panel answers “Done”.\nThe Pressure sensor menu (the address is covered in this manual).\nDisconnect the sensor and connect it again. Return to the home screen with “BACK”. The pressure sensor must report back; while a pressure sensor is missing, its indicator shows a red cross and XXXX.\nThe home screen while the pressure sensors are not plugged in.\nWhen all devices are done, return to “Operator” — set “User” back in “Settings”, or switch the panel off and on — and reconnect all devices.\nHold “MENU” on “Sensor address” to search for a pressure sensor on the harness, as described for the seed sensor — Searching for a sensor. The error messages: The rules of every configuration."},{"p":"mini","t":"Configure a HUB-8 concentrator","h":"","u":"en/mini/configure-hub8.html","x":"Set the eight channels of a HUB-8 concentrator on the Hectare Mini — what each channel stands in for, and the shortcuts that fill all channels at once."},{"p":"mini","t":"Configure a HUB-8 concentrator","h":"","u":"en/mini/configure-hub8.html","x":"A HUB-8 concentrator collects up to eight sensors and stands, on the sensor network, in for each of them: every channel appears to the panel as an input device or as a seed sensor with its own address. The configuration tells each channel what it stands in for and which one it is in its group. It needs the “Service” level. Prepare first as in Device configuration: only the concentrator on the harness, “Sensors Configuration” menu open.\nIn “Sensors Configuration” select “HUB8” and press “MENU”. The menu shows “Device address”, then for each channel a pair “Channel 1 type” and “Channel 1 address”, up to channel 8, and “Set” at the end.\nLeave “Device address” at “AUTO”.\nSelect “Channel 1 type” and choose with “PLUS” / “MINUS” what the channel stands in for: “Input device” or “Seed sensors”.\nSelect “Channel 1 address” and set with “PLUS” / “MINUS” which device of its group this channel is — its sequence number among the input devices or among the seed sensors.\nDo the same for channels 2 to 8.\nSelect “Set” and press “MENU”. The panel answers “Done”.\nDisconnect the concentrator and connect it again. Return to the home screen with “BACK”. The devices on its channels must report back; while the concentrator is unplugged, they are crossed out in red.\nThe home screen while the concentrator is unplugged: dispensers, fan and pressure sensors crossed out.\nWhen all devices are done, return to “Operator” — set “User” back in “Settings”, or switch the panel off and on — and reconnect all devices."},{"p":"mini","t":"Configure a HUB-8 concentrator","h":"Shortcuts","u":"en/mini/configure-hub8.html#shortcuts","x":"Hold “MENU” on one of “Channel 1 type” to “Channel 8 type”: all the following channels get the same type, and their addresses are numbered on from this channel.\nHold “MENU” on one of “Channel 1 address” to “Channel 8 address”: the following channels of the same type are numbered on from this one.\nHold “MENU” on “Device address”: the panel searches for a concentrator and reads its present channel settings into the menu. Hold it again for the next one.\nWhen you change a channel type, the address of that channel jumps to the first free one of the new type. If two channels of the same type end up with the same address, “Set” refuses with “Invalid profile!”. The other messages: The rules of every configuration."},{"p":"mini","t":"How alarms work","h":"","u":"en/mini/how-alarms-work.html","x":"When the Hectare Mini raises an alarm, what the alarm window shows, how to close it, which messages are not alarm windows, and how the alarm topics of this manual are organised."},{"p":"mini","t":"How alarms work","h":"","u":"en/mini/how-alarms-work.html","x":"An alarm is a window that opens over the working screen, with an alarm signal. It tells you that one device — a sensor, a motor, an actuator — or the system as a whole has found a fault. The alarm topics of this section are grouped by device. Look for the words you see on the screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"How alarms work","h":"When the panel raises an alarm","u":"en/mini/how-alarms-work.html#when-the-panel-raises-an-alarm","x":"A fault is not reported the moment it appears. The condition must hold without a break for the time set in “Alarm delay” before the window opens.\nSetting\nValue\nWhere\n“Settings” → “Alarm delay”, “Operator” level — The Settings menu\nRange\n2 to 20 seconds, in steps of 1 second\nDefault\n3 seconds. A stored value outside the range is reset to 3 by itself.\nA short delay reacts fast, but also to a single bump or a short gap in the seed flow. A long delay keeps the screen quiet, but a real fault can run for up to twenty seconds before you hear of it.\nTIP\nIf the system alarms too often on a rough field, raise “Alarm delay” first. Change the sensor settings only if a longer delay does not help.\nTwo cases do not follow the delay:\nA lost connection is counted in polls: “Connection lost” appears after three polls in a row without an answer.\nRight after a sensor has received new settings, its alarms are held back for a short time, so that configuring a device does not cause a burst of alarms."},{"p":"mini","t":"How alarms work","h":"The alarm window","u":"en/mini/how-alarms-work.html#the-alarm-window","x":"“FAILURE!” — the red title of every alarm window, whatever the fault.\n“HOME - close message” — the hint how to close it.\nThe alarm text — the fault. Look it up under this text in the alarm topics.\nThe kind of device, here the fan. The same text can come from different devices; this line tells you which.\nThe number of the device, here fan 1. When several devices of one kind report the same fault, they share one window and are listed together, for example #3-5,8.\n“Close” — closes this alarm.\nThe alarm window of a fan sensor that does not answer.\nThe alarms of the system as a whole — for example about the rate of a product — have no device line."},{"p":"mini","t":"How alarms work","h":"Closing an alarm","u":"en/mini/how-alarms-work.html#closing-an-alarm","x":"What you do\nWhat happens\nPress “HOME”, or touch “Close”\nThis alarm closes. If more alarms are waiting, the next one opens. The other buttons do nothing in the window.\nHold “HOME”\nThe window closes and the panel returns to the home screen.\nWhile the window is open, the panel sounds the alarm signal and lights its alarm indicator. Closing a window does not repair the fault, and the same alarm comes back only after the fault has cleared and appeared again. The active alarms of a sensor stay listed on its status screen — The sensor status screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"How alarms work","h":"Alarms are hidden at the higher access levels","u":"en/mini/how-alarms-work.html#alarms-are-hidden-at-the-higher-access-levels","x":"At “Agronomist” and “Service” no alarm windows are shown. They are not lost: when you return to “Operator”, the panel drops the alarms that have cleared and shows the ones that are still true. See Access levels and User is Agronomist."},{"p":"mini","t":"How alarms work","h":"Not alarm windows","u":"en/mini/how-alarms-work.html#not-alarm-windows","x":"Message\nWhere\nTopic\n“SLOWER”, “FASTER”\nin place of the speed figure\nSpeed sensor alarms\n“NOT UNDER CONTROL”\non the seed diagram\nSeeding alarms"},{"p":"mini","t":"How alarms work","h":"The alarm topics","u":"en/mini/how-alarms-work.html#the-alarm-topics","x":"Any device · Speed sensor · Seeding · Seed sensor state · Fan and dispenser shaft · Hopper · Pressure · Engine · Dosing by weight · Access level · Tramline · Dispenser motors\nEach alarm has its own heading with the text exactly as on the screen. Under it: what the panel has found, and what to check, in the order a mechanic checks it — the quick checks on the panel first, then the machine, then the sensor itself.\nNOTE\nIf the checks do not clear the fault, call the service. The contact details are in the data sheet of the system."},{"p":"mini","t":"Device does not answer: Connection lost, Wrong device type!","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-any-device.html","x":"The two alarms every sensor, motor or actuator of the Hectare Mini can raise — no answer from the device, or an answer from the wrong kind of device — and what to check."},{"p":"mini","t":"Device does not answer: Connection lost, Wrong device type!","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-any-device.html","x":"Every device on the sensor network can raise these two alarms. The device line of the alarm window tells you which one — see How alarms work."},{"p":"mini","t":"Device does not answer: Connection lost, Wrong device type!","h":"“Connection lost”","u":"en/mini/alarms-any-device.html#connection-lost","x":"What it means. The device has not answered three polls in a row. The panel no longer knows its state: nothing about it is watched until it answers again. Its indicator shows a red cross or XXXX; its status screen shows “No connection”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nWas the device recently added to the profile? Then it may not have its address yet — Device configuration.\nThe connectors of the device and of the harness: plugged in fully, pins clean and straight, no water inside.\nThe cable of the device and the harness: crushed, chafed or torn, especially at hinges and folding points.\nThe power supply of the device. It must match the values in the data sheet of the system.\nThe profile: does it list a device that the machine does not have? — Check the profile and the connections.\nThe device itself: swap it with a device of the same kind that works. If the fault moves with it, the device is faulty.\nWhen several devices of one kind are lost at once, look first at what they share: one input device, one HUB-8 concentrator, one branch of the harness."},{"p":"mini","t":"Device does not answer: Connection lost, Wrong device type!","h":"“Wrong device type!”","u":"en/mini/alarms-any-device.html#wrong-device-type","x":"What it means. Something answers on the address of this device, but not the kind of device the profile expects there — for example an input device where a pressure sensor should be.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe profile: the kind of each device as installed — The profile.\nWas a device replaced or re-addressed recently? Two devices may now share an address, or one has the address meant for another. Address it again — Device configuration.\nOn a HUB-8 concentrator: the type of each channel — Configure a HUB-8 concentrator.\nIf you have to keep working with a device that does not answer, you can take it out of control until it is repaired — Take a sensor into or out of control. It is then not watched at all."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed: SLOWER, FASTER and speed sensor alarms","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-speed-sensor.html","x":"What to do when the Hectare Mini shows SLOWER or FASTER, when a speed sensor fails, or when the speed limits cannot be met."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed: SLOWER, FASTER and speed sensor alarms","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-speed-sensor.html","x":"The travel speed outside its limits raises no alarm window. The speed indicator on the home screen shows “SLOWER” or “FASTER” instead of the figure, as long as the speed is wrong — see Indicators. The limits are “Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” of the speed sensor (Travel speed sensor); they are watched only while the panel is controlling the seeding."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed: SLOWER, FASTER and speed sensor alarms","h":"“SLOWER”","u":"en/mini/alarms-speed-sensor.html#slower","x":"What it means. The machine moves faster than “Max speed, km/h” permits.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nDrive slower.\nThe speed on the panel matches the real speed — compare with the tractor or a GPS.\nThe settings of the speed sensor: “Pulses”, “Diameter, cm”, “Factor, %”, “Max speed, km/h”.\nCalibrate the speed sensor — Calibrate the speed sensor.\nThe speed sensor itself is serviceable."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed: SLOWER, FASTER and speed sensor alarms","h":"“FASTER”","u":"en/mini/alarms-speed-sensor.html#faster","x":"What it means. The machine moves slower than “Min speed, km/h” permits.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nDrive faster.\nThe speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThe settings of the speed sensor, including “Min speed, km/h”.\nCalibrate the speed sensor.\nThe speed sensor itself is serviceable.\nWhile the speed is imitated, the indicator shows “INCREASE IMITATION” or “DECREASE IMITATION” instead: change “Imitation, km/h”."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed: SLOWER, FASTER and speed sensor alarms","h":"“Speed sensor #1 failed” / “Speed sensor #2 failed”","u":"en/mini/alarms-speed-sensor.html#speed-sensor-1-failed-speed-sensor-2-failed","x":"What it means. Only with two speed sensors on an input device (“Input device (2)” in the profile): the machine moves, but this sensor has given no pulse for too long.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe gap between the sensor and its target, and the target itself.\nThe connector and cable of the sensor up to the input device.\nThe sensor itself — swap the two sensors; if the fault moves, the sensor is faulty."},{"p":"mini","t":"Travel speed: SLOWER, FASTER and speed sensor alarms","h":"“Error in settings of Min/Max”","u":"en/mini/alarms-speed-sensor.html#error-in-settings-of-minmax","x":"What it means. On a machine whose motors dose by weight: a speed limit set on the speed sensor cannot be met together with the speeds the motors can dose at — the minimum lies at or above the highest dosing speed, or the maximum at or below the lowest.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe status screen of the speed sensor: it shows the limits in force and writes the impossible one in red — The sensor status screen.\n“Min speed, km/h” and “Max speed, km/h” of the speed sensor.\nThe target rates of the products and the speed limits of the motors — Calibrate a motor by weight."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding alarms: rate, skips, doubles, NOT UNDER CONTROL","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-seeding.html","x":"What to do when a seed or fertilizer line of the Hectare Mini sows too little or too much, has too many skips or doubles, sows into a tramline — or when the panel is not controlling at all."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding alarms: rate, skips, doubles, NOT UNDER CONTROL","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-seeding.html","x":"These alarms come from the seed sensors and the fertilizer sensors. The device line tells you which line — for example Seed sensor: #4. The limits are the settings of the group (Seed and fertilizer sensors). Check the speed first in every case: a wrong travel speed makes every rate per metre wrong."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding alarms: rate, skips, doubles, NOT UNDER CONTROL","h":"NOT UNDER CONTROL","u":"en/mini/alarms-seeding.html#not-under-control","x":"What it means. Not an alarm window: the seed diagram shows “NOT UNDER CONTROL”. Seed flows, but not through enough lines to meet the condition for control — fewer than “Percent of sowing,%” of the seed lines sow within the target. Until the condition is met the panel watches no seeding fault.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThe target rate — in “Flow” the alarm level — on the home screen: is it far above what the machine really sows? — Change the target rate.\n“Fraction” of the seed sensors — too high a value hides small seeds.\nEnough seed in the hoppers."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding alarms: rate, skips, doubles, NOT UNDER CONTROL","h":"“Under allowable rate”","u":"en/mini/alarms-seeding.html#under-allowable-rate","x":"What it means. A line sows less than permitted: in “Flow” below the alarm level, in “Hybrid” and “Precise” below the target by more than “Rate error,%”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nEnough seed in the hoppers.\nThe metering unit works and is set right.\n“Fraction” of the sensors — it may have to be lowered.\nThe sensor itself — its state on the status screen (The sensor status screen)."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding alarms: rate, skips, doubles, NOT UNDER CONTROL","h":"“Over allowable rate”","u":"en/mini/alarms-seeding.html#over-allowable-rate","x":"What it means. A line sows more than the target plus “Rate error,%”. Only in “Hybrid” and “Precise”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThe metering unit works and is set right.\n“Fraction” of the sensors — it may have to be raised.\nThe sensor itself — its state on the status screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding alarms: rate, skips, doubles, NOT UNDER CONTROL","h":"“High % of doubles”","u":"en/mini/alarms-seeding.html#high-of-doubles","x":"What it means. The share of doubles over the last stretch is above “Doubles error,%”. Only in “Precise”, and only if that setting is not “No”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe speed on the panel matches the real speed.\n“Doubles error,%” of the seed sensors.\nThe metering unit: vacuum, singulator, disk for the seed.\n“Fraction” — it may have to be raised.\nThe sensor itself — its state on the status screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding alarms: rate, skips, doubles, NOT UNDER CONTROL","h":"“High % of skips”","u":"en/mini/alarms-seeding.html#high-of-skips","x":"What it means. The share of skips over the last stretch is above “Skips error,%”. Only in “Precise”, and only if that setting is not “No”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe speed on the panel matches the real speed.\n“Skips error,%” of the seed sensors.\nEnough seed in the hoppers.\nThe metering unit: vacuum, singulator, disk for the seed.\n“Fraction” — it may have to be lowered.\nThe sensor itself — its state on the status screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding alarms: rate, skips, doubles, NOT UNDER CONTROL","h":"“Seeding to tramline”","u":"en/mini/alarms-seeding.html#seeding-to-tramline","x":"What it means. A line that should be shut off — in a tramline bout, or with “Product application” at “No” — passes more seed than “Toler.sowing to tramline,%” of the target rate.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe tramline actuator of that line shuts off — see Tramline alarms.\nThe setting “Relation of sensors to actuators” — the right lines assigned to the actuator (Seeding machine parameters).\n“Toler.sowing to tramline,%”."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seeding alarms: rate, skips, doubles, NOT UNDER CONTROL","h":"“Old firmware! Wrong S&D calc!”","u":"en/mini/alarms-seeding.html#old-firmware-wrong-sd-calc","x":"What it means. A fertilizer sensor running in “Precise” has firmware too old to count skips and doubles correctly.\nWhat to check: run the fertilizer sensors in “Flow” or “Hybrid”, or have the sensor replaced or updated by the service."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed sensor state: Clogged, Light struck, Broken","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-sensor-state.html","x":"What to do when a seed or fertilizer sensor of the Hectare Mini reports that its optical channel is blocked, that it is blinded by sunlight, or that it has failed."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed sensor state: Clogged, Light struck, Broken","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-sensor-state.html","x":"These three alarms are reported by the seed or fertilizer sensor itself about its own state. The device line names the sensor. Its status screen shows the same state in the line Status: — The sensor status screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed sensor state: Clogged, Light struck, Broken","h":"“Clogged”","u":"en/mini/alarms-sensor-state.html#clogged","x":"What it means. Something blocks the optical channel of the sensor. The coulter or the seed tube may be blocked.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe coulter and the seed tube: soil, a stone, wet seed.\nThe optical channel of the sensor: remove the blockage, clean off dust.\nThe power supply of the sensor — it must match the values in the data sheet of the system."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed sensor state: Clogged, Light struck, Broken","h":"“Light struck”","u":"en/mini/alarms-sensor-state.html#light-struck","x":"What it means. Too much light falls into the optical channel; the sensor cannot count. The panel reports it only while the machine moves (or always, without a speed sensor) — a raised implement at the headland does not cause it.\nWhat to check: keep direct sunlight out of the optical channel — the seat of an open sensor on its coulter, the covers, the position of the sensor."},{"p":"mini","t":"Seed sensor state: Clogged, Light struck, Broken","h":"“Broken”","u":"en/mini/alarms-sensor-state.html#broken","x":"What it means. The sensor has detected a fault in itself. On its status screen it reads “Faulty”. The sensor may have to be replaced.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe power supply of the sensor — it must match the values in the data sheet of the system.\nDisconnect and connect the sensor.\nIf it stays “Faulty”, replace the sensor. A new seed sensor needs the number of its row — Configure a seed sensor."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-rotation.html","x":"What to do when the fan or a dispenser shaft of the Hectare Mini turns too slowly, too fast, or not at all."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-rotation.html","x":"The fan sensor and the dispenser shaft sensor use the same two messages. The device line of the alarm window tells you which one — Fan or Dispenser, with its number. How the window works: How alarms work."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"“Low speed of rotation”","u":"en/mini/alarms-rotation.html#low-speed-of-rotation","x":"What it means. The fan turns slower than “Minimum, rpm”, or the dispenser shaft slower than the travel speed demands, by more than “Maximum error, %”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nDispenser only: the speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThe component being watched — the fan and its drive, or the dispenser and its drive.\nThe settings of the sensor: for the fan “Minimum, rpm” and “Pulses” — Fan sensor; for the dispenser “Maximum error, %” — Dispenser shaft sensor.\nThe sensor itself is serviceable.\nDispenser only: calibrate the rotation again — Calibrate the dispenser rotation."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"“High speed of rotation”","u":"en/mini/alarms-rotation.html#high-speed-of-rotation","x":"What it means. The fan turns faster than “Maximum, rpm” — this is watched also when the machine stands — or the dispenser shaft faster than the travel speed demands, by more than “Maximum error, %”.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nDispenser only: the speed on the panel matches the real speed.\nThe component being watched — the fan and its drive, or the dispenser and its drive.\nThe settings of the sensor: for the fan “Maximum, rpm” and “Pulses”; for the dispenser “Maximum error, %”.\nThe sensor itself is serviceable.\nDispenser only: calibrate the rotation again.\nThe dispenser shaft speed is watched only with “Rotation speed control” at “Yes”, and only while the machine moves."},{"p":"mini","t":"Fan or dispenser shaft: speed alarms","h":"“No rotation”","u":"en/mini/alarms-rotation.html#no-rotation","x":"What it means. The dispenser shaft gives no pulse for longer than “Period, s” while the machine sows or moves. Only the dispenser shaft sensor raises this alarm.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe dispenser and its drive — chain, clutch, shear pin.\n“Period, s” of the sensor: long enough for the slowest shaft speed at work — Dispenser shaft sensor.\nThe gap between the sensor and its target, and the sensor itself."},{"p":"mini","t":"Hopper empty","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-hopper.html","x":"What to do when the Hectare Mini reports that a hopper is empty."},{"p":"mini","t":"Hopper empty","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-hopper.html","x":"The hopper level sensor sits at a fixed height in the hopper. The device line names the hopper: Hopper: #2. On the home screen the hopper indicator turns red — see Indicators."},{"p":"mini","t":"Hopper empty","h":"“Hopper empty”","u":"en/mini/alarms-hopper.html#hopper-empty","x":"What it means. The product in the hopper has fallen below the height of the sensor.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nFill the hopper.\nIf the product is above the sensor and the alarm stays: product bridging or caked on the sensor — clean the sensor.\nThe status screen of the sensor: Pin: reads \"1\" while the panel sees the hopper empty — Hopper and work position sensors.\nThe sensor itself is serviceable.\nThe hopper sensor has no settings; an alarm that does not match the hopper is a matter of the sensor, its mounting height or its cable."},{"p":"mini","t":"Pressure alarms: High pressure, Low pressure","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-pressure.html","x":"What to do when the vacuum measured by a pressure sensor of the Hectare Mini is too high or too low."},{"p":"mini","t":"Pressure alarms: High pressure, Low pressure","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-pressure.html","x":"The panel compares the absolute value of the measured pressure with “Minimum, Pa” and “Maximum, Pa” of the sensor (Pressure sensor), while the machine sows or moves in the working position. The device line names the sensor: Pressure sensor: #1."},{"p":"mini","t":"Pressure alarms: High pressure, Low pressure","h":"“High pressure”","u":"en/mini/alarms-pressure.html#high-pressure","x":"What it means. The measured pressure is above “Maximum, Pa” — too much vacuum.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nLower the speed of the fan or turbine.\nThe damper of the air intake, if the machine has one.\nThe tube to the pressure sensor: connected at the right point, not kinked, not blocked.\n“Maximum, Pa” of the sensor."},{"p":"mini","t":"Pressure alarms: High pressure, Low pressure","h":"“Low pressure”","u":"en/mini/alarms-pressure.html#low-pressure","x":"What it means. The measured pressure is below “Minimum, Pa” — too little vacuum.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nRaise the speed of the fan or turbine.\nThe damper of the air intake, if the machine has one.\nThe air ducts and meters for leaks — hoses, seals, covers.\nThe tube to the pressure sensor: connected at the right point, not kinked, not blocked.\n“Minimum, Pa” of the sensor.\nThe measured pressure is on the status screen of the sensor, P= in kPa — compare it with the limits, which are set in Pa (1 kPa = 1000 Pa)."},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine alarms: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-engine.html","x":"What to do when a sensor of the separate fan-drive engine of the Hectare Mini reports low oil pressure, no battery charge, low fuel or a dirty air filter."},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine alarms: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-engine.html","x":"The sensors of a separate fan-drive engine all raise the same alarm text, “FAILURE”. The device line names the sensor — that line is what you look up here. The settings of these sensors: Engine sensors of the fan drive.\nThe ignition sensor raises no alarm. It keeps the oil pressure and battery charge alarms quiet while the ignition is off."},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine alarms: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“Emergency oil pressure”","u":"en/mini/alarms-engine.html#emergency-oil-pressure","x":"What it means. The low oil pressure sensor of the engine has triggered — with the ignition on, if there is an ignition sensor.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nStop the engine and check the oil level and the oil pressure.\nThe oil pressure sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” of the oil pressure sensor: compare Pin: on its status screen with the real state.\nIf the alarm comes with the engine off: is the ignition sensor in the profile and under control? Is it serviceable?"},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine alarms: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“No battery charge”","u":"en/mini/alarms-engine.html#no-battery-charge","x":"What it means. The battery is not being charged by the engine — with the ignition on, if there is an ignition sensor.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe alternator, its belt and the charging circuit of the engine.\nThe battery charge sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” of the battery charge sensor.\nIf the alarm comes with the engine off: is the ignition sensor in the profile and under control? Is it serviceable?"},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine alarms: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“Low fuel”","u":"en/mini/alarms-engine.html#low-fuel","x":"What it means. Little fuel is left in the tank of the engine.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nRefuel.\nThe fuel level sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” of the fuel level sensor.\n“Signal delay, sec” — fuel slopping in the tank on rough ground can trigger it; the default is 15 seconds."},{"p":"mini","t":"Engine alarms: oil pressure, battery charge, fuel, air filter","h":"“Air filter”","u":"en/mini/alarms-engine.html#air-filter","x":"What it means. The air filter of the engine is dirty.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nClean or replace the air filter.\nThe air filter sensor is serviceable.\n“Activation signal” of the air filter sensor.\n“Signal delay, sec” — the default is 5 seconds."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dosing by weight: product rate too low or too high","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html","x":"What to do when the Hectare Mini reports that a product dosed by weight is applied below or above its target rate, or cannot be dosed at all."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dosing by weight: product rate too low or too high","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html","x":"These are alarms of the system as a whole: the window has no device line. They come only when a product is followed by weight — by a dispenser shaft sensor with “Weight control” at “Yes”, or by dispenser motors. The number in the text is the number of the product, “Product 1” to “Product 3”."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dosing by weight: product rate too low or too high","h":"“Rate of Product 1 is to low!” / “Rate of Product 1 is to high!”","u":"en/mini/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html#rate-of-product-1-is-to-low-rate-of-product-1-is-to-high","x":"What it means. The weight of the product applied per hectare deviates from its target rate by more than “Product rate error,%” of the “Settings” menu (The Settings menu).\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe target rate of the product — Change the target rate.\nThe metering unit: roller, variator or gearbox setting, product flow from the hopper.\nThe settings of the dispenser shaft sensor that applies this product, and its “Type of product” — Dispenser shaft sensor.\nThe dispenser shaft sensor is serviceable.\nCalibrate by weight again — Calibrate the dispenser by weight, or for motors Calibrate a motor by weight. A new roller, another setting of the drive or another seed always need a new calibration."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dosing by weight: product rate too low or too high","h":"“Product dosage is impossible! Check product rate and calibration!”","u":"en/mini/alarms-dosing-by-weight.html#product-dosage-is-impossible-check-product-rate-and-calibration","x":"What it means. On a machine with dispenser motors: with the present target rates and calibrations no travel speed exists at which the motors could apply all products. The panel shows it again each time the seeding starts.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe target rates of the products.\nThe calibration of the motors and their shaft speed limits — Calibrate a motor by weight."},{"p":"mini","t":"User is Agronomist! Alarms will not be shown!","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-access-level.html","x":"The one window the Hectare Mini still shows at the Agronomist or Service level when seeding starts — what it means and how to get the alarms back."},{"p":"mini","t":"User is Agronomist! Alarms will not be shown!","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-access-level.html","x":"At the “Agronomist” and “Service” levels the panel shows no alarm windows (Access levels). This one is the exception."},{"p":"mini","t":"User is Agronomist! Alarms will not be shown!","h":"“User is Agronomist! Alarms will not be shown!”","u":"en/mini/alarms-access-level.html#user-is-agronomist-alarms-will-not-be-shown","x":"What it means. The panel sees seeding while the access level is “Agronomist” or “Service”. It warns you once that no seeding faults will be reported. The message comes only once for the whole time the higher level stays set; it comes again after you have gone down to “Operator” and up again.\nWhat to do: set the level back to “Operator” before you work — in “Settings” set “User” to “Operator” and press “MENU”, or switch the panel off and on. The alarms that are still true are then shown one after another.\nWARNING\nDo not sow at the Agronomist level. A blocked coulter, an empty hopper or a stopped fan stays unreported for the whole field."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline alarms: Illegal state, Stall","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-tramline.html","x":"What to do when a tramline actuator of the Hectare Mini reports both end positions at once, does not reach its position, or when seed passes a line shut off for a tramline."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline alarms: Illegal state, Stall","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-tramline.html","x":"A tramline actuator shuts off the seed lines of the tramline on the bouts the rhythm asks for (Next tramline bout). The device line names it: Tramline actuator: #1."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline alarms: Illegal state, Stall","h":"“Illegal state”","u":"en/mini/alarms-tramline.html#illegal-state","x":"What it means. The actuator reports both end positions at the same time — pulled in and pulled out. Its end position switches contradict each other.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe end position switches of the actuator and their wiring.\nDirt or ice holding a switch.\nThe actuator itself."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline alarms: Illegal state, Stall","h":"“Stall”","u":"en/mini/alarms-tramline.html#stall","x":"What it means. The actuator was told to move but has not reached the position asked for.\nWhat to check, in this order:\nThe shutter: blocked by seed, dirt or a bent part.\nThe power supply of the actuator.\nThe end position switch of the position asked for.\nThe actuator itself.\nOn its status screen the actuator shows Status: “Pulled in”, “Pulled out”, “Working” while it moves, or “Unknown” — The sensor status screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Tramline alarms: Illegal state, Stall","h":"“Seeding to tramline”","u":"en/mini/alarms-tramline.html#seeding-to-tramline","x":"Raised by the seed sensor of a line that should be shut off. See Seeding alarms."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dispenser motor alarms","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-electric-motors.html","x":"What the alarms of an electric dispenser motor of the Hectare Mini mean — voltage, temperature, short circuit, current, jamming — and what to check."},{"p":"mini","t":"Dispenser motor alarms","h":"","u":"en/mini/alarms-electric-motors.html","x":"The controller of each electric dispenser motor reports its faults to the panel. The device line names the motor: Electric motor: #5. The code of the last fault is on the status screen of the motor, line Last fault: — give it to the service. The motor and its settings: The context menu of a motor.\nAlarm text\nWhat it means\nWhat to check, in this order\n“Under voltage”\nThe supply voltage of the motor controller — for the motor or for its electronics — is too low.\nBattery and alternator of the tractor; the power cable and its connectors; the fuse.\n“Over voltage”\nThe supply voltage is too high.\nThe charging voltage of the tractor; the power supply of the system against the data sheet.\n“Over temperature”\nThe controller or its heat sink is too hot.\nLet it cool; a dispenser that runs hard; dirt covering the motor.\n“Short circuit”\nA short on the motor leads — between them, to ground or to the supply.\nThe motor cable and connector; the motor.\n“Over current”\nThe motor draws too much current.\nA dispenser that runs hard or is blocked; the motor.\n“Motor jammed!”\nThe motor is told to turn but does not turn. With “Rollback when jammed” at “Yes” it first runs back briefly to free itself.\nForeign matter in the dispenser; the drive between motor and dispenser; the motor.\n“Device in bootloader!”\nThe motor controller is in its update mode and does not run.\nSwitch the panel and the system off and on. If it stays, call the service.\n“FAILURE”\nAny other fault of the controller.\nNote Last fault: from the status screen and call the service.\nAll motors share one more alarm with every device: “Connection lost” — Device does not answer."},{"p":"mini","t":"The context menu of a dispenser motor","h":"","u":"en/mini/motor-context-menu.html","x":"Every item of the context menu of a dispenser electric motor — what it sets, from which access level it is visible, and what \"Reset to defaults\" really resets."},{"p":"mini","t":"The context menu of a dispenser motor","h":"","u":"en/mini/motor-context-menu.html","x":"On the Motors screen, hold “MENU”, select the motor with “UP” / “DOWN” and press “MENU”. What the menu holds depends on the access level and on the seeding mode of the motor.\nThe context menu of a seed motor, Agronomist level, machine standing still, Flow mode.\nItem\nLevel\nWhat it does\n“Information”\nAgronomist\nOpens the status screen of the motor.\n“Electric motors”\nService\nOpens the service screen of the motors — see The Motors screen.\n“Dispenser calibration(weight)”\nOperator\nStarts the calibration by weight. Shown only while the machine stands still.\n“Type of product”\nAgronomist\nWhich of the three products the motor applies — Assign a product to a motor.\n“Weight, gr” and “Rotations”\nAgronomist\nTogether they describe the dispenser: this weight of product for this number of shaft turns. Set by the calibration; change them by hand only to copy the values of an identical dispenser.\n“Minimum, rpm”, “Maximum, rpm”\nAgronomist\nThe permitted shaft speeds. From them the panel works out the travel speeds at which the rate can be applied. The minimum cannot reach the maximum: the panel moves the other value by one step.\n“Button input”, “Activation signal”\nAgronomist\nA push-button on the motor controller to run the motor from the machine during calibration. With “Button input” at “No”, calibration is run from the panel only.\n“Holes in disk”\nOperator\nThe number of cells in the seed disk; from it the panel works out the disk speed. Only in the “Precise” mode.\n“Numerator”, “Denominator”\nAgronomist\nThe gear ratio between motor and driven shaft, as the machine builder set it. Change it only after the drive has been rebuilt.\n“Rotation direction”\nAgronomist\n“Forward” or “Backward”, as the motor is fitted.\n“Rollback when jammed”\nAgronomist\n“Yes”: when the dispenser jams, the motor runs back briefly to free it. “No”: the motor is only stopped.\n“Reset to defaults”\nService\nResets this motor's own settings — see the caution below.\nFurther items at the Service level are tools of the service engineer and are not described here.\nWeight items only in “Flow” and “Hybrid”. “Dispenser calibration(weight)”, “Type of product”, “Weight, gr”, “Rotations”, the speed limits and the button items appear only if the motor works in “Flow” or “Hybrid”. In “Precise” the rate comes from the seed sensors, there is nothing to calibrate by weight, and “Holes in disk” appears instead.\nTaking the motor into or out of control is not in this menu: “PLUS” and “MINUS” on the Motors screen do that.\nCAUTION\n“Reset to defaults” is a reset, not a setting. “Holes in disk”, “Numerator”, “Denominator”, “Rotation direction” and “Rollback when jammed” of this motor return to their factory values, and the motor is taken back into control without asking. The calibration values, the product type and the speed limits stay as they are."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html","x":"What each of the seven buttons does with a short and a long press — and where the screen takes touches — on the home screen, in menus, in windows and on every working screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html","x":"The seven buttons are touch areas on the frame: a short press is a short touch; a long press is a touch held for about half a second. Holding a button never repeats it — one touch, one step. Where the buttons are: The panel and its seven buttons."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"Home screen — monitoring","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html#home-screen-monitoring","x":"Button\nWhat it does\n“HOME”\nSeeding table.\n“HOME”, long\nLocks or unlocks product application (machines with dispenser motors or a liquid pump). One short beep.\n“MENU”\nMain menu.\n“MENU”, long\nSensor setup mode.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nFirst press selects a rate field (it blinks); the next ones raise or lower its set value.\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nPrevious / next rate field. Nothing if there is only one.\n“BACK”\nReleases a blinking rate field; otherwise opens the tramline bout menu, if the tramline is on.\n“BACK”, long\nWhile seeding: sets the alarm level from the flow applied — Set the alarm level."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"Home screen — sensor setup mode","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html#home-screen-sensor-setup-mode","x":"Button\nWhat it does\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nPrevious / next device.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nTakes the selected sensor into / out of control, after a question the first time.\n“MENU”\nContext menu of the selected device.\n“BACK”\nLeaves the mode.\n“HOME”\nLeaves the mode and goes to the Seeding table."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"Seeding table","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html#seeding-table","x":"Button\nWhat it does\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nNext / previous table.\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nScroll one row.\n“MENU”\nAsks “Zero readings?”.\n“HOME” / “BACK”\nStatistics / home screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"Statistics","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html#statistics","x":"Button\nTouch\nWhat it does\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nSwipe down / up\nScroll one line.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nTwo-finger swipe right / left\nNext / previous field.\n“MENU”\nTwo-finger tap\nMenu of the screen.\n“HOME”\nLong swipe left\nMotors, or the rate control screen.\n“BACK”\n—\nHome screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"Motors","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html#motors","x":"Button\nWhat it does\n“MENU”, long\nSwitches on the selection.\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nPrevious / next motor or actuator.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nInto / out of control, after a question the first time.\n“MENU”\nContext menu of the selected motor.\n“BACK”\nLeaves the selection; then the home screen.\n“HOME”\nRate control screen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"Rate control","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html#rate-control","x":"Button\nWhat it does\n“HOME”\nHome screen.\n“HOME”, long\nLocks or unlocks product application.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nRate change state; then change the selected target.\n“MENU”, long\nSensor setup state.\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nPrevious / next element in either state.\n“MENU”\nContext menu of the selected element; on a product, its motors.\n“BACK”\nLeaves the state. Does not leave the screen.\nTouch and hold\nOn the settings symbol: main menu. On a product: its motors. On liquid fertilizer: the pump."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"Menus","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html#menus","x":"Button or touch\nWhat it does\n“UP” / “DOWN”\nMove the selection; at the end it jumps to the other end.\n“PLUS” / “MINUS”\nChange the value of the selected item by one step.\n“MENU”\nEnters the item; on a value between -[ and ]+, opens the number pad.\n“BACK”\nOne level up.\nTouch an item\nSelects it; a second touch enters it."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"Number pad","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html#number-pad","x":"Touch the digits. “OK” or “MENU” accepts; “BACK” cancels. Touch and hold the backspace to clear the number. Nothing else works. See The number pad."},{"p":"mini","t":"Buttons on every screen","h":"Windows","u":"en/mini/buttons-and-gestures.html#windows","x":"Window\nButtons\nQuestion\nThe two buttons named in the window, or touch “Yes” / “No”. “BACK” or a touch outside cancels.\nInformation\nCloses by itself, with a touch or with a button.\nAlarm\n“HOME” or touch “Close” — closes. “HOME”, long — closes and returns to the home screen. Other buttons do nothing.\nFill the disks\nOnly “BACK”: aborts, or closes when done."},{"p":"mini","t":"Terms used in this manual","h":"","u":"en/mini/terms.html","x":"The words of the Hectare Mini manual in one place — from active field and alarm level to singulation, spacing and tramline rhythm."},{"p":"mini","t":"Terms used in this manual","h":"","u":"en/mini/terms.html","x":"Term\nMeaning\nActive field\nThe field the panel writes its statistics to now, marked (A). Start a new field\nAlarm level\nIn the “Flow” mode: the flow in seeds per second under which a line counts as failing. Drawn as the control line. Set the alarm level\nBout\nOne pass of the drill across the field. Set up the tramline rhythm\nContext menu\nThe menu of one device, opened in the sensor setup mode. Interface basics\nControl line\nThe white line across a diagram: the alarm level or the target rate drawn.\nDoubles\nTwo seeds where one was intended, as a share.\nFlow, Hybrid, Precise\nThe three control modes of a diagram. Flow: seeds per second against an alarm level. Hybrid: seeds per metre against a target rate. Precise: seeds per metre with skips, doubles and quality. The section diagram\nIn control / out of control\nWhether the panel watches a sensor. A sensor out of control raises no alarms. Take a sensor into or out of control\nLock (work pause)\nProduct application stopped by hand: motors and pump stand still. Lock application by hand\nPopulation\nSeeds per hectare, shown in thousands (ks/Ha). Derived from the rate, the working width and the number of rows.\nProduct\nA material applied by weight, in kg/ha. Up to three: “Product 1” to “Product 3”.\nProfile\nThe list of the devices of the machine, set at commissioning. The profile\nQuality (singulation)\nThe share of seeds placed singly, as intended — what is left after doubles and skips.\nRate field\nThe figures beside a diagram: measured on top, set value below.\nRecalculation interval\nThe stretch of path (or time without a speed sensor) over which skips, doubles, quality and rate are collected before they are shown anew. Set by “Recalculation,m”. A short interval answers quickly but fluctuates; a long one is steadier.\nRhythm\nThe number of bouts and which device shuts off on which bout.\nSection, line\nOne seed or fertilizer line with its own sensor: one column of the diagram.\nSensor setup mode\nThe mode in which one device is selected to be switched or set. Hold “MENU”.\nSkips\nA gap where a seed was intended, as a share. Not sown counts the skips over stretches where nothing was sown at all.\nSpacing\nThe distance between seeds — the rate per metre turned round.\nTarget rate\nWhat you set: seeds per metre (“Hybrid”, “Precise”), kg/ha (products), L/ha (liquid). The population target follows from it and is not meant to be changed during the field.\nTramline\nThe lane left unsown for the sprayer. Next tramline bout\nUnknown area\nArea driven while a sensor had no connection or was out of control."},{"p":"mini","t":"Before you contact support","h":"","u":"en/mini/before-you-contact-us.html","x":"What to check, what to write down and which photos to take before you ask for help with a Hectare Mini — and whom to ask first."},{"p":"mini","t":"Before you contact support","h":"","u":"en/mini/before-you-contact-us.html","x":"Most faults can be found with the manual. Go through this list first; with it, support can help you faster."},{"p":"mini","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Checklist","u":"en/mini/before-you-contact-us.html#checklist","x":"The check after installation was done, and you know which step failed.\nYou looked up the text of the alarm in the alarm topics: How alarms work, Device does not answer, Seeding alarms, Seed sensor state, Fan or dispenser shaft, Hopper empty, Pressure alarms, Speed sensor alarms.\nYou did the checks that the topic gives, in the order it gives them."},{"p":"mini","t":"Before you contact support","h":"What to write down","u":"en/mini/before-you-contact-us.html#what-to-write-down","x":"The exact text of the alarm window, with the kind of device and its number — as on the screen.\nThe firmware version — Check after installation, step 7.\nThe machine and the number of rows.\nWhat was done before the fault — a repair, a new sensor, a changed setting, a new field."},{"p":"mini","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Which photos to attach","u":"en/mini/before-you-contact-us.html#which-photos-to-attach","x":"The sensor status screen of the sensor concerned — The sensor status screen.\nThe alarm window.\nHow the devices are connected: the connectors and the harness, so that every plug can be seen."},{"p":"mini","t":"Before you contact support","h":"Whom to contact","u":"en/mini/before-you-contact-us.html#whom-to-contact","x":"The dealer or the partner who installed the system. They know your machine and give the PIN of your panel.\nAgriwelt, if the dealer cannot help: support@agriwelt.com. Send the notes and the photos from the lists above in one message.\nThe contact details of the service are also in the data sheet of the system."}];